const e=[{id:1,code:"FIRE-101",icon:"Flame",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Fire science and combustion dynamics",subtitle:"Fire science and combustion dynamics",summary:"A chemical and physical analysis of fire: the fire tetrahedron, heat transfer mechanisms, the stages of fire development and the flashover and backdraft phenomena.",blocks:[{body:`Introduction: Fire is not merely a visible phenomenon; in engineering and chemistry it is defined as a process of rapid oxidation in which energy is released in the form of heat and light. Since ancient times humanity has used fire to advance civilization, but when fire is not properly controlled it becomes a destructive force. For safety engineers and civil defense specialists, understanding combustion dynamics is not only necessary; it is the fundamental basis for protecting life and property.

The scientific nature of fire: Fire is a complex exothermic (heat-releasing) chemical reaction. The process requires the correct balance between three or four principal elements. The difference between a controlled fire and a fire disaster depends on our understanding of how heat spreads and of the physical changes that materials undergo.

Aim of this module: We will move beyond a basic familiarity with fire toward a more detailed study of (1) the chemistry of combustion: how the molecules of a material break down and become combustible gases; (2) the physics of energy transfer: how heat moves by conduction, convection and radiation; (3) fire behavior in enclosed spaces: an analysis of lethal phenomena such as flashover and backdraft. This knowledge is fundamental for every university student or engineer who intends to design protection systems and firefighting strategies on a scientific basis.`},{heading:"1. Fire tetrahedron theory: a dynamic analysis",body:'At the academic and engineering level, we move beyond the concept of the "fire triangle" and rely on the "fire tetrahedron." The key difference is that the tetrahedron shows how flaming combustion functions as a self-sustaining chemical process. If any one of these four elements is removed, or their balance is disrupted, the fire will go out.',points:["Fuel (reducing agent): fuel can exist in any of the three physical states (solid, liquid, gas). An important scientific note: solids and liquids do not themselves burn with a flame; a solid must first undergo thermal decomposition (pyrolysis) and a liquid must first vaporize. The surface-to-mass ratio is very important: the finer the material, the faster it enters the combustion process.","Oxidizing agent: in most situations, atmospheric oxygen (approximately 21%) is the main source. In general, if the oxygen concentration falls below approximately 16% (the exact figure depends on the fuel and the temperature), flaming combustion weakens or stops and becomes smoldering combustion. An advanced concept: some materials (such as ammonium nitrate) are themselves oxidizers and supply oxygen to the fire, which makes them very difficult to extinguish.","Heat (activation energy): the energy that initiates and sustains combustion. Thermal feedback is the physical phenomenon in which the fire radiates heat back to the fuel that has not yet burned. The autoignition temperature is the lowest temperature at which a material ignites by itself, without a spark or flame.","Uninhibited chemical chain reaction: this element turns a momentary flame into sustained combustion. Free radicals such as H, O and OH react very rapidly with new molecules and release energy. Some extinguishing agents (such as Halon and dry chemical powder) remove these radicals and thereby break the chain reaction.","Scientific notes: Vapor pressure: the higher it is, the more readily a material gives off vapor and the more easily it ignites. Flammability limits: fire occurs only within a suitable range of fuel-to-air ratio; if the gas concentration is too high (rich) or too low (lean), ignition will not occur."]},{heading:"2. Heat transfer mechanisms",body:"Fire does not stay in one place; it spreads by three different mechanisms and transfers energy to other materials. Understanding these mechanisms is essential for engineers in order to know how a fire grows and how it can be prevented:",points:["Conduction: the transfer of heat through direct contact within solid materials. For example, if one side of an iron door or a metal wall is heated by fire, the heat travels through the material to the other side and can bring materials on that side to their ignition temperature. Metals (such as iron, copper and aluminum) conduct heat very well (high thermal conductivity), whereas wood, plastic and concrete conduct it less well. This is one of the reasons why fire-resistant walls are built from materials of low conductivity.","Convection: the transfer of heat through the movement of liquids or gases. Hot air and combustion gases are lighter than cool air and therefore rise (the principle of buoyancy). This is one of the most dangerous mechanisms in enclosed buildings, because hot air, smoke and toxic gases accumulate beneath the ceiling and can reach about 600–800°C, depending on the fuel. When this hot layer reaches materials at a high level, it brings them to their ignition point and causes the fire to spread to other rooms and floors.","Radiation: the transfer of heat energy in the form of electromagnetic waves, particularly infrared radiation. Radiation requires neither direct contact nor air; energy is transferred even through a vacuum. A large fire can therefore bring a nearby building to its ignition temperature without any flame reaching it. For this reason, firefighters wear specialized protective clothing and maintain a safe distance from the fire."]},{heading:"3. Stages of fire development",body:"Every fire, from a small fire on a stove to a fire in a large building, generally passes through four standard stages. Recognizing these stages is essential for firefighters in order to know when to intervene and with which strategy:",points:["Incipient stage: the earliest stage of the fire. The temperature is still low, smoke is present but limited and the fire is still small and confined to one place. At this stage a trained person can control the fire with a portable extinguisher, after the alarm has been raised and 115 called. The oxygen concentration is still approximately 21%. This is the best time to extinguish the fire, because it is still small.","Growth stage: the fire grows rapidly; heat increases, smoke becomes heavy and flames reach nearby materials. The temperature reaches several hundred degrees Celsius and hot gases accumulate beneath the ceiling. At this stage there is a risk of rollover: the ignition of unburned gases in the hot layer beneath the ceiling, which spread as tongues of flame across the ceiling. Without intervention, the fire moves rapidly to the next stage.","Fully developed stage: the hottest and most dangerous stage. After flashover, all the combustible materials in the room have ignited and burn simultaneously. The temperature can exceed 1,000°C, depending on the fuel. Flames emerge from doors and windows and black smoke fills the entire space. Entering the room at this stage is extremely dangerous and is carried out only with full protection and a tactical plan. The fire consumes most of the available oxygen, so combustion becomes ventilation-limited.","Decay stage: once most of the fuel has burned or the oxygen has been depleted, the fire gradually weakens and the temperature falls, but a major danger still remains: backdraft. If a room is filled with unburned combustible gases and someone suddenly opens the door so that oxygen enters, a massive explosion can occur that may kill everyone nearby."]},{heading:"4. Dangerous fire phenomena",body:"In fire science there are two lethal phenomena that every engineer and firefighter must fully understand:",points:["Flashover: the sudden, simultaneous ignition of all the combustible materials in a room. It generally occurs when the temperature of the gas layer beneath the ceiling reaches approximately 500–600°C and the radiant heat from the ceiling brings every material in the room to its ignition point. This transition occurs within a very short time (a few seconds) and turns the room into a furnace. Warning signs of flashover: thick black smoke beneath the ceiling, tongues of flame beneath the ceiling (rollover), smoke being given off by furnishings and a sudden intense rise in heat. When firefighters see these signs, they must leave the room immediately or cool the gas layer with water.","Backdraft: a fire explosion that occurs when oxygen suddenly enters a hot, oxygen-depleted room that still contains large quantities of combustible gases and heat. Such a room is like a bomb waiting for oxygen. When the door is opened, fresh air rushes in, the combustible gases ignite all at once and an explosion occurs that propels a large fireball outward. Warning signs of backdraft: yellowish-brown smoke, windows blackened by smoke, a hot door, smoke pulsing in and out (pulsating smoke) and a whistling sound from the room. In these situations the door must not simply be opened; first, ventilation must be carried out from the highest point (vertical ventilation), or the door must be opened in a controlled manner from a protected position."]},{heading:"5. Thermal decomposition (pyrolysis)",body:"Pyrolysis is the chemical process in which solid materials break down under heat and become combustible gases. In scientific terms, a solid material does not itself burn with a flame; it is the gases produced by pyrolysis that burn. For example, wood itself does not ignite, but when it reaches high temperatures (approximately 200–300°C, depending on the type of wood) its molecules break down and produce combustible volatile gases, and these vapors ignite and burn. The residue of this process is char, which consists mostly of carbon.",points:["During pyrolysis, plastic materials produce highly toxic gases such as hydrogen cyanide (HCN) and carbon monoxide (CO).","Materials such as upholstery foam and polystyrene undergo pyrolysis very rapidly and produce hazardous gases.","Knowing the pyrolysis temperature of materials is important for the design of building materials and furniture that are difficult to ignite."]},{heading:"6. Products of combustion and toxicity",body:"When materials burn, various gases are produced, some of which are lethal:",points:["Carbon monoxide (CO): an odorless, colorless but highly toxic gas. It binds to the hemoglobin in the blood and prevents oxygen from being transported to the tissues and the brain. Most fire deaths are caused by inhaling smoke and toxic gases (such as CO and HCN), not by burns to the body.","Carbon dioxide (CO₂): not a toxic gas at low concentrations, but at high concentrations it becomes toxic in its own right and also causes asphyxiation because it displaces oxygen.","Hydrogen cyanide (HCN): an extremely lethal gas produced by the combustion of plastics, foam and certain fabrics. At high concentrations it can kill within a very short time.","Hydrogen chloride (HCl): a corrosive gas produced by burning PVC (the plastic used in pipes and cable insulation); it damages the lungs and eyes.","Smoke: smoke is not merely a color; it is full of fine particulates, gases and chemical substances. Smoke can cause asphyxiation, temporary blindness and lung damage."]},{heading:"7. Fire classification: the NFPA system and the European EN 2 system",body:"To select the correct extinguisher, you must know the classification of fires. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. Each class requires a specific method of extinguishment:",points:["Class A (both systems) — ordinary solid combustibles: wood, paper, fabric, ordinary plastics. Extinguishment: cooling with water. Water, foam and ABC powder extinguishers are suitable.","Class B — flammable liquids: gasoline, oil, paint, solvents (under NFPA, flammable gases are also included in Class B; under EN 2, gases are Class C). Extinguishment (liquids): smothering with foam, CO₂ or ABC powder. A direct stream of water is unsuitable because it spreads the burning liquid. For alcohol, acetone and other polar solvents: CO₂, ABC powder or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam; ordinary foam breaks down on them. Gas: stop the gas first if it is safe; never put out a gas flame you cannot stop.","Class C (NFPA) — fires involving energized electrical equipment: panels, cables, appliances. Extinguishment: non-conductive agents such as CO₂ or dry chemical powder. If it is safe to do so, de-energize the equipment first. In the EN 2 system, electrical fires are not a separate class and Class C refers to flammable gases.","Class D (both systems) — combustible metals: magnesium, titanium, sodium, potassium, lithium (metal). Extinguishment: only an extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal (Class D powders such as graphite-based or sodium chloride-based powder); the agent differs from metal to metal (lithium, for example, needs an agent listed for lithium). Never use water, ordinary powder or foam: water is extremely dangerous because it can cause a violent reaction and an explosion. Dry sand only if the site fire plan or the metal's safety data sheet names it and it is completely dry. Note: lithium-ion batteries contain no metallic lithium; for fires involving them, water and continuous cooling are recommended.","Class K (NFPA) / Class F (EN 2) — cooking oils and fats: in restaurant and home kitchens. Extinguishment: a wet chemical extinguisher, which produces saponification: the oil turns into a soap-like layer that excludes oxygen and extinguishes the fire. Never pour water on burning oil."]},{heading:"8. An important note for students",body:"Understanding the fire tetrahedron is the key to selecting the correct extinguishing agent. If any one of the four elements of the tetrahedron is removed or interrupted, the fire goes out:",points:["Remove the fuel: move combustible materials away from the fire area, or shut off the gas supply.","Remove the heat: cool with water or with cooling agents.","Remove the oxygen: smother and cover the fire (for example with foam or a fire blanket).","Break the chain reaction: use dry chemical powder or special agents."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_01_fire_dynamics.mp4",title:"Fire dynamics",description:"A complete explanation of the stages of fire development and how fire spreads"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_01_tetrahedron.png",title:"The fire tetrahedron",description:"A complete diagram of the fire tetrahedron and its four principal elements"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_01_flashover.mp4",title:"The flashover phenomenon",description:"A laboratory test demonstrating flashover"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_01_heat_transfer.png",title:"Heat transfer mechanisms",description:"Conduction, convection and radiation, illustrated"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_01_backdraft.mp4",title:"The danger of backdraft",description:"How to recognize the warning signs"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_01_fire_classes.png",title:"Fire classification",description:"A table of fire classes according to NFPA and EN 2, with the class symbols"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_01_extinguisher_types.mp4",title:"Types of extinguishers",description:"How to select and use the correct extinguisher"}],quiz:[{question:"Which element is added to the fire triangle to form the fire tetrahedron?",options:[{text:"Heat from an outside source",correct:!1},{text:"The chemical chain reaction",correct:!0},{text:"A higher level of oxygen",correct:!1},{text:"Fuel",correct:!1}],explanation:"The chemical chain reaction is the fourth element that completes the fire tetrahedron. This process sustains the fire and allows it to propagate by itself."},{question:"Flashover marks the change from which stage to which?",options:[{text:"From incipient to growth",correct:!1},{text:"From growth to fully developed",correct:!0},{text:"From fully developed into decay",correct:!1},{text:"From decay to incipient",correct:!1}],explanation:"Flashover generally occurs at the end of the growth stage, when the temperature rises and all materials approach their ignition point, and carries the fire into the fully developed stage."},{question:"Which gas kills the most people in fires?",options:[{text:"Oxygen",correct:!1},{text:"Carbon monoxide (CO)",correct:!0},{text:"Nitrogen dioxide (NO₂)",correct:!1},{text:"Hydrogen",correct:!1}],explanation:"Carbon monoxide (CO) is the principal lethal gas in fires, because it is odorless and colorless and rapidly stops the transport of oxygen in the blood."},{question:"What is pyrolysis?",options:[{text:"Extinguishing a fire by cutting off its oxygen",correct:!1},{text:"The breakdown of a material by heat into combustible vapors",correct:!0},{text:"A sudden explosion of gases collected in a closed room",correct:!1},{text:"Cooling a burning material with water until the flames go out",correct:!1}],explanation:"Pyrolysis is a chemical process in which materials break down under heat and become combustible gases; these gases then ignite and burn."},{question:"Which extinguisher is best for a Class B fire (flammable liquids)?",options:[{text:"Plain water",correct:!1},{text:"Foam or CO₂",correct:!0},{text:"Soil",correct:!1},{text:"Air",correct:!1}],explanation:"Flammable liquid fires (Class B) require smothering. Foam or CO₂ is suitable because it cuts off the oxygen. Water is unsuitable because it spreads the burning liquid."},{question:"Which of the following is a warning sign of backdraft?",options:[{text:"Thin white smoke and a cool door",correct:!1},{text:"Yellowish-brown smoke and a hot door",correct:!0},{text:"Bright flames and fresh air entering the room",correct:!1},{text:"Light",correct:!1}],explanation:"Backdraft has specific warning signs: yellowish-brown smoke, a hot door and smoke pulsing in and out. These indicate that combustible gases are present in the room and are waiting for oxygen."}],steps:[{title:"Read the stage of the fire",detail:"Before any action, decide which stage the fire has reached: incipient, growth, fully developed or decay. The volume, color, speed and density of the smoke and the heat at the door show how far the fire has developed.",critical:!0},{title:"Recognize the signs of flashover",detail:"Thick black smoke under the ceiling, tongues of flame under the ceiling (rollover), smoke rising from the furniture and a sudden rise in heat: leave the room at once. This change happens within a few seconds.",critical:!0},{title:"Check for backdraft signs before a door is opened",detail:"Yellowish-brown smoke, windows blackened by smoke, a hot door, smoke pulsing in and out and a whistling sound: do not open the door and keep people away from it. The decision to open and ventilate belongs to the 115 team.",critical:!0},{title:"Use an extinguisher only at the incipient stage",detail:"The go/no-go check comes before PASS: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher suits it, the exit is behind you, others are warned and 115 is called, you are trained and smoke does not block the way out. If any answer is no, or the fire does not go out at once, get out and close the door.",critical:!0},{title:"Match the agent to the fuel",detail:"Ask which element of the fire tetrahedron the agent removes: heat (water for Class A), oxygen (foam or CO₂ for liquids) or the chain reaction (dry chemical powder). In a gas fire, stop the gas only if it is safe; never pour water on burning cooking oil."},{title:"Limit the spread and brief the team",detail:"Close doors as you leave: this limits oxygen and stops hot smoke moving by convection into other rooms. Then tell the 115 team what is burning, where and whether anyone is still inside."}],warnings:["Never open a door that shows backdraft signs: the sudden inrush of air can ignite the gases in the room all at once.","Never use water on burning cooking oil or a stream of water on a burning flammable liquid: water spreads the burning liquid and throws burning oil out.",'Do not stay in a room that shows growth-stage signs to "finish" the job: flashover comes within seconds and leaves no time to escape.'],tips:["When you assess a material, ask how fine it is: the higher the surface-to-mass ratio (dust, shavings, foam), the faster it ignites.","In design reviews, look for convection paths: open stairs, vertical shafts and gaps without firestopping.","Keep the SDS of flammable liquids at hand: vapor pressure and flammability limits show how readily the vapor ignites.","Treat smoke as fuel: it holds unburned gases and toxic products such as CO and HCN."],activity:{prompt:"You are a safety engineering student on a walk-through of a workshop with the safety officer. A small fire has started in a waste bin of paper and cardboard. There is little smoke, the alarm has been raised and 115 has been called. A water extinguisher and an ABC powder extinguisher are near you, the exit is behind you and you are trained. What is the best decision?",options:[{text:"I use the water or ABC extinguisher with PASS, because every answer of the check is yes; if it does not go out at once, I leave and close the door.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the fire is incipient and Class A, both extinguishers suit it and every condition of the check is met."},{text:"I wait to see whether the fire grows so that I know its class, then I choose an extinguisher.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the incipient stage is the best time; in the growth stage the risk of rollover and flashover appears."},{text:"I first open the windows wide so that the smoke clears and I can see better.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: fresh air feeds oxygen to the fire and can speed up its growth."},{text:"I pick up the burning bin and carry it out of the workshop through the corridor.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you can be burned, and burning pieces fall along the way and spread the fire."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are the duty safety officer of a university building. A fire has been reported in a closed storeroom on the second floor; the alarm is ringing and the 115 team is on the way. Staff report that the storeroom door is hot, the window is blackened and smoke is pulsing in and out around the door frame. A staff member wants to open the door to see whether anyone is inside. What do you decide?",options:[{text:"I stop him: these are backdraft signs. The door stays closed, nobody goes near it, we evacuate the floor and I give the information to the 115 team when it arrives.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: opening the door lets air into a hot, oxygen-starved room and can cause an explosion. Ventilation is the 115 team's decision."},{text:"We open the door quickly so that the smoke escapes and the room cools faster.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this is exactly what causes a backdraft; air rushes in and the gases ignite."},{text:"We open the door a little and use a CO₂ extinguisher through the gap.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the fire is not incipient and any opening lets air in; this work needs a protected team and a tactical plan."},{text:"We break the storeroom window from outside to ventilate the room.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: uncontrolled ventilation is the same inrush of air; the 115 team ventilates with a plan and from a protected position."}]},{prompt:"The 115 team arrives. The roll call at the assembly point shows that a technician is missing; he was last seen near the storeroom. The team commander asks you for information. What do you give first?",options:[{text:"The missing person's name and last known location, the floor plan, what is in the storeroom (such as flammable liquids or cylinders) and the signs we saw.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: life information and the hazards inside come first; they let the team search safely and quickly."},{text:"A summary of the fire-resistance ratings of the walls and columns from the design documents.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this may help later, but a person is missing and life information comes first."},{text:"I ask permission to go in with the team and help with the search.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: without protective equipment and suitable training you become another casualty; your job is information and accounting for people."},{text:"I say nothing until the fire is out, so that the team is not distracted from its work.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: without your information the team does not know that someone is missing or where to search."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: In a university workshop, a fire started in a pile of packing foam beside a machine. A student saw thick black smoke building up quickly under the ceiling. He remembered that foam undergoes pyrolysis quickly and gives off toxic gases, and that thick smoke under the ceiling is a sign that flashover is approaching. He did not try to fight the fire. He raised the alarm, told everyone to leave, closed the workshop door behind him and called 115. When the 115 team arrived, he told them what was burning and that nobody was left inside. The closed door slowed the spread of smoke into the corridor."},{id:2,code:"CHEM-202",icon:"FlaskConical",accent:"#9333ea",title:"Hazardous materials (HazMat) and chemical safety",subtitle:"Hazardous materials management and chemical safety",summary:"Identifying hazardous materials, the Globally Harmonized System (GHS), the NFPA 704 diamond, responding to chemical spills in laboratories and factories, and personal protection.",blocks:[{body:`In today's world we use thousands of chemical substances every day: in laboratories, factories, hospitals and even at home. Yet many of these substances are dangerous if they are used or stored incorrectly. Chemicals can poison, burn, explode or eat away and destroy other materials (corrosion). For this reason, everyone who works with chemicals, whether a laboratory student, a factory worker or an engineer, must be fully aware of the hazards and of the means of self-protection.

In this module we examine the subject of chemical safety. We will learn how to identify substances using standard systems such as GHS and NFPA 704, how to read a Safety Data Sheet (SDS) and what steps to take in the event of a chemical spill or incident.`},{heading:"1. The Globally Harmonized System (GHS) for hazard identification",body:"GHS is an international system developed under the auspices of the United Nations (UN) for classifying and labeling hazardous substances in a uniform way throughout the world. Its nine pictograms are as follows:",points:["Flame: flammable substances such as acetone, gasoline and alcohol.","Skull and Crossbones: substances with high acute toxicity.","Corrosion: acids and bases that burn the skin and corrode metals.","Exploding Bomb: explosive or unstable substances.","Exclamation Mark: less severe health hazards (such as skin irritation).","Environment: harm to the aquatic environment and to living organisms.","Health Hazard: long-term hazards (such as cancer and lung damage).","Gas Under Pressure: gases stored under pressure (cylinders).","Oxidizer: oxidizing substances that intensify fire."]},{heading:"2. The NFPA 704 diamond (fire diamond)",body:"A visual system for rapid hazard identification, displayed on doors and storage areas. Each of the blue, red and yellow sections carries a number from 0 to 4 (4 = the highest hazard):",points:["Blue — Health Hazard (0–4): the health hazard.","Red — Flammability (0–4): the fire hazard.","Yellow — Instability (0–4): instability and reactivity.","White — Special Hazards: W (reacts dangerously with water), OX (oxidizer), SA (simple asphyxiant gas)."]},{heading:"3. The safety data sheet (SDS): 16 sections",body:"Every hazardous chemical must have its own SDS. Its most important sections for emergency response are:",points:["Section 1: Identification.","Section 2: Hazard Identification.","Section 3: Composition.","Section 4: First Aid.","Section 5: Fire-fighting Measures.","Section 6: Accidental Release Measures.","Section 7: Handling and Storage.","Section 8: Exposure Controls and Personal Protection (PPE)."]},{heading:"4. Steps for a small chemical spill",body:"Structured steps for controlling a small spill, for trained laboratory users only. Identify the substance and put on PPE before you approach the spill. Important note: if the spill is large, the substance is unknown or highly toxic, or there is an odor or fire, do not attempt to control it yourself: evacuate the area and call 115.",points:["1. Stop and warn the people nearby; keep them away.","2. Identify the substance from the label or the SDS, from a safe distance.","3. Evaluate: clean it up yourself only if the spill is small, the substance is known and low-hazard, and you are trained for it. Otherwise leave, close the door behind you and call 115.","4. Put on the PPE the SDS lists and get the spill kit.","5. Contain the spill from the edges inward and clean it up with the kit.","6. Inform the person in charge and record the incident."]},{heading:"5. Personal protective equipment (PPE)",body:"Types of PPE for working with chemicals; the correct selection is made according to Section 8 of the SDS:",points:["Gloves: nitrile or neoprene for most chemicals; the correct type is chosen according to the SDS and the chemical resistance chart.","Safety glasses with side shields, or sealed goggles where there is a splash hazard.","Respiratory protection: an N95 mask filters only fine particles and provides no protection against chemical vapors and gases. For vapors and gases, a respirator fitted with cartridges suitable for that substance, as specified in the SDS, is required; for unknown substances, high concentrations or oxygen deficiency, only a self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA).","Laboratory coat: flame-resistant in areas where flammable substances are present.","Closed-toe shoes that protect the whole foot."]},{heading:"6. Chemical storage",body:"Rules for safe storage:",points:["Segregation: keep acids away from bases, and oxidizers away from flammable substances.","Temperature: if flammable substances need refrigeration, store them only in special explosion-proof refrigerators, never in an ordinary refrigerator.","Locking: keep toxic substances in locked cabinets.","Labeling: label every bottle and container with its name and GHS pictograms.","Lower shelves: keep heavy substances and glass containers on low shelves."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_02_ghs_symbols.png",title:"GHS pictograms",description:"The complete list of international pictograms"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_02_spill_cleanup.mp4",title:"Spill cleanup",description:"Using a spill kit"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_02_nfpa704.png",title:"NFPA 704",description:"An explanation of the colors and numbers"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_02_ppe_demo.mp4",title:"Donning PPE",description:"The correct way to put on and take off PPE"}],quiz:[{question:"In NFPA 704, what does the blue section indicate?",options:[{text:"Fire",correct:!1},{text:"Reactivity",correct:!1},{text:"Health",correct:!0},{text:"Special hazards",correct:!1}],explanation:"Blue indicates the health hazard."},{question:"Which section of the SDS covers first aid?",options:[{text:"Section 1",correct:!1},{text:"Section 4",correct:!0},{text:"Section 9",correct:!1},{text:"Section 11",correct:!1}],explanation:"Section 4 is dedicated to first aid measures."},{question:"What must you do before you approach a small chemical spill?",options:[{text:"Start cleaning it up at once, before it spreads across the floor",correct:!1},{text:"Identify it from the label or SDS and put on the PPE it lists",correct:!0},{text:"Smell it to find out what it is",correct:!1},{text:"Wipe it up with paper towels and throw them in the ordinary bin",correct:!1}],explanation:"First identify the substance from a safe distance and put on the right PPE. If the spill is large, unknown or dangerous, leave, close the door behind you and call 115."},{question:"For general laboratory work with most chemicals, which gloves are the best choice?",options:[{text:"Latex",correct:!1},{text:"Nitrile",correct:!0},{text:"Cotton",correct:!1},{text:"No gloves",correct:!1}],explanation:"Nitrile gloves are a good general choice for most chemicals; for any specific substance, however, the correct type is selected according to the SDS and the chemical resistance chart."},{question:"A drum's NFPA 704 diamond shows W in the white section and 3 in the yellow section. A colleague wants to hose the spill with water. What is the best response?",options:[{text:"Stop him: W means the product reacts dangerously with water.",correct:!0},{text:"Allow it, because water dilutes every chemical spill.",correct:!1},{text:"Allow it only with warm water so that the cleanup is faster.",correct:!1},{text:"Allow it, because the yellow section shows only the fire hazard.",correct:!1}],explanation:"W in the white section means a dangerous reaction with water. The yellow section shows instability and reactivity, not fire (fire is red)."},{question:"You arrive at an outdoor spill of an unknown liquid. From which direction do you approach?",options:[{text:"From upwind and, if you can, from higher ground.",correct:!0},{text:"From downwind, so that the smell reaches you and you can identify it.",correct:!1},{text:"From the lowest point, where the liquid collects.",correct:!1},{text:"From any direction, as long as you wear an N95 mask.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Vapor travels with the wind and collects in low places. Approaching from upwind and from higher ground keeps you out of it; an N95 does not protect against vapors and gases."}],steps:[{title:"Stop, warn and isolate",detail:"Move people away from the spill, isolate the area and deny entry. Approach only from upwind, and from higher ground if you can. Do not guess the isolation distance: follow the ERG guide or the site plan for that substance.",critical:!0},{title:"Identify the substance from a distance",detail:"Read the label, the GHS pictograms, the NFPA 704 diamond and the SDS. Treat an unknown substance as highly hazardous.",critical:!0},{title:"Decide: clean up or get out",detail:"Clean up yourself only if the spill is small, the substance is known and low-hazard and you are trained for it. Otherwise get out, close the door behind you and call 115 from outside the area.",critical:!0},{title:"Protect yourself",detail:"Put on the PPE that section 8 of the SDS lists. An N95 mask gives no protection against vapors and gases."},{title:"Contain and clean up",detail:"Contain the spill from the edges inward with the spill kit. Dispose of the used materials as hazardous waste under the site procedure, not in the ordinary bin."},{title:"Report and record",detail:"Inform the person in charge and record the incident. If 115 comes, give the team the substance name, the SDS, the number of people exposed and where the spill is flowing."}],warnings:["Never smell, touch or taste a substance to identify it: the dose that tells you what it is can be the dose that injures you.","Do not walk through a spill, vapor cloud or smoke, and do not approach from downwind: vapor travels with the wind and may be invisible.","Never mix or neutralize unknown chemicals yourself: the reaction can release heat or toxic gas."],tips:["Before work, read sections 2, 4, 6 and 8 of the SDS for every substance you will use.","Check the contents and location of the spill kit before you start work in the lab, not during the incident.","Look at the white section of NFPA 704: W means do not use water.","Keep a list of the chemicals and their storage locations ready for the 115 team."],activity:{prompt:"You are a teaching assistant in a chemistry lab. A student drops a bottle; a clear liquid spreads on the floor and a sharp smell fills the room. The label is torn and nobody knows what was inside. Two students say their eyes are stinging. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I get everyone out, close the door, keep people away from the area and call 115 from outside; I take the two students for first aid to their eyes under the site plan.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the substance is unknown and has a smell, so this is not a simple small spill; getting out and protecting people come first."},{text:"I put on nitrile gloves and an N95 mask and wipe it up with paper towels.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the substance is unknown and an N95 does not protect against vapors; you expose yourself to them."},{text:"I open the windows and stay in the room to watch the spill until the person in charge arrives.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: staying in the vapor puts you at risk; watching is done from outside a closed door."},{text:"I pour water on the spill to dilute the substance and reduce the smell.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: some substances react dangerously with water (W), and water spreads the spill further."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are the safety officer of a small factory. A forklift has punctured a drum in the storage yard. The drum label shows the GHS flame and skull-and-crossbones pictograms. The pool is flowing toward a drain and the wind is blowing toward the office building. What is your first decision?",options:[{text:"I isolate the area and deny entry, move people out of the downwind path (including the offices), approach only from upwind and call 115 with the product name from the label.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the product is flammable and toxic; isolating and moving people out of the vapor path come first."},{text:"I send two workers with shovels to block the drain at once.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: they would enter a toxic, flammable product without PPE and could become casualties."},{text:"I start the forklift again to move the drum away from the pool.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the engine is an ignition source in flammable vapor, and the machine drives into the spill."},{text:"I hose the pool into the drain so that it is diluted.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this carries the poison into sewers and water and spreads the flammable vapor."}]},{prompt:"People have been moved away and 115 is on the way. A worker who was splashed on the leg is standing at the assembly point and says he feels fine. What do you do next?",options:[{text:"I keep him apart from others, start first aid under section 4 of the SDS if it is safe and give 115 the product name, the SDS and the number of people exposed when it arrives.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the effects can be delayed; first aid under the SDS and briefing the team protect life."},{text:"I let him go home because he says he is fine and shows no signs.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the effects of toxic products can be delayed; he must be assessed."},{text:"I leave him with the other workers at the assembly point so that counting is easy.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: his contaminated clothing can contaminate others."},{text:"I ask him to go back and plug the drum because he has already been exposed.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: more exposure adds harm; nobody goes back into the area without PPE and training."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: In a university laboratory, a technician noticed a strong smell near a storage cabinet and saw liquid underneath it. She did not search for the source. She warned the people in the room, led them out, closed the door and called 115 from outside the area. She stood upwind and kept others away from the door. She gave the 115 team the cabinet's chemical list and the SDS for each substance. The team found a container leaking a corrosive liquid. Because the area had been isolated early, nobody was exposed."},{id:3,code:"ELEC-305",icon:"Zap",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Electrical safety and high voltage",subtitle:"Electrical safety, arc flash and high voltage",summary:"An analysis of electrical hazards, the effects of electric current on the human body, the arc flash phenomenon, the LOTO standard and Ohm's law as a basis for protection.",blocks:[{body:"Electricity is one of humanity's greatest achievements, but at the same time it is a silent and dangerous force. Every year, many people around the world lose their lives or are injured as a result of electric shock. Most of these incidents occur in factories, on construction projects and even at home, as a result of negligence or lack of knowledge."},{heading:"1. Physical principles and Ohm's law",body:"Ohm's law is the fundamental principle of electrical analysis:",points:["I = V / R (current = voltage ÷ resistance).","Voltage: the force that drives the electrons.","Resistance: a wet body = lower resistance = greater current and greater danger.","Amperage (current): it is the current that kills a person, not voltage alone."]},{heading:"2. Biological effects of electric current",body:"Effects of current on the body (approximate values for 50–60 Hz AC):",points:["1 mA: perception.","10–20 mA: let-go threshold (the person cannot release their grip on the source).","50–100 mA: possible ventricular fibrillation (an irregular heart rhythm).","100+ mA: cardiac arrest and internal burns become more likely."]},{heading:"3. The arc flash phenomenon",body:"An explosive release of energy between conductors:",points:["Heat: can reach approximately 19,400°C (about four times hotter than the surface of the sun).","Arc blast: an air pressure wave and flying fragments.","Intense radiation and light: eye damage, with temporary or permanent blindness.","Main causes of injury: working on energized equipment, dropped tools and lack of appropriate PPE."]},{heading:"4. The LOTO (lockout/tagout) standard",body:"The safety protocol during maintenance (in accordance with OSHA 1910.147):",points:["Lockout: locking the energy source.","Tagout: a warning tag (name, time, reason).","Zero energy: verifying with a voltmeter that no energy remains."]},{heading:"5. Personal protective equipment (electrical PPE)",body:"Personal protective equipment for electrical work:",points:["Insulating gloves (Class 0/1).","Arc-rated face shield.","Arc-rated (AR) clothing.","Insulating footwear (EH rated)."]},{heading:"6. First aid for electric shock",body:"First aid steps:",points:["First cut off the electrical supply; do not touch the casualty until the power has been disconnected.","Call 122 (ambulance); if there are fallen wires or a fire, also call 115.","If the person is unconscious and not breathing normally, start CPR immediately and use an AED.","Cool burns under cool running water for 20 minutes; anyone who has received an electric shock must be examined by a doctor."]},{heading:"7. Test before touch",body:'Treat every conductor as live until a test proves it dead. Before the test, check the voltage tester on a known live source; then test the circuit; then check the tester again on the same source. A faulty tester silently shows "zero" on a live circuit, and that can kill. Only a trained and authorized person carries out this test, wearing the PPE the task requires.'},{heading:"8. Fallen lines, wet locations and protective devices",points:["A fallen or damaged power line: do not approach it, keep others away and call 115; the ground around it may also be energized.","A residual current device (RCD, also called GFCI) cuts the power when current leaks to earth; use it with portable tools and in wet locations.","Do not use damaged cables, cracked plugs or overloaded sockets; take them out of service and report them.","Electrical work is done only by a qualified and authorized person; follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_03_loto_steps.mp4",title:"LOTO steps",description:"Locking out and tagging out"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_03_arc_flash.png",title:"Arc flash boundaries",description:"Safety distances"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_03_grounding.mp4",title:"Grounding",description:"The importance of the ground wire"}],quiz:[{question:"What is the lowest level of current that can cause ventricular fibrillation?",options:[{text:"1 mA",correct:!1},{text:"10–20 mA",correct:!1},{text:"50–100 mA",correct:!0},{text:"5,000–10,000 mA",correct:!1}],explanation:"A current of 50–100 milliamperes can cause an irregular heart rhythm (ventricular fibrillation)."},{question:"What is the purpose of LOTO?",options:[{text:"Reducing energy costs during maintenance work",correct:!1},{text:"Isolating the energy and preventing its return",correct:!0},{text:"Increasing the voltage to test the equipment",correct:!1},{text:"Locking the workshop to prevent theft of tools",correct:!1}],explanation:"LOTO ensures that no energy remains in the equipment during maintenance."},{question:"How does the body's resistance change?",options:[{text:"It always stays the same",correct:!1},{text:"It increases when wet",correct:!1},{text:"It decreases when wet",correct:!0},{text:"There is no relationship",correct:!1}],explanation:"When the skin becomes wet, resistance decreases, and the current and the danger increase."},{question:"A tester shows 0 V on a circuit you are about to repair. What must you do before you trust that reading?",options:[{text:"Check the tester again on a known live source.",correct:!0},{text:"Touch the conductor briefly with the back of your hand.",correct:!1},{text:"Repeat the test with the same tester on the same circuit.",correct:!1},{text:"Switch the circuit on and off again, then test.",correct:!1}],explanation:'A faulty tester can show "zero" on a live circuit. Checking it on a known source confirms that the reading is valid. Repeating the test with the same tester confirms nothing.'},{question:"Two technicians work on the same machine. How should it be locked out?",options:[{text:"Each technician puts his own lock and tag on the isolation point.",correct:!0},{text:"One lock from the senior technician protects them both.",correct:!1},{text:"One tag with both names and no lock is enough.",correct:!1},{text:"No lock is needed if they work together.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Each person is protected by his own lock: the machine cannot be started until the last person removes his. One lock for two people lets one of them remove it while the other is still working."},{question:"A worker slips on a wet floor and gets an electric shock while holding a drill. He is now alert and says he is fine. What is the right decision?",options:[{text:"He must be examined by a doctor even though he feels fine.",correct:!0},{text:"He can go back to work if no burns are visible on his hand.",correct:!1},{text:"He should rest for a while and then decide for himself.",correct:!1},{text:"He only needs to cool his hand with water at home.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Current can harm the heart and internal tissues without visible signs on the skin; that is why anyone who has had an electric shock must be examined by a doctor."}],steps:[{title:"Plan and notify",detail:"Identify every energy source of the equipment (electrical and stored energy). Before the shutdown, notify everyone whose work is affected."},{title:"Shut down and isolate",detail:"Stop the equipment with its normal control, then open the breaker or main isolator. A control switch on its own is not isolation.",critical:!0},{title:"Lock and tag",detail:"Each person who works puts his own lock and tag (name, time, reason) on every isolation point: one person, one lock.",critical:!0},{title:"Release stored energy",detail:"Release or restrain the energy left in the equipment (such as capacitors or springs) under the equipment procedure."},{title:"Verify zero energy",detail:"Verify with a voltage tester that no energy remains. Check the tester on a known live source before and after the test. Until this check, treat every conductor as live.",critical:!0},{title:"Restore safely",detail:"Remove the tools, make sure everyone is clear of the equipment and let each person remove only his own lock. Then restore power and record the work."}],warnings:["Never touch a person who is still in contact with an electrical source: the current can pass through you too; cut the supply first.",'Do not work on energized equipment "just for a minute": most arc-flash injuries happen during work on energized equipment and from dropped tools.',"Never remove another person's lock or tag: he may still be working on the equipment."],tips:["Keep hands and tools dry: wet skin lowers body resistance and raises the current.","Inspect insulating gloves for holes and damage before every use.","Label every panel and isolator so that the right one is found quickly in an emergency.","Record every LOTO in the site log with names and times."],activity:{prompt:'You are a new electrical engineer on a maintenance team. A motor must be repaired. The supervisor says: "I switched it off at the control panel; start now, we are late." What do you do?',options:[{text:"I apply full LOTO: isolate at the breaker, put on my own lock and tag, release stored energy and verify zero energy with a checked tester before I touch anything.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a control switch is not isolation and someone can switch it on by mistake. Only LOTO makes sure the energy cannot return."},{text:"I start work because the control switch is off and the motor is not turning.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a control switch can be turned on from elsewhere or by mistake; it is not isolation."},{text:"I ask a colleague to stand by the panel so that nobody switches it on.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a person is not a lock; he may be called away or switch it on by mistake."},{text:"I put a tag only, without a lock, because the job is short.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a tag can be removed or ignored; a lock physically prevents switching on."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are an engineer on a construction project. A worker collapses while holding a damaged extension cable; his hand is still on the cable and the cable is still plugged in. A colleague runs to pull him away by the arm. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I shout to stop the colleague, cut the power first at the breaker or switch, then call 122.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: until the power is cut, anyone who touches him becomes another casualty."},{text:"I let him pull; speed matters more than anything here.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the current will pass through the colleague too and there will be two casualties."},{text:"I pour water on the cable so that it cools and does not catch fire.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: water conducts electricity and lowers resistance, so the risk rises for everyone."},{text:"I start chest compressions at once while his hand is still on the cable.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: CPR only after the power is cut; otherwise you are exposed to the current too."}]},{prompt:"The power is now off. The worker does not respond and is not breathing normally. 122 has been called. An AED is in the site office. What do you do next?",options:[{text:"I start chest compressions at once and send someone for the AED; when it arrives I use it and follow its prompts.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: an unresponsive person who is not breathing normally needs immediate CPR and an AED; every delay lowers the chance of survival."},{text:"I wait for the ambulance; touching him may be dangerous even after the power is off.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the power is off; waiting without CPR lowers the chance of survival."},{text:"I first cool the burns on his hand with water, then check his breathing.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the absence of normal breathing comes first; burns come after CPR, when there is a chance."},{text:"I put him in the recovery position and wait.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the recovery position is only for someone breathing normally; this worker needs CPR."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: A maintenance team had to replace a damaged part in a distribution panel. The supervisor wanted to keep the panel energized so that a production line would not stop. A new engineer reminded the team that most arc-flash injuries happen during work on energized equipment. The team stopped the line, isolated the panel, put on their locks and tags and verified zero energy with a checked tester. During the work, a screwdriver slipped and touched two terminals. Because the panel was de-energized, nothing happened. The team recorded the job in the LOTO log."},{id:4,code:"MED-401",icon:"HeartPulse",accent:"#e11d48",title:"Advanced first aid and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR)",subtitle:"Advanced first aid, CPR and trauma management",summary:"The DRABCDE protocol, cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) in accordance with the AHA 2025 guidelines, use of the AED, control of life-threatening bleeding (Stop the Bleed) and casualty sorting (triage).",blocks:[{body:'First aid is not only a humanitarian act; it is a precise science that can make the difference between life and death. In emergency care we speak of the "Golden Hour" and the "Platinum Ten Minutes": the sooner a casualty receives treatment, the greater the chance of survival. In every medical emergency, call 122 (ambulance).'},{heading:"1. The DRABCDE protocol",body:"The primary sequence for assessing a casualty:",points:["D — Danger: check for danger to yourself and to the casualty.","R — Response: level of consciousness (AVPU).","A — Airway: open the airway using the head tilt–chin lift or the jaw thrust.","B — Breathing: check for normal breathing for no more than 10 seconds; if the person is unconscious and not breathing normally, start CPR immediately with chest compressions (the C-A-B sequence).","C — Circulation: check for life-threatening bleeding and signs of shock (health care professionals also check the carotid pulse).","D — Disability: check the level of consciousness, the eyes and the movement of the arms and legs.","E — Exposure: carry out a general examination of the body and cover the casualty to protect against cold."]},{heading:"2. High-quality CPR",body:"AHA 2025 guidelines:",points:["Position: the lower half of the breastbone (sternum).","Depth: 5–6 cm for all adults; approximately 5 cm for children; approximately 4 cm for infants (one third of the depth of the chest). For infants: the two-thumb encircling-hands technique, or the heel of one hand; the two-finger method is no longer recommended.","Rate: 100–120 compressions per minute.","Recoil: after each compression, allow the chest to return fully to its normal position.","Ratio: 30:2 (compressions:breaths); 15:2 for children and infants when two rescuers are present."]},{heading:"3. Using the AED",body:"The automated external defibrillator:",points:["Switch the device on and follow its prompts.",'"Stand clear": make sure that no one is touching the casualty.',"Deliver the shock, then resume CPR immediately."]},{heading:"4. Controlling life-threatening bleeding (Stop the Bleed)",body:"First make sure the scene is safe and call 122, then proceed in this order:",points:["Direct pressure: apply firm, continuous pressure to the wound with both hands. If the wound is deep, pack it with gauze or a clean cloth (wound packing) and maintain the pressure. If the dressing becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it; add another dressing on top.","Tourniquet: apply one early for life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg, 5–7 cm above the wound (not over a joint); tighten it until the bleeding stops, write down the time of application and never loosen it.","Shock: lay the casualty on their back, keep them warm and give nothing to eat or drink; in trauma cases do not raise the legs."]},{heading:"5. START triage",body:"Sorting casualties in mass-casualty incidents:",points:["Red (Immediate): life is in danger — first priority.","Yellow (Delayed): serious injuries, but the casualty can wait a short time.","Green (Minor): minor injuries — the casualty can walk.","Black (Expectant/Deceased): not breathing even after the airway has been opened."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_04_cpr_full.mp4",title:"CPR",description:"AHA 2025 guidelines"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_04_jaw_thrust.png",title:"Jaw thrust",description:"For suspected neck and spinal injuries"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_04_triage_sim.mp4",title:"Triage",description:"Sorting casualties"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_04_tourniquet.png",title:"Tourniquet",description:"Position and method of application (5–7 cm above the wound)"}],quiz:[{question:"If a neck injury is suspected, how do you open the airway?",options:[{text:"Head tilt",correct:!1},{text:"Jaw thrust",correct:!0},{text:"Moving the head",correct:!1},{text:"Nothing",correct:!1}],explanation:"The jaw thrust opens the airway without moving the neck."},{question:"What is the ratio of compressions to breaths in adult CPR?",options:[{text:"15:2",correct:!1},{text:"30:2",correct:!0},{text:"100:5",correct:!1},{text:"30:1",correct:!1}],explanation:"The standard for adults is 30 compressions to 2 breaths (15:2 for children and infants when two rescuers are present)."},{question:"In START triage, which color is assigned to a person who is still not breathing after the airway has been opened?",options:[{text:"Red",correct:!1},{text:"Yellow",correct:!1},{text:"Black",correct:!0},{text:"Green",correct:!1}],explanation:"Black (Expectant/Deceased): if the person is still not breathing after the airway has been opened."},{question:"What is the rate of chest compressions in CPR?",options:[{text:"60–80",correct:!1},{text:"80–100",correct:!1},{text:"100–120",correct:!0},{text:"140–160",correct:!1}],explanation:"100–120 compressions per minute."},{question:"A bystander who never learned rescue breaths finds an adult in cardiac arrest. What is the best advice?",options:[{text:"Give continuous chest compressions only (Hands-Only CPR).",correct:!0},{text:"Do not touch the person until a trained rescuer arrives.",correct:!1},{text:"Give breaths only, because compressions without training are dangerous.",correct:!1},{text:"Give compressions more slowly, about 60 per minute.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under AHA 2025, a bystander who is untrained or unwilling to give breaths gives Hands-Only CPR at 100–120 per minute. Waiting or slow compressions lower the chance of survival."},{question:"A worker has fallen from scaffolding. He does not respond but is breathing normally and the place is safe. Which action is right?",options:[{text:"Do not move him; call 122 and keep checking his breathing.",correct:!0},{text:"Roll him onto his side at once so that he does not choke.",correct:!1},{text:"Sit him up so that breathing is easier for him.",correct:!1},{text:"Give him water to see whether he can swallow.",correct:!1}],explanation:"After a fall, a neck or back injury is suspected: the person is not moved while the place is safe and CPR is not needed, and there is no recovery position. Nothing is given by mouth to an unresponsive person."}],steps:[{title:"Danger and response",detail:"Make sure the scene is safe, then check whether the person responds and shout for help.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 122 and get an AED",detail:"Name one person to call 122 (phone on speaker) and another to bring the AED.",critical:!0},{title:"Check breathing",detail:"For no more than 10 seconds. If the person is unresponsive and not breathing normally, start CPR at once (C-A-B). Irregular gasping is not normal breathing.",critical:!0},{title:"High-quality compressions",detail:"Lower half of the breastbone, 5–6 cm for all adults, 100–120 compressions per minute, full recoil after each one. If you are trained and willing: 30:2. If not: compressions only (Hands-Only CPR) without stopping.",critical:!0},{title:"Use the AED",detail:'When it arrives, switch on the AED and follow its prompts; "Stand clear": nobody touches the person; deliver the shock and resume CPR at once.',critical:!0},{title:"Continue until the team takes over",detail:"Continue CPR until the ambulance team takes over or the person starts breathing normally. If normal breathing returns and there is no suspected injury to the neck, back, hip or pelvis, place him in the recovery position and keep checking his breathing."}],warnings:["If you are not a health care professional, do not delay compressions to search for a pulse: an unresponsive person who is not breathing normally needs CPR at once.","Do not reduce compression depth for older adults: 5–6 cm applies to all adults; shallow compressions do not move blood to the brain.","After a fall, a crash or a blow to the head, neck or back, do not move the person unless the place is unsafe or CPR is needed; no recovery position with a suspected neck or back injury."],tips:["Count compressions aloud: it keeps the rate and tells the next rescuer when to take over.","Learn where the AEDs are in your building or campus before you need one.",'In a team, give tasks by name: "You, call 122", "You, bring the AED".',"If you are not trained or not willing to give breaths, give Hands-Only CPR without stopping; children, infants and drowning victims especially need breaths."],activity:{prompt:'You are a nursing student in a sports hall. A spectator collapses. He does not respond and is gasping irregularly. His friend says: "He is breathing, let him rest." What do you do?',options:[{text:"I treat it as no normal breathing: I have someone call 122, send someone for the AED and start compressions at once.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: gasping is not normal breathing and can be a sign of cardiac arrest; immediate CPR and an AED raise the chance of survival."},{text:"I put him in the recovery position and keep checking his breathing.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the recovery position is only for normal breathing; gasping is not normal breathing."},{text:"I give him some water so that he comes round.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: nothing is given by mouth to an unresponsive person; it can choke him."},{text:"I check his pulse for a full minute before deciding.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the check takes no more than 10 seconds; every delay lowers the chance of survival."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are the safety officer at a university. A staff member collapses in a corridor. You are trained in CPR; two untrained students are with you. After your check, he does not respond and is not breathing normally. How do you organize the response?",options:[{text:"One student calls 122 on speaker, the other brings the AED and I start compressions at once.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: giving tasks by name lets compressions, the call and the AED start together without delay."},{text:"I call 122 myself and start CPR after I have answered all the questions.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you are the only trained person; the call can be delegated, and this way compressions are delayed."},{text:"Both students look for a doctor in the building before anyone starts.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: searching wastes time; CPR and the AED must start at once."},{text:"I give five rescue breaths first, then start compressions.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: for adults the order is C-A-B: compressions come first."}]},{prompt:"The AED arrives. One of the students wants to help but is not trained in rescue breaths. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I switch on the AED and follow its prompts; nobody touches him during the shock; then CPR at once; the student takes over with Hands-Only CPR when I tire.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the AED and CPR work together, and Hands-Only CPR from someone not trained in breaths is far better than nothing."},{text:"I stop CPR while I read the written instructions on the AED.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the AED gives voice prompts; stopping compressions without need cuts blood flow to the brain."},{text:"I switch off the AED after one shock and wait for the ambulance.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: keep the AED on and follow its prompts; CPR continues until the team arrives."},{text:"I ask the student to give mouth-to-mouth breaths because 30:2 is required.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: 30:2 is for a trained and willing rescuer; an untrained one gives Hands-Only CPR."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: During a lecture, a professor collapsed. A student checked him: he did not respond and was not breathing normally. She asked one classmate by name to call 122 and put the phone on speaker, and another to bring the AED from the corridor. She started chest compressions at once in the center of the chest, hard and fast. When the AED arrived, she followed its voice prompts, made sure nobody touched him during the shock and resumed compressions straight away. Two classmates who had learned Hands-Only CPR took turns with her until the ambulance team took over."},{id:5,code:"STRUC-500",icon:"Building2",accent:"#0891b2",title:"Building safety engineering",subtitle:"Building safety, fire codes and life safety systems",summary:"Engineering codes (IBC/NFPA), fire alarm and suppression systems, fire doors and the design of emergency egress routes.",blocks:[{body:'A building is not only a place to live and work; it must be designed as a "life safety system." Many fire disasters show that engineering deficiencies, such as blocked exits or a lack of fire compartmentation, prevent rapid evacuation and increase the number of victims. Correct design that incorporates alarm systems, automatic suppression and fire-resistant materials can therefore make the difference between life and death. In this module we discuss international codes such as NFPA 101 and the IBC.'},{heading:"1. Building codes and international safety",body:"A code is a set of technical rules that specifies standards for design and construction. Two principal codes are followed in many countries:",points:["IBC (International Building Code): the international building code covering structural aspects, fire protection and life safety (electrical and mechanical systems are regulated by separate codes such as the NEC and the IMC). This code specifies for how many hours the columns and walls of a building must resist fire (fire-resistance rating).","NFPA 101 (Life Safety Code): dedicated to the protection of human life. This standard provides requirements for door widths, corridor lengths and emergency lighting.","Construction types: the IBC divides buildings into five types. Type I, built of concrete and protected steel, has the highest fire resistance, and Type V, built of wood, has the lowest.","Occupant load: the calculation of the maximum number of people who may be in the building at one time, in order to determine how many exits are required and how wide they must be."]},{heading:"2. Fire compartmentation and fire-resistant walls",body:"The aim of this strategy is to divide the building into separate sections (fire compartments) so that fire cannot easily pass from one section to another:",points:["Fire walls: special walls rated by the number of hours of resistance (generally 2 to 4 hours). These walls prevent heat and combustible gases from moving between sections of the building.","Fire doors: fire-resistant doors that must be self-closing and must never be propped open. These doors prevent smoke from entering the stairwells.","Fire dampers: automatic shutters inside air-handling (HVAC) ducts that close by themselves when the temperature rises, so that fire does not spread through the ductwork.","Firestopping: sealing all the openings and gaps in walls and floors through which electrical cables or pipes pass, using fire-resistant materials."]},{heading:"3. Automatic suppression systems",body:"These systems operate without human intervention and bring a fire under rapid control:",points:["Automatic sprinklers: among the most effective systems; according to the NFPA report U.S. Experience with Sprinklers, the death rate in home fires was approximately 89% lower in homes with sprinklers. Only the heads that have been reached by heat operate.","Sprinkler bulbs: the color of the liquid inside the glass bulb indicates the temperature at which it breaks (red = 68°C, the most common type).","Dry pipe and wet pipe: in very cold areas (such as cold-storage warehouses), a dry pipe system is used so that the water in the pipes does not freeze.","Clean agent systems: the use of gases such as FM-200 (HFC-227ea) or FK-5-1-12 (trade name Novec 1230) in server rooms and museums, so that water does not damage equipment and documents."]},{heading:"4. Fire alarm systems",body:"This is the brain of a building's safety, and its task is to detect fire and alert the occupants (NFPA 72):",points:["FACP (fire alarm control panel): the control center that receives signals from the detectors and issues the commands to sound the alarms and notify the fire service.","Smoke detectors: there are two main types: photoelectric detectors, which are better for slow, smoldering fires, and ionization detectors, which are better for fast, flaming fires.","Notification: the use of loud horns together with flashing lights (strobes) for people with hearing impairments.","Emergency power: during a power outage the system must run on batteries: 24 hours in standby mode followed by 5 minutes in alarm mode (NFPA 72)."]},{heading:"5. Emergency egress routes (means of egress)",body:"Designing the route of escape from any point in a building to the outside requires compliance with the following rules:",points:["Exit access: the route from inside a room to the exit door. There must be no obstructions (chairs, tables, furniture) in these routes.","Exit capacity: the width of doors and stairs must be based on the number of occupants. In general, corridors must be at least 1.12 meters (44 inches) wide, and 0.91 meters (36 inches) is permitted only for spaces with fewer than 50 occupants (IBC).","Direction of travel: exit doors serving 50 or more people (and high-hazard areas) must open outward, in the direction of egress, so that a crowd pressing against the door does not hold it shut. All exit doors must open from the inside without a key.","Exit signs: illuminated exit signs that remain visible in darkness and must be visible from all directions.","Dead-end corridors: the length of a dead-end corridor (one that does not lead to an exit) must generally not exceed 6 meters (20 feet); in some sprinklered buildings it may be longer."]},{heading:"6. High-rise safety",body:"Buildings with an occupied floor more than 23 meters (75 feet) above ground present special hazards and therefore require the following systems:",points:["Smoke control: a stairwell pressurization system that keeps smoke out so that occupants are not asphyxiated.","Fire command center: a dedicated room on the ground floor used by firefighting teams to control the systems of the entire building.","Fire service access elevators: special elevators that only firefighters may use during an emergency.","Areas of refuge: protected areas on upper floors for people with disabilities who cannot descend by the stairs."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_05_sprinkler_test.mp4",title:"Sprinkler test",description:"How the glass bulb breaks under the effect of heat"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_05_egress_plan.png",title:"Evacuation plan",description:"Standard design of egress routes according to NFPA"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_05_fire_damper.mp4",title:"How a fire damper works",description:"Preventing the spread of fire through air-handling ducts"}],quiz:[{question:"According to engineering codes, in which direction must the emergency exit doors of crowded spaces open?",options:[{text:"Inward (away from the direction of egress)",correct:!1},{text:"Outward (in the direction of egress)",correct:!0},{text:"Sliding",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"They must open outward, in the direction of egress, so that if a crowd presses against the door it opens easily and does not become an obstruction. The door must open from the inside without a key."},{question:"What is the main purpose of a stairwell pressurization system?",options:[{text:"Cooling the stairwell for the people escaping",correct:!1},{text:"Preventing smoke from entering the escape route",correct:!0},{text:"Increasing the lighting",correct:!1},{text:"Blowing air into the stairwell to put out the fire",correct:!1}],explanation:"This system creates a higher air pressure in the stairwell so that smoke cannot enter, and in this way the escape route stays clear."},{question:"What does the red color of a sprinkler's glass bulb indicate?",options:[{text:"A temperature rating of 68°C",correct:!0},{text:"A temperature rating of 141°C",correct:!1},{text:"It is only decorative",correct:!1},{text:"It indicates the presence of gas",correct:!1}],explanation:"Red is the most common type; it breaks at 68°C and sprays water."},{question:"According to the IBC, which construction type has the highest fire resistance?",options:[{text:"Type V (wood frame)",correct:!1},{text:"Type III (ordinary construction)",correct:!1},{text:"Type I (fire-resistive)",correct:!0},{text:"Type IV (heavy timber)",correct:!1}],explanation:"Type I is built of concrete and protected steel and is designed to resist a severe fire for several hours without collapsing."},{question:"An owner wants to protect paper archives in a cold, unheated warehouse where water in the pipes may freeze. Which system fits?",options:[{text:"A dry pipe sprinkler system.",correct:!0},{text:"A wet pipe system, because it is fastest in every case.",correct:!1},{text:"A clean agent, because water is never used on paper.",correct:!1},{text:"No system, because cold places do not burn.",correct:!1}],explanation:"In cold areas a dry pipe system is used so that water does not freeze in the pipes. Paper is Class A and water suits it; cold does not prevent fire."},{question:"In a review, which finding is the most urgent life-safety deficiency?",options:[{text:"An exit door that opens from inside only with a key.",correct:!0},{text:"A storeroom door without its room number on it.",correct:!1},{text:"A meeting room with fewer windows than the plan shows.",correct:!1},{text:"A reception desk that is smaller than in the design.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Exit doors must open from inside without a key; a door that needs a key can trap people inside during a fire. The other findings do not endanger life."}],steps:[{title:"Establish the use and the occupant load",detail:"Establish the building use and the maximum number of people at one time (occupant load); the number and width of exits are based on it. Compare the real number at peak times with the design number."},{title:"Check the means of egress",detail:"Walk the escape route from the farthest room to the outside: no obstructions, doors open from inside without a key, doors serving 50 or more people open outward, exit signs are lit and dead-end corridors are not too long.",critical:!0},{title:"Check compartmentation",detail:"Fire doors close by themselves and are not held open; every cable and pipe opening in fire walls is sealed with firestopping; fire dampers are in place.",critical:!0},{title:"Check detection and alarm",detail:"The FACP shows no faults, the detector type suits the hazard of the space, horns come with strobes and standby power is ready."},{title:"Check suppression",detail:"Sprinkler heads are not blocked by stored goods or new ceilings, and rooms protected by clean agent are closed and ready."},{title:"Record and report",detail:"Record each deficiency with a photo, location and date, explain why it is dangerous and propose a fix. Follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority."}],warnings:['Never lock or chain an exit door while the building is occupied, even "to stop theft": exit doors must open from inside without a key.',"Never prop open a fire door or remove its closer: smoke then enters the stairwell, which is the escape route.","Do not store goods in corridors, under stairs or in front of exits: they block the way out and add fuel."],tips:["Walk the escape route as an occupant would, from the farthest room to the outside.","Photograph each deficiency with the date and location for the report.","After any renovation, check new cable and pipe openings for firestopping.","Explain to staff why fire doors stay closed; a rule that people understand is kept better."],activity:{prompt:'You are a new engineer checking a renovated lecture hall designed for more than 50 people. You find: the main door now opens inward, another exit is padlocked "to stop theft" and a new bundle of cables passes through a fire wall with an open gap around it. The owner asks which item to fix before the hall reopens. Which answer shows the best judgement?',options:[{text:"The hall does not reopen until all three are fixed; the padlock and the inward door block escape at once.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: an inward door will not open under crowd pressure, a locked exit does not work and the gap lets fire and smoke pass through the wall."},{text:"Only the padlock; the direction of the door does not matter if it is wide.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a door serving 50 or more people must open outward so that crowd pressure does not hold it shut."},{text:"Only the firestopping gap, because the walls protect the whole building.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: compartmentation matters, but without working exits people cannot get out."},{text:"The hall reopens now and all three are fixed at the next maintenance.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: these deficiencies endanger lives from the first day; they cannot wait."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:'You are the safety engineer of a multi-story office building. On a routine walk-through you find the stairwell fire door wedged open on several floors, because staff say it is "easier for moving around". What is your first action?',options:[{text:"I remove the wedges now, check that each door closes and latches by itself and inform the building manager in writing.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: an open door lets smoke into the stairwell at any moment; an immediate fix and a written record are both needed."},{text:"I leave them for now and mention it in the annual report.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a fire does not wait for the report; the danger exists now."},{text:"I remove the door closers so that the doors stay open without wedges.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this turns the fire door into an ordinary door and leaves the stairwell unprotected."},{text:"I put up a sign asking staff to close the doors at night only.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a fire can start during working hours too, when more people are in the building."}]},{prompt:"A week later the wedges are back. The building manager says staff complain that the doors are heavy. What solution do you propose?",options:[{text:"Fix the cause: a qualified technician checks the closers; if doors must stay open, use hold-open devices linked to the fire alarm that release the doors on alarm, as the code allows and the competent authority approves; and explain the reason to staff.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this solves the staff complaint and the doors still close by themselves in a fire."},{text:"Only discipline the staff who use wedges and do nothing else.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the root cause (heavy doors) remains and the wedges will come back."},{text:"Replace the fire doors with light ordinary doors.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: ordinary doors do not resist fire and smoke, and the stairwell stays unprotected."},{text:"Accept the wedges during working hours because people are there to close the doors.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: in a fire nobody thinks of going back to remove the wedges; smoke spreads fast."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: During an inspection of a commercial building, a young engineer walked the escape routes from the farthest shop to the street. She found a stairwell fire door wedged open, boxes stored under the stairs and an exit sign whose light had failed. She also noticed a new bundle of cables passing through a fire wall without firestopping. She recorded each finding with a photo, location and date and explained to the manager how each one would let fire or smoke spread or slow down the evacuation. The deficiencies were corrected and checked again."},{id:6,code:"LAW-600",icon:"Scale",accent:"#475569",title:"Law and legal liability",subtitle:"Legal responsibilities, liability and compliance",summary:"An analysis of legal duties and rights, the penalties for negligence, the civil defense law in Iraq and the responsibility of institutions in relation to disasters.",blocks:[{body:`Safety is not only a moral duty; it is also an important legal obligation. In most countries the law states clearly that building owners, managers, engineers and even ordinary employees bear legal responsibility for ensuring a safe environment. If negligence occurs and causes harm to a person, it can lead to serious legal penalties (civil and criminal), ranging from financial compensation to imprisonment.

In this module we discuss the legal principles of safety, become familiar with the civil defense law of Iraq and learn how individuals can protect themselves and their institutions from legal claims by complying with standards. Note: this module is not legal advice; for any legal question, consult the legal office of the Civil Defense Directorate.`},{heading:"1. The duty of care",body:'The principle of the "duty of care" is one of the main pillars of liability law. Under this principle, any person or party that has control over a place or an activity is responsible for protecting the safety of the people who come to that place or take part in that activity:',points:["Building owners: responsible for providing fire suppression and fire alarm systems and for marking emergency egress routes.","Managers and supervisors: must provide safety training for employees and ensure that personal protective equipment (PPE) is used correctly.","Engineers and technicians: responsible for safe design and for carrying out periodic inspections of all equipment in accordance with approved codes and standards.","Conditions of the duty: for this duty to be established against you, three elements are generally required: 1. the risk was foreseeable; 2. there was a relationship of proximity between you and the injured person; 3. imposing the duty on you is fair, just and reasonable."]},{heading:"2. Negligence and breach of the law",body:"Negligence is the situation in which a person fails to fulfill their legal duty of protection and, as a result, another person suffers harm. Under common law, four elements are required to prove negligence in court. Iraqi civil law expresses the same principle through three elements: fault (transgression), damage and the causal link between them:",points:["1. Duty: it must be shown that you owed a legal duty to that person.","2. Breach of duty: you failed to do what was required (for example, you did not inspect the fire extinguishers).","3. Harm (damage): actual harm must have occurred (physical, material or psychological).","4. Causation: the breach of your duty must have directly caused that harm (for example, if you had repaired the fire alarm system, the injured person would have been able to escape).","Legal defenses: the accused party may defend themselves by proving that the injured person had voluntarily accepted the risk (assumption of risk), or that the harm resulted from an unforeseeable cause beyond human control (force majeure)."]},{heading:"3. The civil defense law in Iraq and the Kurdistan Region",body:"The legal framework for safety is regulated by several laws and official regulations:",points:["Iraqi Civil Defense Law No. 44 of 2013: the principal civil defense law in Iraq, which replaced the former Law No. 64 of 1978. This law assigns to the General Directorate of Civil Defense the duty of inspecting premises and monitoring compliance with safety measures. Its application in the Kurdistan Region should be confirmed with the Directorate's legal office.","Approval of building plans: under the law, municipalities send building plans to Civil Defense so that fire prevention and fire suppression measures can be reviewed before a construction permit is issued.","Inspections and penalties: the Duhok Civil Defense Directorate inspects residential and commercial projects; violations can lead to the closure of the premises and to fines or imprisonment in accordance with the law.","Safety permit: under the law, Civil Defense approval is required before a license is granted for any commercial or industrial activity (particularly the storage and use of hazardous materials)."]},{heading:"4. Corporate liability",body:'A company, as a "legal person," is responsible for incidents that occur on its projects:',points:["Vicarious liability: the company is responsible for the mistakes of its employees if they occur during working hours and in the course of their official duties.","Direct liability: if the company has not provided employees with the necessary training, or has used old and unsafe equipment, it is directly liable.","Financial losses: in addition to compensating the injured, the company suffers damage to its reputation and higher insurance premiums."]},{heading:"5. Legal protection strategies",body:"To protect life and to ensure legal protection, engineers and managers must take the following measures:",points:['Documentation: the golden rule says: "If it was not written down, it did not happen." Record every inspection, repair and training session with the date and a signature.',"Continuous training: ensure that all employees know the types of fire extinguishers and the evacuation protocol.","Internal inspections: before government inspection teams arrive, carry out periodic inspections yourself to identify safety deficiencies.","Formal contracts: between the owner and the contractors, the safety responsibilities of each party must be clearly set out in writing."]},{heading:"6. Ethics and professional responsibility",body:"Beyond the law, engineers have an ethical duty that must not be forgotten:",points:["Engineering ethics: protecting human life comes before financial profit. Never accept decisions that put safety at risk, even if management puts pressure on you.","Reporting: it is your ethical duty to report clearly any existing hazard before a disaster occurs."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_06_negligence_chart.png",title:"The negligence cycle",description:"The four legal elements required to prove negligence in court"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_06_legal_brief.mp4",title:"Legal liability",description:"An analysis of a real fire disaster case under Iraqi law"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_06_documentation_log.png",title:"Documentation example",description:"How to record safety inspections for legal protection"}],quiz:[{question:"Which of the following elements is NOT a requirement for proving negligence in court?",options:[{text:"Existence of a duty",correct:!1},{text:"Criminal intent",correct:!0},{text:"Harm",correct:!1},{text:"Causation (causal link)",correct:!1}],explanation:"Negligence does not require criminal intent; even if you did not cause harm deliberately, if you failed to fulfill your duty of protection you are legally liable."},{question:'What is meant by "vicarious liability"?',options:[{text:"The personal liability of an employee for their own mistakes",correct:!1},{text:"An institution's liability for the actions of its employees",correct:!0},{text:"Firefighting law",correct:!1},{text:"Compensation for damage done to the environment",correct:!1}],explanation:"This term means that an institution or company is responsible for the actions and mistakes of its employees while they are carrying out their duties."},{question:"In Iraq, which law regulates civil defense and the safety measures for institutions and buildings?",options:[{text:"Civil Defense Law No. 64 of 1978",correct:!1},{text:"Civil Defense Law No. 44 of 2013",correct:!0},{text:"Law No. 7 of 2005",correct:!1},{text:"The Traffic Law",correct:!1}],explanation:"Civil Defense Law No. 44 of 2013 is the principal civil defense law in Iraq; Law No. 64 of 1978 was repealed by this law. Confirm its application in the Kurdistan Region with the Directorate's legal office."},{question:'Complete the golden rule of legal protection: "If it was not written down..."',options:[{text:"It is not important",correct:!1},{text:"It can still be added later",correct:!1},{text:"It did not actually happen",correct:!0},{text:"It is legal",correct:!1}],explanation:"In court, records and written documents are the strongest evidence; any work that has not been recorded is treated as if it had never been done."},{question:"A known alarm fault was not repaired. A visitor slipped on a dry staircase and broke his leg; there was no fire. For the alarm fault, which element of negligence is missing?",options:[{text:"Causation: the alarm fault did not cause the injury.",correct:!0},{text:"Duty: owners owe no duty to visitors.",correct:!1},{text:"Harm: a broken leg does not count as harm.",correct:!1},{text:"Breach: not repairing a known fault is acceptable.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Duty, breach and harm are present, but the harm did not come from the alarm fault. Without causation this fault is not the cause of this harm (although it must still be repaired)."},{question:"A trained technician, during his official duties, closed a sprinkler valve by mistake and a fire spread. Under vicarious liability, who is responsible?",options:[{text:"The company too, since he acted during his official duties.",correct:!0},{text:"Only the technician, because the mistake was his alone.",correct:!1},{text:"Nobody, because it was an unintended accident.",correct:!1},{text:"Only the fire team, because it arrived after the spread.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under the principle in this module, a company answers for its employees' mistakes during official duties. This does not wholly remove personal responsibility; for a real case, ask the legal office."}],steps:[{title:"Identify foreseeable hazards",detail:"In the place or activity you control, look for hazards that a careful person could foresee: this is the basis of the duty of care."},{title:"Take reasonable measures",detail:"Remove or reduce the hazard: training, PPE, repair and inspection of systems under the approved codes and standards.",critical:!0},{title:"Document",detail:'Record every inspection, repair and training session with the date and a signature: "If it was not written down, it did not happen."'},{title:"Report hazards you cannot fix",detail:"Report in writing to the person in charge: what, where, when, the risk and the proposed fix. If life is in immediate danger, stop the work and keep people away until it is fixed.",critical:!0},{title:"Write responsibilities into contracts",detail:"Between the owner and the contractors, write down clearly each party's safety responsibilities."},{title:"Ask for legal advice",detail:"This module is not legal advice. For any legal question, consult the legal office of the Civil Defense Directorate."}],warnings:["Never sign a record for an inspection you did not do: a false record hides a real hazard and destroys the value of every record.","Do not accept pressure from management to skip safety measures: protecting life comes before financial profit.","Do not use this module as legal advice or quote laws and article numbers from memory; ask the legal office."],tips:["Use a standard checklist and a log with date, name and signature for every inspection.","Keep training attendance lists and the content of each session.","When you report a hazard, write what, where, when, the risk and the proposed fix.","Check the contracts to see who is responsible for each safety system."],activity:{prompt:`You are a new engineer in charge of building services. In your first week you find that the fire alarm panel in one building has shown a fault for a long time and the maintenance log is empty. Your manager says: "It has always been like this; don't make trouble." What is the best course of action?`,options:[{text:"I report it in writing (date, risk, fix), enter it in the log and follow up until a qualified technician repairs it; if nothing happens, I take it to a higher level in the organization.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: you now know of the hazard and it is foreseeable; a written report and follow-up meet the duty of care and professional ethics."},{text:"I say nothing; it was the previous engineer's responsibility.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you now control this work and know of the hazard; silence leaves the danger in place."},{text:"I disconnect the faulty zone from the panel myself so that the fault stops showing.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this disables detection in that zone completely, and you are not qualified for the work."},{text:"I write in the log that the panel was inspected and works well.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this is a false record that hides the hazard and breaks professional ethics."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are the safety supervisor on a construction project at a university. A subcontractor's worker is working at height without fall protection. The subcontractor says his workers are his responsibility, not yours. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop the work at once because I control the site and the risk is foreseeable; then I record it and inform the main contractor.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the duty of care rests on whoever controls the site; stopping the work protects life and the record shows what you did."},{text:"I let him continue; if something happens, it is the subcontractor's liability.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a life is at risk and you control the site; your duty does not disappear because another party says so."},{text:"I take a photo as evidence and let the work continue.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: records matter, but without stopping the work the danger remains; evidence does not protect the worker."},{text:"I warn him verbally and walk away.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a warning with no follow-up and no record does not stop the danger."}]},{prompt:"The work has stopped. The project manager asks how to prevent a repeat and protect the organization. What do you recommend?",options:[{text:"Write each party's safety responsibilities into the contract, give site induction training with attendance lists and carry out documented inspections.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: clear contracts, training and records both protect life and show that the duty of care was met."},{text:"Have the subcontractor sign that all liability is his.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a piece of paper does not remove your duty to prevent a foreseeable risk on a site you control."},{text:"Bring in more workers so that the job finishes sooner.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this does not reduce the risk; more people at height without protection means more risk."},{text:"Remove the subcontractor's name from the incident record.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: altering records is false and unethical and hides the lessons of the incident."}]}],realCase:`Illustrative scenario: After a small fire in a workshop, the owner was asked to show how the site had been kept safe. The safety engineer opened the records: inspection checklists with names, dates and signatures, the repair order for a faulty alarm detector and proof that it was completed, attendance lists from evacuation training and the contract that set out each party's safety duties. The records showed what had been checked and what had been done. The engineer's lesson for staff: "If it was not written down, it did not happen." Records protect people first, and then the institution.`},{id:7,code:"PSYCH-700",icon:"BrainCircuit",accent:"#0d9488",title:"Disaster psychology and human behavior",subtitle:"Disaster psychology, crowd dynamics and crisis leadership",summary:"An analysis of human biological and psychological responses during disasters, crowd management and the principles of emergency leadership.",blocks:[{body:"In major disasters, whether fire, earthquake or explosion, human behavior itself can become a major cause of casualties: hesitation, crowding at a single door and crowd pressure on the exits. For example, in the fire at The Station nightclub in the United States (2003), 100 people died; according to the NIST investigation, most people tried to leave through the main entrance by which they had come in, and crowding and blockage occurred there, even though other exits were available. Understanding crowd psychology is a core duty of every civil defense leader in order to manage evacuation rapidly and reduce casualties."},{heading:"1. The biological response to danger (fight-flight-freeze)",body:"When the brain perceives a threat to life, the sympathetic nervous system is activated and the body releases the hormones adrenaline and cortisol, which produce several types of response:",points:["Fight: the body prepares to defend itself. In disasters, this can lead to aggressive behavior toward others in order to get through.","Flight: an attempt to escape rapidly. This can cause a crowd to surge in a single direction.","Freeze: the person is unable to move or make a decision and stays in place like a statue. This is one of the most dangerous states, because the person remains in danger.","Fawn (appeasement): the person tries to placate the source of the threat, or follows the group without thinking.",'Changes in the brain: under fear, the amygdala takes control and the prefrontal cortex, which is responsible for rational decision-making, is weakened. This leads to "tunnel vision": seeing only one goal and ignoring other exits.']},{heading:"2. Crowd psychology and herd behavior",body:"In emergencies, people often imitate the majority without thinking, and this can lead to disaster. Note: disaster research shows that uncontrolled panic is less common than people assume; most people behave rationally, but a lack of information and guidance creates hesitation and crowding:",points:["Herd behavior: if one person runs, many others will run after them even if they do not know why. This spreads fear like a virus.","Funnel effect: everyone converging on a single door (often the door by which they entered), which leads to people being asphyxiated and trampled.","Panic contagion: the rapid spread of fear through a crowd, which weakens the authority of the leadership.","Deindividuation: when a person becomes part of a large group, their sense of personal responsibility decreases and they may behave in uncontrolled ways."]},{heading:"3. Crisis leadership",body:"Leadership during disasters requires calmness and direct commands in order to prevent panic:",points:["Calm presence: the leader must speak and act calmly; if the leader shows fear, the people will become afraid.",'Short, clear commands: instead of long speeches, use commands such as "Move to the right," "Stop" and "Follow me."',"Hand signals: when there is loud noise, use clear hand signals to direct people.","Isolating panickers: people who are screaming and spreading fear should be gently separated from the group so that they do not affect others."]},{heading:"4. Psychological first aid (PFA)",body:"The RAPID model (from Johns Hopkins University) is used to reduce psychological trauma among victims: R reflective listening, A assessment of needs, P prioritization, I intervention, D disposition (referral to the necessary services). Key points:",points:["R - Reflective listening: listen and reflect the person's feelings back to them; let the victim express their concerns without being judged.","A - Assessment: assess immediate needs (water, food, contact with family).","Safety and comfort: provide a calm place away from the scenes of the disaster.",'What not to do: never say "Everything will be fine" or "I know what you are going through," because these are unfounded promises and have a negative effect.']},{heading:"5. Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD)",body:"Post-traumatic stress disorder is a risk not only for victims but also for emergency response teams:",points:["Symptoms: nightmares, flashbacks (re-experiencing scenes of the incident) and hypervigilance.","Duration: if the symptoms persist for more than a month or disrupt daily life, help from mental health professionals is needed.","Resilience: psychological preparation before an incident, operational debriefing after missions and peer support. According to WHO guidance, compulsory single-session psychological debriefing is not recommended for preventing PTSD."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_07_crowd_dynamics.mp4",title:"Crowd dynamics",description:"An analysis of how people move under fear"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_07_pfa_model.png",title:"The RAPID model",description:"The steps of psychological first aid"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_07_leadership_drill.mp4",title:"Leadership training",description:"How to direct people by voice and hand signals"}],quiz:[{question:'What is meant by "freeze" in the biological response to fear?',options:[{text:"Running away from the danger as fast as possible",correct:!1},{text:"Fighting to protect one's life",correct:!1},{text:"Freezing and staying in place without moving",correct:!0},{text:"Conforming to the group",correct:!1}],explanation:"Freeze is a psychological state in which a person cannot make any decision or move, and this increases the risk of remaining trapped in the disaster."},{question:"According to the PFA protocol, which behavior is WRONG when helping a psychologically traumatized person?",options:[{text:"Listening calmly",correct:!1},{text:"Providing water, food and a quiet place",correct:!1},{text:'Saying "Everything will be fine"',correct:!0},{text:"Helping them contact their family",correct:!1}],explanation:`Making unfounded promises such as "It will be fine" has a negative effect and reduces the victim's trust.`},{question:"What is the best way for a civil defense leader to give commands during a crisis?",options:[{text:"Shouting and showing fear",correct:!1},{text:"Short, clear commands in a calm voice",correct:!0},{text:"Ignoring the people",correct:!1},{text:"Explaining the chemical details of the disaster",correct:!1}],explanation:"People need direct and clear guidance; the leader's calm reduces fear among the people."},{question:'How does the phenomenon of "herd behavior" work?',options:[{text:"Each person thinking and deciding on their own",correct:!1},{text:"Imitating the behavior of the majority without thinking",correct:!0},{text:"Following the evacuation rules and the instructions of staff",correct:!1},{text:"Rescuing others",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under fear, people are more inclined to follow others without considering whether that route is correct."},{question:"In a fire, people crowd toward the door they came in by while other exits are free. What explains this, and what is the leader's best response?",options:[{text:"Tunnel vision and the funnel effect; direct people by voice and gesture to other exits.",correct:!0},{text:"Panic contagion; stop everyone until they are completely calm.",correct:!1},{text:"Deindividuation; let the crowd sort out the problem by itself.",correct:!1},{text:"The fight response; block the main door with staff.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under fear, tunnel vision makes people see only the familiar door, which creates the funnel. Short commands and hand signals move them to other exits; stopping everyone or blocking the door adds danger."},{question:"Two months after a major incident, a rescuer still has nightmares and flashbacks that disrupt his work. What is the right step?",options:[{text:"Refer him to a mental health professional for help.",correct:!0},{text:"Arrange one compulsory debriefing session for him.",correct:!1},{text:"Tell him to take a holiday; it will pass by itself.",correct:!1},{text:"Give him extra missions to keep him busy.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The symptoms have lasted more than a month and disrupt his life, so he needs professional help. Under WHO guidance, a compulsory single-session debriefing is not recommended for preventing PTSD."}],steps:[{title:"Control yourself first",detail:"Speak and act calmly: if the leader shows fear, people become afraid."},{title:"Give short commands and point to exits",detail:'Use short, clear commands ("This way", "Stop") and hand signals. Direct people to the other exits too, not only to the door they came in by: tunnel vision keeps them from seeing other exits.',critical:!0},{title:"Break the freeze",detail:'Address the frozen person directly: use his name if you know it, give one simple command ("Stand up. Come with me.") and gently take his arm and lead him out.',critical:!0},{title:"Manage the funnel",detail:"Spread people across all exits and stop the crowd pushing at one door; post staff at the less-used exits to call people toward them.",critical:!0},{title:"After the evacuation: psychological first aid",detail:"After accounting for people, listen without judging, assess immediate needs (water, food, contact with family), provide a calm place and make no unfounded promises."},{title:"Look after your team",detail:"After the mission, hold an operational debriefing and offer peer support. If symptoms last more than a month or disrupt daily life, refer the person to mental health professionals. Do not hold a compulsory single-session psychological debriefing."}],warnings:["Never shout in panic or run ahead of the crowd: if the leader shows fear, the fear spreads through the crowd.",'Do not say "Everything will be fine" or "I know what you are going through": unfounded promises do harm.',"Do not force people to describe in detail what happened: listen if they want to talk, but do not pressure them."],tips:["In drills, post staff at the less-used exits to call people toward them.","Prepare a few short commands in the languages people in your building speak.","Agree hand signals with the team in advance so that they work in the noise.","Give people information early: a lack of information creates hesitation and crowding."],activity:{prompt:"You are a student volunteer helping to evacuate a crowded lecture hall after the fire alarm. Most people are pushing toward the main door; a nearby side exit is empty. What do you do?",options:[{text:'I stand at the side exit, call in a loud, calm voice "This way, exit here!" and point with my arm; I ask staff to redirect the back rows.',correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a short command and a hand signal break tunnel vision and spread the crowd across the exits."},{text:"I go to the main door and push people so that they get through faster.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: pushing adds crowd pressure at the door and creates a risk of trampling."},{text:"I wait outside until the crowd thins out.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: people need direction now; waiting loses the chance to spread the crowd out."},{text:'I shout "Fire! Hurry!" again and again.',correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this spreads fear without giving direction; people still head for the same door."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a civil defense trainee helping to evacuate a building after an earthquake. On the stairs a young woman stands frozen and does not respond to the people passing. Others are pushing around her. What do you do?",options:[{text:'I go to her, make eye contact, say one short command, "Come with me", gently take her arm and lead her out.',correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: freezing is one of the most dangerous states; direct speech and a simple command get the person moving again."},{text:"I leave her; she will move by herself when the crowd has passed.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a frozen person may not move and may stay in danger."},{text:"I shout at her very loudly to make her move.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: shouting adds fear and can deepen the freeze."},{text:"I explain to her in detail why the building is dangerous after an earthquake.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: under fear the brain cannot take in long explanations; a short command is needed."}]},{prompt:"At the assembly point she is crying and says she cannot find her brother. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stay with her, listen calmly, help her reach her family or the person counting people at the assembly point and give her water and a quiet place.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this is psychological first aid: listening, immediate needs, safety and contact with family."},{text:`I tell her: "Don't worry, everything will be fine; he is surely safe."`,correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you do not know that he is safe; an unfounded promise breaks trust and does harm."},{text:"I send her back into the building to look for her brother.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the building is dangerous after the earthquake; searching is the rescue teams' job."},{text:"I ask her to describe in detail everything she saw inside so that she can process it.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: pressing a person to tell the details can harm; only listen if she wants to talk."}]}],realCase:`Illustrative scenario: During an evacuation drill at a university, most students headed for the main door they had come in by that morning, and a queue formed there while two side exits stayed empty. In the next drill, staff stood at each side exit, called "This way" in a calm, loud voice and pointed with their arms. The students spread across all the exits. After the drill, the safety team explained the funnel effect and tunnel vision to the students and added the side exits to the building's evacuation map.`},{id:8,code:"ENV-800",icon:"Leaf",accent:"#16a34a",title:"Environmental safety and waste management",subtitle:"Environmental safety and waste management",summary:"The environmental impact of fire and chemicals, the correct management of medical waste, the protection of water sources and environmental responsibility.",blocks:[{body:"When we speak about safety, we must consider not only human safety but also the safety of the environment and of nature. Every disaster or safety incident (such as a fire or a chemical spill) leaves a long-term impact on the environment: smoke pollutes the air, hazardous waste is generated and firefighting water becomes toxic and mixes with groundwater. In this module we discuss waste management, the protection of water and air and the environmental responsibility of institutions in order to protect future generations."},{heading:"1. Medical and biological waste",body:"Hospitals, clinics and laboratories generate special types of waste that can transmit bacteria and viruses. This waste must not be discarded like ordinary garbage; it must be segregated by color. The colors differ from country to country; the World Health Organization (WHO) system is used here, and the hospital's internal guidelines must always be followed:",points:["Sharps (yellow): all items that can puncture the skin, such as needles, surgical blades and broken glass. They must be collected in rigid, puncture-resistant plastic containers (sharps containers).","Infectious/biohazard waste (yellow under WHO; red in some countries, such as the United States): items contaminated with blood or body tissue, and laboratory materials. It must be treated by autoclaving or destroyed by complete incineration.","General waste (black): clean paper, cardboard and plastics that carry no biological risk.","Pharmaceutical and chemical waste (brown under WHO): expired medicines or laboratory chemical waste, which must be treated by special procedures."]},{heading:"2. Water pollution from firefighting (fire runoff)",body:'The water used to extinguish large fires is not merely dirty water; it is a "liquid poison" full of chemicals and metals:',points:['Pollutants: it contains PAHs from burning plastics, heavy metals (such as lead and mercury) and hazardous firefighting foam substances such as PFAS, which are called "forever chemicals" because they do not break down in the environment.',"Containment: safety engineers must build temporary barriers so that this toxic water does not enter sewers or rivers.","Water treatment: this water must be collected and sent to dedicated wastewater treatment plants before it is discharged."]},{heading:"3. Air pollution and smoke chemistry",body:"Large fires, particularly industrial fires and wildfires, release highly hazardous particles into the air:",points:["Fine particulate matter (PM2.5): very small particles that penetrate deep into the lungs and cause heart and lung disease and cancer.","Toxic gases: such as carbon monoxide (CO) and hydrogen cyanide (HCN), which are produced when buildings burn and which poison the surrounding air.","Acid rain: gases such as SOx and NOx combine with rain and damage vegetation and forests."]},{heading:"4. Electronic waste (e-waste)",body:"Broken or burned electronic devices (phones, computers) are a major environmental hazard:",points:["Toxic metals: they contain lead, mercury and cadmium, which, if they enter water or soil, do not break down, persist for a very long time and endanger human health.","Recycling: these devices must not be discarded in public places; they must be sent to dedicated recycling facilities so that valuable metals (gold and silver) can be recovered."]},{heading:"5. Environmental responsibility and the ISO 14001 standard",body:"Every institution should have an environmental management system to reduce harm:",points:["The 3R principle: Reduce, Reuse and Recycle.","ISO 14001: a voluntary international standard for environmental management systems (EMS); institutions that adopt it establish an environmental policy, measurable objectives and periodic internal audits.","Monitoring and inspection: carrying out periodic testing of the soil and water around factories to confirm that no chemicals are being released."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_08_waste_colors.png",title:"The color system",description:"Segregating medical waste by color according to WHO"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_08_hazmat_disposal.mp4",title:"Disposal of hazardous materials",description:"Correct procedures for disposing of chemical waste"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_08_runoff_control.png",title:"Controlling firefighting water",description:"How to prevent toxic water from mixing with river water"}],quiz:[{question:"Under the WHO system, in which container are needles and medical blades discarded?",options:[{text:"Red bag (biohazard waste)",correct:!1},{text:"Black bag (general waste)",correct:!1},{text:"Rigid yellow box (sharps)",correct:!0},{text:"Blue bag",correct:!1}],explanation:"All sharp items must be discarded in a rigid yellow container (sharps container) so that they do not cause injuries or transmit disease."},{question:"What is the main danger of firefighting water (fire runoff)?",options:[{text:"It is only dirty water and dries quickly in the sun",correct:!1},{text:"It carries poisons and heavy metals into the environment",correct:!0},{text:"Its temperature is so high that it kills plants and fish",correct:!1},{text:"There is no danger, because the water dilutes the chemicals",correct:!1}],explanation:"This water carries poisons and burned chemicals with it and can contaminate surface water and groundwater."},{question:"What danger do PM2.5 particles in fire smoke pose to human health?",options:[{text:"They only sting the eyes and the skin for a short time",correct:!1},{text:"They go deep into the lungs and cause serious diseases",correct:!0},{text:"They cause no harm",correct:!1},{text:"They are too large to enter the lungs and are coughed out",correct:!1}],explanation:"PM2.5 are very small particles that penetrate deep into the lungs and cause long-term problems of the heart and respiratory system."},{question:'What is meant by "Recycle" in the 3R principle?',options:[{text:"Burning waste to produce heat and to reduce its volume",correct:!1},{text:"Recovering waste and reprocessing it into new products",correct:!0},{text:"Using fewer materials so that less waste is produced",correct:!1},{text:"Dumping waste outside the city",correct:!1}],explanation:"Recycling means converting waste into new materials in order to protect the environment."},{question:"After a fire in a computer shop, staff want to put burned laptops and phones into the city's general waste. What is the correct guidance?",options:[{text:"Send them to a dedicated e-waste recycling facility.",correct:!0},{text:"Bury them on site so that the metals stay in the ground.",correct:!1},{text:"Burn what is left so that the volume is smaller.",correct:!1},{text:"Put them into general waste in small amounts.",correct:!1}],explanation:"E-waste contains lead, mercury and cadmium, which do not break down in water or soil; it must go to dedicated recycling facilities. Burying and burning spread the danger."},{question:"A laboratory produces expired medicines and used chemical reagents. Under the WHO system, which stream do they go into?",options:[{text:"Pharmaceutical and chemical waste (brown), with special procedures.",correct:!0},{text:"General waste (black), once they are diluted with water.",correct:!1},{text:"The sharps box (yellow), because they come from a lab.",correct:!1},{text:"Infectious waste, to be autoclaved with the rest.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under WHO, expired medicines and laboratory chemical waste go into the brown stream and are treated by special procedures; dilution or autoclaving does not make them safe. Always follow the hospital's internal guidelines."}],steps:[{title:"People first, then the environment",detail:"In any incident, protect life first and follow the 115 team's orders; the environment is protected only from a safe position.",critical:!0},{title:"Contain firefighting runoff",detail:"With the incident commander's approval, block drains with temporary barriers so that toxic water does not enter sewers and rivers and can be collected for treatment."},{title:"Segregate waste at the source",detail:"Sharps in rigid, puncture-resistant containers; infectious, general and pharmaceutical-chemical waste under the color system your facility uses.",critical:!0},{title:"Store and label safely",detail:"Keep hazardous waste in a closed, labeled place until it is collected. Collect broken electronics and batteries in a separate labeled box for recycling."},{title:"Monitor",detail:"After the incident, test the water and soil around the site and record the results to confirm that nothing has spread."},{title:"Report and improve",detail:"Report the incident and feed the lessons into the environmental management system (ISO 14001): policy, measurable objectives and internal audits."}],warnings:["Never put sharps in bags or overfill a sharps container: needlestick injuries can transmit infection.","Never wash spills or firefighting runoff into drains or rivers: it carries poisons and heavy metals into the water.","Do not burn waste, cables or electronics in the open: they release toxic smoke and fine particles."],tips:["Place sharps containers where sharps are used, so that nobody walks around holding a needle.","Keep drain-blocking materials near chemical stores and loading areas.","Mark the drains on the site plan so that the 115 team can find them quickly.","Keep damaged batteries away from heat and apart from other waste until they are collected."],activity:{prompt:"You are an environmental engineer at a factory. The 115 team is fighting a warehouse fire. You see water and foam flowing across the yard toward a storm drain that leads to a nearby river. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I inform the incident commander and, with his approval and from a safe position, block the drain with barriers so that the water stays on site for collection.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: runoff carries poisons and metals; containing it in coordination with the commander protects the river without putting lives at risk."},{text:"I ask the firefighters to stop using water until the drain is blocked.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: protecting life and controlling the fire come first; tactical decisions belong to the commander."},{text:"I do nothing; the water dilutes the chemicals.",correct:!1,feedback:'Wrong: runoff is a "liquid poison" carrying PFAS and heavy metals; dilution does not remove them.'},{text:"I go into the smoke area to close a valve on the drain line.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: entering the smoke puts your life at risk; the environment is protected only from a safe position."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You manage waste in a teaching laboratory of a university hospital. A staff member put a used needle in a black general-waste bag, and the bag has split in the corridor. A cleaner is about to pick up the contents by hand. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I stop the cleaner, keep people away and, wearing gloves, pick up the sharps with a tool (such as tongs) and put them in a rigid sharps container.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: gloves do not stop a needle; a tool and a rigid container reduce the risk of a needlestick."},{text:"I let the cleaner carry on as long as he wears gloves.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a needle easily goes through a glove; sharps are not picked up by hand."},{text:"I sweep everything into another black bag.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the needle stays in general waste and someone else will be injured by it."},{text:"I tell him to throw it all into the general waste bin outside.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this moves the danger to waste collectors and the environment."}]},{prompt:"The area is now safe. How do you prevent this from happening again?",options:[{text:"I place sharps containers at the point of use, train staff on the hospital's color system and record and investigate the incident.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a nearby container, training and finding the cause stop the error at its source."},{text:"I ban the use of needles in the laboratory.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this is not realistic; lab work needs needles. The answer is correct segregation."},{text:"I replace the black bags with thicker bags.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: sharps would still be in the general-waste stream; only a rigid container is suitable."},{text:"I only put a warning notice on the wall.",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a notice alone does not fix the cause of the error (no container, no training)."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: A fire destroyed part of a plastics warehouse. While the 115 team fought the fire, the site's environmental engineer remembered the drain plan. With the incident commander's approval, staff placed barriers over the yard drains from a safe position. The firefighting water collected in the yard instead of reaching a nearby river. After the fire, the water was pumped into tanks and sent for treatment, and the soil near the warehouse was tested. The engineer then placed drain-blocking materials near the loading area and added them to the site emergency plan."},{id:9,code:"MGMT-900",icon:"Network",accent:"#2563eb",title:"The Incident Command System (ICS)",subtitle:"Incident Command System and strategic management",summary:"The standard Incident Command System (ICS) for managing incidents and disasters, interagency coordination and resource management.",blocks:[{body:"When a major disaster occurs, such as a flood, an earthquake or an industrial fire, the biggest problem is often not only a shortage of resources but a lack of coordination and organization. Dozens of different teams (firefighting, police, ambulance and volunteers) frequently arrive at the scene at the same time; without an organized system, this leads to confusion and duplication of effort. The Incident Command System (ICS) is the solution to this problem. The system was developed in the 1970s in the United States (the FIRESCOPE program in California); today it is part of the U.S. National Incident Management System, NIMS (FEMA), and has been adopted by many countries and organizations to manage crises in an organized way."},{heading:"1. The five major functions of ICS",body:"ICS works on the principle of dividing responsibilities. Every incident, whether small or large, requires these five functions (for certain incidents, an Intelligence/Investigations function can be added as a sixth function):",points:["1. Incident Commander (IC): the commander of the incident; a single person who makes the final decisions and is responsible for setting the main objectives and for the overall safety of the teams (in multi-agency incidents, Unified Command is used).","2. Operations: the section that carries out the direct work in the field (such as fire suppression, rescue or medical treatment).","3. Planning: collecting information about the incident, analyzing data and writing the Incident Action Plan (IAP) for the upcoming operational periods.","4. Logistics: meeting all needs (food, water, fuel, communications equipment and accommodation for the teams). Without logistics, field teams cannot sustain their work.","5. Finance/Administration: managing the financial side, recording working hours and equipment costs and handling compensation claims."]},{heading:"2. Span of control",body:"One of the most important principles of ICS is that no supervisor should be overloaded. This principle defines how many people a supervisor can effectively control:",points:["Appropriate number: according to NIMS, each supervisor should control 3 to 7 people or units, and the recommended ratio is 1 to 5.","Why 5? If the number exceeds 7, the supervisor loses control and errors occur; if it is fewer than 3, resources are wasted.","Modular organization: ICS expands in modules; whenever necessary, new units are activated so that the span of control remains within an appropriate range."]},{heading:"3. Common language and standard terminology",body:"In an emergency, a misunderstanding can cost lives. ICS requires all parties to communicate in a common, clear language:",points:["Plain language: ten-codes (such as 10-4) and agency-specific codes are not used; they are a legacy system and their meaning differs from one agency to another. Simple, clear language must be used so that all agencies (police, firefighting, ambulance) understand.",'Standard position titles: the names of positions and units must be the same at every incident (for example, Incident Commander, not "chief" or "person in charge" at one incident and some other title at another).',"Geographic division: dividing the incident scene into divisions in order to control each area separately, and into groups according to function."]},{heading:"4. The Incident Action Plan (IAP)",body:"The IAP is a written document that sets out the course of action for each operational period:",points:["Content: the plan states: What is the objective? Who is responsible? Which radio channel is to be used? And where is the medical post?","Plan duration: the plan is usually written for the next 12 to 24 hours.","SMART objectives: objectives must be specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound (for example: extinguish the fire in the northern warehouse within 4 hours)."]},{heading:"5. Unified Command",body:"When an incident affects several agencies or several administrative jurisdictions, Unified Command is used:",points:["Coordination: instead of each agency working independently, the officials of all agencies gather in one place and agree on a single plan.","Benefit: it prevents conflicts and ensures that the same task is not carried out twice by two teams.","ICP (Incident Command Post): a designated physical location where the incident commanders gather to manage the incident."]},{heading:"6. Demobilization",body:"Ending an operation in an organized way is as important as starting it:",points:["Demobilization plan: teams must be released in an orderly sequence so that no gaps are left in the field and all personnel and equipment are accounted for.","After-Action Review (AAR): holding an evaluation meeting after the incident to identify strengths and weaknesses, so that future operations improve."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_09_ics_structure.png",title:"ICS organizational structure",description:"A diagram of the five major functions of incident command"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_09_command_post.mp4",title:"Incident Command Post (ICP)",description:"How field coordination is managed during disasters"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_09_span_control.png",title:"Span of control",description:"An explanation of the principle of 1 supervisor for 5 personnel"}],quiz:[{question:"According to ICS/NIMS, what is the recommended span of control?",options:[{text:"10 people",correct:!1},{text:"5 people",correct:!0},{text:"2 people",correct:!1},{text:"15 people",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to NIMS, the span of control is between 3 and 7, and a ratio of 1 to 5 is recommended so that the supervisor can monitor the team closely."},{question:"Which section is responsible for providing food, water and communications equipment to the teams?",options:[{text:"Operations",correct:!1},{text:"Planning",correct:!1},{text:"Logistics",correct:!0},{text:"Finance",correct:!1}],explanation:"The Logistics Section is responsible for meeting all physical needs and providing services."},{question:'What is the main purpose of "plain language" in communications?',options:[{text:"So that people do not understand",correct:!1},{text:"To ensure error-free coordination among all agencies",correct:!0},{text:"To keep each radio message short by using special codes",correct:!1},{text:"To make radio messages sound more formal",correct:!1}],explanation:"Using simple, clear language (not codes) allows the police, firefighting and ambulance services to understand one another correctly."},{question:"When is Unified Command used?",options:[{text:"When the incident is small and affects only one area",correct:!1},{text:"When only one agency is responsible at the scene",correct:!1},{text:"When several agencies or jurisdictions are involved",correct:!0},{text:"It is never used",correct:!1}],explanation:"When several agencies or several administrative jurisdictions are involved in the incident, Unified Command is established so that all parties work to a single plan."},{question:"At a large incident you supervise 11 team leaders and important reports are being missed. What does ICS tell you to do?",options:[{text:"Group the teams into divisions or groups under supervisors, so that each one oversees about 5",correct:!0},{text:"Ask the team leaders for one written report only at the end of the shift",correct:!1},{text:"Keep all 11 leaders but move to a faster radio channel",correct:!1},{text:"Cut the teams to 3 by sending the rest away, even if work remains",correct:!1}],explanation:"The span of control is 3 to 7 (usually 5). When the number grows, ICS adds a new level (groups or divisions) in a modular way; cutting reports or teams does not solve the problem."},{question:"A police officer says on the shared channel: “10-50 at the north gate.” The fire crews do not understand. Which ICS principle was not followed?",options:[{text:"The use of plain language on shared channels",correct:!0},{text:"The right number to supervise (span of control)",correct:!1},{text:"The order for releasing teams at the end of the work",correct:!1},{text:"The SMART objectives in the incident action plan",correct:!1}],explanation:"Ten-codes mean different things to different agencies. ICS requires plain language so that police, fire and ambulance crews understand the same thing."}],steps:[{title:"Establish command",detail:"The first qualified person on scene takes command, announces their name and position in plain language and sets up the incident command post (ICP) in a safe place. No incident is left without a commander.",critical:!0},{title:"Size up and set objectives",detail:"Assess the hazards, the people at risk and the resources available. Then set SMART objectives; life safety (of the public and of the teams) always comes first.",critical:!0},{title:"Organize within the span of control",detail:"Activate only the sections you need. Each supervisor oversees 3 to 7 people or units (usually 5); when the number grows, open divisions or groups with new supervisors."},{title:"Write and brief the incident action plan",detail:"Prepare a written plan for each operational period: the objective, who is responsible, which radio channel and where the medical post is. Then brief the supervisors so that everyone works to one plan."},{title:"Coordinate and transfer command",detail:"If several agencies or jurisdictions are involved, set up unified command at the command post. When a new commander arrives, command is handed over with a clear face-to-face briefing."},{title:"Demobilize and review",detail:"Release teams in order, account for all personnel and equipment and hold an after-action review (AAR) so that weak points are fixed before the next incident."}],warnings:["Do not let teams act on their own outside the plan: it breaks accountability and a rescuer may end up somewhere no one knows about.","Do not use ten-codes or agency codes on a shared channel: the same code means something different to another agency.","Do not load one supervisor beyond the span of control: when the number passes 7, add another supervisor."],tips:["Use standard position titles and make roles visible (for example with a marked vest) so that everyone knows who holds which role.","Keep a simple log from the first minute: decisions, times and resources; Planning and Finance will need it.","Practise ICS with the other agencies in tabletop exercises before a real incident.","Write objectives that a team can check: who, what, where and by when."],activity:{prompt:"You are the first Civil Defense officer to arrive at a fire in a large warehouse. Police, an ambulance crew and the warehouse manager arrive at the same time and each starts giving orders. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I take and announce command, set up a command post and ask the agency leads to join me there as unified command",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: an incident needs a commander from the start, and with several agencies unified command prevents conflicting orders."},{text:"I let each agency work independently on its own part to save time",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: independent work leads to duplicated effort and gaps, and no one keeps track of who is where."},{text:"I start fighting the fire with the first crew right away and leave the organizing for later",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: if the commander is busy with a field task, no one coordinates and the organizing never happens."},{text:"I make no decisions until a more senior officer arrives and takes command",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: command is needed from the first minute; when the senior officer arrives, it is handed over with a briefing."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are the incident commander at a flash flood in a residential area. Many teams (Civil Defense, police, ambulance and volunteers) report to you directly by radio and you can no longer follow them all. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I divide the scene into geographic divisions and functional groups, each with a supervisor, so that I deal with only a few supervisors",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this is modular organization; the span of control returns to between 3 and 7 and no information is lost."},{text:"I ask all teams to report less often, only when something important happens",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the problem is how many people report to you directly, not how often; important information will be lost."},{text:"I hand the radio to an assistant and go into the field myself to see for myself",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the commander leaves the command post and the assistant inherits the same overload; nothing is solved."},{text:"I send the volunteers home so that fewer teams report to me",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you lose resources you need; it is better to place the volunteers in a group under a supervisor."}]},{prompt:"By morning the flood has reached a second jurisdiction and another agency’s teams arrive with their own commander. Each commander wants to work to their own plan. What next?",options:[{text:"We set up unified command at one command post and agree on one set of objectives and one IAP for the next operational period",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: each agency keeps its responsibilities, but everyone works to one plan and no task is done twice."},{text:"Each commander runs their own teams independently on their side of the boundary",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the flood ignores boundaries; two separate plans cause conflicts and gaps along the boundary."},{text:"The commander with more teams takes over everything and the other one leaves",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the other agency keeps legal responsibilities in its area; unified command brings those responsibilities together."},{text:"We keep yesterday’s plan unchanged so that no time is lost writing a new one",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: an IAP is written for each operational period; the situation and the agencies have changed, so the plan must be updated."}]}],realCase:"A fire broke out at an industrial site and several agencies arrived at once. In the first hour each team worked on its own: two teams searched the same building while no one entered another. The incident commander then set up unified command, divided the site into divisions, named one radio channel in plain language and wrote a short IAP. Every building was searched and every rescuer was accounted for. In the after-action review the teams said that plain language and a shared map were the most important changes, and joint exercises between the agencies were planned."},{id:10,code:"TECH-1000",icon:"Satellite",accent:"#7c3aed",title:"Future technology and innovation",subtitle:"Future technology in emergency response",summary:"The role of unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs), firefighting robots, thermal imaging cameras, artificial intelligence (AI) and IoT systems in rescue operations.",blocks:[{body:"Over the last ten years, technology has markedly changed the way civil defense and rescue work is carried out. New devices and smart sensors can reach places that people can access only with difficulty, allowing detailed information on hazards to be collected and help to arrive faster. In this module we discuss the technologies currently being developed to become part of civil protection systems around the world."},{heading:"1. Unmanned aerial vehicles (drones / UAVs)",body:"The drone is one of the greatest innovations in the emergency field. These devices can carry out difficult field tasks without putting the lives of rescue teams at risk:",points:["Aerial surveillance: drones can send real-time images and video to the command center so that the overall picture of the disaster becomes clear.","Thermal imaging: locating living people in forests, at night or in thick smoke by detecting body heat.","Emergency delivery: sending medicines, medical supplies or communications equipment to places cut off by floods or earthquakes.","Three-dimensional (3D) mapping: drones can produce a 3D map of the incident scene in a short time so that rescue and evacuation plans can be prepared more effectively."]},{heading:"2. Firefighting and technical rescue robots",body:"Robots act as a support force in places where the temperature or toxic gases exceed human capabilities:",points:["Firefighting robots: large remote-controlled machines that can approach the heart of a fire and spray water at high pressure (such as the Colossus robot).","Snake robots: slender robots that can enter the voids of collapsed buildings to locate casualties.","Exoskeletons: powered mechanical suits that increase firefighters' physical capabilities for carrying heavy loads with less fatigue.","HazMat robots: specialized robots for handling nuclear and chemical materials, so that people are not exposed to poisoning."]},{heading:"3. Artificial intelligence and big data analysis (AI & big data)",body:"Artificial intelligence can analyze millions of data points in a few seconds:",points:["Hazard prediction: analyzing weather and sensor data to predict the risk of floods or wildfires before they occur.","Intelligent response systems: AI can assist call-takers in prioritizing emergency calls (115) by urgency and dispatching the appropriate team; the final decision remains in human hands.","Computer vision: automatically detecting fire or smoke through city surveillance cameras and issuing rapid alerts."]},{heading:"4. The Internet of Things (IoT) and smart buildings",body:"The Internet of Things (IoT) connects devices to a network so that a building becomes an intelligent system:",points:["Smart buildings: sensors can automatically close fire doors and shut down the air-handling system so that smoke does not spread.","Wearables: smartwatches for emergency teams that transmit oxygen levels, heart rate and location (GPS) to the control center.","Connected vehicles: fire engines that can automatically turn traffic lights green (traffic signal preemption) so that they reach their destination faster."]},{heading:"5. Virtual reality (VR) and augmented reality (AR)",body:"Using visual technology for training and field operations:",points:["VR training: allows rescue teams to train on major disaster scenarios without any risk.","AR inside helmets: some new firefighting helmets contain a small display that shows the building plan and the air level in the SCBA cylinder while in smoke."]},{heading:"6. Nanotechnology and new materials",body:"Developing the equipment of the future for body protection:",points:["Nanotechnology: producing firefighting clothing that is lighter but more resistant to heat.","New batteries: sodium-ion or solid-state batteries, which carry a lower risk of ignition and explosion than ordinary lithium-ion batteries."]},{heading:"7. Challenges and ethical aspects",body:"Despite its great benefits, technology raises a number of important questions:",points:["Cost: the high price of these devices is an obstacle to their adoption by all countries.","Data protection: the risk of hacking into smart building systems or command systems.","AI decisions: is it right for artificial intelligence, instead of humans, to make decisions about human lives when sorting casualties (triage)?"]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_10_drone_thermal.mp4",title:"Drones and locating casualties",description:"How thermal cameras are used to locate people in forests"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_10_smart_helmet.jpg",title:"The smart AR helmet",description:"Displaying environmental information on the firefighter's helmet screen"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_10_robot_extinguish.mp4",title:"A firefighting robot in action",description:"Extinguishing a factory fire by remote control"}],quiz:[{question:"How do thermal imaging cameras on drones help rescue teams?",options:[{text:"For taking colorful photographs of the damaged area",correct:!1},{text:"For finding people by their body heat in darkness and smoke",correct:!0},{text:"For extinguishing fires",correct:!1},{text:"For finding people by tracking the signals from their phones",correct:!1}],explanation:"The human body is warmer than the surrounding environment, so thermal cameras can clearly show where people are, particularly at night and in smoke. Their ability to detect people beneath stone and concrete is limited."},{question:"What is the main benefit of exoskeletons for civil defense personnel?",options:[{text:"Flying through the air",correct:!1},{text:"More physical strength to carry heavy loads and less fatigue",correct:!0},{text:"Monitoring the wearer's heart rate and body temperature",correct:!1},{text:"Protecting the whole body from chemicals and heat during a rescue",correct:!1}],explanation:"These mechanical suits add power to human muscles so that the wearer can carry casualties or heavy equipment more easily."},{question:"In the field of safety, what is meant by a smart building?",options:[{text:"A beautiful building",correct:!1},{text:"A building whose networked devices respond automatically to hazards",correct:!0},{text:"A building that guards watch on cameras around the clock",correct:!1},{text:"A building whose lights and air conditioning are controlled from a phone app",correct:!1}],explanation:"In smart buildings, the system itself detects the hazard and, without human intervention, closes doors, shuts down the air-handling system and alerts the teams."},{question:"How can artificial intelligence (AI) predict disasters?",options:[{text:"By magic",correct:!1},{text:"By analyzing big data from weather records and ground sensors",correct:!0},{text:"By asking people on social media what they expect",correct:!1},{text:"It cannot predict anything; it only records what has happened",correct:!1}],explanation:"AI relies on data from past years and on recent changes to identify where the probability of a hazard is highest; a prediction is a probability, not a certainty."},{question:"In a drill, a smart-building system should close the fire doors automatically, but one door stayed open because the network was down. What is the best conclusion?",options:[{text:"Safety functions need a way to work without the network, and the result goes into the review",correct:!0},{text:"Network failures are rare, so this result can be ignored",correct:!1},{text:"Remove the automatic system and rely only on staff closing doors by hand",correct:!1},{text:"Lock the fire doors with a key so that they cannot be opened during a fire",correct:!1}],explanation:"Smart systems are useful but must still work safely without the network. The fault must be recorded in the review and fixed; locking doors with a key blocks the way out."},{question:"Which use of AI in emergency call handling fits this module?",options:[{text:"AI suggests a priority for each 115 call and the call-taker decides",correct:!0},{text:"AI triages casualties at the scene and decides without a medic",correct:!1},{text:"AI replaces the call-takers at night to cut costs",correct:!1},{text:"AI ends the call if the caller is silent for a few seconds",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to the module, AI can help call-takers prioritize calls, but the final decision stays in human hands."}],steps:[{title:"Start from the need",detail:"Before choosing a device, define the operational problem clearly (for example, finding people in smoke or getting medicines to a cut-off village). Technology is a tool, not the goal."},{title:"Check the rules and permissions",detail:"Drone flights, data protection and people’s privacy follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority. At an incident, a drone flies only with the incident commander’s permission.",critical:!0},{title:"Train and test before real use",detail:"Test the new device in exercises and learn its limits; for example, a thermal camera has a limited ability to detect people beneath stone and concrete."},{title:"Keep a human in the decision",detail:"Devices and AI provide information, but the commander or call-taker makes the decision. The final decision about people’s lives always stays in human hands.",critical:!0},{title:"Have a fallback ready",detail:"If the network, battery or GPS fails, the team must be able to carry on manually: backup radios, paper maps and manual accountability of personnel.",critical:!0},{title:"Protect the data and review",detail:"Protect systems from hacking, decide who may see the data and record in the after-action review whether the device helped."}],warnings:["Do not send a team into danger, or end a search, on the strength of one drone image or one sensor: a thermal camera may miss a person under concrete.","Do not fly a drone over an incident without the incident commander’s permission and coordination with the competent authority: it can endanger aircraft and teams.","Do not connect safety systems (alarms, doors) to an unprotected network: hacking can disable them."],tips:["Try new technology first in an exercise, then at a small incident.","Have a battery and charging plan: spare batteries for drones, radios and wearables.","Write down which data are collected, where they are kept and who may see them.","In the after-action review, compare the new device with the old method."],activity:{prompt:"An emergency unit proposes an AI system that ranks 115 calls automatically and dispatches teams without staff review, to save time. As safety adviser, what do you recommend?",options:[{text:"Accept it only as decision support: the call-taker sees the ranking and decides, it is tested in exercises and a manual fallback remains",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: AI can help, but the final decision stays with a person and the system’s errors show up during testing."},{text:"Accept it as offered, because AI is faster than people and time saves lives in emergencies",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: speed without review is risky; if the system wrongly ranks a serious call last, no one corrects it."},{text:"Reject all AI systems, because any system can make mistakes",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: people make mistakes too; controlled use with human review can bring real benefits."},{text:"Accept it but only at night, when staff are few and tired",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the risk of unchecked errors is the same at night, and human review matters more when staff are tired."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"After a night-time earthquake, a drone with a thermal camera flies over a collapsed building and sees no heat signatures. The team leader proposes ending the search there. What do you advise?",options:[{text:"The search does not end: the camera sees little beneath concrete, so the void search continues with listening devices and small cameras",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the drone gives information, not a decision; people under rubble need other search methods."},{text:"End the search and move to the next building, because the camera saw no heat",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: seeing no heat through rubble does not mean no one is there; living people may be left behind."},{text:"Send untrained volunteers into the rubble to check by hand",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: untrained people do not enter damaged structures; a further collapse can make them victims."},{text:"Stop all work until daylight, when the drone can take a clearer image",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: people under rubble have no time to spare, and daylight does not change what a thermal camera sees beneath concrete."}]},{prompt:"During the search the network fails; the drone feed and the team’s wearables stop. What do you do next?",options:[{text:"Switch to the fallback: backup radios, a manual count of all rescuers and a paper map, and the work continues to plan",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the fallback must be ready before the incident; accounting for rescuers matters most when devices stop."},{text:"Stop all work until the network returns, so that no information is lost",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the network may be down for a long time; stopping puts the people under the rubble at risk."},{text:"Let the teams carry on without radios or a headcount, because each knows its job",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: without a headcount, no one will know where each rescuer is if there is a further collapse."},{text:"Move the command post next to the rubble to get a better signal",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the command post must stay in a safe place; moving it next to the rubble puts command at risk and does not fix the network."}]}],realCase:"At a wildfire near several villages, a Civil Defense team used a drone with a thermal camera at night. The drone spotted two lost hikers on a hill, and the incident commander sent a ground team to them by a safe route. During the work the drone’s battery ran low; because there was a battery plan, a second drone took over at once. In the after-action review the team noted that the drone located the hikers, but the decisions and the rescue were human work, and the battery plan and backup radios became part of the standing plan."},{id:11,code:"QUAKE-1100",icon:"Activity",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Seismology and safety engineering",subtitle:"Seismology and earthquake resistant design",summary:"A scientific analysis of tectonic plates, seismic waves, soil liquefaction, earthquake-resistant building design and the Drop, Cover, Hold On procedure during shaking.",blocks:[{body:`Iraq and the Kurdistan Region lie in a seismically active zone, particularly the northern and eastern areas close to the Zagros mountain range. This zone was formed by the collision of the Arabian Plate with the Eurasian Plate. In this module we discuss, at an academic level, the physics of earthquakes and the engineering measures that protect lives and buildings.

Personal protection during shaking (Drop, Cover, Hold On): drop to the ground where you are (Drop), protect your head and neck under a sturdy table (Cover) and hold on to the table leg until the shaking stops (Hold On). Stay away from windows and heavy equipment. Do not go under a doorway and do not rely on the "triangle of life"; these are not approved procedures. Do not run outside or use the elevator during the shaking. After the shaking, shut off the gas only if you smell gas or hear a leak and the valve is close and can be reached safely without going into the area where the gas has collected; otherwise get out and call 115 from outside.`},{heading:"1. The mechanism of earthquakes and tectonic plates",body:"An earthquake is a complex physical process explained by the elastic rebound theory:",points:["Tectonic plates: the Earth's crust consists of several large pieces that move over a softer layer. The Arabian Plate moves approximately two centimeters per year toward the northeast.","Hypocenter and epicenter: the hypocenter is the point beneath the ground where the energy is released. The epicenter is the point directly above it on the Earth's surface, near which the greatest destruction often occurs.","Faults: knowledge of fault systems such as the Zagros Fold-Thrust Belt is essential for civil engineers when designing projects."]},{heading:"2. Analysis of seismic waves",body:"Earthquake energy propagates in the form of waves, which are divided into three main types:",points:["P-waves (primary): the fastest waves; they travel as a push through all materials (solid, liquid and gas). These are the first waves recorded by seismographs.","S-waves (secondary): slower, but their shaking is stronger. They travel only through solid materials, their motion is perpendicular to the direction of travel (side to side or up and down) and they cause major damage to buildings.","Surface waves: they travel along the Earth's surface, last the longest and are the main cause of large-scale destruction in cities."]},{heading:"3. Scientific scales and soil liquefaction",body:"To describe the strength of an earthquake, a distinction must be made between magnitude and intensity:",points:["Magnitude scale: measures the energy released. Today the moment magnitude scale (Mw) is used, which has replaced the Richter scale. Each additional unit means 10 times the wave amplitude on the seismograph and approximately 32 times more energy.","Modified Mercalli Intensity scale: measures the intensity of shaking at each location according to its effects on people and buildings.","Soil liquefaction: in areas of wet, sandy soil, shaking causes the soil to lose its solid properties and behave like a liquid, which makes buildings sink or overturn."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_11_seismic_waves.mp4",title:"The physics of waves",description:"An analysis of how P-waves and S-waves travel through the Earth"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_11_building_design.png",title:"Earthquake-resistant engineering",description:"The base isolation technique for protecting buildings"}],quiz:[{question:"Which type of wave is the fastest and reaches seismographic stations first?",options:[{text:"S-waves",correct:!1},{text:"P-waves",correct:!0},{text:"Surface waves",correct:!1},{text:"Love waves",correct:!1}],explanation:"P-waves (primary) are the fastest waves and travel through all types of material."},{question:"During the shaking of an earthquake, what does this module tell you to do?",options:[{text:"Drop where you are, cover your head and neck under a sturdy table and hold on",correct:!0},{text:"Stand under a doorway, because the door frame protects you from falling objects",correct:!1},{text:"Run outside by the stairs at once, without using the elevator",correct:!1},{text:'Lie down beside a large piece of furniture in the "triangle of life"',correct:!1}],explanation:'Drop, Cover, Hold On is the procedure this module teaches: drop to the ground, protect your head and neck under a sturdy table and hold on until the shaking stops. Doorways and the "triangle of life" are not approved procedures, and you do not run outside during the shaking.'},{question:"After the shaking you smell gas. When may you shut off the gas valve?",options:[{text:"Always, as the first step, even if you have to walk through the gas to reach it",correct:!1},{text:"Only if the valve is close and reachable without entering where the gas has collected",correct:!0},{text:"After switching on the lights, so that you can find the valve more quickly in the dark",correct:!1},{text:"Only after waiting inside for a few minutes to see whether the smell goes away",correct:!1}],explanation:"After the shaking, shut off the gas only if the valve is close and can be reached safely without going into the area where the gas has collected; otherwise get out and call 115 from outside."},{question:"A colleague says a magnitude 6 earthquake releases twice the energy of a magnitude 5. How do you correct him?",options:[{text:"About 32 times more energy, and 10 times the wave amplitude on the seismograph",correct:!0},{text:"About 10 times more energy, because each unit is ten times",correct:!1},{text:"He is right: each extra unit doubles the energy",correct:!1},{text:"About 100 times more energy, and 100 times the wave amplitude",correct:!1}],explanation:"On the Mw scale each additional unit means 10 times the wave amplitude on the seismograph and about 32 times more energy."},{question:"Two towns report very different damage from the same earthquake. Which explanation is correct?",options:[{text:"Intensity (Mercalli) varies with distance, soil and buildings",correct:!0},{text:"Each town experienced a different earthquake magnitude",correct:!1},{text:"Magnitude is measured separately in each town with local instruments",correct:!1},{text:"P-waves reached only the town that had more damage",correct:!1}],explanation:"Magnitude (Mw) is one number for the energy of the earthquake; Mercalli intensity is measured at each place by its effects on people and buildings, so it differs from place to place."},{question:"A new school is planned on wet, sandy soil near a river. Which hazard must the engineer assess in particular?",options:[{text:"Soil liquefaction, which can make the building sink or tip over",correct:!0},{text:"S-waves, because they grow stronger in liquid layers",correct:!1},{text:"P-waves, because they cannot pass through wet soil",correct:!1},{text:"Surface waves, because they do not reach areas near rivers",correct:!1}],explanation:"In wet, sandy soil, shaking makes the ground behave like a liquid and buildings can sink or tip over. S-waves do not travel through liquids, and P-waves travel through all materials."}],steps:[{title:"During the shaking: Drop, Cover, Hold On",detail:"Drop where you are, protect your head and neck under a sturdy table and hold on to its leg until the shaking stops. Keep away from windows and heavy equipment; do not run outside or use the elevator.",critical:!0},{title:"After the shaking: check and get out",detail:"Check yourself and the people around you. If the building is damaged, leave by the stairs and expect aftershocks.",critical:!0},{title:"Gas smell: the valve rule",detail:"If you smell gas or hear a leak: no switches, lights, phones or flames inside. Close the valve only if it is close and can be reached without entering the area where the gas has collected; otherwise do not approach, get out, warn others and call 115 from outside.",critical:!0},{title:"Outside: assembly point and calls",detail:"Go to the assembly point, away from buildings, walls and power lines. Account for the staff and, if anyone is missing or hurt, call 115 and 122 and give the location clearly.",critical:!0},{title:"No re-entry before inspection",detail:"No one goes back into a damaged building until a qualified engineer or the competent authority clearly says it is safe.",critical:!0},{title:"Record and feed back into engineering",detail:"Record the visible damage (cracked columns, leaning walls, equipment that moved) and send it for structural assessment; the results go into the retrofit plan and future drills."}],warnings:["Do not stand in a doorway or rely on the “triangle of life”: these are not approved procedures and leave you exposed to falling objects instead of protected.","Do not go back into a damaged building for your belongings: aftershocks can bring down weakened parts.","If there is a gas smell after an earthquake, do not operate switches or light a flame: one spark can ignite the collected gas."],tips:["Anchor shelves, cabinets and heavy equipment to the wall or floor so that they do not fall during shaking.","Know where the gas valve and the main electrical switch are and who is trained to use them.","Practise Drop, Cover, Hold On regularly in workplace drills and identify where the sturdy tables are.","For projects on wet, sandy soil, require a geotechnical investigation before design."],activity:{prompt:"You are a safety engineer working in a design office with large windows and tall, unanchored shelves. An earthquake starts. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I drop, take cover under a sturdy desk away from the windows and shelves and hold on to its leg",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Drop, Cover, Hold On protects you from falling objects and glass; keeping away from windows and shelves lowers the risk."},{text:"I run to the stairs to get out of the building before it collapses",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: running during shaking makes you fall and takes you toward glass and falling objects."},{text:"I stand in the doorway, because the frame is stronger than the walls",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a doorway is not an approved procedure; in modern buildings it is no stronger than the walls and does not protect your head."},{text:"I crouch beside the tall shelf in the “triangle of life”",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the “triangle of life” is not approved, and an unanchored shelf can fall on you."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"The shaking has stopped. Cracks appear in the columns of the office building, and some staff want to go back for their laptops. As the safety officer, what do you do?",options:[{text:"I take everyone by the stairs to the assembly point, count them and no one goes back in until an engineer inspects",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: cracked columns are a sign of structural damage, and aftershocks can bring down weakened parts."},{text:"I give them five minutes to go back quickly for important items",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: nothing is worth a life, and an aftershock can come during those minutes."},{text:"All staff stay inside until the aftershocks are over",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the building is damaged and no one knows when the aftershocks will end."},{text:"We use the elevator so that staff on the upper floors get out faster",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: after an earthquake an elevator can stop and trap people; the stairs are the safe way."}]},{prompt:"At the assembly point a worker says there was a gas smell in the basement near the kitchen, and the main gas valve is in that basement. What do you do next?",options:[{text:"No one goes back for the valve; I keep people away, call 115 from outside and tell the team where the valve is",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the valve is where the gas collects and the building is damaged; no one ever goes back in for a valve."},{text:"I send the worker back to close the valve quickly, because he knows the place",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: he would enter the area where the gas collects, in a damaged building; the rule forbids this."},{text:"I switch off the main electrical switch in the basement so that no sparks form",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: operating any switch in the gas area can itself make a spark, and the basement is where the gas collects."},{text:"I wait to see whether the smell goes away by itself",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the leak may grow; 115 must be told at once so that the site can be controlled."}]}],realCase:"An earthquake struck a factory during working hours. The staff had practised Drop, Cover, Hold On and the heavy machines were anchored to the floor. After the shaking, supervisors led everyone by the stairs to the assembly point. The headcount showed one person missing; instead of anyone going back in, the supervisor called 115 and gave the person’s last known location. The team found the worker in a storeroom. Engineers later found cracks in two columns, and the factory stayed closed until it was strengthened."},{id:12,code:"PLAN-1200",icon:"ClipboardList",accent:"#4f46e5",title:"Emergency planning and preparedness",subtitle:"Emergency planning and family preparedness",summary:"Preparing an emergency evacuation plan for the family and the workplace, and assembling the basic supplies needed for survival.",blocks:[{body:"Planning before any disaster is the best way to reduce fear and protect life. In this module we will learn how to prepare a scientific plan for families and institutions that covers every possible hazard."},{heading:"1. The family emergency plan",body:"Every family needs to agree on a clear plan:",points:["Escape routes: identify two routes out of every room in the house, so that if one is blocked, the other can be used.","Assembly points: designate two places, one near the house and one outside the neighborhood in case returning home is not possible.","Out-of-area contact: keep the number of a relative who lives outside the city, so that they can serve as the point of contact between family members."]},{heading:"2. The emergency kit",body:"Essential supplies must be ready for at least 72 hours (3 days):",points:["Water and food: about 4 liters of water per person per day, for at least 3 days, together with non-perishable food such as canned goods.","Light and communication: a lantern or flashlight, spare batteries and a battery-powered radio for listening to official information.","Medical needs: family members' specific medications and a first aid kit."]},{heading:"3. Hazard analysis: a plan for which hazards?",body:"A good plan starts with one question: which hazards can happen where we live or work? For each hazard, three questions must be answered:",points:["Warning: how will we know? A smoke alarm, the smell of gas, or official announcements from Civil Defense and the competent authority.","Decision: leave or stay? For a fire or a gas leak, get out; during earthquake shaking, Drop, Cover, Hold On; for a flood, move to higher ground.","Roles: who does what? Who calls 115 and 122, who helps the children and older people, and who carries the emergency kit."]},{heading:"4. People who need help and documents",body:"The plan must include every family member or employee, not only those who can move quickly:",points:["Children, older people and people with disabilities: name a helper for each one and plan an exit route that does not depend on an elevator.","Medicines and special equipment: list each person’s medicines and devices (glasses, hearing aid, wheelchair) in the plan.","Documents and numbers: keep copies of important documents and a paper list of phone numbers in the emergency kit, because phones may not work."]},{heading:"5. Practising and updating the plan",body:"A plan that exists only on paper does not work in an emergency. It must be practised and kept up to date:",points:["Practise getting out with everyone by both routes, all the way to the meeting place.","Check the emergency kit regularly: replace expired food, medicines and batteries.","Update the plan whenever the family, the home or the workplace changes."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_12_plan_making.mp4",title:"How to prepare the plan",description:"The steps for drawing an evacuation map of the building"}],quiz:[{question:"For at least how many days should an emergency kit cover the family's needs?",options:[{text:"1 day (24 hours)",correct:!1},{text:"3 days (72 hours)",correct:!0},{text:"1 week (168 hours)",correct:!1},{text:"1 month",correct:!1}],explanation:"Preparedness guidance (such as Ready.gov and FEMA) recommends preparing supplies for at least 72 hours (3 days), because at the start of a major disaster help may arrive late."},{question:"Why should a family identify two escape routes out of every room?",options:[{text:"So that family members can leave in two separate groups",correct:!1},{text:"So that one route can be kept free for the emergency teams only",correct:!1},{text:"So that if one route is blocked, the other can be used",correct:!0},{text:"So that the family can leave faster by using both routes at once",correct:!1}],explanation:"The family plan identifies two routes out of every room so that if one route is blocked, the other can be used."},{question:"What is the role of the out-of-area contact in the family plan?",options:[{text:"A neighbor who keeps a spare key to the house and checks it after the disaster",correct:!1},{text:"A family member who stays behind at home to guard the property during the disaster",correct:!1},{text:"The Civil Defense officer who is responsible for your neighborhood",correct:!1},{text:"A relative outside the city who is the point of contact between family members",correct:!0}],explanation:"The plan keeps the number of a relative who lives outside the city, so that they can serve as the point of contact between family members."},{question:"A family’s plan has one meeting place only: in front of the house. Because of a gas leak that area has been closed off. Which weakness does this show?",options:[{text:"The plan needs a second meeting place outside the neighborhood",correct:!0},{text:"The plan needs a larger yard in front of the house",correct:!1},{text:"A meeting place is not needed if the phones are working",correct:!1},{text:"The family should gather inside the house instead",correct:!1}],explanation:"The plan calls for two meeting places: one near the home and one outside the neighborhood in case the area near the home cannot be used. With a gas leak, staying inside is dangerous."},{question:"You are checking the emergency kit of a family of four. Which check matters most?",options:[{text:"Water for at least 3 days (about 4 liters per person per day), food and working batteries",correct:!0},{text:"Water and food for one day, because help arrives on the first day",correct:!1},{text:"Phone chargers only, because a radio is no longer needed",correct:!1},{text:"New clothes and shoes for everyone, because water is available everywhere",correct:!1}],explanation:"The kit must cover at least 72 hours, because help may be late; water is about 4 liters per person per day, and the battery radio is for official information."},{question:"During a disaster the phone network is overloaded. How does the family plan keep everyone in touch?",options:[{text:"Everyone reports to the out-of-area contact, whose number is also on paper",correct:!0},{text:"Everyone keeps calling each other until one call gets through",correct:!1},{text:"Everyone goes home, even if the area is not safe",correct:!1},{text:"Everyone waits until the family’s name is read out on the radio",correct:!1}],explanation:"The relative outside the city is the point of contact; reaching one person outside the disaster area is often easier, and the number stays on paper if the phone fails."}],steps:[{title:"Identify the hazards",detail:"List the hazards of your place (fire, earthquake, flood, gas leak, severe weather) and for each one decide how the warning arrives and whether the decision is to leave or to stay."},{title:"Map routes and meeting places",detail:"Mark two routes out of every room and two meeting places: one near the home and one outside the neighborhood. Routes must not depend on an elevator.",critical:!0},{title:"Assign roles and contacts",detail:"Decide who calls 115 and 122 and who helps whom, and write down the number of the relative outside the city who is the point of contact.",critical:!0},{title:"Build the emergency kit",detail:"Prepare supplies for at least 3 days: about 4 liters of water per person per day, food that does not spoil, a flashlight, a battery radio, medicines and a first aid kit, and keep the kit near the door."},{title:"Practise the plan",detail:"Practise getting out by both routes to the meeting place, with the children and the people who need help. Teach the children 115 and the meeting place.",critical:!0},{title:"Review and update",detail:"After every practice, write down what did not work and fix the plan; check the kit regularly and update the plan whenever things change."}],warnings:["Do not plan a route that depends on an elevator or a door locked with a key: elevators stop in a fire, and exit doors must open from the inside without a key.","Do not go back into a home with fire or gas to fetch the kit or belongings: the kit is for a safe exit, not a reason to return."],tips:["Write 115, 122 and 104 and the out-of-area contact’s number on paper and keep it in the kit and in your pocket.","Keep the emergency kit near the exit door so that it can be picked up quickly.","When the network is busy, a short text message often gets through more easily than a call.","Write the plan on one page and post it somewhere visible at home."],activity:{prompt:"You are a young engineer and a family of five on the second floor asks you to review their plan. The grandmother uses a wheelchair, and the plan has only one route: by the elevator. What do you change?",options:[{text:"Two routes without the elevator, a named helper for the grandmother, her medicines in the kit and a practice with everyone",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: elevators stop in a fire; a named helper and practice make sure the grandmother is not left behind."},{text:"I keep the elevator route, because the grandmother cannot use the stairs",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: in a fire the elevator can stop and trap people; the plan must name who helps her."},{text:"I leave the grandmother’s part of the plan to the emergency teams, because they are experts",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: teams may arrive late; the family needs its own plan for every member."},{text:"I only add the grandmother’s medicines to the kit and leave the route as it is",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: medicines matter, but the main problems are the elevator route and the missing helper."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the evening an official flood warning is announced for your neighborhood. In the plan the first meeting place is near the home, but that area is flooding. What does the family do?",options:[{text:"Go to the second meeting place outside the neighborhood, by a higher route that avoids flood water",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the second meeting place exists for this case; never walk or drive through flood water."},{text:"Stay at home and wait for another announcement",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the warning has been given and the water is rising; staying can cut off the escape routes."},{text:"Drive through the shallow water to reach the first meeting place",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: 30 cm of moving water can carry most cars away, and depth cannot be judged by eye."},{text:"Each member goes to a friend’s house and they call each other later",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: splitting up makes it hard to account for everyone; the plan names one place so that no one is lost."}]},{prompt:"At the second meeting place one family member is missing and phone calls are not getting through. What do they do next?",options:[{text:"Text the out-of-area contact, and if the person is still missing, tell 115 their last known location",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the out-of-area contact is the point of contact and texts often get through; searching in the flood is work for specialist teams."},{text:"One of them goes back alone into the flooded street to search",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: 15 cm of moving water can knock a person down; instead of one victim there may be two."},{text:"They wait and tell no one, because the person may turn up alone",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: every minute matters; 115 needs to know early that someone is missing and where they were last seen."},{text:"They post a photo on social media and wait for replies",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this does not replace an official report to 115, and the network may not work anyway."}]}],realCase:"A family had posted their plan on the refrigerator door: two routes, two meeting places and the number of an uncle in another city. One night a fire started in the building’s stairwell. The family used the second route across a shared balcony, and the eldest son helped the grandfather as the plan said. At the meeting place the youngest daughter was missing; the father texted the uncle and told 115 where she had last been seen. She was found outside with a neighbor. After the incident the family added a new rule: every child goes straight to the meeting place."},{id:13,code:"FLOOD-1300",icon:"Waves",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Floods and water safety procedures",subtitle:"Flood dynamics and water rescue",summary:"Understanding the types of flood, vehicle safety and the principles of emergency rescue in water.",blocks:[{body:"Floods are a recurring hazard in the Kurdistan Region. Just a few centimeters of moving water can cause major disasters. In this module we discuss how to protect lives and vehicles when water levels rise. Never walk or drive into flood water, and call 115."},{heading:"1. Types of floods and their hazards",body:"Floods are classified by their speed and location:",points:["Flash flood: occurs within 6 hours of heavy rainfall, and is the most dangerous type because there is very little time to prepare.","Danger to vehicles: just 30 cm of moving water is enough to lift and carry away most vehicles.","Danger to people: 15 cm of moving water can knock a person down and cause drowning."]},{heading:"2. Rescue procedures: Reach, Throw, Row, Go",body:"If you see a person in the water, first call 115, then carry out these steps in order:",points:["REACH: reach the person with a pole or another long object without entering the water yourself.","THROW: throw them something that floats (such as a tire, or a rope tied to a light floating object).","ROW: if a boat and training are available, use them to approach safely.","GO: only specialist teams may enter the water; never enter flood water as a volunteer."]},{heading:"3. Before and during a flood",body:"Many flood victims are on roads and inside vehicles. Follow these rules:",points:["Follow official warnings and the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority; a flash flood can arrive even when it is not raining where you are, because the rain falls upstream.","When water rises quickly, move to higher ground away from rivers, valleys and channels; do not camp or stay in dry riverbeds.","If water covers the road, turn around and choose another route (Turn Around, Don’t Drown); stay off bridges over fast-moving water.","If your vehicle is trapped in fast-moving water, stay inside and call 115; if the water rises inside the vehicle, get onto the roof."]},{heading:"4. After the flood",body:"The danger does not end when the water stops:",points:["Do not return home or to work until the competent authority says it is safe.","Keep away from flood water: it may be contaminated or electrified by fallen power lines.","If there is water inside a building and the power may still be on, do not go in; the power is cut by a qualified electrician or the competent authority.","If you smell gas, follow the gas-leak rule: do not approach, get out and call 115 from outside."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_13_water_safety.mp4",title:"Flood protection",description:"Correct procedures for dealing with the force of water"}],quiz:[{question:"What is the minimum depth of moving water that can lift and carry away an ordinary car?",options:[{text:"10 cm",correct:!1},{text:"30 cm",correct:!0},{text:"100 cm",correct:!1},{text:"5 cm",correct:!1}],explanation:"30 centimeters (12 inches) of moving water is enough to lift and carry away most cars; even shallower water can cause the driver to lose control of the vehicle."},{question:"What depth of moving water can already knock a person down and cause drowning?",options:[{text:"15 cm",correct:!0},{text:"30 cm",correct:!1},{text:"60 cm",correct:!1},{text:"100 cm",correct:!1}],explanation:"Just 15 cm of moving water can knock a person down and cause drowning, so never walk into flood water; call 115."},{question:"In the Reach, Throw, Row, Go sequence, who may enter the flood water?",options:[{text:"Any strong swimmer who volunteers to help",correct:!1},{text:"Only specialist teams",correct:!0},{text:"Anyone who ties a rope around their waist first",correct:!1},{text:"A family member of the person in the water",correct:!1}],explanation:"GO is the last step: only specialist teams may enter the water. Never enter flood water as a volunteer; reach or throw something that floats from the bank instead."},{question:"On a road, water is flowing across a dip and looks shallow. A driver asks you what to do. What is the right answer?",options:[{text:"Turn around and use another route; depth cannot be judged by eye",correct:!0},{text:"Cross slowly in a low gear so that the car does not stall",correct:!1},{text:"Cross quickly before the water rises any further",correct:!1},{text:"Follow the car ahead, which crossed without trouble",correct:!1}],explanation:"30 cm of moving water can carry most cars away, and the road may have washed out underneath. Turn Around, Don’t Drown."},{question:"Which order is correct for rescuing a person in the water?",options:[{text:"Reach, then throw, then row, and go last for specialists only",correct:!0},{text:"Go, then reach, then throw, and row last with a boat",correct:!1},{text:"Throw, then go, then reach, and row last if a boat exists",correct:!1},{text:"Row, then go, then throw, and reach last from the bank",correct:!1}],explanation:"First reach from the bank with something long, then throw something that floats, then use a boat if trained; going into the water comes last and only for specialist teams."},{question:"After a flood, a worker wants to switch on a pump in a flooded basement while standing in the water. What do you tell him?",options:[{text:"Get out of the water; a qualified electrician cuts the power first",correct:!0},{text:"Put on rubber boots and carry on; they will protect you",correct:!1},{text:"Switch it on quickly so that the basement drains sooner",correct:!1},{text:"Just make sure his hands are dry before touching the switch",correct:!1}],explanation:"Water and electricity together create a risk of electric shock. No one enters water inside a building until the power has been cut by a qualified electrician or the competent authority."}],steps:[{title:"Call 115",detail:"Give the exact location (road, bridge, a visible landmark), how many people are in the water and how fast it is flowing. Do not hang up until you are told to.",critical:!0},{title:"Stay out of the water",detail:"Keep yourself and others on the bank and on high ground. 15 cm of moving water can knock a person down; if you go in, there may be two victims.",critical:!0},{title:"Reach",detail:"From the bank, reach the person with a pole, a rope or another long object, and hold on to something solid so that you do not fall in.",critical:!0},{title:"Throw",detail:"If you cannot reach, throw something that floats (a tire, an empty plastic container, a rope tied to a light object)."},{title:"Row and go: trained people only",detail:"Use a boat only with training and safety equipment; going into the water is for specialist teams only. Never enter flood water as a volunteer.",critical:!0},{title:"After the rescue",detail:"Move the person away from the water, keep them warm and call 122; everyone pulled from the water must be checked by a doctor, even if they seem fine."}],warnings:["Never walk or drive into flood water: 15 cm of moving water knocks a person down and 30 cm carries most cars away.","Do not tie a rope around yourself and wade in: fast water can pull you under.","Do not touch power lines or electrical equipment in or near flood water: there is a risk of electric shock."],tips:["Plan alternative routes in advance that avoid low crossings and valleys.","At worksites near water, keep a rope tied to something that floats in a visible, ready place.","Know the high ground near the worksite and show it to the staff.","During heavy rain upstream, keep checking the official warnings."],activity:{prompt:"You are the site engineer at a construction site near a seasonal riverbed. Heavy rain is falling upstream and the water is rising. Workers want to drive the trucks across the low crossing to save the equipment. What do you decide?",options:[{text:"I stop all crossings, move the workers to high ground, follow the official warnings and call 115 if anyone is trapped",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: equipment can be replaced but lives cannot; 30 cm of moving water can carry most vehicles away."},{text:"I let the trucks cross because they are heavy and the water cannot move them",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: moving water lifts and carries large vehicles too, and the road may have washed out under the water."},{text:"I send one truck first to test how deep the water is",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this turns the driver’s life into the test; the depth and speed of the water cannot be judged by eye."},{text:"I send workers to wade in and tie the equipment down with ropes",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: 15 cm of moving water can knock a person down; no equipment is worth a worker’s life."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A man is clinging to a tree in a fast-flowing channel, a few meters from the bank. You have a rope and an empty plastic container. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I call 115, stay on the bank and throw him the container tied to the rope",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: throw from the bank; you stay out of the water and the specialist teams are on their way."},{text:"I swim out to him, because I am a strong swimmer",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: flood water is fast and full of debris, good swimmers drown in it too, and going in is for specialist teams only."},{text:"I tie the rope around my waist and wade in",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: fast water can drag you under while you are tied to the rope; there may be two victims."},{text:"I call 115 and do nothing else until the team arrives",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: calling comes first, but when you can throw something that floats from the bank, you should."}]},{prompt:"The man grabs the container and you pull him to the bank with the rope. He is awake, coughing and very cold. What next?",options:[{text:"I move him away from the water, keep him warm and call 122 so that a doctor checks him",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: everyone pulled from the water must be checked by a doctor, even if they seem fine."},{text:"I let him walk home, because he is talking normally and can walk",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: water may have entered his lungs or he may be getting hypothermia; a doctor must check him."},{text:"I press on his stomach to get the swallowed water out",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: pressing on the stomach does not help and can cause vomiting that goes into the lungs."},{text:"I leave him on the bank and go back to get his car out of the water",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you would enter flood water for property, and the rescued man would be left alone."}]}],realCase:"During heavy rain upstream, water rose quickly over a low road. A driver in a small car wanted to cross, but an engineer nearby stopped him and pointed out a higher route. A few minutes later another car stalled in the water. Its driver stayed inside and called 115; when the water rose inside the car, he climbed onto the roof. The people on the bank did not go into the water; they talked to him from a distance until the specialist team arrived with a boat. A doctor then checked him."},{id:14,code:"GAS-1400",icon:"Fuel",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Gas safety and the physics of explosions",subtitle:"Natural gas and LPG advanced safety",summary:"An analysis of gas density relative to air, the explosive limits (LEL/UEL) and civil defense strategies for controlling gas leaks.",blocks:[{body:"Fuel gas is a major source of energy in industry and in homes, but the smallest mistake can turn it into a devastating bomb. In this module we discuss gas dynamics: from vapor density to autoignition temperature. Understanding this information is essential for every safety engineer in order to design protection systems in accordance with NFPA standards."},{heading:"1. Gas physics and vapor density",body:"To prevent gas from accumulating, you must know whether the gas is lighter or heavier than air (air = 1):",points:["Natural gas (methane): lighter than air (approximately 0.55). If there is a leak, it rises and accumulates beneath the ceiling; high-level ventilation is therefore required.","LPG (propane/butane): heavier than air (approximately 1.5 to 2). When it leaks, it collects like water on the floor and in low places (basements, trenches). This is very dangerous because it can reach sparks from switches and low-mounted appliances.","Gas odorization (mercaptan): pure gas is odorless, so companies add mercaptan compounds (such as ethyl mercaptan) so that people can detect the danger by smell."]},{heading:"2. Explosive limits (flammability limits, LEL/UEL)",body:"A gas explodes only when its concentration in air lies between two limits:",points:["Lower explosive limit (LEL): the lowest concentration of gas in air that can ignite. If the gas concentration is below this, no explosion occurs.","Upper explosive limit (UEL): the highest concentration of gas; if the gas concentration is above this, there is not enough oxygen and no explosion occurs (but when it mixes with air and falls between the two limits, it can ignite).","Asphyxiation hazard: gas accumulating in enclosed spaces displaces oxygen and can cause a silent death (asphyxiation)."]},{heading:"3. Technical emergency procedures",body:"When a gas leak is suspected, Civil Defense follows these rules:",points:["No sources of ignition: operating an electrical switch, using a telephone or any electrical device inside the premises can provide the energy needed for an explosion, as can any open flame.","Actions: open doors and windows (without switching on electric fans), close the main valve only if it is close and can be reached safely (without entering the area where the gas has collected and without operating any switch), evacuate people quietly and call 115 from outside the premises."]},{heading:"4. Gas leak and gas fire: the decision",body:"There are two different situations, and each has its own decision:",points:["Leak without fire: no switches, lights, phones or flames inside. Close the valve only if it is close and can be reached without entering the area where the gas has collected and without operating any switch; otherwise do not approach, get out, warn others and call 115 from outside.","Gas fire: a qualified person stops the gas only if the valve can be reached safely. Never put out a gas flame whose supply cannot be stopped: unburned gas builds up and can explode. Get everyone out, raise the alarm and call 115.","An LPG cylinder exposed to fire can burst violently; do not approach it, move people away and tell 115 that it is there."]},{heading:"5. Engineering prevention",body:"A safety engineer reduces the risk before any leak happens:",points:["Place gas detectors according to the gas density: near the ceiling for natural gas, near the floor for LPG, and as the manufacturer instructs.","Store LPG cylinders upright in a well-ventilated place away from heat and sparks, and never in basements or low areas, where LPG collects.","Label the main valve and keep the way to it clear; hoses and regulators are checked by a competent person. Local inspection and licensing rules follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_14_gas_physics.mp4",title:"Gas explosion dynamics",description:"A laboratory analysis of the LEL and UEL"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_14_gas_detectors.png",title:"Industrial detectors",description:"Types of gas leak detection devices in factories"}],quiz:[{question:"Why does LPG accumulate at ground level when it leaks?",options:[{text:"Because it is colder than the air",correct:!1},{text:"Because it is heavier than air",correct:!0},{text:"Because it has a strong smell",correct:!1},{text:"Because it is natural gas",correct:!1}],explanation:"LPG is heavier than air (vapor density > 1), so it sinks and accumulates in low places."},{question:"Where does natural gas (methane) collect when it leaks, and what ventilation does that require?",options:[{text:"On the floor and in basements, so low-level ventilation is needed",correct:!1},{text:"Evenly through the room, so no special ventilation is needed",correct:!1},{text:"Beneath the ceiling, so high-level ventilation is needed",correct:!0},{text:"In trenches outside the building, so the windows must stay closed",correct:!1}],explanation:"Methane is lighter than air (about 0.55), so it rises and collects beneath the ceiling; high-level ventilation is therefore required. LPG is the gas that collects in low places."},{question:"When a gas leak is suspected, which action follows the rules in this module?",options:[{text:"Switch on an electric fan to blow the gas out of the room",correct:!1},{text:"Turn on the lights so that you can find the source of the leak",correct:!1},{text:"Walk into the room where the gas has collected to close the main valve",correct:!1},{text:"Evacuate people quietly and call 115 from outside the premises",correct:!0}],explanation:"Switches, lights, phones and electrical devices inside can provide the energy for an explosion. Open doors and windows without switching on electric fans, close the main valve only if it is close and can be reached safely without entering the area where the gas has collected, evacuate people quietly and call 115 from outside."},{question:"You are specifying gas detectors for two buildings: one on natural gas and one on LPG. Which placement is correct?",options:[{text:"Natural gas near the ceiling, LPG near the floor",correct:!0},{text:"Natural gas near the floor, LPG near the ceiling",correct:!1},{text:"Both halfway up the wall, at eye level",correct:!1},{text:"Both next to the door, at any height",correct:!1}],explanation:"Natural gas (about 0.55) is lighter than air and collects under the ceiling; LPG (about 1.5 to 2) is heavier and collects near the floor. The manufacturer’s instructions are also followed."},{question:"A meter shows the gas concentration in a closed room is above the UEL. A colleague says: “The room is safe, it cannot ignite.” What is wrong?",options:[{text:"When air gets in, the mixture falls between the limits and can explode",correct:!0},{text:"Above the UEL, gas ignites more easily than at any other level",correct:!1},{text:"Nothing is wrong; anyone can go in without risk",correct:!1},{text:"The UEL applies only to LPG, not to natural gas",correct:!1}],explanation:"Above the UEL there is not enough oxygen, but when a door opens or air gets in, the mixture comes back between the LEL and UEL and can ignite. The gas also displaces oxygen, so there is a risk of asphyxiation."},{question:"A gas flame is burning at a cylinder valve and you cannot reach the valve safely. Which action is correct?",options:[{text:"Do not put out the flame; get people out, keep away and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Quickly pour water on the flame so that it goes out before it spreads",correct:!1},{text:"Use a CO₂ extinguisher to put the flame out in a second",correct:!1},{text:"Cover the cylinder with a wet blanket and stay close to it",correct:!1}],explanation:"A gas flame whose supply cannot be stopped is not put out: unburned gas builds up and can explode. A qualified person stops the gas only if it is safe; otherwise evacuate and call 115."}],steps:[{title:"Recognize the leak",detail:"A smell of gas, a hissing sound or a detector alarm: treat it as a real leak until proven otherwise.",critical:!0},{title:"No ignition sources",detail:"Do not switch on or off any switch, light, phone or doorbell inside, do not light a flame and do not run electric fans. Any spark can ignite the gas.",critical:!0},{title:"Decide about the valve",detail:"Close the valve only if it is close and can be reached without entering the area where the gas has collected and without operating any switch. Otherwise do not approach, and never go back inside for a valve.",critical:!0},{title:"Ventilate and evacuate",detail:"Open doors and windows if they are on your way out and it is safe. Get people out quietly and warn them; with LPG, keep people away from basements and low areas.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 115 from outside",detail:"Call 115 from a safe place outside: the location, the type of gas (natural gas or LPG), where the valve is and whether it was closed, and whether anyone is still inside.",critical:!0},{title:"Do not return until cleared",detail:"No one goes back in until the 115 team or the competent authority says the place is safe."}],warnings:["Never enter the area where gas has collected to close a valve or fetch things: one spark is enough for an explosion, and the gas can also suffocate you.","Do not put out a gas flame whose supply cannot be stopped: unburned gas builds up and can explode.","Do not look for the leak with a flame, a flashlight or a phone light inside: they can make a spark."],tips:["Label the main valve, keep the way to it clear and know who is trained to close it.","If the only valve is inside the gas room, propose, with a qualified installer, a second valve outside the room near the door.","Fit detectors at the right height for the type of gas and test them regularly.","Teach staff in drills where the valve is and when they must not go near it."],activity:{prompt:"You are the safety engineer in a university laboratory building. A technician reports a strong smell of LPG in the basement where the cylinders are stored, and the main valve for the line is in that basement too. What do you do?",options:[{text:"No one goes into the basement; I evacuate the building without using switches, call 115 from outside and say where the valve is",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the valve is in the very place where the LPG collects; under the rule you must not approach it."},{text:"I go down quickly with a flashlight to close the valve before more gas escapes",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you would enter the area where the gas has collected, and a flashlight can make a spark."},{text:"I switch on the basement exhaust fan to push the gas out",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: switching on an electric fan in the gas area can make a spark and cause an explosion."},{text:"I turn off the main electrical switch in the basement so that no equipment runs",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: operating a switch inside the gas area can itself make a spark, and you would be entering the area where the gas collects."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Kitchen staff in the university cafeteria report a smell of gas. You are at the door in the corridor. The kitchen’s gas valve is in the corridor right beside you, outside the gas area. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I close the valve from the corridor without using any switch, then get people out and call 115 from outside",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the valve is close and can be reached without entering the gas area; this is exactly when the rule allows closing it."},{text:"I go into the kitchen to turn off each cooker one by one",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you would enter the gas area, while the corridor valve shuts off the whole kitchen at once."},{text:"I switch on the extractor hood so that the smell goes out",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: switching on an electric fan in the gas area can make a spark."},{text:"I wait to see whether the smell goes away by itself",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the gas keeps building up toward the lower explosive limit (LEL); every minute of waiting raises the risk."}]},{prompt:"During the evacuation a flame appears at the hose connection of an LPG cylinder inside the kitchen; this cylinder is not on the corridor valve and its own valve is in the flame. A worker picks up a fire extinguisher to put the flame out. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop him: we do not put out the flame because the gas cannot be stopped safely; I get everyone out and tell 115 the cylinder is in the fire",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: putting out the flame lets unburned gas build up and explode, and a cylinder in a fire can burst violently."},{text:"I let him put out the flame with the powder extinguisher, then we leave",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: after the flame is out the gas keeps escaping and building up, which can cause an explosion."},{text:"We pour water on the cylinder to cool it and put out the flame",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: that is for the 115 teams and their procedures; putting the flame out with water lets gas build up and brings people close to the cylinder."},{text:"We pick up the cylinder and carry it out of the building quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: going near a cylinder in a fire is very dangerous; it can burst violently."}]}],realCase:"In an apartment building, a resident smelled gas in the basement where LPG cylinders were stored, and the cylinder valve was inside the basement. He did not switch on the light or use his phone there; he went straight out, left the door open and knocked on the neighbors’ doors on the way. From the street he called 115 and said the gas was LPG and the valve was in the basement. The 115 team took control and allowed people back after checking. Afterwards the residents moved the cylinders to a well-ventilated place at ground level."},{id:15,code:"PLAN-1500",icon:"Landmark",accent:"#4f46e5",title:"Strategic emergency planning",subtitle:"Institutional emergency and continuity planning",summary:"Preparing evacuation plans for government institutions, managing crises and ensuring business continuity.",blocks:[{body:"Planning before a disaster makes the difference between success and catastrophe. This module has been prepared for civil defense managers and officers so that they know how to develop an emergency response plan (ERP) for government and commercial buildings."},{heading:"1. The core elements of an ERP",body:"Every good plan needs to contain these three components:",points:["Evacuation strategy: mapping the escape routes and ensuring that there are no obstructions in the path of occupants and emergency teams.","Assembly points: designating safe locations outside the building, away from the building and from the access routes of emergency vehicles, that have been approved through an engineering inspection.","Chain of command: defining responsibilities, such as who gives the order to evacuate and who contacts the external teams (115 and 122)."]},{heading:"2. Survival supplies and sustained resources (survival logistics)",body:"Preparing life-sustaining supplies for the first minutes of an incident and for the first 72 hours of a major disaster:",points:["Medical supplies: trauma kits containing equipment for controlling life-threatening bleeding (such as tourniquets and packing gauze).","Backup communications: VHF/UHF radios and satellite telephones for when communication networks are down.","Essential resources: preparing water and battery or generator power for the management teams while they remain in the disaster area."]},{heading:"3. Business continuity",body:"An ERP protects lives; a continuity plan keeps essential services running after an incident. It starts with a business impact analysis (BIA):",points:["Critical functions: which work must not stop (for example salaries, services to citizens, official records) and how long each can be interrupted.","Dependencies: what each critical function needs: staff, computers and data, power, the building and suppliers.","Recovery strategies: an alternate work site, backup copies of data outside the building and staff trained for more than one job."]},{heading:"4. Accounting for people and those who need help",body:"An evacuation is not finished until everyone is accounted for:",points:["Floor wardens: each floor has a warden who leads the evacuation and reports at the end whether the floor is clear, without putting themselves at risk.","Visitor log: the list of visitors and contractors in the building is kept at reception and taken to the assembly point when the alarm sounds.","A personal plan for each person with a disability: a named helper and an exit route, agreed with the person.","A missing person: no one goes back in to search; their name and last known location are given to the 115 team."]},{heading:"5. Exercises, testing and updates",body:"An untested plan is only paper. There are three levels of testing:",points:["Tabletop exercise: managers talk through a scenario and test the roles and decisions.","Drill or functional exercise: one part of the plan is tested for real, such as the evacuation or backup communications.","Full-scale exercise: with outside agencies, coordinated with Civil Defense. After every exercise an after-action review is held and the plan is updated; how often to exercise and how the plan is approved follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the competent authority."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_15_emergency_drills.mp4",title:"Field exercises",description:"How to conduct evacuation drills in government offices"}],quiz:[{question:"What is the main purpose of assembly points?",options:[{text:"To show off the beauty of the building",correct:!1},{text:"To count staff and confirm that everyone is safe",correct:!0},{text:"To collect waste",correct:!1},{text:"To park emergency vehicles close to the building",correct:!1}],explanation:"Assembly points are used to establish whether everyone has left the building or whether someone remains in danger."},{question:"Where should the assembly points in an emergency response plan be?",options:[{text:"Outside, away from the building and from emergency vehicle routes",correct:!0},{text:"In the main lobby, where all employees can be counted most easily",correct:!1},{text:"Next to the main entrance that the fire engines will use to arrive",correct:!1},{text:"On the roof, where helicopters can see the employees from the air",correct:!1}],explanation:"The plan designates safe locations outside the building, away from the building and from the access routes of emergency vehicles, approved through an engineering inspection."},{question:"What does the chain of command in an emergency response plan define?",options:[{text:"The order in which employees leave the building, by seniority",correct:!1},{text:"Who gives the order to evacuate and who contacts 115 and 122",correct:!0},{text:"The list of companies that supply the building's equipment",correct:!1},{text:"The schedule for cleaning and maintaining the escape routes",correct:!1}],explanation:"The chain of command defines responsibilities, such as who gives the order to evacuate and who contacts the external teams (115 and 122)."},{question:"What is the main output of a business impact analysis (BIA)?",options:[{text:"Which functions are critical, how long they can stop and what they depend on",correct:!0},{text:"A list of staff by salary and grade within the directorate",correct:!1},{text:"The insurance cost of the building and equipment after each incident",correct:!1},{text:"The floor plan of the building with the exit doors marked",correct:!1}],explanation:"A BIA identifies critical functions, how long they can be interrupted and their dependencies (staff, data, power, building, suppliers), so that recovery strategies can be chosen. The evacuation map belongs to the ERP."},{question:"A tabletop exercise shows that no one knows who calls 115. What is the right next step?",options:[{text:"Update the chain of command, train the named people and test again",correct:!0},{text:"Ignore it, because a real incident is different from an exercise",correct:!1},{text:"Let whoever sees the fire decide at the time of the incident",correct:!1},{text:"Publish the number 115 on the directorate’s website only",correct:!1}],explanation:"The point of an exercise is to find gaps before an incident. The gap is fixed in the plan, the people are trained and the plan is tested again; this is the after-action review cycle."},{question:"A plan has one assembly point: next to the main entrance. Why is this a weakness?",options:[{text:"It is close to the building and on the emergency vehicles’ route",correct:!0},{text:"Counting staff next to a door is very difficult",correct:!1},{text:"Because an assembly point must always be on the roof",correct:!1},{text:"There is no weakness if the door is easy to see",correct:!1}],explanation:"The assembly point must be outside, away from the building (falling glass and debris, smoke) and away from the emergency vehicle route so that it does not block the 115 and 122 teams."}],steps:[{title:"Assess the risks",detail:"Identify the site’s hazards (fire, earthquake, flood, gas, hazardous materials) and its critical functions; this is the basis of the whole plan."},{title:"Set the chain of command and roles",detail:"Name who orders the evacuation, who calls 115 and 122 and who the floor wardens are; name a deputy for every post.",critical:!0},{title:"Routes, exits and assembly points",detail:"Keep escape routes clear and make sure exit doors open from the inside without a key. Assembly points are outside, away from the building and from emergency vehicle routes.",critical:!0},{title:"Account for people and plan help",detail:"Set up the headcount (staff lists and the visitor log) and personal plans for people with disabilities. A missing person is reported to 115, not searched for by going back in.",critical:!0},{title:"Plan for continuity",detail:"Use a BIA to identify critical functions, their dependencies and the recovery strategies: an alternate site, off-site data copies and trained staff."},{title:"Train, exercise and update",detail:"Test the plan with tabletop, functional and full-scale exercises, hold an after-action review and update the plan, in coordination with Civil Defense."}],warnings:["Do not chain or lock exit doors for security: exit doors must always open from the inside without a key.","Do not let anyone go back into a burning building to look for a missing colleague: give the name and last known location to 115.","Do not treat a plan that exists only on paper and has never been tested as ready: its gaps will show during the emergency."],tips:["Keep a copy of the plan and the contact list on paper and outside the building.","Involve staff with disabilities in writing their own plans.","Agree the assembly points and vehicle access routes with Civil Defense.","Give floor wardens a short checklist in plain language: what to do, in order."],activity:{prompt:"You are the safety officer of a government directorate building. The security unit wants to chain the side exit doors at night to prevent theft. What do you recommend?",options:[{text:"I refuse; exits must open from the inside without a key. With Civil Defense I propose other measures, such as door alarms and cameras",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: security and safety can go together; a locked exit can trap people in a fire."},{text:"I agree, because only a few guards are in the building at night",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the guards need a way out too, and fires happen at night as well."},{text:"I agree if a key is hung next to each door",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: in smoke and panic, finding a key and opening a chain costs critical time; the door must open without a key."},{text:"I agree and put a sign on the door saying it is locked at night",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a sign does not open the door; anyone who reaches it is still trapped."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"During working hours the fire alarm sounds in the directorate building, and many visitors are inside. At the assembly point the floor wardens report two floors clear, but no one knows whether all the visitors are out. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I compare the visitor log with the wardens’ reports and hand the list of people not found to the 115 team when it arrives",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the headcount gives 115 accurate information on where to search, without anyone going back in."},{text:"I send the floor wardens back inside to look for the visitors",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: going back into a burning building can create new victims; searching is the job of the 115 team."},{text:"I let the staff go home so that the area does not get crowded",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: once they leave, a headcount is impossible and no one knows who is still inside."},{text:"I wait until the fire is over and then do the headcount",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: 115 needs the information on arrival, not after the fire; anyone still inside has no time."}]},{prompt:"Everyone is safe, but the fire has destroyed the server room. The directorate must keep paying salaries and serving citizens. What does the continuity plan do now?",options:[{text:"It activates the alternate site and restores critical functions from off-site backups in the order set by the BIA",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the BIA sets the priorities, and off-site copies let work continue without the damaged building."},{text:"It waits until the building is repaired and then restarts the work",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: repairs may take a long time; the purpose of the plan is that critical functions do not stop."},{text:"It restores all services at the same time",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: resources are limited; the BIA decides what comes first so that the most important work returns sooner."},{text:"It sends staff back into the building to bring out the remaining computers",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: no one enters the damaged building before 115 and the competent authority allow it."}]}],realCase:"In a government building, a tabletop exercise revealed that no one knew who would call 115 and that all the data backups were in the server room itself. The directorate updated the plan: a named contact person and deputy, a visitor log at reception and data copies at another site. A few months later an electrical fire destroyed the server room. Staff and visitors left in order and were accounted for, and the next day critical services restarted from the alternate site. The after-action review found that the tabletop exercise had exposed exactly those gaps."},{id:16,code:"SAR-1600",icon:"Construction",accent:"#16a34a",title:"Advanced search and rescue (USAR)",subtitle:"Structural collapse and urban search and rescue",summary:"Techniques for rescue from beneath rubble, the INSARAG marking system and the use of listening and search devices.",blocks:[{body:"Urban Search and Rescue (USAR) is sensitive and dangerous work. After earthquakes or the collapse of tall buildings, specialist teams need engineering knowledge in order to understand how concrete elements move. This module has been prepared in accordance with the international INSARAG guidelines."},{heading:"1. Phases of rescue",body:"The rescue process is carried out in the following order:",points:["General assessment (size-up / reconnaissance): assessing the extent of the damage, the stability of the structure and hazards such as gas and electricity.","Surface rescue: extricating people who are visible and easily reached.","Void space search: locating casualties in the voids and spaces of collapsed buildings using miniature cameras and listening devices.","Selected debris removal: carefully removing specific pieces with hand tools and hydraulic tools in order to reach casualties; general debris removal with heavy machinery follows."]},{heading:"2. The international marking system (INSARAG marking)",body:"USAR teams place standard markings on the walls of buildings in order to leave information for the teams that follow them:",points:["Worksite marking: under INSARAG, the team writes the worksite ID (sector letter and worksite number, e.g., C-6) on the building so that all teams know it by the same name. The four-quadrant X system (team name, time and date, hazards, victims) is the FEMA system used in the United States.","Victim marking: a large letter (V) is written near the location of a potential or confirmed victim; beneath it, (L) followed by a number for live victims and (D) followed by a number for the deceased. When a victim is removed, the marking is crossed out."]},{heading:"3. Safety at the collapse site",body:"An injured rescuer cannot save anyone. Team safety comes before everything else:",points:["Site hazards: unstable elements, aftershocks, gas, electricity, water and dust. Gas and power are isolated only by qualified people, and a gas valve is closed only if it can be reached without entering the gas area.","A safety officer, a count of rescuers at the start and end of every shift, and an evacuation signal that everyone knows (such as a distinct sound).","Personal protective equipment (PPE): helmet, gloves, sturdy boots, eye protection and a dust mask.","Untrained people do not enter damaged or collapsed buildings. They help from outside as the incident commander directs: informing 115, pointing out where missing people were and keeping the crowd back."]},{heading:"4. Calling out and listening: silent periods",body:"The voice of a person under rubble is often weak, so teams use this procedure:",points:["The commander announces a silent period: all heavy machinery, generators and talking on the site stop.","Rescuers call out loudly, ask the trapped person to tap on something, then listen and work out the direction of the sound.","The spot is marked with the INSARAG (V) marking and the information is sent to the command post so that other teams know it by the same name."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_16_search_tech.mp4",title:"Search technology",description:"Using listening devices and radar to detect the sounds and movements of casualties beneath rubble"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_16_marking_standard.png",title:"INSARAG guidelines",description:"How to record information on collapsed buildings"}],quiz:[{question:"Under INSARAG, what does the letter (L) beneath the (V) marking on a building wall indicate?",options:[{text:"A gas leak",correct:!1},{text:"The presence of live victims (Live)",correct:!0},{text:"Total collapse",correct:!1},{text:"The search at that spot is complete",correct:!1}],explanation:"L stands for Live; together with a number, it shows how many confirmed live victims are at that location."},{question:"What is the first phase of the rescue process in this module?",options:[{text:"Removing rubble at once with heavy machinery to reach casualties sooner",correct:!1},{text:"Searching the voids with miniature cameras and listening devices",correct:!1},{text:"Assessing the damage, the structure's stability and hazards",correct:!0},{text:"Extricating the people who are visible and easy to reach",correct:!1}],explanation:"Rescue starts with the general assessment (size-up): the extent of the damage, the stability of the structure and hazards such as gas and electricity. Surface rescue, void space search and selected debris removal follow."},{question:"When is general debris removal with heavy machinery carried out?",options:[{text:"First, so that the teams can reach the voids more quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Straight after the assessment, before any surface rescue",correct:!1},{text:"At the same time as the surface rescue of visible people",correct:!1},{text:"Last, after the void search and selected debris removal",correct:!0}],explanation:"The module gives the order of the phases: assessment, surface rescue, void space search, then selected debris removal with hand and hydraulic tools; general debris removal with heavy machinery comes last."},{question:"After an earthquake, a group of people want to go into a damaged building to search for others. You have no USAR training. What is your correct role?",options:[{text:"Keep people out and give 115 the exact location",correct:!0},{text:"Go in with them, because a larger group can search the rooms faster",correct:!1},{text:"Wait for daylight, then all go in together to search",correct:!1},{text:"Let them go in as long as each person wears a helmet",correct:!1}],explanation:"Untrained people do not enter damaged buildings: a further collapse, gas and dust make them victims. They help by informing 115, pinpointing the location and keeping people back; a helmet alone does not protect against a collapse."},{question:"Why are silent periods called during a USAR search?",options:[{text:"So that faint voices and tapping from trapped people can be heard",correct:!0},{text:"So that tired rescuers can rest for a while and drink water",correct:!1},{text:"To save fuel for the generators and the heavy machinery",correct:!1},{text:"So that the teams can listen to the latest news on the radio",correct:!1}],explanation:"A trapped person’s voice is usually weak; when machines and talking stop, rescuers can call out, listen and work out where the sound is coming from."},{question:"A team writes the ID “C-6” on a building. Under INSARAG, what is this marking for?",options:[{text:"So that all teams know and refer to that worksite by one name",correct:!0},{text:"To show the number of live victims in that building",correct:!1},{text:"To describe the type of collapse and the level of damage",correct:!1},{text:"To show the team’s country code and number of members",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under INSARAG the worksite ID (sector letter and worksite number, such as C-6) is written so that all teams know the site by one name; victims are marked with (V) and (L) or (D)."}],steps:[{title:"Size up and make the site safe",detail:"Assess the stability of the structure and the gas, power and water. Gas and power are isolated only by qualified people and only when it is safe; no one enters a gas area to reach a valve.",critical:!0},{title:"Set a cordon and control the site",detail:"Set a cordon around the site and keep untrained people and the public outside it. Name a safety officer and an evacuation signal, and keep a count of rescuers.",critical:!0},{title:"Surface rescue",detail:"Remove the people who are visible and easy to reach, without entering unstable parts of the structure.",critical:!0},{title:"Search the voids",detail:"Use silent periods, calling and listening, small cameras and listening devices; mark the worksite and victim locations with the INSARAG system."},{title:"Selected debris removal",detail:"Remove specific pieces carefully with hand and hydraulic tools to reach the trapped person; heavy machinery comes last."},{title:"Hand over and account",detail:"Hand casualties over to the medical teams (122), update the wall markings and account for every rescuer at the end of each shift."}],warnings:["Untrained people do not enter damaged or collapsed buildings: a further collapse, gas or dust can make them new victims; they inform 115 and help from outside.","Do not use heavy machinery before the voids have been searched: it can crush survivors inside them.","Do not light flames or make sparks at a collapse site until it has been checked for gas: leaking gas can explode."],tips:["Plan shifts and rest for rescuers; fatigue causes mistakes and injuries.","Keep a shared map with the worksite IDs (such as C-6) at the command post.","Brief incoming teams on the wall markings and worksites before they start.","Record what witnesses say (who was where at the moment of collapse); it is some of the most valuable search information."],activity:{prompt:"You are an engineering graduate volunteering after an earthquake. People ask you to go into a partly collapsed house because a neighbor can be heard calling from inside. You have no USAR training or equipment. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I do not go in; I call 115 with the exact location and what was heard, keep people back and brief the team when it arrives",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: untrained people do not enter damaged buildings, and your accurate information speeds up the team’s work."},{text:"I go in quickly using my phone light to find the neighbor",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: an aftershock or an unstable piece can trap you too; there would be two victims instead of one."},{text:"With the neighbors, we start pulling stones off the top of the pile",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: removing debris without an assessment can shift the pile and collapse the void the person is in."},{text:"I leave, because without training there is nothing I can do",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you can call 115, pinpoint the location, keep people back and talk to the neighbor from a safe place."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Your USAR team arrives at a collapsed building. Voices can be heard under the rubble, neighbors are digging by hand on top of the pile and there is a smell of gas. As team leader, what do you do first?",options:[{text:"I stop the digging, move people outside a cordon and size up; the gas is isolated only by qualified people if it is safe",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: unassessed digging and gas can cause a further collapse or an explosion; site safety comes first."},{text:"I put my team on the pile with the neighbors so that the digging goes faster",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: more people on the pile add load on the voids, and the gas hazard is not under control."},{text:"I call for an excavator to clear the rubble quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: heavy machinery comes last; it can crush survivors in the voids."},{text:"We start the void search at once and leave the gas for later",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: one spark from a tool can ignite the gas; size-up and safety come before the search."}]},{prompt:"The site has been made safe and a qualified team has isolated the gas. During the void search you hear faint tapping, but a heavy machine is running at the next worksite. What next?",options:[{text:"I ask the command post for a silent period, call out and listen, pinpoint the spot and mark it (V)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: silence lets faint sounds be heard, and the (V) marking keeps the information for other teams."},{text:"I bring the heavy machine over to open the spot quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: heavy machinery can collapse the void and crush the survivor; selected removal is done by hand."},{text:"I mark the spot and move to another worksite without confirming the sound",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: an unconfirmed sound may be lost; a silent period and an exact location are needed first."},{text:"I shout louder without stopping the machines",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the problem is hearing the faint reply, not your voice; the tapping cannot be heard while the machines run."}]}],realCase:"After an earthquake a three-story building collapsed. Neighbors had started digging by hand and there was a smell of gas. The USAR team stopped the digging, set a cordon, and a qualified crew isolated the gas. The neighbors told the team who had been in which room. During a silent period the team heard tapping under the rubble, located the spot with a small camera and marked it (V). After selected removal of specific pieces with hand tools, a woman was brought out of a void and handed over to the medical team. Heavy machinery was used only after the search was finished."},{id:17,code:"TRAFFIC-1700",icon:"CarFront",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Road traffic collisions and technical extrication",subtitle:"Advanced vehicle extrication and road safety",summary:"Managing serious road traffic collisions, using hydraulic rescue tools (Jaws of Life) and protecting the spine.",blocks:[{body:"Responding to road traffic collisions is among the main daily tasks of Civil Defense. In this module we discuss the stages of extricating casualties from vehicles without causing further injury to the spine and neck. In every collision involving casualties, call 115 and 122."},{heading:"1. Scene safety",body:"Before any intervention, the scene must be made safe:",points:["Warning zone: establish the danger zone and place signs and cones ahead of the collision at a distance appropriate to the road speed, in coordination with the traffic police (104).","Vehicle stabilization: use wooden blocks or supports (cribbing/blocks) beneath the vehicle and in front of and behind the tires so that it does not move during the operation.","Energy isolation: disconnect the negative battery cable to reduce the risk of fire and of accidental airbag deployment. Note: airbags can remain live for a short time even after the battery has been disconnected, and hybrid and electric vehicles have high-voltage systems."]},{heading:'2. Extrication techniques with the "Jaws of Life"',body:"Using hydraulic power to reshape the vehicle body:",points:["Cutting: cutting the vehicle's pillars (A, B, C) so that the roof can be removed completely.","Spreading: forcing open buckled doors and creating space for the medical teams.","Neck protection: before any movement, stabilize the casualty's head with your hands (manual in-line stabilization), and if required by the medical protocol, apply a cervical collar (C-collar)."]},{heading:"3. Command and teamwork at the scene",body:"Extrication is a team operation with clear roles:",points:["Incident commander: one person leads, sets the plan and decides when the plan changes.","Safety officer: watches traffic, fuel, fire and the vehicle's stability and can stop the work at any moment.","Tool crew: cuts and spreads only on command and announces each action before it starts.","Medical lead: decides with the commander when and by which route the casualty is removed, according to the casualty's condition."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_17_extrication_tech.mp4",title:"Technical extrication",description:"How metal is cut with hydraulic tools"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_17_airbag_safety.png",title:"Airbag safety",description:"The dangers of accidental airbag deployment during rescue"}],quiz:[{question:"Why is vehicle stabilization the first step before extricating a casualty?",options:[{text:"So the vehicle can be towed away more quickly",correct:!1},{text:"So the vehicle cannot move and worsen neck or back injuries",correct:!0},{text:"So that traffic can pass the scene safely and without delay",correct:!1},{text:"It is not important",correct:!1}],explanation:"Any movement of the vehicle while metal is being cut can damage the spine and cause permanent paralysis of the casualty."},{question:"Why is the negative battery cable disconnected, and what risk remains afterward?",options:[{text:"To reduce fire and airbag risk, but airbags can stay live for a short time",correct:!0},{text:"To make the airbags completely safe at once, so they can be ignored",correct:!1},{text:"To remove every electrical hazard, including in hybrid and electric vehicles",correct:!1},{text:"Only to stop the horn and lights during the operation; no risk remains",correct:!1}],explanation:"Disconnecting the negative cable reduces the risk of fire and of accidental airbag deployment, but airbags can remain live for a short time, and hybrid and electric vehicles have high-voltage systems."},{question:"Before the casualty is moved at all, what must be done to protect the neck?",options:[{text:"Ask the casualty to turn their head to check for pain",correct:!1},{text:"Stabilize the casualty's head with your hands",correct:!0},{text:"Pull the casualty out quickly through the nearest door",correct:!1},{text:"Lay the seat back fully and slide the casualty out",correct:!1}],explanation:"Before any movement, stabilize the casualty's head with your hands (manual in-line stabilization); a cervical collar is applied only if the medical protocol requires it."},{question:"You arrive first at a collision with a trapped driver. Which order of actions is correct?",options:[{text:"Scene safety, vehicle stabilization, energy isolation, then access and extrication",correct:!0},{text:"Extrication first, then scene safety, then stabilization of the vehicle",correct:!1},{text:"Energy isolation, extrication, then stabilization and the warning zone last",correct:!1},{text:"Access to the casualty, then cutting and the warning zone at the same time",correct:!1}],explanation:"The scene and the vehicle must be safe before anyone enters or cuts; energy isolation reduces the fire and airbag risk; extrication comes last."},{question:"During cutting, a rescuer sees an orange cable inside the pillar of a hybrid car. What should the team do?",options:[{text:"Stop cutting there and choose another cut point, following the vehicle's emergency guide",correct:!0},{text:"Keep cutting quickly, because the ordinary battery has already been disconnected",correct:!1},{text:"Pour water on the cable first and then continue cutting through the pillar",correct:!1},{text:"Cut the cable with insulated tools so that the high-voltage system shuts down",correct:!1}],explanation:"Orange cables carry high voltage in hybrid and electric vehicles and are never cut; disconnecting the ordinary battery does not by itself make them safe."},{question:"A trapped driver is conscious and the car is stabilized when flames appear in the engine compartment. What changes?",options:[{text:"Immediate danger allows a rapid removal with the best neck protection possible",correct:!0},{text:"Nothing changes: the planned roof removal continues at the same pace",correct:!1},{text:"The whole team withdraws and waits for the fire to burn itself out",correct:!1},{text:"The casualty is left in place and given a collar before anything else",correct:!1}],explanation:"A casualty with a possible spinal injury may be moved when the place is unsafe, such as a fire; the neck is protected as far as possible while a crew controls the fire."}],steps:[{title:"Make the scene safe",detail:"Park your vehicle so that it shields the scene, wear PPE (helmet, eye protection, gloves and high-visibility clothing), set up the warning zone with the traffic police (104) and check for leaking fuel, fire, hybrid or electric vehicles and unstable loads.",critical:!0},{title:"Stabilize the vehicle",detail:"Chock the wheels and place cribbing under the vehicle before anyone enters it or starts cutting; a vehicle on its side or roof needs more support points. Check stability again after every major cut.",critical:!0},{title:"Isolate energy and identify the vehicle",detail:"Switch off the ignition and disconnect the battery. For a hybrid or electric vehicle, identify the type and follow the manufacturer's emergency response guide; never cut orange high-voltage cables. Treat airbags that have not deployed as live and keep out of their path.",critical:!0},{title:"Reach the casualty and protect the neck",detail:"One rescuer enters the vehicle or reaches through a window, holds the head in line with the body, talks to the casualty and checks breathing and severe bleeding. Life-threatening bleeding is controlled at once; keep 122 informed.",critical:!0},{title:"Create space and remove the casualty as one unit",detail:"Agree the exit route with the medical lead, remove the glass, cut the pillars and roof or spread the doors, then move the casualty in line with the spine on the medical lead's command and according to the medical protocol."}],warnings:["Never cut or spread before the vehicle is stabilized: movement can worsen a spinal injury and injure a rescuer.","Never cut orange high-voltage cables and never work in front of an airbag that has not deployed: both can seriously injure the rescuer.",'Do not pull a casualty out quickly "to save time" unless there is fire or another immediate danger: a rapid removal is only for immediate danger.'],tips:["Name one safety officer who watches traffic, fuel, fire and vehicle stability throughout the operation.","Talk to a conscious casualty all the time and warn them before every loud cut: it reduces fear and movement.","Cover the casualty with a protective sheet or a hard shield before you break glass or cut metal."],activity:{prompt:"You are the first Civil Defense officer at a two-car collision on a fast road at dusk. One car is on its side; the driver is conscious and has neck pain, and fuel is dripping. The rescue tools will arrive in a few minutes. What is your first priority?",options:[{text:"Open the door with a bar and pull the driver out, so the tools are not needed",correct:!1,feedback:"Pulling out a conscious casualty with neck pain without protecting the neck can worsen a spinal injury; there is no fire, so the scene is made safe first."},{text:"Secure the scene: warning zone with 104, a fire watch on the fuel leak, stabilize the car on its side, then one rescuer holds the head in line",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: scene safety, the fire risk and vehicle stability come first, then manual stabilization of the neck while the tools are on the way."},{text:"Start cutting the roof as soon as the tools arrive, to save time",correct:!1,feedback:"Cutting before stabilization and energy isolation can make the car move and set off an airbag."},{text:"Wait away from the cars until the traffic police arrive, before doing anything",correct:!1,feedback:"You coordinate with 104 but you do not wait: you call 115 and 122, set up the warning zone and begin the safe steps yourself."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A car has hit a tree. The front is crushed, the driver's legs are trapped and he is conscious; the passenger airbag has not deployed. The car is already stabilized. What do you do before cutting?",options:[{text:"Disconnect the battery, check whether the car is hybrid or electric and keep everyone out of the path of the undeployed airbag",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: energy isolation and the airbag zone come before cutting; an airbag can stay live for a short time after the battery is disconnected."},{text:"Cut the A-pillar at once, because the trapped legs need space quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Cutting before energy isolation risks setting off the airbag and touching high-voltage parts."},{text:"Lean across the dashboard and try to free the legs by hand",correct:!1,feedback:"That puts your head and body in the path of the undeployed airbag and can move the casualty's spine."}]},{prompt:"The battery is disconnected and the car runs on ordinary fuel. Now the driver's breathing becomes fast and he turns pale; the medical lead says he is getting worse. What extrication plan do you choose?",options:[{text:"Keep the slow full-roof removal exactly as first agreed, whatever his condition",correct:!1,feedback:"The plan follows the casualty's condition; when he gets worse, the medical lead and the commander change the timing."},{text:"Agree a faster planned route with the medical lead, such as spreading the door, while one rescuer keeps holding the head in line",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: when the casualty gets worse, the team switches to a faster planned route but keeps protecting the spine as far as possible."},{text:"Stop the extrication and wait for him to become stable inside the car first",correct:!1,feedback:"A trapped casualty who is getting worse usually cannot be stabilized inside the car; waiting increases the risk."}]}],realCase:"One night on a main road, a minibus left the road and rolled onto its side. The first team parked its vehicle behind the scene as a shield, set up warning signs with the traffic police and named a safety officer. The rescuers stabilized the minibus with cribbing before anyone climbed onto it, disconnected the battery and opened the roof as a wide exit. One rescuer held the trapped driver's head in line the whole time, and the medical team set the order in which the casualties were removed. The lesson: scene safety, stabilization and energy isolation, in that order, protect both casualties and rescuers."},{id:18,code:"IND-1800",icon:"Factory",accent:"#4b5563",title:"Industrial and factory safety",subtitle:"Industrial safety and factory risk management",summary:"Preventing industrial accidents, occupational safety standards (OSHA) and the LOTO system for energy isolation.",blocks:[{body:"Factories and industrial zones are full of complex hazards. Under occupational safety standards (such as OSHA in the United States and the international standard ISO 45001), every institution must have a risk assessment system. This module teaches you how to provide a safe working environment based on engineering principles."},{heading:"1. The LOTO system (lockout/tagout)",body:"The safety standard for machine maintenance (OSHA 1910.147):",points:["Lockout: physically locking the main electrical switch or the pressure valve.","Tagout: attaching a warning tag bearing the name of the person carrying out the work, so that no one switches the machine on.","Zero energy state: verifying that no energy (electrical, pressure, thermal or mechanical) remains in the system before work begins."]},{heading:"2. Machine guarding and PPE",body:"Preventing body contact with moving parts:",points:["Machine guarding: installing metal barriers around the belts and sharp wheels of machines.","Protective equipment: using steel-toe footwear, acid-resistant gloves and hearing protection in places where noise exceeds an average of 85 decibels (dBA) over 8 hours."]},{heading:"3. The hierarchy of controls",body:"Risks are reduced in this order, from the most effective to the least effective:",points:["Elimination: remove the hazard completely, for example by stopping a task that is not needed.","Substitution: replace a dangerous material or process with a less dangerous one.","Engineering controls: machine guards, local exhaust ventilation and interlocks that separate people from the hazard.","Administrative controls: procedures, work permits, training, signs and job rotation.","PPE: the last line of defense, used together with the other controls and not instead of them."]},{heading:"4. Emergency readiness in the factory",body:"Every factory needs a written emergency plan (OSHA 1910.38) that the staff have practised:",points:["Alarm and reporting: how a fire or a leak is reported inside the plant and to 115.","Escape routes and assembly point: exits are kept clear and open from the inside without a key, and people are counted at the assembly point.","Safety data sheets (SDS): available for every chemical, so that the 115 team knows what is stored.","Local rules: follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority for inspections and permits."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_18_loto_training.mp4",title:"The LOTO protocol",description:"How energy lockout is implemented in factories"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_18_ppe_factory.png",title:"PPE",description:"A checklist of protective equipment for industrial workers"}],quiz:[{question:'What is meant by "zero energy state" in machine maintenance?',options:[{text:"Running the machine on a low power setting while it is being repaired",correct:!1},{text:"Making sure no stored energy is left to discharge or move the machine",correct:!0},{text:"Switching the machine off at the main button before work starts",correct:!1},{text:"None of these",correct:!1,fixed:!0}],explanation:"All stored pressure and electricity (in batteries and capacitors) must be released so that the machine does not start up unexpectedly during maintenance."},{question:"In the LOTO system, what is lockout?",options:[{text:"Switching the machine off with its normal stop button",correct:!1},{text:"Hanging a warning sign on the machine's switch without fitting any lock",correct:!1},{text:"Physically locking the main power switch or the pressure valve",correct:!0},{text:"Locking the workshop door so that visitors cannot get in",correct:!1}],explanation:"Lockout is the physical locking of the main electrical switch or the pressure valve before maintenance (OSHA 1910.147); the warning tag is the separate tagout step."},{question:"Where is hearing protection required, according to this module?",options:[{text:"Only where noise goes above 120 dBA for a few minutes",correct:!1},{text:"Only where a worker complains that the noise is too loud",correct:!1},{text:"Wherever noise is above 60 dBA at any moment of the shift",correct:!1},{text:"Where noise averages more than 85 dBA over 8 hours",correct:!0}],explanation:"The module requires hearing protection in places where noise exceeds an average of 85 decibels (dBA) over 8 hours."},{question:"Under the hierarchy of controls, which measure is most effective against the noise of a loud compressor?",options:[{text:"Replacing it with a quieter model or enclosing it in a sound-proof housing",correct:!0},{text:"Giving every worker hearing protection and a short safety talk",correct:!1},{text:"Putting warning signs on the door of the compressor room",correct:!1},{text:"Limiting the time each worker spends next to it in every shift",correct:!1}],explanation:"Elimination, substitution and engineering controls come before administrative controls and PPE because they reduce the hazard at its source; PPE and time limits are support."},{question:"A worker has finished locking out a pump. Which step must come before any work begins?",options:[{text:"Verification: trying to start it and testing that no energy remains",correct:!0},{text:"Telling the manager by phone that the lock is in place",correct:!1},{text:"Putting on gloves and safety glasses before opening the casing",correct:!1},{text:"Writing the date on the tag and photographing the lock",correct:!1}],explanation:"A lock alone does not prove that the energy is gone; verifying zero energy is the last LOTO step before anyone touches the machine."},{question:"Which of these practices is wrong under LOTO?",options:[{text:"A supervisor removes an absent worker's lock without the special procedure",correct:!0},{text:"Each worker on the machine applies a personal lock and a tag",correct:!1},{text:"Stored pressure is bled off before the casing is opened",correct:!1},{text:"Guards are refitted before the machine is restarted",correct:!1}],explanation:"A lock is removed only by the person who applied it; if that person is absent, the employer's special procedure applies (confirm the person is not on site and inform them)."}],steps:[{title:"Prepare and notify",detail:"Using the written procedure, identify every energy source of the machine (electrical, compressed air, hydraulic, thermal and stored mechanical energy) and tell the affected workers that the machine will be shut down."},{title:"Shut down and isolate",detail:"Stop the machine with the normal controls, then open the isolating devices: the main switch, the valves and blocks for moving parts. The stop button alone is not enough.",critical:!0},{title:"Apply locks and tags",detail:"Every worker who works on the machine puts a personal lock and tag on the isolation point; nobody removes another person's lock.",critical:!0},{title:"Release stored energy",detail:"Bleed the pressure, discharge capacitors, lower suspended parts or block them mechanically, and wait until hot parts have cooled.",critical:!0},{title:"Verify zero energy",detail:"Before touching any part, try to start the machine with the normal controls (then return them to off) and test with a meter that no voltage or pressure remains.",critical:!0},{title:"Restore safely",detail:"Remove the tools, refit the guards and check that everyone is clear; then each worker removes his or her own lock and the machine is restarted after the workers are told."}],warnings:["Never rely on a stop button or a control switch as isolation: control circuits can fail or someone else can switch them on.","Never remove another person's lock or tag, and never reach past a guard into a running machine to clear a jam.","Do not treat PPE as a replacement for guards and isolation: it protects only when the other controls are already in place."],tips:["Keep a written LOTO procedure next to each complex machine, with a photo of each isolation point.","Give each worker a personal lock with a single key that stays with that worker.","At shift change, hand over the LOTO status to the new shift face to face, not by a message.","Include contractors and night shifts in LOTO training."],activity:{prompt:'You are the shift engineer. A conveyor belt has jammed; an operator wants to clear it by hand "for one second", with the belt stopped by the emergency stop button. What do you decide?',options:[{text:"Allow it, because the emergency stop has halted the belt",correct:!1,feedback:"An emergency stop is not isolation; the belt can restart or the tension stored in it can be released."},{text:"Apply the LOTO procedure: isolate, lock, tag, release the stored tension and verify before anyone reaches in",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: only locked isolation and a check for zero energy keep the machine safely stopped, even for a short task."},{text:"Let him wear thick gloves so that his hand is protected",correct:!1,feedback:"Gloves do not stop a moving belt from crushing a hand; isolation comes first."},{text:"Ask another worker to stand at the control panel and watch the button",correct:!1,feedback:"A person watching a button is not a lock; people get distracted and controls can fail."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"During maintenance on a hydraulic press, two mechanics from different teams will work at the same time. One lock is already on the main switch. What is required?",options:[{text:"Each mechanic adds his own lock and tag (on a group lockout device if needed)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: each worker controls his own safety; the machine cannot be started until every lock has been removed."},{text:"One lock is enough because the switch is already locked",correct:!1,feedback:"If the first worker finishes and removes his lock, the second worker is left unprotected."},{text:"The second mechanic writes his name on the first person's tag",correct:!1,feedback:"A name on a tag does not physically prevent start-up; each person needs a lock."}]},{prompt:"The press is locked out. While checking, the mechanics see that the ram is still raised. What next?",options:[{text:"Block the ram mechanically or lower it, bleed the hydraulic pressure, then verify zero energy",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: gravity and stored hydraulic pressure are energy; they must be released or blocked before work starts."},{text:"Start work quickly under the ram because the power is locked out",correct:!1,feedback:"Electrical isolation does not stop a raised ram from falling."},{text:"Ask the operator whether the ram has ever fallen by itself",correct:!1,feedback:"Past experience is not verification; stored energy has to be released and checked."}]}],realCase:"In a food factory, a large mixer stopped during cleaning. A worker reached in to remove a stuck tool, and a colleague who did not know someone was inside switched the mixer on from another panel. The worker was injured. The investigation found no written LOTO procedure, shared keys and no verification step. The factory then wrote a procedure for each machine, gave every worker a personal lock and trained all shifts. The lesson: a stop button is not isolation, and everyone who works on a machine needs his or her own lock."},{id:19,code:"HIGH-1900",icon:"Building",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Firefighting in high-rise buildings",subtitle:"High-rise fire dynamics and standpipe operations",summary:"The physical characteristics of fire in high-rise buildings, standpipe systems, the stack effect and evacuation strategies.",blocks:[{body:"High-rise buildings are a major challenge for firefighting teams, because reaching the upper floors with vehicle-mounted aerial ladders is often impossible. In this module we discuss air dynamics in high-rise buildings and how the building's internal water systems are used to extinguish fires at heights of more than 23 meters (75 feet)."},{heading:"1. The stack effect and smoke movement",body:"In high-rise buildings, the temperature difference between the inside and outside of the building creates an air movement known as the stack effect:",points:["Stack effect: in winter, warm air inside the building rises and rapidly spreads smoke through stairwells and elevator shafts.","Reverse stack effect: in summer, cool air-conditioned air flows downward through vertical shafts and can carry smoke down to the lower floors.","Mushrooming: when smoke reaches the top of a building or an enclosed space, it spreads horizontally and then banks back down, filling the upper floors."]},{heading:"2. Standpipe and FDC system engineering",body:"Because fire engines cannot easily deliver water to very high floors, firefighters rely on a fixed piping system (NFPA 14):",points:["Class I system: 2.5-inch hose outlets used only by firefighting teams, with their own hoses.","Fire department connection (FDC): the inlet on the outside of the building through which a fire engine pumps water to boost the system pressure for the upper floors.","Pressure-regulating valves: valves that regulate pressure so that excessive water pressure on the lower floors does not endanger firefighters and hoses."]},{heading:"3. Evacuation protocols and elevator recall",body:"Managing occupants in high-rise buildings requires detailed plans to protect life:",points:["Do not use the elevators: during a fire, elevators enter Phase I recall, travel to the main floor and stop there so that people are not trapped inside; thereafter only firefighters may use them in Phase II mode.","Phased evacuation: first evacuate the occupants of the fire floor and the floors immediately above and below it, then the other floors in sequence.","Areas of refuge: protected areas on the floors for people with disabilities who cannot descend by the stairs."]},{heading:"4. Instructions for occupants: stay or leave",body:"What occupants are told depends on the building's design and its fire plan:",points:["Never use the elevators in a fire; use the protected stairs.","If the fire is in your apartment or office: get out, close the door behind you, raise the alarm and call 115.","If the fire is elsewhere and smoke blocks the stairs: stay where you are, close the doors, seal the gaps with wet cloths, call 115, give your floor and signal from a window.","Whether residents of a tower stay put or evacuate is set by the building's fire plan and the instructions of Civil Defense; this policy is not yet confirmed locally."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_19_highrise_tactics.mp4",title:"High-rise tactics",description:"How fires are extinguished on very high floors"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_19_standpipe_diagram.png",title:"Standpipe diagram",description:"An analysis of the water piping system inside the building"}],quiz:[{question:"What causes the stack effect in high-rise buildings?",options:[{text:"Wind blowing against the side of the building",correct:!1},{text:"The temperature difference between inside and outside",correct:!0},{text:"A shortage of water",correct:!1},{text:"Only the height of the building and the number of floors",correct:!1}],explanation:"The stack effect is caused by differences in air temperature and density, and it moves air upward or downward through vertical shafts (stairwells and elevator shafts)."},{question:"Why must occupants not use the elevators during a fire in a high-rise building?",options:[{text:"They are recalled to the main floor; after that only firefighters use them",correct:!0},{text:"They are kept only for people with disabilities, who escape by elevator",correct:!1},{text:"They may still be used as long as the fire is more than two floors away from you",correct:!1},{text:"They keep running normally, but the stairs are always the faster way out",correct:!1}],explanation:"During a fire, elevators go into Phase I recall: they travel to the main floor and stop so that people are not trapped inside; after that only firefighters may use them in Phase II. Areas of refuge are provided for people who cannot descend by the stairs."},{question:"In phased evacuation, who is evacuated first?",options:[{text:"The top floors first, because smoke spreads up through the stairwells",correct:!1},{text:"The fire floor and the floors directly above and below it",correct:!0},{text:"The ground floor first, so that the stairs stay clear",correct:!1},{text:"Everyone at once, beginning with the lowest floors",correct:!1}],explanation:"Phased evacuation first evacuates the occupants of the fire floor and the floors immediately above and below it, then the other floors in sequence."},{question:"In a high-rise fire, where should the attack crew connect its hose?",options:[{text:"To the standpipe outlet in the stair below the fire floor",correct:!0},{text:"To the outlet on the fire floor, as close to the flames as possible",correct:!1},{text:"To the outlet on the top floor, so that the water flows down by gravity",correct:!1},{text:"To the street hydrant, carrying the hose up the stairs",correct:!1}],explanation:"Connecting below the fire floor keeps the crew's water supply and escape route in a protected stair if conditions get worse."},{question:"In winter, smoke from a fire on a lower floor appears on upper floors far from the fire. What is the most likely explanation?",options:[{text:"The stack effect carries warm smoke up through the stairs and shafts",correct:!0},{text:"The reverse stack effect pushes cool air from the roof downward",correct:!1},{text:"The sprinkler system spreads the smoke when it starts to discharge",correct:!1},{text:"Wind from open windows on the fire floor carries it sideways",correct:!1}],explanation:"In winter, warm air inside the building rises through stairs and elevator shafts and takes the smoke with it; this is the stack effect."},{question:"Which instruction for occupants of a high-rise building is wrong?",options:[{text:"Take the elevator if the fire is several floors below you",correct:!0},{text:"Close the door behind you when you leave the burning unit",correct:!1},{text:"If smoke blocks the stairs, stay put, seal the gaps and call 115",correct:!1},{text:"Tell the 115 call taker your floor and apartment number",correct:!1}],explanation:"Occupants never use the elevators in a fire, however far away it seems; the other three instructions protect occupants."}],steps:[{title:"Confirm the fire floor and control the elevators",detail:"Read the alarm panel to confirm the fire floor and the device that activated, and check that the elevators have returned to the main floor (Phase I Recall). Occupants never use the elevators; only firefighters do, under the Phase II procedure.",critical:!0},{title:"Supply the system",detail:"Connect a pumper to the FDC to boost the standpipe and sprinkler pressure before the attack crews go up."},{title:"Stage below the fire",detail:"Crews climb by the stairs, stage on a floor below the fire and connect their hose to the standpipe outlet in the stair below the fire floor, not on the fire floor itself.",critical:!0},{title:"Protect the evacuation stair",detail:"Name one stair for the attack and another for evacuating occupants; keep the stair doors closed as far as possible so that smoke does not fill the escape route.",critical:!0},{title:"Evacuate in phases and search",detail:"Evacuate the fire floor and the floors directly above and below it first, search for occupants and send a team to the areas of refuge to help people who cannot use the stairs."}],warnings:["Never use an ordinary elevator during a fire: it can stop or open on the fire floor; occupants always use the stairs.","Do not connect the attack hose on the fire floor itself: if conditions get worse, the crew is cut off from the stair.","Do not open the roof door or the top vents without the incident commander's order: it can change the smoke flow and draw smoke into the stairs."],tips:["Learn the alarm panel, the FDC and the stair numbering of the high-rise buildings in your area before an emergency.","Carry spare air cylinders, hose and tools to the staging floor: climbing the stairs takes time and energy.","Ask the building manager for the list of occupants who need help to evacuate and for the locations of the areas of refuge."],activity:{prompt:"You are the safety officer of a 20-floor office tower. The alarm panel shows smoke on floor 12. Staff on floor 15 phone the reception and ask whether they should take the elevator down. What instruction do you give?",options:[{text:"Take the elevator quickly before the fire spreads upward",correct:!1,feedback:"In a fire, elevators return to the main floor or may open on the fire floor; they are not an escape route."},{text:"Do not use the elevator; follow the evacuation plan, go down the protected stairs when told to and close the doors behind you",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the protected stairs are the way out, and phased evacuation sets the order of the floors so that the stairs do not become crowded."},{text:"Stay at your desks and do not tell anyone where you are",correct:!1,feedback:"If people remain, the 115 team must know where they are; hiding their location makes the search harder."},{text:"Go up to the roof and wait for a helicopter",correct:!1,feedback:"A roof rescue is not a plan: smoke rises and the roof door may be locked."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"At night, a fire breaks out on floor 8 of a residential tower. The first engine arrives. The alarm panel shows the fire floor and the elevators are on the ground floor in recall. What is the first crew's first task?",options:[{text:"Connect the pumper to the FDC and send a crew with hose up the stairs to the standpipe below the fire floor",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the fixed piping system is designed for this, and the attack starts from the protected stair below the fire."},{text:"Take an elevator straight to floor 8 to save time",correct:!1,feedback:"Elevators are not used to reach the fire floor; the door can open into smoke and heat. The crew climbs the stairs."},{text:"Wait for the aerial ladder to reach floor 8",correct:!1,feedback:"Aerial ladders often cannot reach high floors or every side of the building; waiting wastes time."}]},{prompt:"Residents of floors 9 and 10 phone 115: the corridor outside their doors is full of smoke. What advice is given on the phone?",options:[{text:"Stay in the apartment, close the door, seal the gaps with wet cloths, signal from a window and give your floor",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: when smoke blocks the way out, a closed apartment gives protection until firefighters arrive; giving the floor speeds up the search."},{text:"Run through the smoky corridor to the stairs as fast as you can",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke in the corridor can make a person collapse before reaching the stairs."},{text:"Use the elevator because the stairs are full of smoke",correct:!1,feedback:"Elevators are in recall during a fire and can trap people in smoke."}]}],realCase:"A fire started in a kitchen on a middle floor of a residential tower. A woman left her apartment and closed the door behind her, which kept smoke out of the corridor for longer. Some neighbors tried to use the elevator, but it had already returned to the ground floor in recall. The first crew connected to the FDC, climbed the stairs and attacked from the standpipe below the fire floor, while a second crew brought residents of the floors directly above down another stair. The lesson: closed doors, the stairs and a planned order save lives."},{id:20,code:"WILD-2000",icon:"TreePine",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Wildland and vegetation firefighting",subtitle:"Wildfire dynamics and vegetation fire safety",summary:"Wildfire dynamics, the influence of weather, the design of defensible space and firebreak techniques for stopping fire spread.",blocks:[{body:"Forest and grassland fires (wildfires) are a distinct hazard because the weather and the wind direction directly affect them. In the Kurdistan Region, summer fires recur and can damage wheat fields and forests. In this module we discuss scientific strategies for preventing and extinguishing these fires."},{heading:"1. The wildfire behavior triangle",body:"The behavior of an outdoor fire depends on three main factors:",points:["Fuel: the type of trees and dry vegetation and their moisture content, which determine how intensely the fire spreads.","Topography: fire travels much faster uphill on slopes, because heat and flames rise and preheat the fuel above.","Weather: temperature, strong wind and low humidity are the three main factors in the spread of fire."]},{heading:"2. Types of wildfires",body:"Fires are classified according to the level at which burning occurs:",points:["Surface fire: the burning of grass and leaves on the ground, which is easier to control.","Crown fire: the most dangerous type; the fire reaches the treetops and, driven by the wind, spreads at very high speed.","Spotting: embers and burning brands are carried by the wind and start new fires farther away (sometimes several kilometers)."]},{heading:"3. Protection strategy: defensible space",body:"To protect villages and buildings near forests, three zones are defined according to CAL FIRE guidelines:",points:["Zone 0 (0–1.5 meters): a zone with no fuel; there must be no trees, dry vegetation or combustible items next to the wall.","Zone 1 (1.5–9 meters): reducing tree density, regularly clearing dry leaves and spacing trees apart.","Zone 2 (9–30 meters): reducing fuel and removing the lower branches of trees, so that fire cannot climb from the ground to the treetops (ladder fuels)."]},{heading:"4. Crew safety in the field (LACES)",body:"Firefighters at vegetation fires follow the basic safety rules known as LACES:",points:["Lookouts: someone watches the fire and the weather and warns the crew.","Communications: all members stay in contact by radio or another agreed way.","Escape routes: at least two planned routes to the safety zone, known to every crew member.","Safety zones: a large burned or cleared area where the crew can stay without danger."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_20_wildfire_behavior.mp4",title:"Fire behavior in mountains",description:"How fire spreads according to wind direction and slope"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_20_defensible_space.png",title:"Protection map",description:"Defining safety zones around houses"}],quiz:[{question:"Why does fire spread faster on mountain slopes than on flat ground?",options:[{text:"Because there are more trees there",correct:!1},{text:"Because heat and flames rise and preheat the fuel above",correct:!0},{text:"Because the cold mountain wind always pushes the fire downhill",correct:!1},{text:"There is no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to the laws of physics, heat rises; therefore, the steeper the slope, the faster the fire spreads uphill."},{question:"Which type of wildfire does this module call the most dangerous, and why?",options:[{text:"Surface fire, because it burns the dry grass and leaves lying on the ground",correct:!1},{text:"Spotting, because the embers always land right beside the main fire",correct:!1},{text:"Crown fire: it reaches the treetops and spreads very fast with the wind",correct:!0},{text:"A grass fire in a wheat field, because the dry grass burns for hours",correct:!1}],explanation:"The module calls the crown fire the most dangerous type: the fire reaches the treetops and, driven by the wind, spreads at very high speed. A surface fire is easier to control, and spotting carries embers far away, sometimes several kilometers."},{question:"What does Zone 0 of defensible space, right next to the wall, require?",options:[{text:"Trees spaced apart, with the dry leaves cleared regularly",correct:!1},{text:"Lower tree branches removed so fire cannot climb to the treetops",correct:!1},{text:"A dense row of green shrubs that shields the wall from the heat",correct:!1},{text:"No fuel at all: no trees, dry plants or combustible items",correct:!0}],explanation:"Zone 0 is the zone with no fuel: there must be no trees, dry vegetation or combustible items next to the wall. Spacing trees apart belongs to Zone 1 and removing lower branches to Zone 2."},{question:"The fire is running fast uphill. Where is the safest place to start the attack?",options:[{text:"From an anchor point at the base or the flank, with an escape route",correct:!0},{text:"Above the fire on the slope, to meet the head of the fire",correct:!1},{text:"In the narrow valley next to the fire, where the wind is weaker",correct:!1},{text:"Directly in front of the head, with as many people as possible",correct:!1}],explanation:"The anchor point and the flanks are safest because the fire runs uphill; working above it, in a narrow valley or in front of the head can trap the crew."},{question:"Your crew has lookouts and radios but no agreed safety zone. What should the leader do?",options:[{text:"Identify the safety zone and escape routes before the attack starts",correct:!0},{text:"Start the attack and look for a safe place if the fire turns",correct:!1},{text:"Rely on the lookout alone to warn the crew in time",correct:!1},{text:"Ask each crew member to choose his own escape route",correct:!1}],explanation:"A warning is of no use without a safety zone and a planned route; LACES is completed before the attack, not after the fire turns."},{question:"Why does a crew patrol a burned area after the flames are out?",options:[{text:"Hidden embers can restart the fire, especially when the wind picks up",correct:!0},{text:"To count the burned trees for the damage report later",correct:!1},{text:"Because the burned area is the most dangerous place to rest",correct:!1},{text:"So that the soil cools and the area can be farmed again",correct:!1}],explanation:"Embers stay in roots and dry material and can restart the fire hours later; mop-up and patrol are part of putting the fire out."}],steps:[{title:"Size up the fire",detail:"Read the fuel, the slope and the wind, find where the head of the fire is going and what is at risk: villages, roads, fields or forest."},{title:"Set up LACES before the attack",detail:"Post a lookout, set up radio communications and agree on escape routes and a safety zone before the attack begins; every crew member must know them.",critical:!0},{title:"Anchor and work the flanks",detail:"Start from a safe anchor point (a road or burned ground) and work along the flanks toward the head; never attack the head of a fast fire from in front of it.",critical:!0},{title:"Build a control line",detail:"Remove the fuel down to bare soil, use roads and plowed strips, wet the edge of the line and look beyond the line for spot fires started by embers."},{title:"Mop up and patrol",detail:"Put out embers near the line, turn over and wet smoldering material, and patrol the area until no smoke is left; fires restart from hidden embers."}],warnings:["Never work upslope of a fire or in a narrow valley (a chimney): fire runs uphill very fast and can trap the crew.","Never enter a marked or suspected mined area to fight a fire, even if the fire is spreading there; report it. If you find you are inside one: stop, stay still, call for help and wait.","Do not burn stubble or waste in windy, hot or dry weather; follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority on open burning."],tips:["Watch the wind all the time: a change in direction turns a flank of the fire into a new head.","Drink water often and rest in the shade between work periods: heat illness is a common danger for crews.","Before the summer, agree with village leaders on firebreaks around fields and houses and on an assembly point."],activity:{prompt:"You lead a Civil Defense crew at a grass fire on a hillside on a windy afternoon. The fire is moving uphill toward a village at the top. A farmer asks you to take the crew up the slope above the fire to stop it before it reaches the houses. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Take the crew upslope above the fire as the farmer asks, because the village is at risk",correct:!1,feedback:"Fire runs fast uphill; a crew above it can be trapped with no escape route."},{text:"Attack from an anchor point at the bottom and along the flanks, warn the village and request help to protect the houses from a safe position with escape routes",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: crew safety under LACES and a flank attack control the fire, and warning the village protects people in time."},{text:"Wait until the fire reaches the road at the top, without warning anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting without a warning leaves the village no time to prepare; a safe attack starts from the flanks."},{text:"Light a backfire at once without the commander's order",correct:!1,feedback:"A backfire is a specialist tactic; done wrong, it creates a second fire."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A wheat field fire is spreading with the wind toward a road. Your lookout reports that embers are starting small fires on the other side of the road. What is your decision?",options:[{text:"Send a small team, guided by the lookout, to catch the spot fires while they are small, with escape routes",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: spot fires are easy to control while they are small; if left, they become a new head behind the line."},{text:"Ignore them; the road itself is a firebreak",correct:!1,feedback:"Embers can fly over roads and firebreaks; spot fires must be put out quickly."},{text:"Move the whole crew across the road at once",correct:!1,feedback:"That leaves the main line unprotected and can trap the crew between two fires."}]},{prompt:"The wind suddenly changes direction and the fire turns toward your crew's position. Your lookout warns you. What now?",options:[{text:"Withdraw at once along the planned escape route to the safety zone and count the crew",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this is exactly what LACES is for; the head count makes sure nobody is left behind."},{text:"Stay and keep working; the control line will hold",correct:!1,feedback:"With a wind change the fire can cross the line quickly; the crew's lives come first."},{text:"Each person escapes by the shortest way he can see",correct:!1,feedback:"An unplanned escape splits the crew and can lead people into the fire; the planned route and a head count are needed."}]}],realCase:"In early summer, a fire started in dry grass near a village and the wind pushed it toward wheat fields. The Civil Defense team posted a lookout on a hill, agreed escape routes to a plowed field and attacked the flanks from the road. When embers crossed the road, a small team put out the spot fires early. The villagers had cleared dry grass around their houses before the summer, which helped the team protect them. The lesson: preparation before the season and crew safety rules during the fire work together."},{id:21,code:"WATER-2100",icon:"LifeBuoy",accent:"#0891b2",title:"Water rescue and flood hydrology",subtitle:"Advanced water rescue and drowning prevention",summary:"An analysis of the hydraulic force of water, emergency rescue techniques and drowning prevention.",blocks:[{body:"Drowning is one of the leading causes of death during outings and disasters. Understanding the force of water and how the body functions underwater (the mammalian dive reflex) is very important for rescue teams. In this module we discuss the science of water rescue and protection protocols. In any water incident, call 115 and 122."},{heading:"1. The physics of fast-moving water (swiftwater hydrology)",body:"Fast-moving water generates an enormous force that a person cannot withstand:",points:["Force of water: if the speed of the water doubles, its force on a body or a vehicle increases fourfold.","Strainers: obstacles in the water such as tree branches or nets that let water pass through but trap solid objects (such as a person), causing rapid drowning.","Eddies: calm areas behind large rocks that rescue teams use for resting and approaching."]},{heading:"2. The golden rules of water rescue",body:"To protect the rescuer's life, this sequence must be followed:",points:["REACH: use a pole or a ladder without entering the water yourself.","THROW: throw a rope or something that floats (such as a tire).","ROW: use a boat to approach the casualty.","GO: the last option, only for trained specialists wearing personal flotation devices (PFDs).","HELO: the use of helicopters by specialized teams in major floods."]},{heading:"3. First aid for drowning victims",body:"When a person has been brought out of the water, every second counts:",points:["Breathing: drowning is fundamentally a problem of oxygen deprivation; if the person is unconscious and not breathing normally, start CPR and add rescue breaths if you are trained (according to the ERC, beginning with 5 initial breaths).","Hypothermia: people brought out of the water often suffer from hypothermia; remove wet clothing and rewarm them gradually.",'Delayed lung deterioration: anyone who has been brought out of the water and has a cough, shortness of breath or confusion must be examined by a doctor, because lung deterioration can appear after several hours. Outdated terms such as "secondary/dry drowning" are no longer used medically.']},{heading:"4. Rescue from the bank and flood water",body:"Most rescues by people who are not water rescue specialists are done from the bank:",points:["Talk and reach: shout clear instructions, then reach out with a pole, a branch or a ladder while lying down or holding on to something fixed.","Throw: throw a rope or a floating object (a ring or a closed empty container) beyond the person and pull them gently toward the bank.","Wear a PFD whenever you work near moving water, even on the bank.","Never walk or drive into flood water: 15 cm of moving water can knock a person down and 30 cm can carry a car away."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_21_swiftwater_rescue.mp4",title:"Emergency rescue",description:"International techniques for extracting people from fast-moving water"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_21_hydrology.png",title:"Analysis of the force of water",description:"Demonstrating the force of water on the human body"}],quiz:[{question:"What is the first safe step in rescuing a person in the water?",options:[{text:"Jumping into the water",correct:!1},{text:"REACH (extending a pole or an object)",correct:!0},{text:"ROW (rowing a boat out to the person)",correct:!1},{text:"Waiting until the teams arrive",correct:!1}],explanation:"REACH is the safest method, because you do not enter the water and your own life is not put at risk."},{question:"Why should people rescued from the water who have signs such as coughing or shortness of breath go to the hospital, even if they appear well?",options:[{text:"To check for cuts and bruises from the rescue",correct:!1},{text:"Because lung damage can appear several hours later",correct:!0},{text:"To change their name",correct:!1},{text:"There is no need if they can walk and talk normally",correct:!1}],explanation:"After drowning, water that has entered the lungs can gradually cause lung deterioration, and symptoms may appear after several hours; a medical examination is therefore necessary."},{question:"What is a strainer in fast-moving water, and why is it dangerous?",options:[{text:"An obstacle that lets water pass but traps a person, causing rapid drowning",correct:!0},{text:"A calm area behind a large rock that rescuers use for resting",correct:!1},{text:"A net that rescue teams stretch across the river to catch people who are swept away",correct:!1},{text:"A filter on a rescuer's mask that keeps dirty water out of the lungs",correct:!1}],explanation:"Strainers are obstacles in the water, such as tree branches or nets, that let water pass through but trap solid objects such as a person, causing rapid drowning. The calm areas behind large rocks are eddies."},{question:"A person is struggling in a pool close to the edge. You are not trained in water rescue. What is the best first action?",options:[{text:"Lie down at the edge and reach out a pole or a towel while shouting for help",correct:!0},{text:"Jump in and hold the person from behind until help arrives",correct:!1},{text:"Throw a heavy object near the person so that they can grab it",correct:!1},{text:"Run to find a lifeguard and leave the person alone and out of sight",correct:!1}],explanation:"Reaching from the edge keeps the rescuer safe; going in without training or leaving the person alone increases the danger."},{question:"Why is a rope never tied around a rescuer's waist in moving water, unless it is a quick-release harness?",options:[{text:"The current can pin the rescuer under the water by the rope",correct:!0},{text:"The rope gets wet and becomes too heavy to throw again",correct:!1},{text:"The rescuer moves too slowly to reach the victim in time",correct:!1},{text:"The rope could hurt the victim when it is pulled tight",correct:!1}],explanation:"In moving water, the force of the current on a tied rope can hold the rescuer under with no way to get free; specialist teams therefore use quick-release harnesses."},{question:"A boy was pulled from a river; he is talking and says he is fine, but he keeps coughing. What do you advise?",options:[{text:"Have him checked by a doctor, because lung problems can appear hours later",correct:!0},{text:"Let him go home, because he is talking and breathing normally",correct:!1},{text:"Give him a hot drink and let him rest by the river for an hour",correct:!1},{text:"Encourage him to vomit to empty the water from his stomach",correct:!1}],explanation:"Coughing, shortness of breath or confusion after drowning are signs of a lung problem that can appear late; everyone pulled from the water must be checked by a doctor."}],steps:[{title:"Call for help and keep the person in sight",detail:"Call 115 and 122, keep the person in the water in sight and point at them so the team can find them; if they go under, remember the spot.",critical:!0},{title:"Talk and reach",detail:"Stay on the bank, keep low or hold on to something fixed, and reach out with a pole, a branch or a ladder; never let the person in the water pull you in.",critical:!0},{title:"Throw",detail:"If you cannot reach, throw a rope or anything that floats beyond the person, guide them to it, then pull them gently toward the bank."},{title:"Do not go in without training",detail:"Do not enter the water unless you are a trained water rescuer wearing a PFD and working with a team; untrained entry adds a second victim.",critical:!0},{title:"Care after the rescue",detail:"If the person is not breathing normally, start CPR (with breaths if you are trained); remove wet clothing, warm the person gradually and send everyone pulled from the water for a medical check.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never enter the water to rescue someone without training and equipment: a panicking person can pull the rescuer under, and fast currents and strainers trap swimmers.","Never walk or drive through flood water, even if it looks shallow: its depth and speed cannot be judged and the road may be gone.","Do not tie a rope around a rescuer's body in moving water unless it is a quick-release rescue harness: the current can hold the rescuer under."],tips:["Keep a throw bag or a rope with a floating object near the rivers, pools and canals where your team works.","Practise throwing a rope accurately on dry land before you need it.","In floods, report people trapped on roofs or vehicles to 115 with an exact location instead of trying to reach them yourself."],activity:{prompt:"You are a civil engineering student on a site visit near an irrigation canal. A worker slips into the fast-flowing canal and grabs a branch a few meters downstream. You are not a strong swimmer. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Jump in and swim to him, because there is no time to wait",correct:!1,feedback:"Fast water can carry away even a strong swimmer; untrained entry creates a second victim."},{text:"Shout for help and call 115, lie down on the bank and reach a pole to him or throw him a rope or a floating object",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: reaching and throwing from the bank keep the rescuer safe and are often enough."},{text:"Run along the canal to look for a boat and leave him alone without telling anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"Leaving him alone without raising the alarm can lose sight of him; call for help first and stay with him."},{text:"Form a human chain with your colleagues into the canal",correct:!1,feedback:"A human chain in fast water can be swept away together; reach or throw from the bank."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"During a flood, a car is stuck in moving water on a road and the driver is on its roof. Bystanders want to wade out with a rope tied around one man's waist. You are the first Civil Defense member on the scene and have no water rescue equipment. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop the bystanders from going in, ask 115 for the water rescue team, keep the driver in sight and tell him to stay on the roof",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the car roof is safer than the moving water for now, and stopping the bystanders prevents new victims."},{text:"Let them go and hold the rope tightly from the bank yourself",correct:!1,feedback:"A rope tied around the waist in moving water can hold the man under; this is only for trained teams with special equipment."},{text:"Drive your vehicle closer through the water to reach him",correct:!1,feedback:"Moving water can carry your vehicle away too; 30 cm of moving water can carry a car away."}]},{prompt:"The water rescue team is still on the way. The water is rising toward the car roof. A throw bag has been brought from a nearby team vehicle. What now?",options:[{text:"Throw the bag to the driver from a stable spot on the bank and pull him to the bank, without anyone entering the water",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: when a safe throw from the bank is possible, it is the best option; nobody enters the water."},{text:"Wade in up to your knees to throw from closer",correct:!1,feedback:"15 cm of moving water can knock a person down; throw from the bank."},{text:"Wait for the team even if the water covers the roof",correct:!1,feedback:"When a safe option from the bank exists, you act; waiting may cost his life."}]}],realCase:"On a family outing, a teenager was carried away by the current of a river. His uncle wanted to jump in, but a neighbor who had attended a Civil Defense course stopped him, called 115 and 122 and ran downstream along the bank. Where the river bent toward the bank, she lay down and held out a long branch, and the teenager grabbed it. Out of the water he kept coughing, so he was taken to hospital for a check even though he said he felt fine. The lesson: reach or throw from the bank, and everyone pulled from the water sees a doctor."},{id:22,code:"WEATHER-2200",icon:"CloudLightning",accent:"#9333ea",title:"Severe weather and natural emergencies",subtitle:"Severe weather emergencies and survival",summary:"An analysis of lightning, high winds and dust storms, and of the measures for protecting the body against extreme cold and heat.",blocks:[{body:"Severe weather can cause major disasters and loss of life within a short time. In the Kurdistan Region we face lightning, severe dust storms and very high or very low temperatures. Recognizing the warning signs and knowing the protective measures before an event is the key to survival."},{heading:"1. The lightning phenomenon (lightning safety)",body:"Lightning can carry up to approximately 300 million volts and tens of thousands of amperes (NOAA), so protection is essential:",points:["Shelter: the best places during lightning are enclosed buildings or fully enclosed vehicles with a metal roof.","Keep away: never stand under tall trees, and keep away from open and elevated areas and from water.","If you can hear thunder, you are close enough to be struck by lightning: go indoors immediately (When Thunder Roars, Go Indoors), and stay there for 30 minutes after the last thunder. The older 30-30 rule used the same 30-minute waiting period.","Electrical appliances: at home, unplug sensitive appliances so that a sudden voltage spike (power surge) does not damage them."]},{heading:"2. Dust storms and high winds",body:"Dust storms are a major health and traffic hazard in our region:",points:["Driving: if visibility becomes very poor, pull completely off the road and stop on the right-hand side, switch off the vehicle's lights and apply the parking brake, so that other vehicles do not follow your lights and collide with you.","Respiratory protection: use N95 masks or a damp cloth to reduce the entry of fine particles into the lungs.","High winds: during high winds, keep away from glass windows and stay in interior rooms of the building."]},{heading:"3. Temperature emergencies (heat and cold stress)",body:"Every first aid provider needs to understand hypothermia and heat stroke:",points:["Heat stroke: when the body temperature exceeds 40°C and changes in mental status (confusion, disorientation) appear. The skin may be dry, red and hot, but in exertional heat stroke from work or exercise there may be sweating. This is a life-threatening condition: call 122 and cool the body with cold water immediately.","Hypothermia: when the body's core temperature falls below 35°C. Signs: violent shivering, slurred speech and a slowing heart rate.","Frostbite: the freezing of body tissue (fingers, ears, nose) as a result of very severe cold; never rub the frostbitten area and never rewarm it with very hot water."]},{heading:"4. Warnings and preparation before the event",body:"Prepare before the season and act on warnings:",points:["Follow the official forecasts and weather warnings and the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority.","Plan outdoor work and events around the forecast: move them, or set a stop rule for thunder, dust and heat.","Keep an emergency kit: water, a flashlight, a radio, batteries, personal medicines and masks for dust.","Check on older people, people with chronic illness and outdoor workers during heat and cold waves."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_22_weather_survival.mp4",title:"Protective measures",description:"How to deal with lightning and high winds"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_22_heat_stroke.png",title:"Signs of heat illness",description:"The difference between heat exhaustion and life-threatening heat stroke"}],quiz:[{question:"During a thunderstorm, when should you go indoors?",options:[{text:"Only when it starts to rain",correct:!1},{text:"As soon as you hear thunder",correct:!0},{text:"Only when fewer than 30 seconds pass between the flash and the thunder",correct:!1},{text:"Only when the storm is directly overhead",correct:!1}],explanation:"If you can hear thunder, you are close enough to be struck by lightning: go indoors at once (When Thunder Roars, Go Indoors). There is no safe place outside during a thunderstorm, and waiting outside to count seconds is not safe. Stay inside until 30 minutes after the last thunder (NWS)."},{question:"Which of the following is a sign of heat stroke?",options:[{text:"Heavy sweating with cool, pale skin, a normal temperature and a clear mind",correct:!1},{text:"Body temperature above 40°C with confusion (skin may be dry or sweating)",correct:!0},{text:"Shivering after a long time in the sun",correct:!1},{text:"Sneezing",correct:!1}],explanation:"In heat stroke, the body loses its ability to cool itself and its temperature rises above 40°C, which damages the brain. Immediate cooling and a call to 122 are required."},{question:"Visibility drops very low while you are driving in a dust storm. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Keep driving slowly with your hazard lights on, following the car in front of you",correct:!1},{text:"Pull completely off the road, switch off your lights and apply the parking brake",correct:!0},{text:"Stop in your lane and switch on all your lights so that others can see you",correct:!1},{text:"Speed up to get out of the dust storm before visibility gets any worse",correct:!1}],explanation:"Pull completely off the road, stop on the right-hand side, switch off the vehicle's lights and apply the parking brake, so that other vehicles do not follow your lights and collide with you."},{question:"A storm is passing. The last thunder was heard 20 minutes ago and the rain has stopped. Can the team go back outside?",options:[{text:"Not yet: wait until 30 minutes have passed without thunder",correct:!0},{text:"Yes: the rain has stopped, so the danger is over",correct:!1},{text:"Yes: if no lightning flash has been seen for 10 minutes",correct:!1},{text:"Yes: if they stay away from metal tools and fences",correct:!1}],explanation:"Lightning can strike even after the rain stops; the rule is to stay indoors until 30 minutes after the last thunder."},{question:"Which sign best separates heat stroke from heat exhaustion in a worker?",options:[{text:"Confusion or strange behavior",correct:!0},{text:"Heavy sweating and thirst",correct:!1},{text:"Headache and tiredness after work",correct:!1},{text:"Cramps in the leg muscles",correct:!1}],explanation:"Changes in mental state (confusion, strange speech, collapse or seizure) point to heat stroke; sweating, thirst, headache and cramps can also occur in heat exhaustion."},{question:"In a dust storm with very poor visibility, why does a driver switch off the lights after pulling off the road?",options:[{text:"So that other drivers do not follow the lights and hit the parked car",correct:!0},{text:"To save the battery during a long storm so the car can start later",correct:!1},{text:"Because dust damages the headlights when they are hot",correct:!1},{text:"Because the light reflects off the dust and blinds the driver",correct:!1}],explanation:"In dust, other drivers follow the lights in front of them; if a parked car has its lights on, they may drive into it."}],steps:[{title:"Check forecasts and warnings",detail:"Before outdoor work or an event, check the official forecasts and warnings and name the person who decides to stop."},{title:"Lightning: stop and shelter",detail:"When thunder is heard, stop outdoor work and move everyone into a building or a fully enclosed hard-top vehicle; resume 30 minutes after the last thunder.",critical:!0},{title:"Dust storm: protect breathing and driving",detail:"Bring people indoors, close the windows and use masks; drivers pull completely off the road, switch off the lights and apply the parking brake.",critical:!0},{title:"Heat: water, shade and rest",detail:"Schedule heavy work for the cooler hours, provide water, shade and rest, and watch for confusion: heat stroke is an emergency, so call 122 and cool the body at once.",critical:!0},{title:"Cold: shelter and dry clothing",detail:"Provide warm, dry clothing and breaks in a warm place; a person who is shivering and confused is moved somewhere warm and rewarmed gradually."}],warnings:["Never shelter under a tall tree, in an open tent or under a shelter with open sides during lightning: none of them protects you.","Do not give drinks to a confused person with heat stroke and do not wait for a thermometer: cool the person first and call 122.","Never rub frostbitten skin or warm it with very hot water or by a fire: the tissue is damaged further."],tips:['Agree on a simple stop signal with outdoor teams, such as "thunder heard, everyone inside".',"Keep spare masks and drinking water in every field vehicle during the dust and summer seasons.","Pair outdoor workers so that each one watches the other for signs of heat or cold illness."],activity:{prompt:"You coordinate a university outdoor sports day. Dark clouds are approaching and thunder can be heard in the distance, but the sky over the field is still clear. The organizers want to finish the last match first. What do you decide?",options:[{text:"Finish the match quickly, because the storm is still far away",correct:!1,feedback:"If thunder can be heard, lightning can strike you; a clear sky overhead does not mean safety."},{text:"Stop the event now and move everyone into buildings; resume only 30 minutes after the last thunder",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: when thunder roars, go indoors; no place outside is safe."},{text:"Move the players under the large trees at the edge of the field",correct:!1,feedback:"Tall trees attract lightning, and sheltering under them is very dangerous."},{text:"Carry on but tell people to lie flat on the ground if lightning gets close",correct:!1,feedback:"No body position outside is safe; the only option is to get into a building or an enclosed vehicle."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A Civil Defense field team is working on a road in the summer heat. One worker becomes confused, talks strangely and stumbles; his skin is still sweating. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Call 122 and start cooling him at once: shade, cold water, wet towels and fanning",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: confusion in the heat is a sign of heat stroke; rapid cooling saves lives."},{text:"Give him water to drink and let him carry on more slowly",correct:!1,feedback:"A confused person must not be given anything to drink; this is an emergency and the work must stop at once."},{text:"Do nothing yet, because sweating shows it is not heat stroke",correct:!1,feedback:"In exertional heat stroke the skin may still sweat; the key sign is confusion."}]},{prompt:"While you are cooling him, a dust storm reaches the site and visibility drops quickly. The ambulance is on its way. What now?",options:[{text:"Move him into the nearest building or an enclosed air-conditioned vehicle, keep cooling, protect his breathing and update 122 with your exact location",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: cooling does not stop, dust harms breathing, and an exact location gets the ambulance there."},{text:"Drive him yourself at high speed on the road despite the poor visibility",correct:!1,feedback:"Driving in thick dust risks a serious crash; stay in a protected place and keep cooling."},{text:"Stop cooling and wait outside for the ambulance so that it can see you",correct:!1,feedback:"Stopping the cooling puts his life at risk; give the exact location by phone."}]}],realCase:"On a very hot day, a dust storm reached a construction site. The site supervisor had checked the forecast in the morning, moved heavy work to the early hours and kept water and masks in the site office. When the dust arrived, the workers were brought inside and the drivers stayed where they were instead of leaving. Later, a worker who had kept working outside became confused; his colleagues called 122 and cooled him with water and wet towels until the ambulance arrived. The lesson: planning by the forecast and rapid cooling protect workers."},{id:23,code:"SUPP-2300",icon:"BellRing",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Fire alarm and fire suppression systems",subtitle:"Fire detection and automatic suppression engineering",summary:"A technical analysis of fire detectors, sprinkler systems and clean agent extinguishing technology.",blocks:[{body:"Automatic protection systems are the first line of defense in modern buildings. These systems are designed to detect a fire in its incipient stage and to control it without human intervention. This module explains the engineering principles in accordance with the standards NFPA 13 (sprinklers) and NFPA 72 (fire alarms)."},{heading:"1. Detection and alarm systems",body:"Detectors are the eyes of the building and operate by several methods:",points:["Photoelectric smoke detector: relies on a beam of light; it is the best type for detecting slow, smoldering fires.","Ionization detector: more sensitive to the very small particles produced by fast, flaming fires.","Heat detectors: used in places where smoke or steam is normally present (such as kitchens and factories), and activated by a rise in temperature.","Notification appliances: the use of alarm sounders and flashing lights to alert the entire building."]},{heading:"2. Sprinkler system engineering",body:"This system uses water pressure to control a fire at its point of origin:",points:["Glass bulb: most sprinkler heads have a liquid-filled glass bulb that breaks at a specified temperature and allows water to discharge.","System types: wet pipe, which is the most common and in which the pipes are filled with water, and dry pipe for places where there is a risk of the water freezing.","A scientific fact: only the heads exposed to the heat of the fire open, not all the heads in the building at once (as shown in movies)."]},{heading:"3. Extinguishing with clean agents (clean agent systems)",body:"In places where water would damage the contents (such as server rooms and museums), gaseous agents are used (NFPA 2001):",points:["FM-200 and FK-5-1-12 (Novec 1230): these agents act mainly by absorbing heat and also partly break the chain reaction, and they leave no residue.","CO₂ systems: very effective but pose an asphyxiation hazard to people, so they are generally used only in unoccupied spaces, with a pre-discharge alarm and system lockout (NFPA 12).","Saponification: the method of extinguishing kitchen fires (Class K under NFPA, Class F under EN 2) by converting the burning oil into a soap-like substance."]},{heading:"4. Keeping systems in service",body:"A system that is switched off or blocked protects nobody:",points:["Keep the space under sprinkler heads clear: storage piled too high blocks the water spray; follow the design and NFPA 13.","Never paint, cover or hang anything on sprinkler heads or detectors.","Keep sprinkler control valves open and secured (sealed or locked), and report any closed valve at once.","Inspection, testing and maintenance follow NFPA 25, NFPA 72 and the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority; record every impairment and fix it quickly."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_23_sprinkler_physics.mp4",title:"Sprinkler physics",description:"An analysis of how the automatic water system operates"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_23_detector_types.png",title:"Types of detectors",description:"A comparison of how smoke and heat detectors work"}],quiz:[{question:"Which type of smoke detector is better at detecting slow, smoldering fires?",options:[{text:"Ionization smoke detector",correct:!1},{text:"Photoelectric detector",correct:!0},{text:"Heat detector",correct:!1},{text:"Flame detector",correct:!1}],explanation:"Photoelectric detectors use a beam of light to detect thick smoke very quickly, which is characteristic of slow fires."},{question:"In a standard sprinkler system, do all the sprinkler heads open at the same time?",options:[{text:"Yes, all heads open together as soon as the alarm sounds",correct:!1},{text:"No, only the head whose bulb breaks from the heat opens",correct:!0},{text:"Only if firefighters open the main valve by hand",correct:!1},{text:"Only at night",correct:!1}],explanation:"A standard system operates locally; each head operates independently so that water damage in other areas is reduced."},{question:"Why are CO₂ suppression systems generally used only in unoccupied spaces?",options:[{text:"They leave a heavy residue that damages people's skin",correct:!1},{text:"They only work when the room is empty of furniture",correct:!1},{text:"They pose an asphyxiation hazard to people",correct:!0},{text:"They are weaker when people open and close the doors",correct:!1}],explanation:"CO₂ systems are very effective but pose an asphyxiation hazard to people, so they are generally used only in unoccupied spaces, with a pre-discharge alarm and system lockout (NFPA 12)."},{question:"The kitchen of a student hostel has frequent false alarms from its smoke detector. Which solution is correct?",options:[{text:"Replace it with a heat detector suitable for the kitchen",correct:!0},{text:"Cover the detector with a bag while people are cooking",correct:!1},{text:"Take out its battery and report the fault later",correct:!1},{text:"Lower its sensitivity without any technical check",correct:!1}],explanation:"Heat detectors are used where smoke or steam is normally present; covering or disabling the detector removes the protection completely."},{question:"Why is a dry pipe system chosen for an unheated warehouse in a cold region?",options:[{text:"Water in the pipes could freeze, so the pipes hold air until a head opens",correct:!0},{text:"Dry pipes discharge more water than wet pipe systems at each head",correct:!1},{text:"Dry pipes open all the heads at once to cover the whole warehouse",correct:!1},{text:"Water damage is lower because dry pipes never release any water",correct:!1}],explanation:"In a dry pipe system the pipes are filled with pressurized air; when a head opens the air escapes and water follows. This prevents freezing; only heated heads open."},{question:"Which statement about a CO₂ total flooding system is correct?",options:[{text:"People must leave before discharge because the concentration can kill",correct:!0},{text:"It is the best choice for occupied offices because it leaves no residue",correct:!1},{text:"It is safe to enter the room right after discharge to check the fire",correct:!1},{text:"It works by cooling only, so it is used on kitchen oil fires",correct:!1}],explanation:"CO₂ puts out fire by lowering the oxygen, and the extinguishing concentration is lethal to people; it is therefore generally used in unoccupied spaces with a pre-discharge alarm and system lockout (NFPA 12)."}],steps:[{title:"Treat every alarm as real",detail:"Start the evacuation according to the fire plan and call 115; do not silence the alarm until its cause is known.",critical:!0},{title:"Read the panel",detail:"Find the zone and the device that activated on the alarm panel, and send only trained staff to check, and only if it is safe."},{title:"Clean agent or CO₂ rooms",detail:"When the pre-discharge alarm sounds, everyone leaves the room at once and the doors are closed; nobody goes back in until the room has been ventilated and cleared.",critical:!0},{title:"Keep the sprinkler valve open",detail:"Do not close the sprinkler control valve to limit water damage; only the 115 team decides when the fire is out and the valve can be closed. Limit water damage with covers and drainage.",critical:!0},{title:"Restore and report",detail:"After the incident, a qualified contractor resets the system, replaces the heads that opened and recharges the agent; record every period the system is out of service and inform Civil Defense as required."}],warnings:["Never close a sprinkler valve during a fire to limit water damage: the fire can grow again; only the fire service decides.","Never enter a room protected by CO₂ after a discharge without permission from the fire service: the concentration that puts out the fire can kill a person.","Do not disable a detector because of false alarms; find the cause (dust, steam or the wrong type of detector) and fix it."],tips:["Put the zone map next to the alarm panel so that staff can find the source of an alarm quickly.","Train staff who work in clean agent rooms on what the pre-discharge alarm means and on the way out.","Choose heat detectors for kitchens and dusty or steamy areas to reduce false alarms."],activity:{prompt:"You are the facility manager of a university building. A sprinkler head has opened in a storeroom; the fire seems to be out and water is spreading in the corridor. A colleague wants to close the main sprinkler valve at once to protect the library below. What do you decide?",options:[{text:"Close the valve at once, because the fire looks out",correct:!1,feedback:'A fire that "looks out" can keep burning inside boxes or behind goods and flare up again; the 115 team decides.'},{text:"Keep the valve open, evacuate, call 115 and limit the water damage with covers and drainage until the team confirms the fire is out",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: water damage can be limited, but a fire that flares up again puts lives and the building at risk."},{text:"Close the valve halfway to reduce the flow",correct:!1,feedback:"Reducing the pressure can stop the sprinkler head from controlling the fire; this decision belongs to the fire service."},{text:"Break other sprinkler heads to cool the room faster",correct:!1,feedback:"Breaking heads adds water damage and lowers the system pressure at the head that is controlling the fire."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A server room is protected by a clean agent system. The pre-discharge alarm sounds while a technician inside is finishing a backup. What must happen?",options:[{text:"The technician leaves at once and the door is closed; a backup is not worth a life",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: NFPA 2001 requires people to leave before discharge; the fire itself also produces toxic gases."},{text:"He stays, because clean agents are harmless to people",correct:!1,feedback:"Clean agents are designed for occupied spaces at the right concentration, but NFPA 2001 still requires people to leave; smoke and fire products are toxic."},{text:"He opens the door wide to let air in during the discharge",correct:!1,feedback:"An open door lets the agent escape before it reaches the concentration needed, and the fire goes on."}]},{prompt:"The agent has discharged, the panel still shows the room in alarm, and the IT manager wants to go in to check the servers. What do you decide?",options:[{text:"Keep the room closed and wait for the 115 team; enter only after it has been checked and ventilated",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the agent needs time to hold the fire, and the air in the room after a discharge may not be safe to breathe."},{text:"Let him go in quickly with a wet cloth over his mouth",correct:!1,feedback:"A wet cloth does not protect against low oxygen or fire gases."},{text:"Open the door to see whether there is still smoke",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening the door lets the agent out and air in; the fire can flare up again."}]}],realCase:"In a university laboratory building, a heat detector in a storeroom set off the alarm and one sprinkler head opened above a burning box of waste. Staff evacuated according to the plan and called 115. A technician wanted to close the sprinkler valve because water was running into the corridor, but the safety officer kept it open until the fire team confirmed the fire was out. The damage stayed in one room. The lesson: a single sprinkler head can control a fire where it starts, and valves stay open until the fire service decides."},{id:24,code:"EVAC-2400",icon:"Tent",accent:"#0d9488",title:"Mass evacuation and shelter management",subtitle:"Mass evacuation strategy and shelter operations",summary:"Mass evacuation strategies in major disasters, the organization of relief camps and compliance with the international humanitarian standards for shelters (Sphere).",blocks:[{body:"In major disasters such as devastating earthquakes or widespread floods, evacuating thousands of people to safe locations requires a complex logistical plan. Shelter management is not only about providing space; it is about ensuring health, security and human dignity in accordance with international standards (such as the Sphere Handbook). This module explains the principles of organizing people and shelters."},{heading:"1. Strategic evacuation planning",body:"The process of evacuating people must follow organized phases so that confusion does not arise:",points:["Designating evacuation routes: mapping the main and alternative routes, and designating the routes reserved for emergency vehicles only.","Traffic management: coordinating with the traffic police (104) to prevent congestion and the blocking of exit routes.","Public warning system: using loudspeakers, SMS, radio and local television to inform people when the evacuation begins."]},{heading:"2. Managing shelters and emergency camps",body:"According to international standards (Sphere), every shelter must meet the following conditions:",points:["Site classification: distinguishing between short-term shelters (such as schools and sports halls) and long-term camps (such as tented camps).","Registration and database: recording the names of all displaced persons, and separately registering families and individuals who are alone (particularly unaccompanied children) in order to protect their security.","Health and hygiene: providing at least 15 liters of water per person per day (for drinking, cooking and personal hygiene), and one toilet for every 20 people (at the onset of an emergency, one for every 50 people as the minimum)."]},{heading:"3. Care of vulnerable groups",body:"During disasters, certain groups need special protection:",points:["Older people and people with disabilities: providing suitable routes for wheelchairs and providing medicines for chronic illnesses.","Children: setting up dedicated, protected areas (child-friendly spaces) so that they are kept away from psychological harm and other dangers.","Pregnant women: providing dedicated medical teams in the shelter for unexpected births."]},{heading:"4. Running the shelter and protection",body:"Running a shelter day by day:",points:["Coordination: one shelter manager, a daily meeting with the health, water, food and security teams, and a link to the Civil Defense operations center.","Information: a notice board and regular announcements in the languages people speak, about services, rules and missing persons.","Protection: lit paths, separate toilets and washing areas for women and men that lock from the inside, and a safe way to report abuse.","Fire safety: no open flames or heaters in tents without supervision, clear lanes between rows of tents, and firefighting equipment placed according to Civil Defense instructions."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_24_shelter_org.mp4",title:"Camp organization",description:"How tents and services are laid out according to international standards"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_24_evac_map.png",title:"Evacuation map",description:"An example of a city evacuation plan during a flood"}],quiz:[{question:"According to the humanitarian standards (Sphere), how many liters of water per day does each person in a shelter need as a minimum?",options:[{text:"2 liters",correct:!1},{text:"5 liters",correct:!1},{text:"15 liters",correct:!0},{text:"50 liters",correct:!1}],explanation:"The Sphere standard states that at least 15 liters are needed for drinking, hygiene and cooking."},{question:"What is the main purpose of child-friendly spaces in camps?",options:[{text:"To keep children in one place so that their parents can work in the camp",correct:!1},{text:"To protect children from trauma and give them a safe play area",correct:!0},{text:"To distribute food and clothes to the children's families",correct:!1},{text:"For learning languages",correct:!1}],explanation:"These spaces are set up so that children are in a calm environment and are not exposed to violence or psychological problems after the disaster."},{question:"Why does shelter registration record people who are alone, particularly unaccompanied children, separately?",options:[{text:"To give them larger food rations",correct:!1},{text:"To move them to a different camp",correct:!1},{text:"To speed up the registration of families",correct:!1},{text:"To protect their security",correct:!0}],explanation:"Shelter registration records all displaced persons and registers families and individuals who are alone, particularly unaccompanied children, separately in order to protect their security."},{question:"A shelter houses many families. Which toilet arrangement meets protection standards?",options:[{text:"Separate lockable toilets for women and men, lit at night",correct:!0},{text:"Shared toilets in a dark corner far from the sleeping area",correct:!1},{text:"One large toilet block for everyone without doors to save money",correct:!1},{text:"Toilets open only in the daytime to reduce cleaning",correct:!1}],explanation:"Sphere calls for toilets separated by sex that lock from the inside and are lit, to protect women and children from abuse."},{question:"During an evacuation, which group must the transport plan cover specifically?",options:[{text:"People without vehicles, older people and people with disabilities",correct:!0},{text:"Families with the most belongings to carry to the shelter",correct:!1},{text:"People who live closest to the shelter or the main road",correct:!1},{text:"Shop owners who need to protect their goods first",correct:!1}],explanation:"People who cannot leave on their own depend on organized transport; if the plan leaves them out, they stay in danger."},{question:"A rumor spreads in the shelter that the area is safe to return to tomorrow. What is the right response?",options:[{text:"Give only information confirmed by the competent authority and correct the rumor",correct:!0},{text:"Let families return if they sign a form accepting the risk",correct:!1},{text:"Ignore the rumor because people will not believe it",correct:!1},{text:"Announce a return date to calm people and change it later if needed",correct:!1}],explanation:"An early return can expose people to remaining hazards (water, weakened buildings, electricity); only the competent authority sets the return date."}],steps:[{title:"Decide and warn",detail:"The evacuation order comes from the competent authority; announce it through official channels with clear routes, what to bring and where to go.",critical:!0},{title:"Move people in order",detail:"Evacuate the most at-risk areas first; arrange transport for people without vehicles, older people and people with disabilities; keep the emergency routes open with the traffic police (104).",critical:!0},{title:"Register on arrival",detail:"Register every person and family, record unaccompanied children and people on their own separately, and reunite families.",critical:!0},{title:"Meet the basic standards",detail:"Provide water, toilets, food, shelter and health care according to the Sphere minimum standards, and check them every day."},{title:"Protect and inform",detail:"Set up child-friendly spaces, protect women, older people and people with disabilities, and keep people informed about services and return."}],warnings:["Never separate children from their families during transport or registration: unaccompanied children face a high risk of abuse and trafficking.","Do not announce evacuation routes, shelters or return dates that the competent authority has not confirmed: false information causes crowding and danger.","Do not crowd more people into a shelter than its safe capacity: it spreads disease and blocks the exits."],tips:["Prepare a shelter kit before a disaster: registration forms, signs, lighting, first aid supplies and a contact list.","Use simple numbered cards for each family so that its members stay together during registration.","Involve community leaders, women and young people from among the displaced in running the shelter."],activity:{prompt:"You manage a school that has become a shelter after a flood. At night a 10-year-old boy arrives on his own with neighbors; he says his parents were taken to hospital. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Let him stay with the neighbors without registering anything, since they know him",correct:!1,feedback:"Without registration nobody is formally responsible for him, and his parents cannot find him."},{text:"Register him as an unaccompanied child, place him in safe care, inform the child protection officer and start tracing his parents",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: registration, safe care and family tracing protect the child and open the way to reunification."},{text:"Send him by taxi to the hospital to look for his parents",correct:!1,feedback:"Sending a child alone at night puts him in danger; tracing is the staff's job."},{text:"Put him in the adult men's hall until the morning",correct:!1,feedback:"An unaccompanied child needs a protected place and a responsible adult, not a general hall for adults."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Flood water is rising in a low-lying neighborhood. The competent authority orders an evacuation. Many families have no car and some older people refuse to leave. What is your plan?",options:[{text:"Organize buses at known pickup points, visit older and disabled residents door to door with a team, and record and report those who refuse",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: transport and direct visits reach the most vulnerable, and reporting those who refuse leaves the decision to the authority."},{text:"Announce that everyone must find their own transport",correct:!1,feedback:"People without cars or who cannot move on their own will be left in danger; the plan must cover them specifically."},{text:"Carry the older people out by force against their will",correct:!1,feedback:"Explaining the risk and persuading come first; forced removal is a decision for the authorities, not the team."}]},{prompt:"The shelter has taken in more people than planned. Water is below the minimum per person and the toilets are overflowing. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Report the gap to the coordination center at once, request water trucks and extra toilets, and give drinking water priority",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: lack of water and sanitation spreads disease fast; a quick report and priority for drinking water come first."},{text:"Ration water to drinking only and close the toilets until tomorrow",correct:!1,feedback:"Closing the toilets increases open contamination and spreads disease; the solution is to request support."},{text:"Stop accepting new arrivals and send them away with nowhere to go",correct:!1,feedback:"Turning people away with nowhere to go puts them in danger; the authority must open another shelter."}]}],realCase:"After heavy rain, a river flooded a low-lying neighborhood at night. The local Civil Defense team used loudspeakers and an agreed message, and buses waited at known pickup points. A sports hall became the shelter. Volunteers registered every family at the door and separately recorded the children who arrived without their parents; they were reunited with their families through the registration list. The lesson: clear warnings, planned transport and careful registration protect people and keep families together."},{id:25,code:"TECH-2500",icon:"Mountain",accent:"#475569",title:"Technical and field rescue",subtitle:"Technical rescue: rope, confined space and structural collapse",summary:"Rope rescue, rescue from confined spaces and heights, and the engineering of stabilizing collapsed structures.",blocks:[{body:"Technical rescue is an advanced level of civil defense work in which specialized teams rescue casualties using engineering equipment and mechanical systems. This work requires detailed planning and knowledge of physics so that it does not cause further harm to the casualty or to the team."},{heading:"1. Rope rescue",body:"The use of rope systems in accordance with the NFPA 2500 standard (which has replaced the former NFPA 1670, NFPA 1858 and NFPA 1983):",points:["Two systems: use of a main line and a belay line; there must always be two systems so that if one fails, the other protects the casualty and the rescuer.","Mechanical advantage: using 3:1 or 5:1 systems with pulleys to raise a casualty or heavy loads with the least possible muscular effort.","Essential knots: using knots such as the figure 8 on a bight and the double fisherman's, which do not slip under heavy load."]},{heading:"2. Confined space rescue",body:"Spaces such as fuel tanks, sewer pipes and underground pits are silent hazards (OSHA 1910.146):",points:["Atmospheric testing: before entry, the oxygen level (at least 19.5%), flammable gases (below 10% of the LEL) and toxic gases such as H₂S and CO must be tested.","Team roles: the team is divided into three roles: the entrant (who goes inside), the attendant (who monitors from outside and never goes in) and the supervisor (the commander).","Forced ventilation: using air blowers to remove toxic gases and bring in clean air before the team enters and throughout the operation."]},{heading:"3. Structural collapse rescue",body:"Stabilizing collapsed buildings so that teams can safely enter the voids:",points:["Shoring: using timber and steel props to support ceilings and walls that are at risk of collapse.","Search equipment: using acoustic and seismic listening devices to hear the tapping and voices of casualties, and radar devices to detect their breathing and movements beneath rubble.","K9 teams: using trained dogs to detect the scent of living people in large rubble piles."]},{heading:"4. Rescue team safety",body:"Technical rescue starts with sizing up the scene, not with going in. The incident commander is responsible for the safety of the whole team:",points:["Work zone: set a cordon and let in only people with a task and the right protective equipment.","Energy isolation: electricity, gas, water and moving machinery are cut off and locked out (LOTO) before work starts.","Protective equipment: helmet, gloves, eye protection, a harness and SCBA when the air is not safe.","Rotation and rest: fatigue causes fatal mistakes, so teams work in rotation.","Accountability (PAR): at set times and after every major change, the commander confirms that every member is accounted for."]},{heading:"5. Handing over the casualty",points:["Hand the casualty to the medical team (122) at the triage point or another safe point, not inside the danger zone.","Tell them how long the casualty was trapped, where they were, whether something heavy was on them and what air they breathed.","Move the casualty only as much as you must, and decide on spinal protection together with the medical team."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_25_rope_systems.mp4",title:"Rope systems",description:"An analysis of the 3:1 system for raising casualties in mountainous terrain"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_25_confined_entry.mp4",title:"Confined space entry",description:"Safety procedures and the use of atmospheric monitoring equipment"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_25_shoring_types.png",title:"Types of shoring",description:"Demonstrating engineering methods for supporting walls at risk of collapse"}],quiz:[{question:"Why are two systems (main and belay) always required in rope rescue?",options:[{text:"So that the casualty can be raised twice as fast",correct:!1},{text:"If one rope or system fails, the other protects the casualty and team",correct:!0},{text:"So the weight is shared between two ropes and neither wears out quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Only for appearance",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to international standards, the belay (backup) system is a fundamental safety requirement when working at height."},{question:"What is the minimum oxygen concentration at which entry into a confined space without breathing apparatus is permitted?",options:[{text:"10%",correct:!1},{text:"16.5%",correct:!1},{text:"19.5%",correct:!0},{text:"21%",correct:!1}],explanation:"If oxygen is below 19.5%, the atmosphere is oxygen-deficient and the team must not enter without a self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA)."},{question:"In a confined space rescue, what is the attendant's role?",options:[{text:"Monitors from outside and never goes in",correct:!0},{text:"Goes in first to test the air for the team",correct:!1},{text:"Commands the operation and assigns the roles",correct:!1},{text:"Goes in to help the entrant if they get into trouble",correct:!1}],explanation:"The team has three roles: the entrant goes inside; the attendant monitors from outside and never goes in; the supervisor is the commander."},{question:"The air test at the opening of a confined space shows 18% oxygen. What does the team do?",options:[{text:"No entry without SCBA; ventilate and test again",correct:!0},{text:"Enter, because 18% is close to normal and the job is short",correct:!1},{text:"Enter with a dust mask so the air is filtered",correct:!1},{text:"Send only the attendant in for a quick look",correct:!1}],explanation:"In this module, oxygen below 19.5% means an oxygen-deficient atmosphere: no entry without SCBA. A dust mask adds no oxygen, and the attendant never goes in."},{question:"While raising a casualty on rope, the belay line tangles and cannot follow the raise. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop the raise and fix the belay before continuing",correct:!0},{text:"Finish the raise quickly on the main line alone",correct:!1},{text:"Untie the belay so it does not hold up the raise",correct:!1},{text:"Pull harder so the tangle frees itself",correct:!1}],explanation:"There must always be two systems: if the main line fails during the raise and the belay is not working, the casualty falls. Stopping to fix it is safer than speed."},{question:"In a collapsed building, a team finds a void with a cracked slab above it. What comes first?",options:[{text:"Shore the slab and keep a way out open",correct:!0},{text:"Go in fast before the slab cracks further",correct:!1},{text:"Lift rubble off the slab to lighten its load",correct:!1},{text:"Send the K9 dog into the void instead of the team",correct:!1}],explanation:"In this module, shoring supports ceilings and walls at risk of collapse so teams can enter voids safely. Lifting rubble off the slab shifts the load, and K9 teams detect scent; they are not sent into unstable spaces."}],steps:[{title:"Size up the scene first",detail:"Identify the hazards: the air in a confined space, an unstable structure, height, electricity, gas and water. Cordon off the area, call 115 and 122 and request a specialized team if needed. A rescuer who becomes a casualty rescues no one.",critical:!0},{title:"Control the hazards before entry",detail:"In a confined space, test the air (oxygen, flammable gases and toxic gases), keep forced ventilation running and repeat the test. Isolate and lock out energy sources (electricity, machinery and pipes) according to the site procedures.",critical:!0},{title:"Assign roles and rig the systems",detail:"Name the entrant, the attendant and the supervisor; the attendant never goes in. On rope, there is always a main line and a belay line, and a second person checks every system before it is loaded.",critical:!0},{title:"Stabilize before you enter voids",detail:"In a collapsed building, shore ceilings and walls at risk of collapse before anyone enters a void, and keep an exit route open. Do not pull rubble off the pile by hand without a plan, because the pile may shift.",critical:!0},{title:"Search for and reach the casualties",detail:"Use listening devices, radar and K9 teams, and stop all noisy work and machinery while you listen. Record where each casualty is and report it to the incident commander."},{title:"Hand over and account for the team",detail:"Hand the casualty over to the ambulance team (122) at a safe point with the facts: where they were, for how long and what happened. Then run a PAR to confirm that every team member is out safely."}],warnings:["Never enter a confined space to rescue a collapsed person without an air test, a self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA) and an attendant outside: many confined-space deaths are people who went in to help.","Never load a rope system without a belay line, and never use anchors that have not been checked: if a single point fails, the casualty and the rescuer fall.","Do not enter a void in a collapsed building before it is stabilized and there is a way out: an aftershock or shifting rubble can trap the team."],tips:["Practise knots and rigging with gloves on and in the dark, until you can do them quickly and correctly.","Before every entry, agree on an emergency signal and an evacuation signal with the whole team.","Keep a written log: who is inside, when they went in and the air-test readings throughout the job.","Inspect ropes and hardware before and after every use, and take damaged items out of service as the manufacturer instructs."],activity:{prompt:"You supervise a municipal crew. A worker has collapsed inside a sewer pit and does not respond. His colleague wants to climb straight down the ladder. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop the colleague, keep him at the opening as attendant, call 115 and 122 and let no one go down before the air is tested and ventilated",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the air may lack oxygen or be toxic; only a team with SCBA and a retrieval line can go down safely."},{text:"I let him go down quickly holding his breath, to pull his friend out in a few seconds",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: holding your breath does not protect you; toxic gases can drop a person within a few breaths, and you will have two casualties."},{text:"I turn a fan toward the pit and send the colleague down with a wet cloth mask to help",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a cloth mask does not protect against gas or low oxygen, and ventilation without a test does not show that the air is safe."},{text:"I move everyone away and wait for the gas to clear by itself, without calling anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: moving people away is right, but without calling 115 and 122 the worker is left without rescue and time is lost."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"After an earthquake, a two-story building has collapsed. Your team is first on scene. A weak voice is heard under the rubble, and neighbors have started pulling rubble off the pile by hand. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I calmly get everyone off the pile, cordon the area, cut electricity and gas if it is safe and pinpoint where the voice is",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: people moving on the pile can bring rubble down on the casualty; make the scene safe first, then work to a plan."},{text:"I join the neighbors in pulling rubble, because every minute counts and there are many hands",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: unplanned digging can shift the pile, close the void that is keeping the casualty alive and injure rescuers too."},{text:"I go straight into a gap in the rubble alone to reach the voice and see how many people are there",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: entering an unstable void alone, without shoring or a way out, turns the rescuer into a second casualty."},{text:"I wait for the K9 teams and the listening devices and do nothing on scene until they arrive",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the voice can be heard now and its location is known; secure the scene and pinpoint the voice instead of only waiting."}]},{prompt:"The scene is cordoned and the voice is coming from a void under a broken slab. The slab is cracked and resting on a leaning column. The shoring team will arrive shortly. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I talk to the casualty to reassure them, prepare the shoring materials and send the team in only after the slab is supported",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: talking calms the casualty and confirms their position; a cracked slab without support can fall on the casualty and the team."},{text:"I straighten the leaning column with a hydraulic jack so the slab lifts and the casualty can get out fast",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: lifting or straightening the column changes the load on the slab and can bring it down completely; shoring holds the load, it does not move it."},{text:"I break a hole through the slab with a hammer to reach the casualty from above without going under it",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: breaking a cracked slab without support can drop pieces onto the casualty; any breaching needs stabilization and a plan."},{text:"I send the team in quickly before the shoring team arrives, with one person outside watching the slab",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: watching the slab does not hold it up; if it falls, the person outside can do nothing and the team becomes casualties."}]}],realCase:"A worker went into an underground water tank to clean it and collapsed. His colleague rushed down to help and collapsed too. A third worker saw that neither of them was moving. He stopped the others, let no one else go down, called 115 and 122 and stayed at the tank opening to brief the team. The rescue team tested the air, started ventilation and brought both workers out using SCBA and a retrieval line. The main lesson: the urge to help without preparation is the greatest danger in a confined space."},{id:26,code:"COMM-2550",icon:"Radio",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Emergency communications and dispatch systems",subtitle:"Emergency communications and radio dispatch systems",summary:"Radio communication systems, the call-taking and dispatch protocol, interagency interoperability and the use of plain language.",blocks:[{body:"Effective communication is the backbone of all disaster management. In this module we discuss digital and analog radio systems, the dispatch protocol and how coordination is achieved between different agencies (police, firefighting, ambulance). Understanding these systems is essential for every civil defense officer."},{heading:"1. Radio systems",body:"Types of wireless communication systems:",points:["VHF/UHF: common frequencies for local communication; in general, VHF is better for open areas and UHF is better inside buildings.","Trunked radio: a system (today usually digital) that automatically shares multiple frequencies among different groups, such as the P25 systems in the United States.","Satellite communications: for remote areas or when terrestrial networks are down.","LTE/FirstNet: a dedicated network for emergency teams in the United States that gives communications priority and high quality."]},{heading:"2. The call-taking and dispatch protocol",body:"Standard procedures for receiving emergency calls and dispatching teams:",points:["Call-taking: using a CAD (computer-aided dispatch) system to record information quickly.","Priority classification: determining the level of risk (Priority 1, 2, 3) according to the type of incident and the content of the call.","EMD protocol (emergency medical dispatch): giving instructions to the caller by telephone (for example, CPR instructions) until the ambulance arrives.","Response time: under NFPA 1225 (formerly NFPA 1221), 90% of emergency calls must be answered within 15 seconds and 95% within 20 seconds; team arrival times are measured against the Directorate's internal targets."]},{heading:"3. Plain language and standard radio terminology",body:"Under NIMS/ICS, radio communications are conducted in simple, clear language, not in codes, so that all agencies understand one another:",points:['Ten-codes: codes such as 10-4 and 10-20 are a legacy system, and their meaning differs from one agency to another; they are therefore not used at joint incidents. Instead of "10-4" say "Understood," and instead of "10-20" state your location by name and landmark.',"Phonetic alphabet: using Alfa, Bravo, Charlie... to clarify letters over the radio.","Mayday: the international distress call (spoken three times) used when a firefighter is in life-threatening danger; everyone must clear the channel for them.","PAR (personnel accountability report): a check that all team members at the scene are accounted for."]},{heading:"4. Interagency interoperability",body:"How communication is achieved between different agencies:",points:["Shared channels: designating specific frequencies for communication between the police, firefighting and ambulance services.","Gateway devices: devices that link different systems together (such as VHF with UHF).","Communications in ICS: the Communications Unit Leader is responsible for preparing the communications plan.","NIMS typing: classifying communications resources according to national standards (Type I, II, III)."]},{heading:"5. Command centers",body:"Establishing central locations for controlling communications:",points:["EOC (emergency operations center): the main center for disaster management at the city or governorate level.","Mobile command: a special vehicle that serves as a field command center.","Status boards: display boards showing the location of teams and the status of the incident.","Redundancy: having backup systems in case the main system fails."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_26_radio_ops.mp4",title:"Working with the radio",description:"The principles of radio communication and the phonetic alphabet"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_26_dispatch_center.png",title:"The emergency call center",description:"Organization of the CAD room and its equipment"}],quiz:[{question:'What does the word "Mayday" mean on the radio?',options:[{text:"An order for all crews to start the operation",correct:!1},{text:"A firefighter's call for help when their life is in danger",correct:!0},{text:"A message that the operation has ended and crews can leave",correct:!1},{text:"A radio check",correct:!1}],explanation:"Mayday is the international distress call used when a firefighter's life is in serious danger and immediate assistance is needed."},{question:"What is the main difference between VHF and UHF?",options:[{text:"VHF is better inside buildings and UHF is better for open areas",correct:!1},{text:"UHF carries farther than VHF over open areas",correct:!1},{text:"VHF is better for open areas and UHF is better inside buildings",correct:!0},{text:"There is no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"VHF waves are longer and carry better over open areas, whereas UHF penetrates buildings more easily."},{question:"What does the EMD (emergency medical dispatch) protocol do?",options:[{text:"Sends the nearest ambulance straight away without asking the caller any questions",correct:!1},{text:"Gives the caller phone instructions, such as CPR, until the ambulance arrives",correct:!0},{text:"Records every call so that it can be reviewed after the incident",correct:!1},{text:"Transfers every medical call to the police on 104 for checking",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under the EMD protocol the dispatcher gives the caller instructions by telephone (for example, CPR instructions) until the ambulance arrives."},{question:"A caller says, “There is a fire, come quickly!” and then goes quiet. Which piece of information do you need first?",options:[{text:"The exact location: area, street, landmark and floor",correct:!0},{text:"The caller’s full name and the owner’s name",correct:!1},{text:"What caused the fire and when it started",correct:!1},{text:"The color of the smoke and which window it comes from",correct:!1}],explanation:"Without a location no team can arrive, and if the call drops, the location is the one fact that is enough to dispatch. Cause and smoke color matter, but they come after the location."},{question:"At a joint incident, a police officer asks on the shared channel, “10-20?” What is the best reply?",options:[{text:"State your location in plain language with a landmark",correct:!0},{text:"Answer “10-4” to show that you heard the message",correct:!1},{text:"Reply with another internal code from your own agency",correct:!1},{text:"Do not reply, because ten-codes are not allowed on the shared channel",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under NIMS/ICS, joint incidents use plain language. Replying in code adds confusion and silence breaks communication; the best reply gives your location by name and landmark."},{question:"When may a call-taker give CPR instructions by phone?",options:[{text:"Under the approved EMD protocol, after training in it",correct:!0},{text:"Whenever the call-taker feels it would help",correct:!1},{text:"Only if the caller says they are a doctor or nurse",correct:!1},{text:"Only after the ambulance has reached the scene",correct:!1}],explanation:"The EMD protocol sets what the call-taker says and when; instructions outside the protocol can cause harm. Once the ambulance arrives, the team takes over."}],steps:[{title:"Answer and get the location first",detail:"Answer calmly and ask for the location first: area, street, a landmark and the floor, then the caller’s phone number. If the call drops, these details let you send help or call back.",critical:!0},{title:"Find out what happened and the dangers",detail:"Ask what happened, how many people are hurt or trapped and whether there is fire, smoke, gas, electricity, chemicals or water. These facts decide which team is needed and with what protection.",critical:!0},{title:"Set the priority and dispatch",detail:"Set the priority using your center’s approved protocol and dispatch quickly; once the location and type of incident are known, dispatch should not wait for every question. The local protocol is set by Civil Defense."},{title:"Give the caller safety instructions",detail:"Give only the instructions that are in your approved protocol and that you are trained in: for example, get out of a burning building and call from outside, move away from a gas smell, or CPR instructions under the EMD protocol.",critical:!0},{title:"Update the teams and other agencies",detail:"Pass every new fact (a trapped person, gas, a more exact location) at once and in plain language to the teams on the way, and inform 122 and 104 according to the plan. Record everything in CAD or the written log."}],warnings:["Do not let a long list of questions delay help: once the location and type of incident are known, dispatch according to your center’s protocol and continue the questions afterward.","Never give medical or rescue instructions outside the approved protocol, and never tell a caller to go into fire, smoke or a gas area to rescue someone.","Do not use ten-codes or local abbreviations on shared channels: another agency may understand them differently."],tips:["Read the location and phone number back to the caller to confirm them; it cuts errors in names and numbers.","Keep an up-to-date list of landmarks (mosque, school, bridge, market) at hand, because many callers do not know the street name.","Keep your voice calm and slow and ask short questions: a frightened caller answers better to a calm voice.","Keep backup paper forms and a printed map ready for when CAD or the network is down."],activity:{prompt:"You work in a call-taking center. A frightened woman says, “Smoke is coming out of my neighbor’s house, come quickly!” and talks fast without giving a location. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I calmly ask her for the area, street, a nearby landmark and her phone number, then whether anyone is inside",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: without a location no team can get there, and a calm voice helps her answer better."},{text:"I send the nearest team at once to the area the number seems to come from, then ask",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a phone number does not give an exact location; the team may go to the wrong area and precious time is lost."},{text:"I tell her to go into the neighbor’s house to see whether there is fire or only smoke, then call back",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: going into a smoke-filled building can make her a casualty; the caller stays outside and gives information."},{text:"I transfer the call straight to the police on 104, because she is not calm and it may be a false call",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: smoke from a house is a 115 call; a frightened caller is not a sign of a false call, and transferring wastes time."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Within a minute, three calls come in about a road crash on a highway outside the city; a car is on fire. Each caller gives a slightly different location. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I link the calls as one incident, confirm the location with a landmark, dispatch 115 and inform 122 and 104 under the plan",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: three calls are usually one incident; linking them avoids sending three teams to three places, and every agency needed is informed."},{text:"I send a separate team for each call so that one of them reaches the right place",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this ties up teams for nothing and may leave another incident uncovered; confirming the location with a landmark is better."},{text:"I wait for a fourth call so the exact location is fully clear before dispatching",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the highway and the burning car are known; the team can set off and the location can be refined on the way."},{text:"I send only a 122 ambulance, because road crashes usually involve injuries rather than fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the car is on fire; fire and rescue are 115’s job, and an ambulance alone cannot put out the fire or free a trapped person."}]},{prompt:"Teams are on the way. A new caller says a driver is trapped in the car, fuel is spilling on the road and people are moving closer. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I pass the update to the teams at once in plain language and tell the caller to keep people away from the car and fuel, with no smoking",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the teams need to know about the trapped driver and the fuel to prepare; keeping people back and flames away protects lives."},{text:"I tell the caller to get people to pull the driver out quickly before the fire grows",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: that is a rescue instruction next to fire and fuel; people may be burned and the driver further injured by pulling. That is 115’s job."},{text:"I record the update in CAD and wait for the teams to arrive and see for themselves",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: recording is good but not enough; the teams must know before they arrive so they can prepare the right equipment and tactics."},{text:"I end the call to free the line for other calls, because the main facts were already known",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this caller has new, important information and can keep people back; ending the call loses the chance to guide him."}]}],realCase:"An elderly man called the center in fear and said his wife had collapsed and was not responding. The call-taker first took the area, the address, a nearby landmark and the phone number, and dispatched an ambulance at once. Then he asked whether she was breathing normally; when the man said no, the call-taker gave him CPR instructions under the approved EMD protocol until the team arrived, and did not end the call. The main lesson: location comes first, and phone instructions are given only under an approved protocol."},{id:27,code:"CBRN-2600",icon:"Radiation",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Defense against lethal agents (CBRN)",subtitle:"Chemical, biological, radiological and nuclear defense",summary:"Protection strategies against chemical, biological, radiological and nuclear threats, PPE levels and decontamination procedures.",blocks:[{body:"The term CBRN refers to four types of lethal hazards: chemical, biological, radiological and nuclear. This module has been prepared for officers and engineers so that they know how to respond to non-routine situations. Here we discuss the physics of radiation, the chemistry of nerve agents and mass decontamination protocols in accordance with international standards."},{heading:"1. Chemical threats (chemical agents)",body:"Chemical agents are classified according to their effects on the body:",points:["Nerve agents: such as sarin and VX; they disable the nervous system. Their signs are recognized by the mnemonic SLUDGEM (pinpoint pupils, tearing, urination, diarrhea and muscle twitching).","Choking agents: such as chlorine and phosgene; they cause severe damage to the lungs.","Blister agents: such as mustard gas; they cause severe chemical burns to the skin, eyes and lungs.","Blood agents: such as cyanide; they prevent cells from using oxygen."]},{heading:"2. Biological and microbial threats",body:"The use of bacteria, viruses and biological toxins as weapons:",points:["Bacteria: such as anthrax and plague; anthrax spores can persist in the environment for a very long time.","Viruses: such as Ebola and the smallpox virus, which can spread from person to person.","Biological toxins: such as ricin, which is produced from the seeds of the castor plant and is an extremely lethal poison.","Measures: using HEPA filters and negative-pressure rooms to isolate infected people."]},{heading:"3. Radiation physics and nuclear protection",body:"Understanding the three principles of protection (time, distance and shielding):",points:["Types of radiation: alpha is stopped by a sheet of paper, beta requires plastic or aluminum, but gamma is attenuated only by dense, thick materials such as lead, concrete or earth.","Dirty bomb: a conventional explosive containing radioactive material in order to contaminate an area; it is not a nuclear explosion.","The TDS principle: Time (minimize the time spent near the source), Distance (increase the distance from it) and Shielding (place a barrier between yourself and the source)."]},{heading:"4. Levels of protective clothing (PPE levels)",body:"Civil Defense personnel must know which suit to use and when:",points:["Level A: the highest protection; a fully encapsulating, gas-tight suit with a self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA) worn inside the suit.","Level B: the highest respiratory protection (SCBA) with a liquid-splash-resistant suit, but the suit is not gas-tight.","Level C: an air-purifying respirator and a chemical suit; only when the substance and its concentration are known and oxygen is sufficient.","Level D: an ordinary work uniform with gloves; no chemical respiratory hazard."]},{heading:"5. The decontamination protocol",body:"The process of removing contaminants from the body and from equipment:",points:["Stages: removal of clothing (which, according to international guidance, removes approximately 80–90% of the contamination), washing with water and soap, and checking (with a Geiger counter for radioactive contamination).","Zones: the hot zone, which is contaminated; the warm zone, where decontamination takes place; and the cold zone, where the command post and medical teams are located.","Wind direction: the clean zone and the command post must always be upwind and on higher ground."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_27_cbrn_ppe.mp4",title:"Levels of protection",description:"How to don a Level A suit correctly"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_27_radiation_chart.png",title:"Types of radiation",description:"An analysis of the penetrating power of gamma and beta radiation"}],quiz:[{question:"Which PPE level provides full protection against gases and chemical contact?",options:[{text:"Level D",correct:!1},{text:"Level C",correct:!1},{text:"Level B",correct:!1},{text:"Level A",correct:!0}],explanation:"Level A is a fully encapsulating suit that protects the wearer from lethal chemical gases and liquids."},{question:"According to the TDS principle, how can you reduce the radiation hazard to your body?",options:[{text:"By spending less time near the source and keeping farther from it",correct:!0},{text:"By drinking plenty of water to wash the radiation out of the body",correct:!1},{text:"By running in any direction",correct:!1},{text:"By wearing a simple paper mask near the source",correct:!1}],explanation:"Time, distance and shielding are the three scientific principles for protecting people from radiation."},{question:"Where must the clean zone and the command post always be placed?",options:[{text:"Downwind, so that vapors blow away from the hazard",correct:!1},{text:"In the warm zone, next to the decontamination line",correct:!1},{text:"Upwind and on higher ground",correct:!0},{text:"Downhill and close to the hot zone for quick access",correct:!1}],explanation:"The module's rule: the clean zone and the command post must always be upwind and on higher ground; the hot zone is the contaminated area and decontamination takes place in the warm zone."},{question:"You reach an open-air market where several people have collapsed with no obvious cause. Where do you stop?",options:[{text:"Far back, upwind and on higher ground",correct:!0},{text:"Next to the casualties, to help them quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Far back, but downwind and in a low spot",correct:!1},{text:"In the basement of a building near the market",correct:!1}],explanation:"In this module, the clean zone is always upwind and on higher ground. Many substances travel with the wind or collect in low places and basements, and approaching casualties without protection makes you one too."},{question:"A chemical cloud is outside and you are inside a building. Which action is right?",options:[{text:"Stay inside, switch off ventilation and follow instructions",correct:!0},{text:"Drive out of the area fast before the cloud arrives",correct:!1},{text:"Open the windows so air circulates through the building",correct:!1},{text:"Go up to the roof to see the cloud better",correct:!1}],explanation:"Staying inside with doors and windows shut and ventilation off is the fastest way to cut exposure; driving out may take you into the cloud. Evacuate only when officials say so."},{question:"A person exposed to a liquid or dust reaches the warm zone. What is the first decontamination step?",options:[{text:"Remove outer clothing carefully and bag it",correct:!0},{text:"Drink plenty of water to flush the substance out",correct:!1},{text:"Wipe the skin with a dry cloth and put the clothes back on",correct:!1},{text:"Go quickly to the cold zone and change clothes there",correct:!1}],explanation:"In this module, removing clothing takes off most of the contamination, then comes washing with soap and water. Going to the cold zone in contaminated clothes spreads it."}],steps:[{title:"Suspect it and do not rush in",detail:"When several people collapse together in one place or show the same signs (burning eyes, coughing, dizziness) with no obvious cause, or there is an unusual smell, suspect a hazardous substance. Stop: if you rush in, you become a casualty too.",critical:!0},{title:"Approach upwind and uphill",detail:"Check the wind direction and approach from upwind and higher ground, keeping a wide distance. Cordon off the area, let no one in and direct people toward fresh air.",critical:!0},{title:"Report and request the specialist team",detail:"From a safe place, call 115 and 122: the location, what you saw, how many people are affected, the wind direction and a safe approach route. Request the hazardous materials (HazMat/CBRN) team according to the local plan.",critical:!0},{title:"Protect people: shelter or move away",detail:"If the substance is released outdoors, people go inside a building, close doors and windows, switch off ventilation and fans and follow official instructions. If it is inside a building, get people out by a route away from it into fresh air.",critical:!0},{title:"Decontaminate affected people",detail:"Gather affected people in a separate place in the warm zone; they take off their outer clothing carefully and bag it, then wash with soap and water, and then go to the medical team. Do not touch them with bare hands."},{title:"Only trained teams enter the hot zone",detail:"Only teams with the correct PPE level and training enter the hot zone, on the incident commander’s order. Everyone else works in the cold zone: the cordon, recording people’s names and supporting the medical team."}],warnings:["Never enter the hot zone or touch casualties without the correct protection level and training: a contaminated rescuer becomes another casualty and spreads the contamination.","Do not carry contaminated clothing, equipment or people who have not been decontaminated into the cold zone, vehicles or a hospital without the specialist team’s direction.","Do not touch, move or open a suspicious package, bag or device: move away, keep people away and call 115 and 104."],tips:["Read the wind quickly from flags, smoke or nearby trees; it tells you where to stand.","Mark the decontamination point and water sources in the building or site plan before an incident.","Practise putting on and taking off protective equipment in pairs regularly; much contamination happens while taking it off.","Record the names and phone numbers of everyone who was at the scene so health teams can follow up later."],activity:{prompt:"You are the safety officer of a university lecture building. After an unusual smell, several students in one corridor complain of burning eyes and coughing. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I get people out by a route away from the smell into fresh air, close the corridor, call 115 and 122 from a safe place and keep the affected students together",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: distance from the source and fresh air are the first protection; keeping affected people together helps the medical and decontamination teams."},{text:"I send a staff member into the corridor to find the source before calling anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: an unprotected staff member becomes another casualty, and delaying the call delays the specialist team."},{text:"I open all the windows and let lectures continue, because the air will clear the smell",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: people stay near an unknown substance and may be exposed further; evacuation and reporting are needed."},{text:"I tell the affected students to go home quickly and wash their eyes there",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: they leave without medical assessment and may spread contamination; medical and decontamination teams cannot reach them."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A chemical tanker has overturned on a road near campus and a colored cloud is drifting toward the university. The incident commander has not arrived yet. As the university safety officer, what do you decide?",options:[{text:"I get everyone inside, close doors and windows, switch off ventilation and wait for official instructions",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: with a cloud approaching and no time for a safe evacuation, sheltering inside and shutting off the air is fast protection; evacuation only on an official order."},{text:"I walk all students off campus quickly through the nearest gate toward the road",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the nearest gate faces the crash road; people would walk through the cloud and be outside without protection."},{text:"I turn the ventilation fans to full power to push the cloud away from the buildings",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: ventilation fans pull outside air in; they must be switched off when sheltering inside."},{text:"I wait for the incident commander and make no decision until he arrives and sees the scene",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the cloud is coming now; the site’s safety officer must take quick protective action and then hand over to the commander."}]},{prompt:"Everyone is inside. A group of students who were outside reach the building door; their eyes are burning and their clothes smell of the substance. Hundreds of people are inside. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I keep them in a separate area by the door; they remove outer clothing into bags and wash with soap and water, and I inform 122 and 115",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this follows the module’s protocol: removing clothing takes off most contamination, then washing with soap and water; keeping them separate protects everyone else."},{text:"I keep the door shut on them and let no one in, so the building stays fully clean",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: leaving them outside in the cloud increases their exposure; they need protection and decontamination in a separate area."},{text:"I let them into the main hall with everyone so they get away from the outside air fast",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: contaminated clothing carries the substance into the hall and exposes hundreds of others."},{text:"I drive them quickly to hospital in university cars without changing their clothes",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: they carry contamination into the car and hospital and expose the driver; decontaminate first, then transport as 122 directs."}]}],realCase:"In an underground storeroom at a facility, two workers became dizzy after a sharp smell. The supervisor stopped the others and let no one go down. He moved everyone out of the building to higher ground upwind, cordoned off the area and called 115 and 122 from outside. He gave the specialist team the list of materials in the store. The team went in with suitable protective equipment, and after decontamination the two workers were handed to the medical team. The main lesson: distance, fresh air and accurate information save lives."},{id:28,code:"INVEST-2700",icon:"Search",accent:"#d97706",title:"Fire cause investigation",subtitle:"Scientific fire and arson investigation",summary:"The science of determining the origin of a fire, the analysis of burn patterns and the NFPA 921 methodology.",blocks:[{body:"Fire cause investigation is a science that combines police work and engineering. The aim is to establish the facts of the incident: was the fire accidental, or was it set deliberately (arson)? This module is based on the NFPA 921 guide, which sets out the scientific method for collecting and analyzing evidence."},{heading:"1. Origin determination",body:"Finding the point at which the fire started:",points:["V-pattern: a mark in the shape of the letter V forms on walls and shows the direction of the fire from bottom to top. The base of this pattern is often close to the origin of the fire.","Lowest point of burning: because heat rises, the lowest location showing fire damage is often close to the point of origin (although after flashover these patterns can be altered).","Calcination: chemical changes in gypsum (drywall) walls that indicate how intensely and for how long the fire burned at that location."]},{heading:"2. Accelerants",body:"Identifying deliberate fires through fuel residues:",points:["Hydrocarbons: detecting residues of gasoline, diesel or kerosene in the debris.","Detection dogs: investigation teams use trained dogs (K9) that can detect the scent of accelerants within a short time; however, under NFPA 921, every canine alert must be confirmed by laboratory analysis.","Laboratory analysis: using gas chromatography–mass spectrometry (GC-MS) to confirm the presence of chemicals in samples of debris and soil."]},{heading:"3. Arson indicators",body:"Certain signs raise suspicion of a crime:",points:["Multiple origins: if the fire started in several places at the same time, with no connection between them.","Disabled systems: if the sprinkler or fire alarm system had been shut off in advance.","Trailers: the presence of a line of fuel or fabric on the floor to carry the fire from one room to another."]},{heading:"4. Evidence preservation (chain of custody)",body:"Legal procedures to ensure that evidence is accepted in court:",points:["Chain of custody: recording every person who has handled an item of evidence, from the time it is found until it reaches the laboratory and the court.","Documentation: taking close-up (macro) photographs of fine details and drawing a sketch of the scene.","Spoliation: guarding against the destruction of evidence during fire suppression; firefighters must be aware of burn patterns and must not disturb the scene after extinguishment."]},{heading:"5. Safety at the investigation scene",body:"A burned site is still dangerous after the fire is out. The investigation starts only after the incident commander declares the scene safe:",points:["Structure: burned ceilings, stairs and floors can collapse; an engineering check is needed when in doubt.","Energy: electricity and gas are shut off according to procedures before work inside starts.","Air and ash: smoke and ash contain toxic substances; use respiratory protection, gloves and protective clothing and wash your hands and face after work.","Hot spots: fire can flare up again under debris; a fire crew should stay nearby.","Never work alone: someone else must know where you are."]},{heading:"6. The first crew’s role in preserving evidence",points:["Record what you saw on arrival (smoke, flames, doors, windows, alarms and people present).","During overhaul, move only what you must.","Cordon off the scene and log the name and time of everyone who enters.","Give all your observations to the investigator; decisions on cause or responsibility belong to the competent authority."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_28_fire_patterns.mp4",title:"Burn patterns",description:"An analysis of the difference between a V-pattern and a clean burn"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_28_arson_dog.jpg",title:"K9 dogs",description:"The role of trained dogs in detecting accelerants"}],quiz:[{question:"What does a V-pattern on a building wall indicate?",options:[{text:"The direction in which people escaped from the room",correct:!1},{text:"The direction the fire spread upward from its origin",correct:!0},{text:"An electrical fault",correct:!1},{text:"How quickly the fire cooled down after it was put out",correct:!1}],explanation:"This pattern shows where the fire started and in which direction it traveled, helping investigators to find the point of origin."},{question:"Why is the chain of custody important in an investigation?",options:[{text:"To make the investigation report look more professional",correct:!1},{text:"So that evidence is accepted in court and cannot have been altered",correct:!0},{text:"So that the firefighters can finish their work at the scene faster",correct:!1},{text:"It has no importance",correct:!1}],explanation:"If the chain of custody is broken, defense lawyers can argue in court that the evidence was altered, and the case may be dismissed."},{question:"What must firefighters do to avoid spoliation of evidence?",options:[{text:"Remove all the debris quickly so that the investigators can see the floor clearly",correct:!1},{text:"Wash down the walls after the fire to cool them and remove the soot",correct:!1},{text:"Collect any suspicious items themselves and take them to the station",correct:!1},{text:"Watch for burn patterns and leave the scene undisturbed after the fire",correct:!0}],explanation:"Spoliation is the destruction of evidence during fire suppression; firefighters must be aware of burn patterns and must not disturb the scene after extinguishment."},{question:"During overhaul you find a burned electric heater where the V-pattern starts. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Leave it in place, photograph it and tell the investigator",correct:!0},{text:"Carry it outside so it cannot flare up again",correct:!1},{text:"Open it up to see whether the wires inside are broken",correct:!1},{text:"Wash it with water so the ash comes off and it is clearer",correct:!1}],explanation:"In this module, the base of a V-pattern is often near the origin, so this heater may be key evidence. Moving, opening or washing it destroys its position and condition (spoliation)."},{question:"Which action is most likely to destroy evidence?",options:[{text:"Washing walls and shoveling out debris before the investigator arrives",correct:!0},{text:"Taking wide and close photos before moving anything",correct:!1},{text:"Cordoning the scene and logging everyone who enters",correct:!1},{text:"Writing down arrival notes on smoke and doors",correct:!1}],explanation:"The other three actions preserve evidence. Washing and shoveling out debris wipe out burn patterns and residues; this is the spoliation the module warns about."},{question:"Part of a burned building has collapsed and the investigator wants to go in at once. Which decision is right?",options:[{text:"No entry until the structure is checked and energy is cut",correct:!0},{text:"Go in quickly before rain wipes out the evidence",correct:!1},{text:"Go in alone so that no one else is put at risk",correct:!1},{text:"Clear the collapsed debris with machinery to open a quick route",correct:!1}],explanation:"Safety comes before investigation: a burned building can collapse further, and working alone or clearing debris with machinery both endanger lives and destroy evidence."}],steps:[{title:"Life and safety before evidence",detail:"Rescue and extinguishment always come first. After the fire is out and before anyone goes in, check the structure, electricity, gas, hidden hot spots and smoke, and use protective equipment and respiratory protection.",critical:!0},{title:"Note what you saw on arrival",detail:"Write down quickly the color and location of smoke and flames, whether doors and windows were open or forced, whether alarms and sprinklers were working and who was at the scene. These observations are easily forgotten later."},{title:"Disturb as little as possible and secure the scene",detail:"Move only the debris needed to put out hot spots and for safety; do not wash walls or clean up. Cordon off the scene, keep a single way in and out and log the name of everyone who enters.",critical:!0},{title:"Photograph before anything moves",detail:"If something must be moved for safety, first photograph it in place and mark its position on the scene sketch. Do not collect or remove suspicious items: that is the authorized investigator’s job."},{title:"Hand the scene over to the investigator",detail:"Hand your notes, photos and the entry log to the fire investigator or the competent authority, following Civil Defense procedures. Do not state the cause of the fire or who is to blame to the public or the media yourself."}],warnings:["Never enter a burned building to look for evidence before it has been declared safe: ceilings can fall, hot spots can reignite and ash and smoke are toxic.","Do not collect, open or remove suspicious items (fuel containers, unknown devices) yourself; if something looks like an explosive, move away, keep people back and call 115 and 104.","Do not wash down walls and floors or shovel debris out after the fire is out unless safety requires it: this destroys burn patterns and residues."],tips:["Carry a small notebook or work phone and record what you see with the time and place.","Photograph from wide to close: the whole scene first, then the room, then the fine details.","Investigators use a new, clean container and new gloves for each sample so samples are not cross-contaminated.","If there are security cameras or witnesses, note their names and locations for the investigator."],activity:{prompt:"After a fire in a university storeroom is out, the dean asks your crew to clear the debris quickly so classes can start tomorrow. You noticed two separate burn areas with no link between them. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I calmly explain that the scene stays as it is, cordon it off and report the two burn areas to the commander and the investigator",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: multiple origins are an important indicator in this module; cleaning up before the investigation erases them."},{text:"We clear the debris fast but take one general photo of the room so nothing is lost",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: one general photo does not preserve where residues lie, the burn patterns under the debris or how they relate."},{text:"We wash walls and floor with water to cool them and remove the smoke smell, then the investigator can come",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: washing destroys marks such as the V-pattern and accelerant residues; this is spoliation of evidence."},{text:"I collect the suspicious burned items and take them to the station to keep them safe until the investigator comes",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: moving them loses their exact positions and breaks the chain of custody; items stay in place and the scene is guarded."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"At night your crew arrives at a fire in a shop. The back door is open and its lock is broken. Someone may be inside. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Search and extinguishment come first, but I report the broken lock to the commander and we touch the door no more than needed",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: life comes before evidence, but a quick note and minimal handling preserve an important clue."},{text:"I wait for the 104 police to see and photograph the broken lock before we go in",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: someone may be inside and the fire is growing; no evidence is more important than a life."},{text:"We take the door completely off its frame to widen the way in",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the door is already open; removing it is unnecessary and destroys the forced-entry marks on the lock and frame."},{text:"I first search the shop for fuel containers to find out whether the fire was set deliberately",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: that is investigation, not the first task; while the fire is still burning, searching for people and extinguishing come first."}]},{prompt:"The fire is out and no one was inside. Near where the V-pattern starts there is a melted plastic container that smells of gasoline. The shop owner wants to take it. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I let no one touch it, photograph it in place, guard the scene and inform the investigator and 104",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this item may be important evidence; it must stay in place until the authorized investigator takes it with proper procedures."},{text:"I let the owner take it, because it is his property and he can give it to the investigator later",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: once the item leaves the scene, no one can confirm what happened to it; this breaks the chain of custody."},{text:"I pick up the container with my gloves and keep it in the fire truck until the investigator arrives",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: moving it spoils its exact position, and work gloves can add or remove fuel residues."},{text:"I pour water on it so the gasoline smell goes and it does not reignite, then leave it in place",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the container is already out; water can wash away the fuel residues the laboratory needs."}]}],realCase:"A fire started in a room of a house and the family got out safely. After extinguishment, a firefighter noticed that the V-pattern started near a wall outlet with a melted extension cord there. He let no one move the debris in that spot, took photos and told the commander. The crew put out only the hot spots that needed it. The investigator found the scene as it was, and the investigation pointed to an overloaded extension cord. The main lesson: minimal disturbance and quick notes make an investigation stronger."},{id:29,code:"COMM-2800",icon:"RadioTower",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Communication systems and radio protocol",subtitle:"Emergency communications and radio protocols",summary:"Radio voice procedures, emergency channels, plain language and keeping communications working during disasters.",blocks:[{body:"Communication is one of the most important elements during disasters. Without good communication there can be no coordination and teams cannot work together. This module shows how radios and communication systems are used in emergencies."},{heading:"1. Principles of radio communication",body:"The basic rules for speaking on the radio:",points:["Listen first, then speak: before transmitting, make sure the channel is clear.",'Response words: "Received," "Understood," "Affirmative."',"Phonetic alphabet: Alfa, Bravo, Charlie...","Brief and clear: keep your message short and clear.","Repetition: repeat important messages (locations, numbers, orders) or ask the receiver to read them back."]},{heading:"2. Plain language instead of codes",body:"Under NIMS/ICS, at incidents involving several agencies, communication is conducted in simple, clear language. Ten-codes are a legacy, local system, and their meaning differs from one agency to another. Examples:",points:['Instead of the old code "10-4," say: "Received" or "Understood."',`Instead of "10-33," say: "Emergency, immediate assistance required" (when a firefighter's life is in danger: Mayday).`,'Instead of "10-9," say: "Say again."','Instead of "10-20," state your location by name and landmark, for example: "I am on the third floor, north side."',"Over: my message is finished and I am waiting for a reply / Out: the conversation is finished."]},{heading:"3. Communication systems",body:"Types of communication systems:",points:["VHF/UHF: standard handheld and vehicle radios.","Satellite: for when terrestrial networks are not working.","Push-to-talk (PTT): talking by pressing a button, via a radio or mobile applications.","Repeater: a device that amplifies and retransmits the signal over a longer range.","Mesh network: a self-organizing network in which each device relays messages to other devices."]},{heading:"4. Coordination during disasters",body:"How teams are coordinated:",points:["Coordination channel: a designated shared channel for all teams at the incident, in accordance with the communications plan.","Command channel: only unit leaders and the incident commander speak on this channel.","Scheduled reports: such as the PAR (personnel accountability report), according to the incident plan or at times of significant change.","Distress call: Mayday is spoken three times (Mayday, Mayday, Mayday), and everyone clears the channel.","GPS: tracking the location of each team."]},{heading:"5. Backup methods when networks fail",body:"When normal communications are not working:",points:["Runners: people who deliver messages by hand or in writing.","Prearranged assembly point: all teams return to an agreed location at a set time.","GPS tracker: a location-tracking device for each team.","Light signals: with a lamp or flashlight, particularly at night.","Sound signals: designated whistle or bell signals whose meaning all teams know."]},{heading:"6. The structure of a radio message",body:"A message organized in the same order every time is understood faster and prevents mistakes:",points:["Call: the receiver’s name, then yours (“Command, this is Team Two”).","Message: who, where, what happened and what is needed, in plain language without codes.","Read-back: the receiver repeats orders, locations and key numbers so understanding is confirmed.","Close: “Over” when you expect a reply and “Out” when the conversation is finished.","Privacy: radio is not secure; do not give people’s names or personal details on an open channel."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_28_radio.mp4",title:"Using the radio",description:"How to speak on the radio"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_28_phonetic.png",title:"The phonetic alphabet",description:"The phonetic alphabet with examples"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_28_coordination.mp4",title:"Coordination",description:"Coordinating teams"}],quiz:[{question:'How is the old code "10-4" expressed in NIMS plain language?',options:[{text:"Emergency",correct:!1},{text:"Received / understood",correct:!0},{text:"My location",correct:!1},{text:"Say again / repeat that",correct:!1}],explanation:'10-4 is an old code meaning "received"; under NIMS/ICS, instead of the code, plain language is used: "Received" or "Understood," because codes do not have the same meaning in every agency.'},{question:'When is the word "Mayday" used?',options:[{text:"When a crew only needs more water, hoses or tools at the scene",correct:!1},{text:"In a serious emergency: a life is in danger and help is needed",correct:!0},{text:"At the end of an operation, to tell all crews to leave",correct:!1},{text:"During a rest break",correct:!1}],explanation:"Mayday is the word for a serious emergency: it means that someone's life (particularly a firefighter's) is in danger and immediate help is needed."},{question:"What should you do before you transmit on the radio?",options:[{text:"Listen first and make sure the channel is clear",correct:!0},{text:"Press the button and start speaking at once",correct:!1},{text:'Say "Over" to warn others that you are about to speak',correct:!1},{text:"Switch to the command channel, which is always free",correct:!1}],explanation:'The first rule of radio communication is listen first, then speak: before transmitting, make sure the channel is clear. "Over" is said at the end of a message, and only unit leaders and the incident commander speak on the command channel.'},{question:"You are about to report your water supply on the radio when you hear “Mayday, Mayday, Mayday.” What do you do?",options:[{text:"Go silent, keep the channel clear and listen for orders",correct:!0},{text:"Give my short report quickly, then go silent",correct:!1},{text:"Ask the Mayday caller where he is so I can go myself",correct:!1},{text:"Switch to the command channel to give my report there",correct:!1}],explanation:"During a Mayday everyone clears the channel; only the incident commander and the person in danger speak. Going on your own without orders disrupts the organized rescue, and the command channel is only for unit leaders and the commander."},{question:"Command says, “Team Two, go to the north stairwell, third floor.” What is the best reply?",options:[{text:"“Team Two: north stairwell, third floor, over”",correct:!0},{text:"“OK, got it,” and I head to the stairs at once",correct:!1},{text:"“10-4,” so the reply on the channel is short and fast",correct:!1},{text:"No reply, to keep the channel clear, and go straight there",correct:!1}],explanation:"Reading the order back shows you understood the location and floor correctly. “OK” or a code does not confirm understanding, and silence leaves the commander unsure."},{question:"Radios and phones are down in an area and teams must report. Under this module, which method is the planned backup?",options:[{text:"Runners and the assembly point at the agreed time",correct:!0},{text:"Sounding vehicle horns until someone answers",correct:!1},{text:"Each team decides for itself when to come back",correct:!1},{text:"Stopping work until the network is back",correct:!1}],explanation:"The module names runners and a prearranged assembly point at a set time as backups. Horns without an agreed meaning add confusion, each team deciding alone breaks the plan and stopping work delays rescues."}],steps:[{title:"Listen and prepare your message",detail:"Make sure the channel is clear and that you are on the right channel under the communications plan. Know who you are calling and what you will say before you key up."},{title:"Press the button, then speak",detail:"Press the PTT button and pause briefly before speaking so the first word is not cut off. Hold the microphone close to your mouth but not directly in front of it and speak in a normal, calm voice."},{title:"Call and identify yourself",detail:"Say the receiver’s name first, then your own: “Command, this is Team Two.” Wait for an answer before you give your message."},{title:"Give a short message in plain language",detail:"Speak without codes: who, where (by name and landmark), what happened and what is needed. Repeat locations, numbers and orders or ask the receiver to read them back, and end with “Over” or “Out.”"},{title:"Mayday: clear the channel or call it",detail:"If your life is in danger: “Mayday, Mayday, Mayday,” then who, where, what happened and what you need. If you hear a Mayday, go silent and clear the channel unless the commander tells you to speak.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never talk over someone else’s Mayday: any other message can block the information needed to rescue a firefighter.","Do not use ten-codes or local abbreviations on shared channels: their meaning is not the same in every agency and can cause a dangerous mistake.","Do not give casualties’ names or personal details on an open channel: radio is not secure and others can listen."],tips:["Do a radio check at the start of every shift and carry a charged spare battery.","Organize your message in your head or on paper before you press the button; short messages keep the channel free for others.","In noisy or windy places, shield the microphone from the wind with your hand and speak slower, not louder.","Agree on the backup assembly point, its time and the whistle signals with the whole team before work begins."],activity:{prompt:"You lead a team inside a smoke-filled building. Your partner says his SCBA air is running low, and you have lost the way out. What do you say on the radio?",options:[{text:"“Mayday, Mayday, Mayday,” then: Team Two, two people, third floor north side, lost the way out, low on air",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: life is in danger; Mayday clears the channel, and who, where and what is needed help the rescue team reach you fast."},{text:"“Can anyone hear me? I need help,” without giving my location",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: without Mayday the channel is not cleared, and without a location and head count the rescue team does not know where to search."},{text:"“10-33, 10-33,” using the emergency code so the message is short and no time is lost",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: codes do not mean the same in every agency; this module says to use Mayday and plain language instead of codes."},{text:"I quickly look for a way out with my partner without the radio and report once we are out",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: air is low and the way out is lost; delaying the Mayday wastes the rescue team’s time."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"During flood rescue work, the phone network and the radio repeater fail. Four teams are working in four neighborhoods. As commander, what do you do?",options:[{text:"I activate the plan’s backups: runners and the assembly point at the agreed time, and run a PAR there",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the module names runners and a prearranged assembly point as backups; the PAR confirms that every member is accounted for."},{text:"I stop all teams at once until the network returns, because work without communications is unsafe",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this halts rescues, and the network may not return for a long time; backup methods exist for exactly this."},{text:"I drive alone around all four neighborhoods to see for myself where the teams are",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the commander leaves the command post, no one knows where he is and he puts himself at risk alone in flood areas."},{text:"I send text messages to members’ personal phones in the hope that some get through",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the phone network is down; hope is not a plan. Manual backups at set times are needed."}]},{prompt:"At the agreed time, three teams reach the assembly point, but the fourth does not arrive. It was last seen near a bridge. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I treat the missing team as an emergency, send a rescue team to its last known location and a runner to the command center",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the PAR exists to detect a missing team quickly, and the last known location is the best starting point for a search."},{text:"I wait for the fourth team until the next assembly time, since it may be busy with another task",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: time matters in a flood; a team missing near a bridge may be in danger, and waiting can cost lives."},{text:"I send all three teams together into the flood to search for the fourth, leaving no one at the point",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this puts every team at risk and leaves the assembly point empty; if the fourth team returns, no one is there."},{text:"I blow the general recall whistle signal and then end the day’s work",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the whistle will not reach the team’s last position near the bridge, and ending work leaves the missing team unsearched."}]}],realCase:"At a large warehouse fire, two agencies were working on a shared channel. A team leader said “10-7,” meaning that his team was out of service. The other agency understood the code differently and sent another team to the same spot. The incident commander at once ordered that no codes be used on the shared channel and that every order be read back. After that, the confusion stopped. The main lesson: plain language and read-backs prevent dangerous mistakes."},{id:30,code:"LOG-2900",icon:"Package",accent:"#059669",title:"Logistics and resource management in disasters",subtitle:"Disaster logistics and supply chain management",summary:"Supplying food, medicine and materials to thousands of people, and managing warehouses and transportation.",blocks:[{body:"Disaster logistics is the science of delivering supplies and services to people in need, within a short time and in a fair manner. This module is important for leaders and managers who need to know how to manage resources."},{heading:"1. Logistics planning",body:"Advance preparation:",points:["Warehouses: designating storage locations for supplies before a disaster.","Supply list: food, water, medicine, tents and blankets.","Transportation: vehicles, aircraft and other means.","Teams: trained logistics personnel.","Relationships: contracts and relationships with suppliers."]},{heading:"2. Warehouse management",body:"How supplies are stored and distributed:",points:["Inventory: counting and recording all supplies.","Coding: a specific code for each type of supply.","Expiration: monitoring the expiration dates of food and medicines (whatever expires first is distributed first).","Protection: from heat, cold and humidity.","Security: preventing theft and damage."]},{heading:"3. Transportation and distribution",body:"Delivering supplies to where they are needed:",points:["Vehicles: within the city and to nearby areas.","Aircraft: to distant or isolated locations.","Helicopters: to mountainous locations and places where roads are blocked.","Distribution points: designated, clearly marked locations for distributing supplies.","Recording: who received what, and how much."]},{heading:"4. The supply chain",body:"Coordination with suppliers:",points:["Suppliers: supply companies and organizations.","Requests: how supplies are requested (requisition).","Delivery: specifying the time and place of delivery.","Finance: budget and payment.","Tracking: monitoring supplies from the supplier to the recipient."]},{heading:"5. Shelter management",body:"Shelter services (according to the Sphere standards):",points:["Tents: setting up and organizing the shelter.","Food: providing food for thousands of people.","Water: at least 15 liters of clean water per person per day.","Toilets and washing facilities: in sufficient numbers and in safe locations for women and children.","Health: medical services and first aid."]},{heading:"6. Logistics in the incident command system (ICS)",body:"At large incidents, the Logistics Section is one of the main sections of ICS. Its job is to make sure field teams receive everything they need to work:",points:["Supply: ordering, receiving, storing and tracking materials.","Facilities: warehouses, rest areas for teams and the command post.","Ground support: vehicles, fuel and equipment repair.","Communications, food and medical care for responders: radios, food and water and first aid for staff.","Every request goes through the chain of command, and every resource is logged at check-in so its location is known."]},{heading:"7. Needs assessment and fair distribution",points:["Rapid assessment: the number of people, their location and the needs of vulnerable groups (infants, older people, people with disabilities and the sick).","Distribution criteria are clear and public and are explained to everyone in a language they understand.","There is a complaints and feedback channel so people can report gaps.","Distribution points are safe and close by, with open exits and organized lines."]},{heading:"8. Warehouse safety",points:["Keep exit routes and fire extinguishers clear and visible.","Put heavy loads on low shelves and do not load shelves beyond their limit.","Keep food off the floor and protect it from rodents and insects.","Store fuel and gas cylinders outdoors or in a separate, ventilated place away from tents and food."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_29_logistics.mp4",title:"Disaster logistics",description:"The principles of logistics"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_29_warehouse.png",title:"Warehousing",description:"Warehouse management"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_29_distribution.mp4",title:"Distribution",description:"Distributing supplies"}],quiz:[{question:"What is disaster logistics?",options:[{text:"Extinguishing fires",correct:!1},{text:"Delivering supplies and services to people in need",correct:!0},{text:"Giving first aid to the injured at the scene of a disaster",correct:!1},{text:"Rescuing people trapped under rubble",correct:!1}],explanation:"Disaster logistics is the science of delivering supplies and services (food, water, medicine) to people in need."},{question:"Why is it important to monitor the expiration dates of food and medicines?",options:[{text:"To keep the storeroom neat and tidy",correct:!1},{text:"To protect people's health",correct:!0},{text:"It is pointless",correct:!1},{text:"For speed",correct:!1}],explanation:"Expired food and medicines can harm people's health, so monitoring expiration dates is very important."},{question:"Under the Sphere standards in this module, what is required for toilets and washing facilities in a shelter?",options:[{text:"A single block at the far edge of the camp, apart from the tents",correct:!1},{text:"Enough of them, in safe locations for women and children",correct:!0},{text:"Placement next to the water supply so they are easy to clean",correct:!1},{text:"Facilities for staff only, while residents use public ones",correct:!1}],explanation:"Shelter services under the Sphere standards include toilets and washing facilities in sufficient numbers and in safe locations for women and children."},{question:"The warehouse has two pallets of the same medicine: one expires next month and the other next year. Which is issued first?",options:[{text:"The one expiring next month, because it expires sooner",correct:!0},{text:"The one expiring next year, because it is stronger and lasts longer",correct:!1},{text:"The one at the front of the warehouse, because it is easier to reach",correct:!1},{text:"Whichever arrived first, in order of receipt",correct:!1}],explanation:"In this module, whatever expires first is issued first. Shelf position and arrival order do not set the right order, and both pallets hold the same medicine."},{question:"A shelter holds 300 people. Under the Sphere standard in this module, what is the minimum clean water for one day?",options:[{text:"4,500 liters",correct:!0},{text:"1,200 liters",correct:!1},{text:"3,000 liters",correct:!1},{text:"15,000 liters",correct:!1}],explanation:"The module sets at least 15 liters per person per day: 300 × 15 = 4,500 liters. 1,200 liters is only 4 liters per person, the household drinking-water figure for preparedness, not a shelter’s full need."},{question:"Which of these practices is wrong in an aid warehouse?",options:[{text:"Storing gas cylinders next to sacks of flour",correct:!0},{text:"Putting heavy loads on the lowest shelves",correct:!1},{text:"Raising food sacks off the floor on pallets",correct:!1},{text:"Keeping exit routes and extinguishers clear",correct:!1}],explanation:"Gas cylinders carry a fire and explosion risk; they belong in a separate, ventilated place away from food. The other three are correct storage practices."}],steps:[{title:"Assess the needs",detail:"Find out how many people need help, where they are and which groups need special support: infants and children, older people, people with disabilities, pregnant women and people with chronic illness. Record it in numbers, not guesses."},{title:"Request and receive supplies",detail:"Request supplies through the chain of command with a requisition form. On arrival, check and record the quantity, type, expiration date and condition before anything goes into the warehouse."},{title:"Store safely",detail:"Code every item, issue first whatever expires first, protect stock from heat and humidity and lock the warehouse. Store fuel, gas cylinders and chemicals in a separate place away from food and medicine.",critical:!0},{title:"Distribute fairly and safely",detail:"Distribute at marked, clearly visible distribution points, set up a separate line for older people and people with disabilities and explain the distribution criteria to people. Record who received what and how much.",critical:!0},{title:"Track and report",detail:"Update the stock list daily and report shortages to the logistics section or the emergency operations center (EOC) before an item runs out, so new orders arrive in time."}],warnings:["Do not distribute food or medicine that is expired, damaged or unlabeled or of unknown origin: it can endanger people’s health.","Do not store fuel, gas cylinders or chemicals inside tents, near food or in warehouse exit routes: they raise the risk of fire and poisoning.","Do not start a distribution without organized lines and stewards: pushing at distribution points can cause injuries."],tips:["Set up advance agreements with suppliers before a disaster so time is not lost negotiating during the emergency.","Use one list of item codes for all warehouses so counting and transfers between them are easy.","Discuss needs and distribution criteria with representatives of the shelter residents (women, young people and older people).","Keep backup paper forms for record-keeping when there is no power or internet."],activity:{prompt:"You run a distribution point after a flood. The line is long, some people are pushing from the back and an elderly woman has fallen in the line. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I pause distribution briefly, help the elderly woman, open a separate line for older people and explain the process calmly",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a short pause eases the pushing, a separate line protects vulnerable people and explaining the process reduces fear that supplies will run out."},{text:"I speed up distribution so the line shortens fast and people stop pushing",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the woman is still on the ground and the pushing continues; speed without organization can injure more people."},{text:"I close the point and send everyone home until the next day",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: people need the supplies, and a sudden closure can increase anger and pushing; organizing is better than closing."},{text:"I call the 104 police at once and wait for them without changing anything",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: police support may be needed under the plan, but the fallen woman and the pushing need action now; you are in charge of the point."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"An aid truck reaches the warehouse. It carries cartons of medicine, some unlabeled and some expired, along with blankets and food. As warehouse manager, what do you do?",options:[{text:"I check and record the whole load, set aside and hold the unlabeled and expired medicines and report them to the medical team and logistics lead",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: checking on receipt stops harmful items from being issued; the medical team decides about the medicines."},{text:"I store the whole load as it is, and staff can spot the bad items at distribution",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: staff work fast during distribution and bad items can reach people; checking must happen on receipt."},{text:"I hand out the expired medicines quickly, because they are often still fine after the date",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: that is a medical decision, not a warehouse one; under this module, expired medicines can harm people’s health."},{text:"I send the whole load back to the supplier without recording it, because some items are bad",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: good blankets and food go back too and people are left without supplies; without records no one knows what came and what went back."}]},{prompt:"Cold weather is starting. The warehouse has only 200 blankets, but the shelter has 300 families. A new order will arrive in a few days. How do you distribute?",options:[{text:"I give first by clear criteria (infants, older people and the sick), explain it to people, record it and request the shortfall at once",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: clear, open criteria make distribution fair and prevent resentment; a quick order closes the gap."},{text:"I give out by queue: first come, first served until the blankets run out",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the strongest and fastest get them and vulnerable people such as older people and families with infants may be left out."},{text:"I cut the blankets in half and give each family one half so everyone is equal",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: half a blanket protects no one from the cold; equality without assessing need is not proper aid."},{text:"I keep all blankets in the warehouse until there are 300, so no one says it was unfair",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: 200 families can be protected now; holding supplies in the warehouse during the cold harms vulnerable people."}]}],realCase:"After a flood, a shelter was opened for families who had left their homes. The logistics team had pre-positioned supplies in a nearby warehouse. Every load was checked on receipt and unlabeled medicines were set aside. Whatever expired first was issued first. There was a separate line for older people. Through daily records, the team noticed in the first days that infant diapers were running low, and the new order arrived before they ran out. The main lesson: records and planning prevent shortages and unfairness."},{id:31,code:"TUNNEL-3000",icon:"Construction",accent:"#57534e",title:"Tunnel and underground space safety",subtitle:"Tunnel and underground space safety",summary:"Fire dynamics in enclosed and underground spaces, rescue in long tunnels and the effects of smoke.",blocks:[{body:"Tunnels and underground spaces (such as road tunnels, mines and underground parking garages) present particular hazards. Fire in these spaces is far more dangerous because ventilation is limited, escape is difficult and smoke accumulates quickly. This module is important for road tunnels and underground parking garages in the cities of the Kurdistan Region (principal standard: NFPA 502)."},{heading:"1. Characteristics of tunnels",body:"How a tunnel differs from an ordinary building:",points:["Air: natural ventilation is limited and the fire consumes oxygen rapidly.","Exits: often there are only two portals, and in newer tunnels intermediate emergency doors (cross-passages).","Smoke: spreads quickly beneath the low ceiling and travels in two directions or in the direction of the ventilation.","Heat: the walls and ceiling radiate heat back, and heat builds up.","Length: it can be several kilometers, which lengthens the time it takes teams to reach the fire."]},{heading:"2. Fire dynamics in tunnels",body:"How fire behaves in a tunnel:",points:["Spread: in tunnels with longitudinal ventilation, smoke and heat travel in the direction of the airflow; this protects people on the other (upstream) side from the smoke.","Smoke stratification: initially, smoke forms a layer beneath the ceiling and clean air remains below; excessive airflow or applied water can disrupt this layer.","Backlayering: smoke beneath the ceiling flows back against the direction of ventilation when the air velocity is below the critical velocity.","Longitudinal ventilation: ventilation along the length of the tunnel using special jet fans that push the smoke in one direction.","Rate of development: fires involving cars and trucks in a tunnel can quickly generate very high temperatures."]},{heading:"3. Tunnel safety systems",body:"Protective systems:",points:["Alarm system: heat and smoke detectors, and alarm sounders.","Fire suppression: automatic water systems (deluge or water mist) and hose outlets for firefighters.","Ventilation: special fans for smoke extraction and smoke control.","Emergency exits: special doors and protected routes with illuminated signs.","Communications: an in-tunnel radio system and emergency telephones."]},{heading:"4. Rescue in tunnels",body:"How people are rescued:",points:["Pedestrian walkway: a dedicated route for escaping on foot.","Direction of escape: always toward fresh air and away from the smoke.","SCBA: self-contained breathing apparatus for all firefighters entering the tunnel.","Casualty sorting point (triage): outside the tunnel and in a safe location.","Traffic: closing the road to vehicles in coordination with the traffic police."]},{heading:"5. Training and planning",body:"Advance preparation:",points:["Emergency plan: a specific plan for each tunnel.","Training: periodic evacuation drills.","Maps: tunnel maps for firefighting teams.","Coordination: with the police and the ambulance service.","Experience: learning lessons from past incidents."]},{heading:"6. What tunnel users should do",body:"Many lives are saved in the first minutes by what drivers and passengers do, before crews arrive:",points:["If you see smoke or fire ahead, do not drive into it; pull over and switch off the engine.","Leave the vehicle and walk to the nearest emergency door away from the smoke, following the lit signs.","Use the emergency telephone or alarm button and call 115.","Help older people, children and people with disabilities, but do not go back into the smoke.","Wait in the safe area and follow the instructions of emergency crews and the tunnel operator."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_30_tunnel_fire.mp4",title:"Tunnel fire",description:"Fire dynamics in tunnels"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_30_ventilation.png",title:"Ventilation",description:"The tunnel ventilation system"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_30_evacuation.mp4",title:"Evacuation",description:"Evacuating a tunnel"}],quiz:[{question:"Why is a fire in a tunnel more dangerous than one in an ordinary building?",options:[{text:"Ventilation is limited, escape is difficult and smoke accumulates",correct:!0},{text:"There is no difference",correct:!1},{text:"Ordinary buildings are worse, because they have more fuel",correct:!1},{text:"It is not more dangerous: tunnels are built of concrete that does not burn",correct:!1}],explanation:"In a tunnel, ventilation is limited, exits are few and far apart and smoke accumulates quickly beneath the low ceiling; all of this increases the danger."},{question:"What is backlayering?",options:[{text:"Fresh air being blown into the tunnel by fans",correct:!1},{text:"Smoke flowing back against the ventilation",correct:!0},{text:"Rescue",correct:!1},{text:"Smoke being pushed out of the tunnel by fans",correct:!1}],explanation:"Backlayering is a phenomenon in which smoke beneath the ceiling flows back against the direction of ventilation when the air velocity is insufficient; this is very dangerous for occupants and teams."},{question:"In a tunnel fire, in which direction should people escape?",options:[{text:"Toward the nearest portal, even if it means walking through the smoke",correct:!1},{text:"In the direction of the ventilation airflow, along with the smoke",correct:!1},{text:"Toward fresh air and away from the smoke",correct:!0},{text:"Back to their vehicles, to wait there for the rescue teams",correct:!1}],explanation:"The direction of escape is always toward fresh air and away from the smoke. With longitudinal ventilation, smoke and heat travel in the direction of the airflow, so people on the upstream side are protected."},{question:"In a tunnel with longitudinal ventilation, on which side of the fire are people better protected?",options:[{text:"The upstream side, where the air comes from",correct:!0},{text:"The downstream side, following the smoke",correct:!1},{text:"Right by the fire, because heat lifts the smoke",correct:!1},{text:"Both sides are the same; airflow does not matter",correct:!1}],explanation:"In this module, smoke and heat travel with the airflow, so people on the upstream side are protected while the other side fills with smoke."},{question:"Traffic has stopped in a tunnel and there is smoke ahead. Which action is right?",options:[{text:"Leave the car and walk to an emergency door away from the smoke",correct:!0},{text:"Stay in the car, close the windows and turn off the ventilation",correct:!1},{text:"Reverse the car back to the portal to get out quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Walk toward the portal that is beyond the smoke",correct:!1}],explanation:"A car does not protect against smoke, reversing between stopped cars blocks the crews’ route and walking through smoke is dangerous. Emergency doors exist for exactly this."},{question:"Why must changes to fan speed during a tunnel fire be coordinated between the commander and the operator?",options:[{text:"Because it can cause backlayering or break up the smoke layer",correct:!0},{text:"Because fans use a lot of power and raise the cost",correct:!1},{text:"Because fan noise drowns out the crews’ radios",correct:!1},{text:"Because fans never work at all without the operator",correct:!1}],explanation:"In this module, backlayering occurs when air velocity is below the critical velocity, and excessive airflow can break up the smoke layer. Either can push smoke onto escaping people or crews."}],steps:[{title:"Do not drive into smoke",detail:"If you see smoke or fire ahead inside a tunnel, do not drive through the smoke. Pull over to the side, switch off the engine and leave the vehicle.",critical:!0},{title:"Raise the alarm",detail:"Use the tunnel emergency telephone or alarm button and call 115: which tunnel, which direction, near which marker and what you see.",critical:!0},{title:"Walk toward fresh air",detail:"Walk along the pedestrian walkway to the nearest emergency door or cross-passage, away from the smoke and following the lit signs. Close the emergency door behind you so smoke does not follow.",critical:!0},{title:"Crews: approach from the fresh-air side",detail:"Crews enter from the upstream (fresh-air) side wearing SCBA. Ventilation is changed only in coordination between the incident commander and the tunnel operator, and the road is closed with the traffic police.",critical:!0},{title:"Triage outside the tunnel",detail:"Set up the casualty triage point outside the tunnel in a safe place on the fresh-air side. Count everyone who came out and record their names to find out whether anyone is still inside."}],warnings:["Do not stay in your vehicle waiting for help during a tunnel fire: smoke can reach you quickly and the vehicle will not protect you.","Do not walk through smoke to reach the tunnel portal: choose the nearby emergency doors and fresh air.","Crews: do not change fan speed or ventilation direction without coordination, and do not spray water into the smoke layer above people escaping: this can cause backlayering or bring the smoke down on them."],tips:["When you enter a tunnel, notice the signs for emergency doors and emergency telephones so you know where they are.","Keep a good distance from the vehicle ahead in a tunnel, even when stopped, so the way stays open for crews and escape.","If the tunnel has its own radio broadcast or public address, listen to the instructions and follow them.","Crews: make pre-incident visits to the tunnels in your area, learn the maps, water outlets and emergency doors and train with the operator."],activity:{prompt:"You are driving through a long tunnel. Traffic stops and you see smoke under the ceiling ahead moving toward you. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I switch off the engine, leave the car, walk away from the smoke to the nearest emergency door and call 115 from the emergency phone",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: in this module the escape direction is always toward fresh air and away from smoke, and the car is not a safe place."},{text:"I close the windows, switch off the car’s ventilation and wait inside for the rescue teams",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a car does not protect you from tunnel smoke and heat, and crews may take a long time to reach you."},{text:"I drive past the stopped cars along the edge to get out of the tunnel fast",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you head toward the smoke and fire and block the walkway and the crews’ route; escape is on foot and away from the smoke."},{text:"I walk toward the fire to see how big it is, then decide",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: smoke spreads fast under the low ceiling; walking toward the fire puts you in the smoke and wastes escape time."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Your crew reaches a tunnel with longitudinal ventilation. The operator says the fans push the smoke east, and there are people on both sides of the fire. From which side does the crew go in?",options:[{text:"From the west (upstream) side with SCBA, and I request another crew to search the east side under a separate plan",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the upstream side is a cleaner, safer route for the crew; people on the smoke side need a dedicated plan with full protection."},{text:"From the east side, because people in the smoke are in more danger and must be reached at once",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: going in with the smoke and heat exposes the crew to great danger and poor visibility; searching that side needs a separate plan."},{text:"I ask the operator to switch the fans off at once so the smoke is equal on both sides",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a sudden shutdown can send smoke toward the clean side and endanger people who are protected now."},{text:"I wait at the portal until all the smoke is out, then go in",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: people inside need help; the upstream side is a safe way in now."}]},{prompt:"Your crew is near the fire. The smoke is still a layer under the ceiling and a group of people is walking beneath it toward an emergency door. A crew member wants to spray water into the smoke. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I do not allow water into the smoke layer above the people, help them out through the door quickly and then attack the fire",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: in this module, applied water can break up the smoke layer and bring smoke down onto people who are escaping."},{text:"I let him spray, because water cools the smoke and helps people see",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: cooling the smoke makes it heavier and brings it down; the people under the layer would end up in the smoke."},{text:"I ask the operator to raise fan speed sharply so the smoke clears fast",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: excessive airflow can break up the smoke layer, as the module explains; ventilation changes need coordination and assessment first."},{text:"I ask the people to stop where they are until the fire is fully out",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: right now they can get out safely under a stable smoke layer; stopping wastes time and the layer may come down."}]}],realCase:"In a road tunnel, a truck caught fire and traffic stopped. Some drivers stayed in their cars, but one remembered the safety messages he had seen before: he switched off the engine, got out and warned the others. They walked along the pedestrian walkway, following the lit signs to an emergency door, and he called 115 from the emergency telephone. The crews arrived from the upstream side and the operator kept the ventilation steady. The main lesson: getting out quickly on foot toward fresh air saves lives."},{id:32,code:"UNI-332",icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Fire extinguishers — complete training",subtitle:"Fire extinguisher — complete training (PASS)",summary:"Identifying all types of extinguishers, fire classes under NFPA and EN 2, the PASS technique in detail and the inspection and maintenance of extinguishers.",blocks:[{body:"The fire extinguisher is an essential device for putting out small fires in their incipient stage. In universities and research laboratories, chemicals and electrical equipment raise the risk of fire. Every student and faculty member must be able to use an extinguisher quickly and correctly. Small extinguishers empty quickly, often in a few seconds, and the exact discharge time is on the label, so there is no time to lose and the technique must be right the first time. If the fire grows or smoke blocks the escape route, leave the extinguisher, get out and call 115."},{heading:"1. Fire classes according to NFPA",body:"Under the American NFPA system (NFPA 10), fires are divided into five classes. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. The five NFPA classes:",points:["Class A (ordinary solid combustibles): wood, paper, fabric, plastic. Extinguishers: water, ABC powder, foam.","Class B (flammable liquids and gases): gasoline, oil, grease, alcohol, laboratory solvents, LPG. Liquids: foam, CO₂ or ABC powder (never use water!); for alcohol, acetone and other polar solvents: CO₂, ABC powder or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam, because ordinary foam breaks down on them. Gas: stop the gas first if it is safe; never put out a gas flame you cannot stop.","Class C (energized electrical equipment): switches, transformers, panels, generators, servers. Extinguishers: CO₂, ABC powder, clean agent. (Do not use water or foam unless the label shows a Class C rating or an electrical-test marking, as on some water-mist units!) If it is safe to do so, isolate the electrical supply first.","Class D (combustible metals): magnesium, powdered aluminum, titanium, lithium (metal). Extinguisher: only an extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal; the agent differs from metal to metal (lithium, for example, needs an agent listed for lithium). Never use water, ordinary powder or foam (water can cause a violent reaction and an explosion!). Dry sand only if the site fire plan or the metal's safety data sheet names it and it is completely dry. Note: lithium-ion battery fires are not Class D; water and continuous cooling are recommended.","Class K (cooking oils; Class F under EN 2): cooking oils and fats. Extinguisher: wet chemical (in the UK it carries a yellow band under BS 7863; read the label). (Water causes burning oil to erupt violently and spreads the fire!)"]},{heading:"2. Types of extinguishers and their colors",body:"Color is not the fire class. Under the European standard BS EN 3 the extinguisher body is red; in the UK a colored band (BS 7863) shows the agent: cream = foam, blue = dry powder, black = CO₂, yellow = wet chemical; water has no band. Other countries use other colors and the color practice in Duhok is not yet confirmed, so always read the label and its class symbols. The main types:",points:["Water extinguisher: all red, no band. For Class A only (wood, paper). Never use it on electrical equipment or oil!","CO₂ extinguisher: black band. For Class B and live electrical equipment (NFPA B and C; under EN 2, C means gas and CO₂ is not rated for gas fires). A very cold gas is discharged and the discharge horn becomes extremely cold: hold only the handle, never the horn, which can cause a cold burn (frostbite) to your hand. CO₂ has a short range (see the label) and is not for deep-seated wood or paper fires. In a small room, leave and ventilate after use, because CO₂ pushes out the oxygen.","ABC dry powder extinguisher: blue band. For Classes A, B and C (not for D and not for K/F). The most widespread type. Drawbacks: it reduces visibility and contaminates equipment.","Foam extinguisher (AFFF): cream band. For Classes A and B. It spreads foam over the liquid and cuts off the oxygen. Do not use it on electrical equipment! Note: AFFF contains PFAS and is being replaced by fluorine-free foam in many countries.","Clean agent extinguisher (portable): for sensitive equipment, laboratories and server rooms. The class rating depends on the model (often B and C, some also A), so read the label; the minimum room size is also on the label. It leaves no residue; leave the room after use.","Special extinguisher (Class D): for combustible metals — it contains a special agent that differs from metal to metal. Use only one whose label lists that metal, and use it as the label and the manufacturer's instructions say."]},{heading:"3. The PASS technique in detail",body:`Before you use an extinguisher (every answer must be yes):
• The fire is small and just starting, like a fire in a wastebasket or a pan.
• The extinguisher is the right type for this fire.
• Your exit is behind you and the fire is not between you and the exit.
• The alarm has been raised: others are warned and 115 has been called.
• You are trained to use the extinguisher.
• No smoke or heat is blocking your way out.
• If the fire does not go out at once, stop, get out and close the door behind you.

PASS is the general method for most hand-held extinguishers. Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions. Start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label or in the manufacturer's instructions, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller. Never turn your back on the fire:`,points:["P = Pull: pull out the safety pin. This pin prevents the extinguisher from being discharged by mistake.","A = Aim: aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the flames above!","S = Squeeze: squeeze the handle firmly so that the agent is discharged.","S = Sweep: sweep the nozzle from side to side across the base of the fire until the fire is completely out, and watch to make sure it does not reignite."]},{heading:"4. Inspection and maintenance of extinguishers (NFPA 10)",body:"Inspection and maintenance are five separate tasks; do not mix them up. The intervals below follow NFPA 10 and OSHA (29 CFR 1910.157); the rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed:",points:["Visual check (monthly, by the person in charge): in its place, visible and easy to reach; safety pin and tamper seal intact; label readable and facing out; no damage, rust or leaks; nozzle and hose not damaged or blocked.","Pressure gauge: the needle must be in the green zone, if there is a gauge. CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full.","Inspection and maintenance (yearly, by a qualified person).","Recharge: after any use, even a short one, by a qualified person.","6-year maintenance: stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers that need a 12-year hydrostatic test are emptied and checked inside every 6 years.","Hydrostatic test (pressure test of the cylinder), by type: every 5 years for water, foam (AFFF), CO₂, wet chemical and stainless-steel dry chemical extinguishers; every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells. A hydrostatic test is not a replacement.","Removal from service: an extinguisher is taken out of service if it fails an inspection or test, is damaged, obsolete or recalled, has no readable label, or is a non-rechargeable unit past the service life on its label."]},{heading:"5. Specific hazards in universities",body:"Universities are sensitive environments because of:",points:["Chemistry laboratories: solvents and alcohols (Class B), and certain reactive metals (Class D).","Server and IT rooms: energized electrical equipment (Class C under NFPA): clean agent or CO₂.","University kitchens: cooking oil (Class K under NFPA / F under EN 2).","Libraries and archives: paper and wood (Class A).","Placement: place extinguishers near doors and corridors; under NFPA 10, the travel distance to reach an extinguisher in Class A areas must not exceed 23 meters (75 feet), and in Class B areas it is shorter (9–15 meters)."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_fire_ext.mp4",title:"The fire extinguisher",description:"The PASS technique and the types of extinguishers"}],quiz:[{question:"Under NFPA letters, for which fire classes is a CO₂ extinguisher suitable?",options:[{text:"A and D",correct:!1},{text:"B and C",correct:!0},{text:"A and K",correct:!1},{text:"D and K",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under NFPA letters, CO₂ is suitable for flammable liquids (B) and live electrical equipment (C) because it does not conduct electricity and leaves no residue. Under EN 2, C means gas, and CO₂ is not rated for gas fires."},{question:"In the PASS technique, where should the extinguisher nozzle be aimed?",options:[{text:"At the top of the flames",correct:!1},{text:"At the base of the fire",correct:!0},{text:"Away from the fire",correct:!1},{text:"At the ceiling",correct:!1}],explanation:"Always aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, because that is where the fuel is and where the fire is extinguished."},{question:"A fire involving a flammable chemical liquid breaks out in a university laboratory; which extinguisher do you use?",options:[{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"CO₂ or ABC powder",correct:!0},{text:"Special Class D powder",correct:!1},{text:"Wet chemical",correct:!1}],explanation:"A flammable liquid fire is Class B: CO₂ or ABC powder is suitable. Use it only if the fire is small and just starting, the alarm has been raised and 115 called, you are trained and your exit is behind you; if the fire grows, leave the area and call 115."},{question:"Which situation means “no, get out” instead of using an extinguisher?",options:[{text:"The fire is between you and the only exit",correct:!0},{text:"The extinguisher label shows this fire’s class",correct:!1},{text:"The fire is small and started in a wastebasket",correct:!1},{text:"The alarm is sounding and a colleague has called 115",correct:!1}],explanation:"If the fire is between you and the exit, one answer is “no” and you must get out. The other three are “yes” answers in the go/no-go check."},{question:"Oil is burning in a large pan in the university kitchen. If every go/no-go answer is yes, which extinguisher is right?",options:[{text:"Wet chemical (Class K under NFPA / F under EN 2)",correct:!0},{text:"Water, because water cools the oil down",correct:!1},{text:"CO₂, because it leaves no residue in the kitchen",correct:!1},{text:"ABC powder, because it covers every fire type",correct:!1}],explanation:"In this module, cooking oil is Class K (EN 2: F) and wet chemical is the agent for it. Water makes the oil erupt, ABC covers only A, B and C (not K/F) and CO₂ is not the listed choice for this fire."},{question:"You used an extinguisher for a few seconds and the fire went out. The pressure gauge is still in the green. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Report it so a qualified person recharges it",correct:!0},{text:"Hang it back, since the gauge is still green",correct:!1},{text:"Put the pin back in and leave it in place",correct:!1},{text:"Wait for the yearly inspection to refill it",correct:!1}],explanation:"In this module, recharge follows any use, even a short one. A green gauge does not mean the extinguisher will work at the next fire, and the yearly inspection may be months away."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm",detail:"Shout “Fire!”, activate the fire alarm and make sure 115 has been called, or tell a named person to call. Others must be warned before you pick up an extinguisher.",critical:!0},{title:"Go/no-go check",detail:"Every answer must be yes: the fire is small and just starting; the extinguisher suits this fire (read the label); your exit is behind you; the alarm is raised and 115 informed; you are trained; no smoke or heat blocks your way out. If any answer is no, get out.",critical:!0},{title:"Check the extinguisher quickly",detail:"Look at the fire-class symbols on the label (not the extinguisher’s color), check the safety pin and tamper seal and, if there is a pressure gauge, that the needle is in the green. Start from the distance shown on the label."},{title:"Use PASS",detail:"Pull the pin, aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, squeeze the handle and sweep from side to side across the base. Move closer only as the fire gets smaller and never turn your back on it. Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units are used as their label says."},{title:"If it does not go out at once, get out",detail:"If the fire does not go out at once, grows or the smoke increases, stop, get out, close the door behind you and let no one go in. Outside, tell the 115 crews what was burning and which extinguisher you used.",critical:!0},{title:"After use",detail:"Watch the area so the fire does not reignite. After using CO₂ or a clean agent in a small room, leave and ventilate. Report the extinguisher to the person in charge so a qualified person recharges it, even after a short use."}],warnings:["Never put water on a cooking-oil fire: the oil erupts violently and the fire spreads. Use a wet chemical extinguisher if every go/no-go answer is yes.","Do not put out a gas flame if you cannot stop the gas: gas builds up and can explode. Stop the gas only if it is safe; otherwise get out and call 115.","Do not hold the CO₂ horn: it gets extremely cold and can cause a cold burn. Hold only the handle."],tips:["Know where the extinguishers on your floor are and what type they are before you need them, and read their labels.","Practise with training extinguishers under an instructor; your first use should not be at a real fire.","Report any used, missing or damaged extinguisher at once, and never hang an empty extinguisher back in its place.","In laboratories, learn from the person in charge which chemicals are used and which extinguishers suit them before you start work."],activity:{prompt:"In a chemistry lab, alcohol in a small container on the bench has caught fire; the fire is small. A colleague has raised the alarm and called 115. The door is behind you, you are trained and there is a CO₂ and a water extinguisher nearby. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I take the CO₂, start from the distance on its label, use PASS and, if it does not go out at once, leave and close the door",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: alcohol is a Class B fire and this module lists CO₂ for it; every go/no-go answer is yes."},{text:"I take the water extinguisher because it is bigger and holds more agent",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a water extinguisher is for Class A only; water can spread a burning liquid. Size does not tell you the right type."},{text:"I pick up the burning container with gloves and carry it to the sink to put it out",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: carrying a burning container can spill burning liquid on you and the floor and spread the fire."},{text:"I quickly spray CO₂ from close up at the top of the flames to put it out fast",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: PASS says aim at the base, not the flames, and starting too close can splash the liquid. Start from the label distance."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the university server room, a burning smell and a little smoke are coming from one rack; a small flame is visible. The alarm is raised and 115 informed. There is a CO₂ and a clean agent extinguisher at the door. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I run the go/no-go check, cut power to the rack by the site procedure if safe, then use CO₂ or clean agent with PASS from the label distance",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this is an energized-equipment fire (NFPA Class C); the module says isolate the power first if safe and use CO₂ or a clean agent."},{text:"I bring the water extinguisher from the corridor, because water cools faster",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: water on energized equipment can cause electric shock; the module says no water or foam unless the label shows a Class C rating."},{text:"I prop the door open and set up a fan to clear the smoke before fighting the fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: air feeds the fire and spreads the smoke, and time is lost while the fire is still small."},{text:"I pull the rack away from the wall by hand to see where the fire starts",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: touching burning electrical equipment risks electric shock and burns and wastes time."}]},{prompt:"You used the CO₂ and the flame died down, but smoke is still coming from the rack and the room is filling with smoke. The extinguisher is emptying fast. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I stop, get out, close the door and tell the 115 crew that it is an electrical fire and CO₂ was used",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the fire did not go out at once and smoke is increasing, which is a “no.” After CO₂ in a small room, leave and give crews accurate information."},{text:"I grab the clean agent extinguisher too and stay in the smoke until the fire is fully out",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: staying in a room full of smoke and CO₂ endangers your breathing; if the fire does not go out at once, getting out is right."},{text:"I leave the door open so the smoke escapes and wait by the door for the crew",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: an open door feeds air to the fire and spreads smoke into the corridor; the door must be closed."},{text:"I hang the empty extinguisher back so no one thinks it is missing, and go back to work",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the fire is still burning, and an empty extinguisher can mislead someone later; every used extinguisher is reported and recharged."}]}],realCase:"In the university cafeteria kitchen, oil in a large pan caught fire. A new worker quickly grabbed the water extinguisher, but a trained colleague stopped him and said water makes the oil erupt. He shouted “Fire!”, sounded the alarm and another colleague called 115. He turned off the heat under the pan safely, checked that his exit was behind him and used the wet chemical extinguisher as its label directs. The fire went out and they watched the area. The main lesson: the right type of extinguisher and the go/no-go check save lives."},{id:33,code:"CAMPUS-3100",icon:"School",accent:"#0f766e",title:"Campus safety",subtitle:"Laboratories, residence halls, lecture-hall evacuation, campus events and reporting hazards",summary:"A university brings together laboratories with chemicals and gas, residence halls where students cook, use heaters and charge devices, and lecture halls and events with large numbers of people. This course explains the main hazards in each place, the rules that prevent most incidents, how to evacuate a lecture hall with everyone in it, including people who need help, and how to report a hazard to the university safety office and to 115.",blocks:[{body:"Safety on campus is everyone's responsibility: students, academic staff, administrative staff and contractors. The university safety office and the people in charge of each building, laboratory and residence hall set the local rules and the emergency plan, train people and keep records. Follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority and the rules of your university. This course gives the general principles; it does not replace the training that each laboratory requires before you work there."},{heading:"1. Laboratories: chemicals, gas cylinders and safety equipment",body:"Before you work in a laboratory, learn its rules and its emergency plan, and know where the exits, the fire alarm, the extinguishers, the eyewash, the safety shower and the phone are and how each one is used.",points:["Chemical storage: store chemicals by hazard group and compatibility, not in alphabetical order. Keep flammable liquids in a cabinet made for flammable liquids, oxidizers away from fuels and highly toxic chemicals in a locked cabinet.","Never store chemicals on bench tops, on the floor, in a fume hood, in an exit route or on shelves above eye level.","Labels and SDS: every container shows its contents and hazards. Read the label and the safety data sheet (SDS) before you use a chemical, never use food containers for chemicals and never eat or drink in the laboratory.","Gas cylinders: secure every cylinder, full or empty, to a wall or a bench, keep the valve cap on when a cylinder is stored or moved, and move cylinders only on a proper cart, never by rolling them.","Fume hoods: use the hood when toxic vapors, dust or gases may be released. Keep the sash as low as possible and your head outside the hood, do not use the hood for storage, and report a hood that is not working properly.","Eyewash and safety shower: keep the way to them clear. If a chemical splashes into the eyes, flush them at once at the eyewash for at least 15 minutes, keeping the eyelids open; for a large splash on the body, use the shower and take off contaminated clothing. Then call 122 and say which chemical it was.","Never work alone with hazardous chemicals or procedures: plan your work so that another trained person is nearby.","Report every spill, injury, incident and near miss to the person in charge of the laboratory."]},{heading:"2. A smell of gas or a fire in the laboratory",body:`If you smell gas:
1. Do not switch any equipment, light or flame on or off, and do not use a phone inside.
2. Close the gas valve only if it is close to you and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected and without making a spark; otherwise do not go near it.
3. Warn everyone, get out, raise the alarm and call 115 from outside.

If a fire starts: use an extinguisher only if the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher suits this fire, your exit is behind you, the alarm has been raised and 115 called, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out (see the course on fire extinguishers). Otherwise leave, close the door behind you and call 115. Tell the responders which chemicals and gas cylinders are in the laboratory, and do not go back in until they say it is safe.`,critical:!0},{heading:"3. Residence halls",body:"Follow the rules of your residence hall, and:",points:["Cooking: cook only where it is allowed and only when you are alert, stay in the kitchen while you cook, and keep the cooking area clean and free of anything that can burn.","Heaters: use only the heaters that the residence hall allows. Turn them off when you sleep or leave the room, keep them away from anything that can burn, and plug electric heaters directly into a wall outlet, never into an extension cord or a power strip.","Chargers: charge phones and laptops on a hard surface, never on the bed. Follow the residence hall's rules on e-bikes and e-scooters, and never charge or leave them in corridors, stairwells or next to exits.","Outlets: do not overload outlets, use extension cords only for a short time and never plug one into another. Check the residence hall's rules before you bring in electrical appliances.","Smoke alarms: never disable or cover a smoke alarm or remove its battery. Report an alarm that is not working to the residence hall staff.","Candles and smoking: many residence halls do not allow candles; use flameless ones. Smoke only outside, where it is allowed.","Keep corridors, exits and stairs clear, and keep your key or access card near your bed. When the alarm sounds, get out quickly and stay out."]},{heading:"4. Evacuating a lecture hall",points:["Know at least two ways out of every lecture hall, laboratory and room that you use: the main entrance may not be usable in an emergency.","When the alarm sounds, the lecturer stops the class at once and everyone leaves by the nearest safe exit, closing the doors behind them. Use the stairs: do not use the elevators unless the firefighters tell you to.","Go to the assembly point and stay there. The lecturer or the floor warden checks that everyone is out and tells the responders at once about anyone missing.","People who need help: students and staff with a disability, an injury or another need agree in advance with the safety office on a personal evacuation plan and a helper. If you cannot reach a person who needs help, get out and tell the firefighters at once where they are waiting.","Do not go back inside until the firefighters or the safety office say it is safe.","Take part in every drill as if it were real, and tell the safety office about anything that slowed the evacuation."]},{heading:"5. Events on campus",body:"Concerts, graduations, exhibitions and sports events bring many people into one place. Organizers plan each event in advance with the university safety office and follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority: how many people the place can hold safely, clear exits and aisles, stewards, first aid and how to call 115 and 122. Everyone who attends can also protect themselves:",points:["When you arrive, look for all the exits: some may be behind you, and you may not be able to use the main entrance.","Check that aisles and exit doors are clear and not locked or chained; exit doors must open from the inside without a key. If they are blocked, tell the organizers, and leave if it is not fixed.","If the place is overcrowded, stay where you can reach an exit easily, and leave if you do not feel safe.","Agree on a meeting place outside with the people you came with, in case you are separated.","Watch for fire hazards such as candles, fireworks or other open flames indoors, and report them to the organizers.","For how crowds behave in an emergency, see the course on disaster psychology and human behavior."]},{heading:"6. Reporting hazards",points:["Report a hazard as soon as you see it to the university safety office or the person in charge of the building: blocked or locked exits, a smoke alarm or extinguisher that is missing or damaged, damaged wiring or overloaded outlets, a chemical leak, a fume hood that is not working, a smell of gas or an unsafe event.","In an emergency (fire, smoke, a smell of gas, a chemical release or a person trapped), call 115 first; for a medical emergency call 122. Then inform the safety office.","When you call, give the location first: the building, the floor and the room. Then say what happened, how many people are hurt, which chemicals or gas are involved, and your name and phone number. Do not hang up until you are told to.","Report every injury, incident and near miss, even when nobody was hurt: it helps to prevent the next one.","Managers: keep the emergency plan, the chemical hygiene plan of each laboratory and the training records up to date, review the emergency plan with every new member of staff, and follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_campus_safety.webp",title:"Safety on campus",description:"Students leave a lecture hall through two marked exits toward the assembly point; in a laboratory nearby, gas cylinders are chained to the wall and the eyewash and safety shower are clear."}],quiz:[{question:"How should chemicals be arranged in a laboratory store?",options:[{text:"By hazard group and compatibility, never in plain alphabetical order",correct:!0},{text:"In strict alphabetical order, so that every bottle is quick to find",correct:!1},{text:"By bottle size, with the heaviest containers always kept on the top shelves",correct:!1},{text:"On the bench tops and in the fume hood, where everyone can see them",correct:!1}],explanation:"Chemicals are stored by compatibility, not alphabetically, so that substances that react dangerously with each other are never side by side; for example, oxidizers are kept away from fuels. Heavy containers go on low shelves, and nothing is stored on bench tops, on the floor or in fume hoods."},{question:"A chemical splashes into a student's eyes in the laboratory. What is the right first action?",options:[{text:"Flush the eyes at the eyewash at once for at least 15 minutes, then call 122",correct:!0},{text:"Wipe the eyes gently with a dry tissue and wait to see whether the pain stops",correct:!1},{text:"Look up the chemical in the SDS first, and only then decide whether to rinse",correct:!1},{text:"Rinse the eyes quickly for a few seconds with a small bottle of drinking water",correct:!1}],explanation:"Every second counts: flush the eyes at the eyewash straight away for at least 15 minutes, keeping the eyelids open, then call 122 and say which chemical it was."},{question:"You smell gas in a laboratory. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Touch no switch, get everyone out and call 115 from outside the building",correct:!0},{text:"Switch on the extractor fans and the lights to find where the leak is",correct:!1},{text:"Use your phone's flashlight to look for the leaking pipe in the laboratory",correct:!1},{text:"Light a match near each gas tap to find out which one is leaking",correct:!1}],explanation:"Any spark can ignite the gas. Do not switch anything on or off and do not use a phone inside; close the valve only if it is close and you can reach it without entering the area where the gas has collected; get everyone out and call 115 from outside."},{question:"What should a student do with the smoke alarm in a residence hall room?",options:[{text:"Keep it working and report it to the staff if it does not work",correct:!0},{text:"Cover it with a plastic bag while cooking so that it does not sound",correct:!1},{text:"Take out its battery to use in the remote control when it is needed",correct:!1},{text:"Switch it off at night so that it does not wake people for nothing",correct:!1}],explanation:"A working smoke alarm gives the early warning you need to get out. Never disable or cover a smoke alarm or remove its battery; report a faulty alarm to the residence hall staff."},{question:"The fire alarm sounds during a lecture. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Leave at once by the nearest safe exit and go to the assembly point",correct:!0},{text:"Finish the last part of the lecture first, in case it is a false alarm",correct:!1},{text:"Take the elevator down, because it is quicker than using the stairs",correct:!1},{text:"Wait in the hall until someone comes to confirm that there is a fire",correct:!1}],explanation:"Treat every alarm as real: leave at once by the nearest safe exit, use the stairs and not the elevator, and go to the assembly point so that the lecturer or the warden can check that everyone is out."},{question:"When the fire alarm sounds, a student who uses a wheelchair is in your third-floor class and her assigned helper is absent. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Use her plan, or tell firefighters where she is",correct:!0},{text:"Take her down in the elevator, since stairs do not suit the chair",correct:!1},{text:"Carry her and the chair down the stairs on your own",correct:!1},{text:"Stay with her in the hall until the alarm stops",correct:!1}],explanation:"In this module, people who need help have a personal evacuation plan and a helper; if you cannot reach them safely, get out and tell the firefighters at once where they are. No elevator unless firefighters say so, and carrying her alone puts you both at risk."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm and call",detail:"Sound the fire alarm and shout. From a safe place, call 115 (122 for a medical emergency): building, floor and room first, then what happened and which chemicals or gas are involved.",critical:!0},{title:"Stop the class and get out",detail:"The lecturer stops the class at once. Everyone leaves by the nearest safe exit, closes doors behind them and uses the stairs, not the elevator.",critical:!0},{title:"Help people who need assistance",detail:"Follow the personal evacuation plan of anyone with a disability or other need. If you cannot reach or move them safely, get out and tell the firefighters at once where they are waiting.",critical:!0},{title:"Account for people at the assembly point",detail:"Stay at the assembly point. The lecturer or floor warden checks that everyone is out and reports anyone missing to the 115 crews at once."},{title:"Do not go back until told it is safe",detail:"No one goes back in for belongings, laptops or books until the firefighters or the safety office say it is safe. Afterward, report anything that slowed the evacuation to the safety office.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never lock or chain an exit door or put things in front of it: in a fire it may be the only way out alive.","Never cover or disable a smoke alarm or remove its battery, not even while cooking: early warning gives you time to get out.","Never work alone with hazardous chemicals, and never use a chemical before reading its label and SDS."],tips:["At the start of every term, learn two ways out of your lecture hall and laboratory.","Keep your key or access card by your bed so you can get out quickly at night.","Take every evacuation drill as if it were real and report afterward what slowed people down.","Report every near miss in the laboratory, even when no one was hurt."],activity:{prompt:"You are one of the organizers of a concert in the university’s main hall. Before it starts, you see that two rear exit doors are chained shut. The event manager says it is to keep out people without tickets. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I explain that exit doors must open from inside without a key, ask for the chains to be removed and a steward put at the door, and report to the safety office if it is not fixed",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: exit doors must open from inside without a key; a steward at the door achieves the same goal safely."},{text:"I keep the chains but hang a key on the wall by the door so it can be opened in an emergency",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: in smoke and a crush no one will find the key and open the lock; the door must open without one."},{text:"I do nothing, because the large main entrance is open and enough for everyone",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: in this module, the main entrance may be unusable in an emergency; people need every exit."},{text:"I cut the chains myself with a tool from the storeroom without telling anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the chains may simply be put back because no one knows why; explaining and reporting stop it happening again."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"At night the fire alarm sounds in the residence hall. You test your door with the back of your hand and it is cool; there is a smell of smoke in the corridor, but you can see well. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I take my key, get out quickly by the stairs, close the door behind me and go to the assembly point",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a cool door and a clear corridor mean the way is open; closing the door holds back smoke and the assembly point makes the head count easier."},{text:"I pack my laptop and books first, since I can see well and there is time",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: smoke can block the way within minutes; there is no time for belongings."},{text:"I stay in my room and wait to find out whether the alarm is real or false",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: in this module, when the alarm sounds you get out quickly and stay out; the smoke smell shows the fire may be real."},{text:"I take the elevator, because it is faster than the stairs and the smoke is still light",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: in a fire the elevator may stop or open onto the fire floor; use the stairs."}]},{prompt:"In the nearby stairwell, thick smoke is rising from the floor below and you cannot see. The second stairwell is at the end of the corridor. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I close the stairwell door and use the second stairs; if that is blocked too, I go back to my room, close the door, seal the gaps, call 115 and signal from the window",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the module teaches two ways out; if both are blocked, staying behind a closed door and alerting 115 is safer than walking through smoke."},{text:"I hold my breath and run down the nearby stairs through the smoke",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: thick smoke can knock you unconscious in a few breaths and you cannot see; there is a second way out."},{text:"I go up to the roof, because smoke rises and there is air up there",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: smoke rises up the same stairwell, and the roof may be locked or out of reach of rescuers."},{text:"I leave the stairwell door open so others can quickly see where the way is",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: an open door lets smoke into the corridor and cuts off the second route too; close doors behind you."}]}],realCase:"In a residence hall, a laptop charger left on a bed overheated and the blanket caught fire. A working smoke alarm woke the roommate. Both got out quickly, closed the door behind them and sounded the fire alarm. At the assembly point the floor warden counted the students, noticed one was missing and told the firefighters at once. The firefighters found him on the floor and brought him out safely. The main lesson: charging on a hard surface, a working smoke alarm, closing the door and a head count save lives."},{id:34,code:"PRO-FIR-550",icon:"HardHat",accent:"#b91c1c",title:"Firefighter core operations",subtitle:"PPE and SCBA, hose and water supply, ladders, forcible entry, ventilation, search, RIT and Mayday, rehab and live-fire training",summary:"This professional course covers the basic tasks every firefighter must perform safely on the fireground: wearing PPE and SCBA, advancing a hose line and securing water supply, raising ladders, forcible entry, coordinated ventilation, search, the rapid intervention crew and the Mayday call, rehab and the safety rules for live-fire training. It is based on international standards and does not replace practical training under qualified instructors.",blocks:[{body:"This is a general course based on international standards: NFPA 1001 (firefighter professional qualifications), NFPA 1500 (occupational safety and health program), NFPA 1403 (live-fire training evolutions), NFPA 1584 (rehabilitation) and the IFSTA firefighting manuals. The Directorate's own SOPs and the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority take precedence over this text. Skills are learned only through supervised practical training and assessment; reading this course does not qualify anyone for interior firefighting."},{heading:"PPE and SCBA",body:"Structural PPE (helmet, hood, coat, trousers, gloves and boots) and the self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA) protect you only when they are complete, correctly worn and in good condition.",points:["Inspect PPE before every shift and after every use: tears, contamination, worn reflective trim or damaged closures mean it goes out of service.","Check the SCBA before every shift: cylinder pressure, face piece seal, low-air alarm and the personal alert safety system (PASS) device.","Put the face piece on and breathe from the SCBA before entering any smoke or any atmosphere that may be toxic; smoke is always treated as poisonous.","Never remove the face piece inside the hazard area, even when the smoke looks light.","Watch your own air: plan the exit so that you leave the hazard area before the low-air alarm, not because of it.","After the fire, clean PPE as the maker and the Directorate's procedure require, because soot and fire residue are harmful to health."]},{heading:"Hose, water supply, ladders and forcible entry",points:["Water supply comes first: an attack line without a reliable supply can run dry at the worst moment. Follow the SOP for hydrant or tanker supply.","Advance the charged hose line with a crew, keep it free of kinks and open the nozzle in short bursts to test it before entering.","Choose the nozzle pattern for the task: a straight or solid stream for reach, a fog pattern for protection and cooling of gases, used as trained.","Before using a ladder, check it, place it on firm ground at the correct angle, heel or secure it and keep clear of overhead power lines.","Forcible entry: try before you force, because the door may be unlocked. Control the door after opening it, since an open door feeds the fire with air."]},{heading:"Ventilation and search",points:["Ventilation must be coordinated with the attack: opening the building without water ready on the fire can cause rapid fire growth and flashover.","Every opening, including the entry door, is ventilation; the incident commander decides when and where the building is opened.","Search in teams, keep contact with the hose line or a wall and tell command which areas have been searched.","Search the most dangerous areas first: near the fire, bedrooms and the places where victims are usually found such as behind doors and under windows.","Watch the signs of flashover and backdraft: heat that forces you down, rolling flames in the smoke, dark turbulent smoke or smoke pulsing at openings. Withdraw and report."]},{heading:"RIT, Mayday, rehab and live-fire training",critical:!0,points:["Never enter a hazardous atmosphere alone. Crews work in pairs or more, and a rescue-ready crew stays outside under NFPA 1500 and the Directorate's SOP.","The rapid intervention team (RIT) has one task: to be ready to rescue firefighters. It is not used for other work.","If you are lost, trapped, out of air or injured, call Mayday at once: who you are, where you are, what happened and the air you have left. Then activate the PASS device, stay calm and save air.","When a Mayday is heard, all other radio traffic stops and command sends the RIT.","Rehab: crews rest, drink, cool down and are medically checked between work periods as NFPA 1584 describes; a firefighter who is not fit does not go back in.","Live-fire training follows NFPA 1403: a written plan, a safety officer, qualified instructors, a checked building or prop, full PPE and SCBA and no real person ever used as a victim."]}],steps:[{title:"Check PPE and SCBA before the shift",detail:"Inspect every item, test the SCBA, the low-air alarm and the PASS device and take anything defective out of service.",critical:!0},{title:"Enter only with a crew and a water supply",detail:"Never enter alone. Confirm the hose line is charged, the water supply is secured and a rescue-ready crew is outside.",critical:!0},{title:"Coordinate ventilation with the attack",detail:"Open the building only when the incident commander orders it and water is ready on the fire."},{title:"Search systematically and report",detail:"Keep contact with the line or a wall, search the most dangerous areas first and report searched areas to command.",critical:!0},{title:"Call Mayday early, then rehab",detail:"If you are in trouble, call Mayday at once and activate the PASS device. After each work period, go to rehab and be checked before you return.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never remove the SCBA face piece in smoke, because fire smoke contains toxic gases that can kill quickly.","Never ventilate before water is ready on the fire, because the added air can cause rapid fire growth and flashover.","Never use a real person as a victim in live-fire training; NFPA 1403 forbids it."],tips:["Practice the Mayday call in every drill so it becomes automatic.","Know your SCBA so well that you can check and don it with gloves on and in the dark.","Drink water during long incidents before you feel thirsty."],activity:{prompt:"During a house fire, your crew is ready at the door with a charged line. Another crew asks to break the windows at the back now. What do you advise?",options:[{text:"Break them all now so the smoke clears fast",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: uncoordinated ventilation feeds the fire with air and can cause flashover."},{text:"Enter without water to find the fire faster",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: entering without a charged line leaves the crew unprotected."},{text:"Wait for the commander to coordinate it with the attack",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: ventilation is opened on the commander's order when water is ready on the fire."},{text:"Open the door wide and leave it open for air",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: an open door also feeds the fire; doors are controlled."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are searching the upper floor of a burning house with your partner. You lose contact with the hose line and cannot find the way back. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Keep searching quietly until you find the line",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: delay uses up air; a lost firefighter calls for help at once."},{text:"Call Mayday with who, where, what and air left",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: an early Mayday gives the RIT the most time to reach you."},{text:"Remove the face piece to see and breathe better",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: fire smoke is toxic; the face piece stays on."},{text:"Separate from your partner to search two ways",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: partners never separate; it creates two lost firefighters."}]},{prompt:"Command acknowledges the Mayday and sends the RIT. You and your partner are in a room with a wall and a window. What now?",options:[{text:"Activate the PASS, stay by the wall and save air",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the PASS and the radio help the RIT find you; calm breathing saves air."},{text:"Turn the radio off to save battery for later",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: command and the RIT need to reach you by radio."},{text:"Walk around the floor to look for the stairs",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: moving without a reference makes you harder to find and uses air."}]}],realCase:"A crew entered a house fire with a charged line while another crew prepared to open the windows. The commander held the ventilation until water was on the fire. Upstairs, one firefighter became separated in heavy smoke and called Mayday at once, giving his position and air. All radio traffic stopped, and the rapid intervention team followed the hose line and the sound of his PASS device and brought him out. After the incident, every crew went to rehab and was checked. The review found that the early Mayday and the ready RIT made the rescue possible.",media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w10_firefighter_core_ops.webp",title:"Firefighter core operations",description:"A crew in full PPE and SCBA advances a charged hose line at a doorway while a second crew stands ready outside as the rapid intervention team."}],quiz:[{question:"Your SCBA low-air alarm sounds while you are still inside. What does it show about your planning?",options:[{text:"It is normal; keep working until the cylinder is empty",correct:!1},{text:"The exit should have started before the alarm sounded",correct:!0},{text:"The alarm is faulty and the SCBA should be ignored",correct:!1},{text:"You should remove the face piece to save the air",correct:!1}],explanation:"Air is planned so that the crew leaves the hazard area before the low-air alarm; the alarm is a warning, not the signal to start leaving."},{question:"Smoke in a room looks light and thin. Which action is right?",options:[{text:"Keep the SCBA face piece on until you leave the hazard",correct:!0},{text:"Lift the face piece to talk more clearly by radio",correct:!1},{text:"Take the face piece off because thin smoke is harmless",correct:!1},{text:"Share your face piece with your partner in turns",correct:!1}],explanation:"Fire smoke contains toxic gases even when it looks thin, so the face piece stays on in the whole hazard area."},{question:"Which ventilation decision is correct?",options:[{text:"Break every window as soon as you arrive",correct:!1},{text:"Open the roof before any hose line is laid",correct:!1},{text:"Open on the commander's order with water ready",correct:!0},{text:"Leave the entry door open wide during the attack",correct:!1}],explanation:"Ventilation must be coordinated with the attack; air without water on the fire can cause rapid fire growth."},{question:"A firefighter is trapped and out of air. What must the radio message include?",options:[{text:'Only the words "help me" repeated',correct:!1},{text:"Who, where, what happened and air left",correct:!0},{text:"The name of the officer in charge",correct:!1},{text:"A request to call 122 for an ambulance",correct:!1}],explanation:"A Mayday gives command and the RIT what they need to find the firefighter: identity, location, problem and air."},{question:"Which of these is wrong for live-fire training?",options:[{text:"A written plan and a safety officer",correct:!1},{text:"Full PPE and SCBA for all participants",correct:!1},{text:"A building checked before the training",correct:!1},{text:"A real person used as the victim",correct:!0}],explanation:"NFPA 1403 forbids using a live person as a victim in live-fire training; the other items are required."},{question:"After a hard work period a firefighter is dizzy and very hot. What is the right step?",options:[{text:"Keep the firefighter in rehab and get a medical check",correct:!0},{text:"Send the firefighter back in after drinking water",correct:!1},{text:"Give the firefighter a lighter task inside",correct:!1},{text:"Let the firefighter decide alone when to go back",correct:!1}],explanation:"Rehab includes rest, cooling, fluids and a medical check; a firefighter who is not fit does not return to work."}]},{id:35,code:"PRO-FIR-551",icon:"Car",accent:"#c2410c",title:"Vehicle fires and alternative-fuel vehicles",subtitle:"Cars, buses and trucks; electric vehicles with lithium-ion batteries; LPG and CNG vehicles",summary:"This professional course covers fires in cars, buses and trucks and the special hazards of alternative-fuel vehicles: electric and hybrid vehicles with lithium-ion batteries, and vehicles running on LPG or CNG. It explains safe approach and positioning, how to identify the vehicle and its fuel, how to immobilize it, the tactics for each type, and why an electric vehicle fire needs large amounts of water for a long time and can reignite later.",blocks:[{body:"This is a general course based on international standards and guidance: the IFSTA firefighting manuals, NFPA alternative-fuel vehicle safety training, NFPA 52 (CNG vehicle fuel systems), NFPA 58 (LPG), the Emergency Response Guidebook (ERG 2024), ISO 17840 rescue sheets and the vehicle makers' emergency response guides. The Directorate's own SOPs and the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority take precedence over this text. It does not replace practical training."},{heading:"Approach and size-up for every vehicle fire",points:["Approach from upwind and uphill where possible, and stay out of any fuel or liquid running from the vehicle.","Park the apparatus to protect the crew from traffic and set up a safe work zone with traffic control.","Wear full PPE and SCBA: smoke from burning plastics, tyres and batteries is highly toxic.","Approach from the side corners and keep out of the front and rear, because bumpers, gas struts and tyres can burst or fly out when heated.","Ask whether anyone is still inside and treat rescue as the first priority.","For buses, check that all passengers are out and kept away from the vehicle; for trucks, look for placards and papers and use the ERG if the cargo is hazardous."]},{heading:"Identify, immobilize and stabilize",points:["Identify the vehicle and fuel before the attack: badges, a charging port, orange high-voltage cables, gas tank labels or a filling valve. A vehicle with no exhaust pipe may be electric.","Use the maker's emergency response guide or the ISO 17840 rescue sheet for that model to find the battery, the high-voltage cut points and the gas tanks.","Immobilize: chock the wheels, put the vehicle in park, switch the ignition off and keep the key away from the vehicle.","An electric vehicle may be silent while it is still on; never assume it is off because it is quiet.","Never cut, touch or pierce orange high-voltage cables or battery casings."]},{heading:"Electric vehicle fires (lithium-ion batteries)",critical:!0,points:["A lithium-ion battery fire is driven by thermal runaway inside the cells; it makes its own heat and releases toxic and flammable gases that can ignite suddenly.","The method is cooling with water: large amounts of water, applied for a long time, as the maker's guide describes. Class D powder is for lithium-metal fires and is not the method for lithium-ion vehicle batteries.","Stranded energy: a damaged battery keeps its charge after the fire is out, so it can still shock and reignite.","Reignition can happen hours or days later. Monitor the battery after the fire, if available with a thermal imaging camera, and warn the towing service and the storage site.","Store a burned electric vehicle outdoors and away from buildings and other vehicles, as the competent authority directs."]},{heading:"LPG and CNG vehicle fires",critical:!0,points:["Gas vehicles have tanks with a pressure relief device (PRD) that can release gas with a loud noise and a long jet of flame when the tank is heated.","Never stand in line with the tank ends or the PRD outlet, and do not approach a tank that is being heated by fire without cooling it first from a protected position.","Stop the gas only if it is safe, for example by closing the tank valve when you can reach it without danger.","Never put out a gas flame if the flow cannot be stopped: escaping gas can collect and explode. Let it burn under control and cool the tank and the area around it.","LPG is heavier than air and collects in low places; CNG is lighter than air and rises. Keep sources of ignition away."]}],steps:[{title:"Size up and position safely",detail:"Approach upwind and uphill, protect the scene from traffic and wear full PPE and SCBA.",critical:!0},{title:"Identify the vehicle and its fuel",detail:"Look for badges, charging ports, orange cables or gas tank labels and use the maker's emergency response guide."},{title:"Rescue and immobilize",detail:"Get everyone out and away, chock the wheels, switch the vehicle off and keep the key away.",critical:!0},{title:"Attack by fuel type",detail:"Conventional fuel: hose line from the side corners. Electric: large amounts of water to cool the battery for a long time.",critical:!0},{title:"Handle gas vehicles with care",detail:"Stop the gas only if safe, never put out a gas flame that cannot be stopped and cool the tank from a protected position.",critical:!0},{title:"Overhaul, monitor and hand over",detail:"Check for hidden fire, monitor the battery for reignition and warn the towing service about the hazards."}],warnings:["Never cut or touch orange high-voltage cables, because they can carry a lethal electric charge even after the fire.","Never put out a gas flame that cannot be stopped, because the escaping gas can build up and explode.","Never stand in front of or behind a burning vehicle or in line with a gas tank, because parts and gas jets can fly out."],tips:["Learn to recognize the common signs of electric and gas vehicles during training.","Plan for a large and long water supply at every electric vehicle fire.","Tell the towing service in writing that the vehicle is electric and may reignite."],activity:{prompt:"You arrive at a burning car in a car park. The driver says it is electric and the fire is under the floor. Which tactic is right?",options:[{text:"Use a Class D powder extinguisher on the battery",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: Class D is for lithium-metal fires, not for lithium-ion vehicle batteries."},{text:"Cool the battery with large amounts of water for a long time",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: cooling with large and sustained water is the method for lithium-ion battery fires."},{text:"Cut the orange cables to isolate the battery first",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: orange cables are high voltage and must never be cut or touched."},{text:"Let it burn out without water and leave the scene",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the fire can spread to other vehicles and the battery can reignite."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A small van is burning at the roadside. You see a gas tank label and hear a loud hiss with a jet of flame from under the vehicle. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Put out the jet flame at once with a hose line",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: if the gas flow cannot be stopped, putting out the flame lets gas build up and explode."},{text:"Approach from the rear to close the tank valve",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: standing in line with the tank and PRD is dangerous."},{text:"Clear the area and cool the tank from a protected position",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the PRD is venting; keep people away, protect the crew and cool the tank."},{text:"Open the doors to look for the gas valve inside",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: approaching a heated tank without cooling and protection is unsafe."}]},{prompt:"The tank has been cooled and the jet flame is getting smaller. A crew member can now reach the tank valve safely. What next?",options:[{text:"Put the flame out first and then close the valve",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: putting out a gas flame before the flow is stopped lets gas escape and collect."},{text:"Close the valve, then let the flame die and keep cooling",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: stop the gas only if safe, and the flame goes out when the fuel stops."},{text:"Leave the valve and send the crew back to the engine",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: stopping the gas safely is the way to end a gas fire."}]}],realCase:"A crew arrived at an electric car burning in a car park. They approached from upwind, confirmed the car was empty and saw a charging port and orange cables. They chocked the wheels, kept away from the cables and cooled the battery area with large amounts of water for a long time, as the maker's guide described. The flames went down, but a thermal camera still showed heat in the battery. The crew kept monitoring, warned the towing service in writing and asked for the car to be stored outdoors away from buildings. The battery reignited the next day in the storage yard without harm to anyone.",media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w10_vehicle_afv_fires.webp",title:"Vehicle fires and alternative-fuel vehicles",description:"Firefighters in full PPE and SCBA cool the battery of an electric car from the side corner, with the wheels chocked and the orange high-voltage cables visible and left untouched."}],quiz:[{question:"Which sign helps you recognize an electric vehicle at a fire?",options:[{text:"A large exhaust pipe at the rear",correct:!1},{text:"A diesel smell from the engine",correct:!1},{text:"Orange cables and a charging port",correct:!0},{text:"A gas filling valve on the bumper",correct:!1}],explanation:"Orange high-voltage cables, a charging port and the lack of an exhaust pipe are common signs of an electric vehicle."},{question:"An electric vehicle fire has been put out. What is the main hazard now?",options:[{text:"Reignition of the battery hours or days later",correct:!0},{text:"The vehicle will roll away by itself",correct:!1},{text:"The tyres will deflate and damage the road",correct:!1},{text:"The paint will release harmless steam",correct:!1}],explanation:"A damaged lithium-ion battery keeps stranded energy and can reignite much later, so it is monitored and the storage site is warned."},{question:"Which is wrong at an electric vehicle fire?",options:[{text:"Chocking the wheels before work",correct:!1},{text:"Cutting the orange cables first",correct:!0},{text:"Using the maker's response guide",correct:!1},{text:"Applying large amounts of water",correct:!1}],explanation:"Orange cables carry high voltage and must never be cut or touched; the other actions are correct."},{question:"A CNG tank PRD is venting with a jet of flame. Where should the crew stand?",options:[{text:"Directly behind the tank to reach the valve",correct:!1},{text:"In front of the vehicle near the engine",correct:!1},{text:"Close to the PRD outlet to cool it fast",correct:!1},{text:"Out of line with the tank, in a protected spot",correct:!0}],explanation:"The PRD and tank ends can release gas and flame or fail; the crew works from a protected position out of line with them."},{question:"Gas is burning from a damaged LPG line and the valve cannot be reached. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Put out the flame quickly with dry powder",correct:!1},{text:"Let it burn under control and cool the area",correct:!0},{text:"Cover the flame with a wet blanket at once",correct:!1},{text:"Spray foam directly onto the flame",correct:!1}],explanation:"A gas flame is never put out when the flow cannot be stopped, because the gas could collect and explode."},{question:"You arrive at a burning bus at a stop. What comes first?",options:[{text:"Make sure all passengers are out and away",correct:!0},{text:"Open the engine cover to find the fire",correct:!1},{text:"Move the bus to the side of the road",correct:!1},{text:"Look for the fuel type in the papers",correct:!1}],explanation:"Life safety comes first: confirm everyone is out and kept away before the attack."}]},{id:36,code:"PRO-CHM-550",icon:"Biohazard",accent:"#b45309",title:"Hazardous-materials operations",subtitle:"Using the ERG, upwind approach, work zones, defensive actions, PPE levels and decontamination",summary:"This course is for operations-level responders who arrive at a hazardous-materials incident: a chemical release from a tanker, a cylinder or a plant. They learn how to identify the material with the ERG 2024, how to approach from upwind and uphill, how to isolate the scene and set hot, warm and cold zones, which defensive actions they may take under NFPA 470 without touching the material and how PPE and the decontamination corridor protect responders and the public.",blocks:[{body:"This is a general course based on international standards: the Emergency Response Guidebook (ERG 2024), NFPA 470 for hazardous-materials responders, the NFPA 704 marking, the UN Globally Harmonized System of classification and labelling (GHS) and OSHA 29 CFR 1910.120. The Directorate's standard operating procedures (SOPs) and the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority take precedence over this text. The course covers the operations level: an operations-level responder works from a distance, protects people and stops the material from spreading without touching it. Plugging leaks and working directly on the container is the job of specialist hazardous-materials technicians."},{heading:"1. Identifying the material with the ERG",points:["Look for markings from a distance, using binoculars: placards on tankers and trailers, the four-digit UN number, GHS labels on containers, the NFPA 704 marking on buildings and the shipping papers held by the driver.","Find the UN number or the material name in the yellow pages (by number) or the blue pages (by name) of the ERG: they give the guide number. Then read that guide in the orange pages: fire and health hazards, public safety, protective clothing, evacuation and emergency response.","If the material is highlighted in the yellow or blue pages, it is toxic by inhalation or gives off a toxic gas with water: take the initial isolation and protective-action distances from the green tables, according to whether the spill is small or large and whether it is day or night.","If the material cannot be identified, the ERG directs you to use guide 111 until more information arrives, and to treat any material as the worst case."]},{heading:"2. Approach, isolation and work zones",body:"Never walk straight into smoke, vapor or spilled material. Approach from upwind, uphill and upstream, and stop at a safe distance. Do not test the material by looking closely, smelling or touching. Then isolate the scene according to the ERG, move uninvolved people away and set the zones: the hot zone, which is contaminated and entered only by teams in suitable PPE; the warm zone, where the decontamination corridor stands; and the cold zone, for command, medical teams and support. Entry to and exit from the hot zone is only through the decontamination corridor. Keep watching the wind direction, because it can shift and move the zones."},{heading:"3. Operations-level defensive actions (NFPA 470)",body:"Defensive actions aim to hold the material back from a distance and keep it away from people, water and drains. They are taken only with training, suitable PPE, the incident commander's order and in line with the SOPs:",points:["Diking, damming and diverting: stop or redirect the liquid with soil, sand or absorbent material, away from drains and rivers.","Absorption and retention: use absorbents that suit the material, as the ERG guide and the SDS direct.","Vapor dispersion and suppression: with water spray or foam, only if the ERG guide allows it; some materials become more dangerous with water.","Remote shut-off and removing ignition sources: close an emergency valve from outside the hot zone if it is safe, and remove every source of sparks.","Work on the container itself (plugging, patching or transferring the product) is not an operations-level task: leave it to the specialist teams."]},{heading:"4. PPE levels and the decontamination corridor",body:"The level of protection is chosen by the hazard of the material, not by what is at hand. Structural firefighting clothing and a self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA) give limited protection, and the ERG guide for each material says whether that clothing is enough. PPE levels under OSHA 1910.120: level A is a fully encapsulating vapor-protective suit with SCBA; level B is a liquid-splash suit with SCBA; level C is a splash suit with an air-purifying respirator (APR), only when the material and its concentration are known; level D is ordinary work clothing with no respiratory protection. The decontamination corridor is set up in the warm zone, upwind of the hot zone, and everyone leaving the hot zone passes through it: first gross decontamination, then removal of PPE in a set order, then technical decontamination. Contaminated victims receive emergency decontamination at once: clothing removed and flushing with plenty of water if the material allows it. All contaminated items are kept in marked bags, and medical teams check responders before entry and after exit."},{heading:"5. Reporting and command",critical:!0,points:["From a safe place, call 115 (122 for casualties): the location, the UN number or material name, the color of the cloud if visible from a distance, the wind direction and the number of people affected.","Set up incident command early in the cold zone, with every team working under one commander.","The decision to evacuate or shelter in place, and the authority to announce it, follow the Directorate's SOPs and the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority."]}],steps:[{title:"1. Stop far back and upwind",detail:"Approach from upwind, uphill and upstream and stop at a safe distance. Then call 115.",critical:!0},{title:"2. Identify the material from a distance",detail:"Read the UN number, placards and labels with binoculars and find the guide in the ERG. If the material is unknown, use guide 111."},{title:"3. Isolate the scene and set the zones",detail:"Take the isolation distance from the orange guide or the green tables, deny entry and set the hot, warm and cold zones.",critical:!0},{title:"4. Protect the public",detail:"As the incident commander and the ERG direct, evacuate people downwind or keep them sheltered indoors, and send contaminated victims to emergency decontamination."},{title:"5. Defensive actions and decontamination",detail:"Contain the material from a distance only with training, suitable PPE and an order, and pass everyone leaving the hot zone through the decontamination corridor."}],warnings:["Never test the material by smelling, touching or moving closer: many toxic gases have no color or smell.","Do not spray water on every material: some react violently with water or give off a toxic gas. Read the ERG guide first.","Do not enter the hot zone to pull out a victim without suitable PPE and training: you may become the next casualty."],tips:["Keep a printed or electronic ERG in every vehicle and practice using it before an incident.","Use a small flag or ribbon on the vehicle to keep seeing the wind direction.","Ask the driver or the plant supervisor for the shipping papers and the SDS: they give more exact information than the placard.","Have the decontamination corridor ready before the first team enters the hot zone."],activity:{prompt:"A tanker has overturned on the road and is leaking a liquid. It carries a placard and the wind blows from the tanker toward you. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Pull back and reposition upwind and uphill",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: you are in the path of the vapor. Protect yourself first, then identify the material from a distance."},{text:"Move closer to read the UN number clearly",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: moving closer downwind puts you inside the vapor. Read it with binoculars from a safe place."},{text:"Spray water on the liquid at once",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the material is not yet identified and may become more dangerous with water."},{text:"Pull the driver from the cab without PPE",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: without PPE and downwind you may become a casualty too. Call 115 and act on orders."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"At a plant loading bay a large cylinder is leaking and a yellow-green cloud is spreading. Workers say it is chlorine. Your team reaches the plant gate. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop upwind, find the guide and green tables in the ERG and isolate the scene",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: chlorine is toxic by inhalation and highlighted in the ERG; isolation and protective distances come from the green tables."},{text:"Go in wearing structural gear to close the cylinder valve",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: working on the container is for specialist teams in suitable PPE, not for the operations level."},{text:"Gather all the workers at the gate on the downwind side",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: gathering people downwind puts them inside the cloud."}]},{prompt:"Some workers reach the gate coughing with burning eyes, their clothing contaminated. The decontamination team has not arrived yet. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Start emergency decon: clothing off and plenty of water, then 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: emergency decontamination is done at once on scene, then the victims go to the medical teams."},{text:"Put them in an ambulance to hospital in their contaminated clothes",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: they carry the material with them and contaminate the ambulance crew and the hospital."},{text:"Wait for the decon team and do nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: every minute the material stays on the skin and eyes adds to the injury."},{text:"Neutralize the chemical on their skin with another chemical",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: neutralizing on skin creates heat and more injury; water is the right choice if the material allows it."}]}],realCase:"On a road outside a town, a trailer ran off the road and vapor began to spread from its tank. The first team stopped at a distance, positioned upwind and read the UN number with binoculars. The ERG showed the material as toxic by inhalation, and the team took the isolation distance from the green tables. The police then closed the road, and residents of a nearby village downwind stayed indoors until the vapor cleared. A specialist team in level A entered through the decontamination corridor and stopped the leak. No responder was injured.",media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w10_hazmat_operations.webp",title:"Hazardous-materials operations",description:"A tanker is leaking while responders upwind read the placard with binoculars; the decontamination corridor is ready in the warm zone."}],quiz:[{question:"You have read the UN number. What do you do next in the ERG?",options:[{text:"Find the guide in the yellow pages and read it in the orange",correct:!0},{text:"Spray water at once and look in the ERG later",correct:!1},{text:"Look up the number in the blue pages, which list by number",correct:!1},{text:"Walk up close to read the GHS label as well",correct:!1}],explanation:"The yellow pages list by UN number and give the guide number; the guide in the orange pages explains the hazards and emergency actions. The blue pages list by name."},{question:"The material is highlighted in the ERG. What does that mean?",options:[{text:"Toxic by inhalation; use the green tables",correct:!0},{text:"The material is harmless and needs no isolation",correct:!1},{text:"It is only dangerous if there is a fire",correct:!1},{text:"It must be washed down with water at once",correct:!1}],explanation:"Highlighted materials are toxic by inhalation or give off a toxic gas with water; isolation and protective distances come from the green tables."},{question:"Where is the decontamination corridor set up?",options:[{text:"In the warm zone, on the upwind side",correct:!0},{text:"Inside the hot zone, next to the leak",correct:!1},{text:"In the cold zone, beside the command post",correct:!1},{text:"Downwind so that the water runs away",correct:!1}],explanation:"The corridor lies between the hot and cold zones, in the warm zone and upwind, so that contamination does not reach the cold zone."},{question:"Which of these is not an operations-level task?",options:[{text:"Patching the hole in the tanker",correct:!0},{text:"Building a soil dike at a distance",correct:!1},{text:"Closing the emergency valve remotely",correct:!1},{text:"Removing sources of ignition",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under NFPA 470, work on the container such as patching and plugging is for specialist teams; the operations level takes defensive actions from a distance."},{question:"The material and its airborne concentration are unknown. Which PPE level is unsuitable?",options:[{text:"Level C with an air-purifying respirator",correct:!0},{text:"Level A with an encapsulating suit and SCBA",correct:!1},{text:"Level B with a splash suit and SCBA",correct:!1},{text:"Staying outside the hot zone until information arrives",correct:!1}],explanation:"An air-purifying respirator is used only when the material and its concentration are known; an unknown material needs SCBA."},{question:"The wind changes direction during the incident. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Reassess the zones and where teams and people are",correct:!0},{text:"Keep everything as it is, since the zones are already set",correct:!1},{text:"Move the decon corridor into the hot zone",correct:!1},{text:"Wait until the wind dies down",correct:!1}],explanation:"When the wind shifts, upwind and downwind change: command, the corridor and the public may end up in the vapor, so the zones are reassessed."}]},{id:37,code:"PRO-MGT-550",icon:"GraduationCap",accent:"#4338ca",title:"Instructor development and assessment",subtitle:"NFPA 1041 instructor levels, lesson plans, objectives, practical assessment, training safety and records",summary:"This course is for staff who teach, run training and assess trainees. They learn the instructor levels of NFPA 1041, how to write a lesson plan and measurable objectives, how to teach with the four-step method, how to assess practical skills against written criteria, how to keep training safe, especially in live-fire evolutions (NFPA 1403), and how to keep records. Certificate criteria, hours and pass marks are Directorate policy.",blocks:[{body:"This is a general course based on international standards: NFPA 1041 for fire and emergency services instructor qualifications, NFPA 1403 for live-fire training evolutions, NFPA 1500 for member safety and the IFSTA instructor manual. The Directorate's standard operating procedures (SOPs) and the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority take precedence over this text. Who may become an instructor, the certificate criteria, the training hours and the pass marks in this app are set by the Directorate and still need official confirmation."},{heading:"1. Instructor levels under NFPA 1041",points:["Instructor I: delivers a lesson from a prepared lesson plan, prepares the training area and equipment, adapts the lesson to the trainees without changing the objectives and administers and records prepared tests.","Instructor II: writes lesson plans and assessment tools, supervises Instructor I staff and analyzes test results to improve the lesson.","Instructor III: designs complete training programs and curricula, identifies training needs and evaluates the program as a whole.","An instructor must also be competent in the subject: whoever teaches rescue must be trained to that level."]},{heading:"2. Lesson plans and objectives",body:`A lesson plan is the instructor's guide, so that the same content reaches trainees at the same standard every time. A good plan includes: the lesson title and level, the learning objectives, equipment and materials, the prior knowledge needed, safety notes, the content in order, practical activities and the assessment method. A learning objective must be a measurable behavior, not "understand" or "know": who (the trainee), does what (for example, uses a fire extinguisher), under what conditions (with training equipment) and to what standard (meeting every item on the checklist). Use the IFSTA four-step method: preparation (why this lesson matters), presentation (explaining and demonstrating), application (the trainee does it under supervision) and evaluation. When you deliver the app's tracks (children, adults, university and professional), adapt the language and examples to the group, but do not change the lesson content itself without the approval of the training officers.`},{heading:"3. Assessing practical skills",points:["Each practical skill is assessed with a written skills checklist: every item names a clear, observable action, and all evaluators use the same list.","Mark the critical safety criteria on the checklist: if the trainee misses one of them, the result is a fail even if every other item is complete.","Explain the criteria to trainees before the test and assess everyone the same way: the test must be fair and repeatable.","Written questions should test application and judgment, not only memory, and each question should link to an objective.","When a trainee does not pass, give clear feedback on the missing action, allow extra practice and then reassess in line with Directorate policy."]},{heading:"4. Safety during training",critical:!0,body:'No training is worth injuring a trainee. Under NFPA 1500 and NFPA 1403, inspect the site and equipment before every session, brief safety and escape routes and stop the session whenever anything is unsafe. In live-fire training evolutions, NFPA 1403 requires: an instructor-in-charge and a separate safety officer with the authority to stop the evolution; a walk-through of the site for all participants before ignition; full PPE and SCBA for all participants; a primary and a backup water supply; a rapid intervention crew; medical services on scene; known and limited fuel; and never a live person used as a "victim" inside the fire. Only the planned fire is lit, and every participant must have completed the required level of training beforehand.'},{heading:"5. Records",points:["Record for every session: the lesson title and level, the date, the instructor, attendance, assessment results and the evaluator.","Keep the signed checklists and the reports of training injuries and near misses.","How long records are kept, who may read them and how certificates are issued follow Directorate policy."]}],steps:[{title:"1. Write the objectives",detail:"Write measurable objectives: who, does what, under what conditions and to what standard."},{title:"2. Prepare the lesson and safety plan",detail:"Set out the content, equipment, activities and safety notes, and inspect the site and equipment before the session.",critical:!0},{title:"3. Teach with the four steps",detail:"Preparation, presentation, supervised application and evaluation."},{title:"4. Assess with the written checklist",detail:"Assess every trainee with the same checklist and the same critical items, and give clear feedback."},{title:"5. Record",detail:"Record attendance, results and any incident in line with Directorate policy."}],warnings:["Never use a live person as a victim inside a training fire: NFPA 1403 does not allow it.","Do not start a live-fire evolution without a separate safety officer and a backup water supply.","Do not invent a certificate or a pass mark: criteria, hours and pass marks are Directorate policy."],tips:["Open the lesson with a real-life question so that trainees see why it matters.","Give more time to doing than to talking: trainees learn by doing.","Hold a short debrief after every session: what went well and what must improve.","Have new instructors teach their first lessons alongside an experienced instructor."],activity:{prompt:"Which of these objectives is correct and measurable?",options:[{text:"The trainee uses an extinguisher on a training fire meeting every checklist item",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the objective states who, what, under what conditions and to what standard."},{text:"The trainee understands why extinguishers matter",correct:!1,feedback:'Wrong: "understanding" cannot be observed or measured; there is no action or standard.'},{text:"The instructor explains the topic of extinguishers",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: an objective describes what the trainee does, not what the instructor does."},{text:"The trainee knows everything about fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: it is far too broad and cannot be measured."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are the instructor-in-charge of a live-fire evolution in a training building. Before ignition you see that the backup water supply has not arrived and two trainees have incomplete gear. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Hold ignition until the water arrives and everyone has full PPE",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: NFPA 1403 requires a backup water supply and full PPE before ignition."},{text:"Light the fire and keep the two trainees outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: without backup water every participant is at risk."},{text:"Make the fire smaller and start with the water at hand",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: even a small fire can grow fast; the requirements are not reduced."}]},{prompt:"After the session, a trainee completes every item in the assessment except the SCBA check before entry, which is a critical item on the checklist. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Record a fail, give feedback and reassess later",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a missed critical item means a fail, and the trainee gets more practice and a new assessment."},{text:"Record a pass, since every other item is complete",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: critical items cannot be offset by other items; this gap can cost a life."},{text:"Lower the score but still record a pass",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: this breaks the written criteria and makes the test unfair."},{text:"Record nothing so the trainee is not upset",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: complete and accurate records are needed for fairness and for the safety of later training."}]}],realCase:`During a live-fire evolution, an instructor added extra fuel to make the fire look "more real." The fire spread quickly and cut off the trainees' exit. The safety officer stopped the evolution, and the rapid intervention crew brought the trainees out. The debrief then showed that the fuel was not in the plan and that the pre-ignition check had been rushed. The lesson plan was updated to specify the fuel exactly, and every instructor received new training on NFPA 1403.`,media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w10_instructor_development.webp",title:"Instructor development",description:"An instructor assesses a trainee on SCBA with a checklist while the safety officer stands nearby."}],quiz:[{question:"An instructor teaches from prepared plans but does not write them. What level is this?",options:[{text:"Instructor I",correct:!0},{text:"Instructor II",correct:!1},{text:"Instructor III",correct:!1},{text:"Safety officer",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under NFPA 1041, Instructor I teaches from prepared plans; Instructor II writes plans and Instructor III designs programs."},{question:"Which objective is best for a CPR lesson?",options:[{text:"The trainee performs CPR on a manikin to the checklist",correct:!0},{text:"The trainee appreciates the importance of CPR",correct:!1},{text:"The instructor shows the trainees a CPR video",correct:!1},{text:"The trainee knows all about the heart",correct:!1}],explanation:'A good objective is an observable, measurable action with conditions and a standard; "appreciating" cannot be measured, and objectives describe what the trainee does.'},{question:"In a live-fire evolution, which of these goes against NFPA 1403?",options:[{text:"A trainee stays in the room acting as a victim",correct:!0},{text:"A separate safety officer is on site",correct:!1},{text:"A rapid intervention crew stands ready",correct:!1},{text:"All participants walk through before ignition",correct:!1}],explanation:"NFPA 1403 does not allow a live person to be used as a victim in the fire; the others are requirements of that standard."},{question:"A trainee misses one critical safety item but does the rest well. What is the result?",options:[{text:"A fail, with feedback and reassessment",correct:!0},{text:"A pass, since most items are correct",correct:!1},{text:"A pass with a lower score",correct:!1},{text:"The instructor decides as he likes",correct:!1}],explanation:"Critical items are not offset by others; written criteria protect fairness and safety."},{question:'What happens in the "application" step of the four-step method?',options:[{text:"The trainee does the skill under supervision",correct:!0},{text:"The instructor only demonstrates the skill",correct:!1},{text:"Certificates are handed to the trainees",correct:!1},{text:"The instructor explains why the lesson matters",correct:!1}],explanation:"In application the trainee does it and the instructor corrects errors; explaining why is preparation and demonstrating is presentation."},{question:"The app shows pass marks. How should an instructor treat them?",options:[{text:"As Directorate policy that needs confirmation",correct:!0},{text:"As an international rule from NFPA 1041",correct:!1},{text:"Changes them freely for each group",correct:!1},{text:"Ignores them and grades by feel",correct:!1}],explanation:"NFPA 1041 does not set pass marks; marks, hours and certificate criteria are Directorate decisions, and an instructor does not change them alone."}]},{id:38,code:"PRO-LAW-550",icon:"ClipboardCheck",accent:"#b45309",title:"Fire inspection and code enforcement",subtitle:"The inspector role, risk-based inspection, checklists, plan review, findings and enforcement",summary:"A fire inspection checks that a building can prevent fire, warn people, control a fire and let everyone get out. This course explains the inspector role in NFPA 1031, risk-based inspection in NFPA 1730, a practical checklist, plan review basics, how to write findings and how enforcement moves from advice to action, including what to do when you find an imminent danger.",blocks:[{body:"This is a general course based on international standards: NFPA 1031 (professional qualifications for fire inspector and plan examiner), NFPA 1730 (fire prevention inspection and code enforcement), NFPA 101 (Life Safety Code), NFPA 10 (portable extinguishers), NFPA 13 and NFPA 25 (sprinklers) and NFPA 72 (fire alarms). The Directorate's own SOPs and the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority take precedence over this text. The applicable law, the powers of an inspector, penalties, inspection intervals and who may enforce are set locally; this course does not state them."},{heading:"1. The inspector role",body:"NFPA 1031 describes what a fire inspector must know and do. The inspector is not there to punish but to make the building safe for the people inside it.",points:["Know the building: occupancy type, number of people, construction, processes and hazardous materials.","Check that fire protection systems exist, work and are maintained.","Recognize hazards, explain them to the owner and agree on how they will be corrected.","Write clear, factual reports that another inspector can follow later.","Work only within the powers given by the Directorate and the competent authority."]},{heading:"2. Risk-based inspection",body:"NFPA 1730 links inspection work to a community risk assessment. Buildings are grouped by risk: places where many people sleep, gather or cannot move by themselves, or where hazardous materials are stored, are inspected more often and more deeply than low-risk places. The groups and the actual intervals are decided by the Directorate and the competent authority, not by the inspector on the day. Use previous reports, fire history and complaints to decide what to look at first."},{heading:"3. Inspection checklist",body:"Walk the whole building in a fixed order, outside first, then each floor, so nothing is missed.",points:["Exits: exit doors open from the inside without a key, corridors and stairs are clear, exit signs and emergency lighting work.","Alarms: the fire alarm panel shows no fault, detectors are not covered and test records are available.","Sprinklers: control valves are open, heads are not painted or blocked and storage stays below the heads.","Extinguishers: the right type for the hazard, visible, reachable, charged and inspected.","Storage: no combustible storage in exits, boiler rooms or electrical rooms, and tidy waste handling.","Electrical: no overloaded sockets, damaged cables or chained extension cords, and panels are closed and clear.","Hazardous materials: labeled containers, safety data sheets, separation of incompatibles and approved storage cabinets."]},{heading:"4. Plan review basics",points:["Plan review checks drawings before construction or a change of use, when correcting a problem is cheapest.","Check the number, width and travel paths of exits for the expected number of people.","Check where alarms, sprinklers, extinguishers and fire-resisting walls and doors are shown.","Compare the finished building with the approved plans before it is occupied.","Refer specialist questions to a qualified engineer as the Directorate's procedures require."]},{heading:"5. Findings, enforcement and imminent danger",critical:!0,body:"Write each finding with its location, what you saw, why it is a hazard, the standard or rule it relates to and the agreed correction. Enforcement usually moves step by step: explain and advise, issue a written notice with a deadline, re-inspect, then escalate through the procedure set by the competent authority. An imminent danger, such as locked exits with people inside, an active gas leak or a fire system shut off in an occupied building, cannot wait for a deadline: protect people first, inform your supervisor at once and act under the Directorate's SOPs. Call 115 when there is fire, smoke or a gas leak."}],steps:[{title:"Prepare",detail:"Read previous reports, the risk group, approved plans and open complaints, and carry your identification and protective equipment."},{title:"Meet the person in charge",detail:"Explain the purpose of the visit and ask for test and maintenance records and someone who can open every room."},{title:"Walk the building in order",detail:"Go outside first, then each floor, and check exits, alarms, sprinklers, extinguishers, storage, electrical and hazardous materials."},{title:"Act on imminent danger at once",detail:"If people are at immediate risk, protect them first, call your supervisor and follow the Directorate's SOPs before continuing.",critical:!0},{title:"Record and explain findings",detail:"Write each finding factually, explain it to the person in charge and agree on corrections within the procedure."},{title:"Follow up",detail:"Re-inspect as the procedure requires and escalate only through the steps set by the competent authority."}],warnings:["Never leave a building with an imminent danger unreported because the owner promised to fix it later.","Do not quote a penalty, deadline or legal power unless the Directorate has confirmed it.","Do not enter hazardous rooms without the right protective equipment and someone who knows the room."],tips:["Photograph each finding with a clear reference to its location.","Use the same checklist on every visit so reports can be compared.","Explain the reason behind each correction; owners comply better when they understand it.","Praise what is done well as well as listing what must change."],activity:{prompt:"During a routine inspection of a busy shop you find the rear exit chained shut while customers are inside. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Have the exit opened now and report it as imminent danger",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a locked exit with people inside is an imminent danger; it is opened now and reported under the SOPs."},{text:"Write it in the report and give the owner a deadline",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a deadline leaves people trapped if a fire starts today; imminent danger needs action now."},{text:"Finish the checklist first and mention it at the end",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the danger exists right now; finishing the checklist first delays the most important correction."},{text:"Tell the owner a fine amount to make him act quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: penalties are set locally and must not be invented; the priority is opening the exit."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You inspect a small factory. The sprinkler control valve is closed and solvent drums are stored beside the electrical panel. Workers are inside. What is your first action?",options:[{text:"Treat the closed valve as imminent danger and inform your supervisor now",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: an occupied building without working sprinklers and with a fire load near ignition is an imminent danger."},{text:"Open the valve yourself and continue the inspection",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the valve may be closed for a reason such as a leak; the system owner must restore it safely and it must be recorded."},{text:"Note both issues and return next month",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the workers are at risk today; a later visit does not protect them."}]},{prompt:"The owner has the valve restored and moves the drums. Two weeks later you re-inspect and find the drums back beside the panel. What next?",options:[{text:"Record the repeat finding and escalate through the set procedure",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a repeated violation is documented and moves to the next enforcement step set by the competent authority."},{text:"Give another verbal warning and leave without a record",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: without a record the history is lost and enforcement cannot move forward."},{text:"Close the factory yourself on the spot",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: closure powers are local; act only within the powers and procedure the Directorate has set."},{text:"Move the drums yourself so the report is clean",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the owner is responsible for correcting hazards, and hiding the repeat finding defeats enforcement."}]}],realCase:"An inspector visited a wedding hall in the afternoon. Staff had stacked chairs in the side corridor and chained one exit to stop guests leaving without paying. The manager promised to clear it before the evening. The inspector explained that the hall would fill within hours and that a promise does not open a door. She asked for the chain to be removed while she waited, photographed the corridor before and after, informed her supervisor and wrote the finding. On the re-inspection the exits were clear and staff had been briefed.",media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w10_fire_inspection.webp",title:"Fire inspector checking an exit",description:"An inspector with a checklist checks that an exit door opens and the corridor is clear."}],quiz:[{question:"Which building would risk-based inspection usually visit most often?",options:[{text:"A hospital ward where patients cannot walk",correct:!0},{text:"A small storage shed with no people",correct:!1},{text:"An empty office used once a month",correct:!1},{text:"A closed garage with no fuel inside",correct:!1}],explanation:"NFPA 1730 ties frequency to risk; people who cannot move by themselves raise the risk. The intervals are set locally."},{question:"You find a sprinkler control valve closed in an occupied store. What first?",options:[{text:"Write a finding with a one-month deadline",correct:!1},{text:"Treat it as imminent danger and inform your supervisor",correct:!0},{text:"Ignore it if the alarm system is working",correct:!1},{text:"Ask a customer to keep watch for fire",correct:!1}],explanation:"An occupied building without working sprinklers cannot wait; act at once under the SOPs."},{question:"Which finding is written correctly?",options:[{text:"Exits are in bad condition, fix them soon",correct:!1},{text:"Rear exit on ground floor chained shut",correct:!0},{text:"The owner seems careless about fire safety",correct:!1},{text:"Several problems were found in the building",correct:!1}],explanation:"A finding states location and what was seen in facts another inspector can check."},{question:"An owner asks what fine he will pay. What is the right answer?",options:[{text:"Name a typical amount to warn him",correct:!1},{text:"Say there is no fine for safety problems",correct:!1},{text:"Explain the procedure set by the competent authority",correct:!0},{text:"Promise no fine if he pays you now",correct:!1}],explanation:"Penalties are local law; never invent them, and never accept money."},{question:"When is plan review most useful?",options:[{text:"After the first fire in the building",correct:!1},{text:"Only when a neighbor complains",correct:!1},{text:"After people have moved in",correct:!1},{text:"Before construction or a change of use",correct:!0}],explanation:"Correcting exits and systems on drawings is far easier than after construction."},{question:"Which inspection action is wrong?",options:[{text:"Checking that exit doors open without a key",correct:!1},{text:"Skipping locked rooms to save time",correct:!0},{text:"Asking for alarm test records",correct:!1},{text:"Checking storage below sprinkler heads",correct:!1}],explanation:"Locked rooms often hide storage and electrical hazards; ask for them to be opened."}]},{id:39,code:"BUS-ELE-450",icon:"Sun",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Solar panels and battery storage hazards",subtitle:"Rooftop solar, home and business batteries, isolation, thermal runaway and what owners and responders must know",summary:"Rooftop solar panels and battery storage are now common in homes, shops and farms. Panels keep producing electricity whenever there is light, and batteries can overheat, release toxic and flammable gases, explode and reignite. This course explains the hazards, isolation and rapid shutdown, safe actions for owners and responders and why only a qualified installer should design, install and maintain these systems.",blocks:[{body:"This is a general course based on international standards: NFPA 70 (National Electrical Code) Article 690 for solar photovoltaic (PV) systems and Article 706 for energy storage systems, NFPA 855 (installation of stationary energy storage systems), UL 9540 and UL 9540A (safety and fire testing of storage systems) and IFSTA guidance for responders. The Directorate's own SOPs and the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority take precedence over this text. Installation rules, permits and inspection requirements are set locally; this course does not state them."},{heading:"1. Solar panels stay energized",body:"A solar panel turns light into direct current (DC). Turning off the main breaker or the grid does not stop the panels: as long as there is daylight, and even under strong artificial light, the panels and the cables from them carry voltage.",points:["Treat every panel, cable and inverter as energized during daylight.","DC does not pass through zero like grid current, so a DC arc can keep burning and is hard to break.","Broken or burned panels can still produce voltage and can energize metal roof parts or water.","Never cut, step on or break panels, and never cover them as a way to make them safe unless trained and equipped."]},{heading:"2. Isolation and rapid shutdown",body:"NFPA 70 Article 690.12 requires many rooftop PV systems on buildings to have rapid shutdown: one labeled switch that reduces the voltage in conductors outside the array and limits it within the array. It reduces the hazard but does not make the panels themselves dead. Isolation means opening the DC disconnect, the AC disconnect and the battery disconnect in the order the installer has labeled. Owners should know where each switch is, and labels at the main panel should show that a PV system and a battery are present."},{heading:"3. Battery storage and thermal runaway",critical:!0,body:"Most home and business batteries are lithium-ion. Damage, overcharging, a manufacturing fault, water entry or overheating can start thermal runaway: a cell heats itself, vents gases, catches fire and passes heat to the next cell.",points:["The vented gases are toxic and flammable, and can include carbon monoxide, hydrogen and hydrogen fluoride.","Gas collecting in a closed room or cabinet can explode before any flame is seen.","A battery fire can reignite hours or days after it seems to be out.","Lithium-ion batteries are cooled with large amounts of water; Class D powder is for lithium-metal batteries only.","Signs of trouble: swelling, hissing or popping, a sweet or chemical smell, white or gray vapor and heat from the enclosure."]},{heading:"4. Safe installation and owner duties",points:["Use only a qualified installer and listed equipment, and follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority on permits and inspection.","NFPA 855 and UL 9540A guide where batteries may be placed, spacing, ventilation, detection and protection.","Install batteries away from exits, bedrooms and fuel, in a ventilated place protected from heat, sun and water.","Keep labels, disconnects and a simple site plan visible, and tell responders that PV and a battery are present.","Have the system checked and repaired only by qualified people, and never use damaged or swollen batteries."]},{heading:"5. Responder hazards",body:"For responders, the main hazards are electric shock from energized panels and cables, roof collapse and trip hazards from heavy arrays, toxic smoke, explosion of gases from a battery and reignition. Size up from a distance, ask the owner what is installed, approach batteries from upwind and treat the system as energized. Use full protective equipment with breathing apparatus, do not open battery enclosures, apply water from a safe distance and keep watching the battery after the fire seems out. Follow the Directorate's SOPs for isolation, ventilation and handover; call 115 for any fire, smoke or vapor from a battery."}],steps:[{title:"Recognize the signs",detail:"Smoke, vapor, swelling, hissing or a chemical smell from a battery or inverter means danger."},{title:"Get everyone out and away",detail:"Leave the room or building, close the door behind you and keep people upwind and away.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 115",detail:"Tell the dispatcher that the building has solar panels and a battery and where they are."},{title:"Isolate only if safe",detail:"Use the labeled rapid shutdown or disconnect only if it is outside the danger area and you can reach it without smoke."},{title:"Do not go back in",detail:"Do not try to fight a battery fire or re-enter the room; gas can explode and the fire can reignite.",critical:!0},{title:"Brief the responders",detail:"Give them the site plan, switch locations and battery type, and keep the area closed after the fire."}],warnings:["Panels produce voltage whenever there is light, even after the grid and the main breaker are off.","Never open, cut or move a hot, swollen or smoking battery.","A battery that seems out can reignite later; keep the area closed and watched."],tips:["Ask the installer to label every switch and show you how to use rapid shutdown.","Keep the battery area clear of stored goods, fuel and waste.","Check the battery and inverter for warnings, heat or smell during routine visits.","Keep a copy of the site plan near the main electrical panel for responders."],activity:{prompt:"A shop owner smells a sharp chemical odor and sees white vapor coming from the battery cabinet in the storeroom. What should he do first?",options:[{text:"Get everyone out, close the door and call 115",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: vapor from a battery means thermal runaway may have started; gas can be toxic and explosive."},{text:"Open the cabinet to see which battery is hot",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: opening the cabinet adds air and exposes him to toxic gas and possible explosion."},{text:"Spray the cabinet with a powder extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: powder does not cool lithium-ion cells; this is a job for responders with water."},{text:"Switch off the main breaker and wait inside",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the battery keeps reacting without grid power; staying inside exposes him to the gases."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You arrive at a house fire. The roof has solar panels and the owner says a battery is in the garage. Smoke comes from the garage. What is your first priority?",options:[{text:"Size up, confirm people are out and treat the system as energized",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: life safety first, then hazards: energized panels and a battery that may explode."},{text:"Send a firefighter onto the roof to cover the panels",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: climbing on an energized and possibly weakened roof is a serious shock and fall risk."},{text:"Open the garage door wide to vent the smoke at once",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: opening suddenly can mix flammable gas with air and cause an explosion; ventilation follows the SOP."}]},{prompt:"The fire is knocked down. An hour later the battery is quiet but still warm. What next?",options:[{text:"Keep cooling and monitoring and hand over with a reignition warning",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: lithium-ion batteries can reignite hours or days later; the owner and the next team must be warned."},{text:"Declare the scene safe and let the owner move the battery",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a warm damaged battery can go back into thermal runaway; only qualified people remove it."},{text:"Cover the battery with a tarp to keep rain off",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: covering traps heat and gas and hides signs of reignition."},{text:"Reset the rapid shutdown so the house has power",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: re-energizing a damaged system is a shock and fire hazard; a qualified installer must inspect it first."}]}],realCase:"A family had a battery installed in a small closed room next to the kitchen, with no labels and no ventilation. One evening the room filled with white vapor and a sweet smell. The father opened the door to look and saw no flame, so he called a relative instead of emergency services. Minutes later the gas ignited with a loud bang that broke a window. Everyone had moved outside by then. Responders cooled the battery for a long time and stayed to watch it. The installer later moved the system to a ventilated place.",media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w10_solar_battery_hazards.webp",title:"Rooftop solar panels with a battery cabinet",description:"A house with rooftop solar panels, a labeled rapid shutdown switch and a battery cabinet in a ventilated place."}],quiz:[{question:"The grid and main breaker are off at a house with rooftop solar. It is midday. What is true?",options:[{text:"The panels and their cables can still carry voltage",correct:!0},{text:"The whole solar system is now completely dead",correct:!1},{text:"Only the inverter still carries any voltage",correct:!1},{text:"The panels stop working when the grid goes off",correct:!1}],explanation:"Panels produce DC whenever there is light; switching off the grid does not stop them."},{question:"What does rapid shutdown under NFPA 70 690.12 do?",options:[{text:"It makes every panel completely safe to touch",correct:!1},{text:"It reduces voltage in conductors outside the array",correct:!0},{text:"It puts out a battery fire automatically",correct:!1},{text:"It disconnects the house from the grid only",correct:!1}],explanation:"Rapid shutdown lowers the hazard in conductors, but the panels themselves can stay energized."},{question:"A battery cabinet is releasing white vapor but no flame is visible. What first?",options:[{text:"Open the cabinet to find the faulty cell",correct:!1},{text:"Wait to see whether a flame appears",correct:!1},{text:"Get everyone out, close the door and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Turn on a fan inside the room to clear it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Vapor means thermal runaway; the gas is toxic and can explode before any flame appears."},{question:"Which agent is right for cooling a lithium-ion home battery fire?",options:[{text:"Large amounts of water applied by responders",correct:!0},{text:"Class D powder because it is a lithium battery",correct:!1},{text:"Sand thrown on it until the smoke stops",correct:!1},{text:"A carbon dioxide extinguisher from close up",correct:!1}],explanation:"Lithium-ion cells need cooling with water; Class D is for lithium-metal batteries."},{question:"Which owner action is wrong?",options:[{text:"Using a qualified installer and listed equipment",correct:!1},{text:"Keeping the battery away from exits and bedrooms",correct:!1},{text:"Placing the battery in a closed room without air flow",correct:!0},{text:"Labeling the disconnects near the main panel",correct:!1}],explanation:"Gas from a failing battery collects in a closed room and can explode; place it where it is ventilated."},{question:"A battery fire was put out an hour ago. Why do responders keep watching it?",options:[{text:"Because the panels on the roof may fall",correct:!1},{text:"Because the battery may reignite later",correct:!0},{text:"Because the owner must sign a paper first",correct:!1},{text:"Because water must dry before leaving",correct:!1}],explanation:"Damaged cells can return to thermal runaway hours or days later."}]}],t={lessons:e};export{t as default,e as lessons};
